window.SEARCH_INDEX=[{"t":"1. A Room too Small for this World","k":"Chronicles · chapter","u":"chronicles/c1.html","s":"The first thing I saw when I opened my eyes was the ceiling. Yellowed plaster, swollen where water had soaked through over the years, cracked like dried mud. A slow tick came from somewhere near the warped fan, like the whole room was being held together by habit instead of nails. I breathed in. The air scraped through my chest like it had to fight for the space. Cough. It tore out of me on its own—a dry, ugly spasm that shook my whole body. Pain flared under my ribs. The room blurred, then settled. It was a small room. One bed, more frame than mattress. A wooden stool doing the job of a bedside table. Shelves nailed together from mismatched planks, sagging under plastic containers and a few folded clothes. One grimy window let in grey afternoon light, and with it, the noise of traffic and the buzz of something electric outside. \"Arin!\" A woman leaned over me from the left. Dark hair tied back in a fraying band, deep lines carved around her eyes. She looked older than she probably was. Her hands were rough, the nails broken, stained at the tips with something like dye. She pressed the back of her fingers to my forehead. \"Fever again,\" she muttered. \"You just won't listen, will you?\" I knew that face now. That voice. In this life, she was my mother. \"Ma,\" I croaked. The word felt familiar and wrong at the same time, \"Don't talk.\" She reached for a dented kettle on the stove across the room. \"Here. Drink. Slowly.\" I pushed myself up on shaking elbows. My arms felt hollow, like someone had scooped out the muscle and left only skin and stubbornness behind. The thin blanket slid down. Ribs showed through my chest, each breath lifting them too sharply. The cup touched my lips. Bitter, over‑boiled herbs, and under that, the chalky taste of cheap medicine. I drank, because fighting her would cost energy I didn't have. While I drank, my mind kept working anyway. It always did. This body is trash. Not a complaint. A fact. I went through it the same flat way I used to sort topics for an exam: weak lungs, probably scarred. A heart that thudded unevenly behind my sternum. Low muscle, no fat to spare. No marks on the skin—nothing to say I'd ever been touched by anything stronger than bad luck. Thirteen years old, and already running on empty. In short, nothing about this body looked like it was built to survive whatever this world expected of it. I let my eyes drift over the room. Concrete walls, paint flaking at the corners. The floor swept clean, though the cracks between the tiles still held old dust. On the far wall, an old screen flickered weakly, sound turned low, words crawling along the bottom. \"…disturbance near the Western Rim has settled… joint response alongside a visiting delegation… officials say there is no danger to nearby districts…\" The pictures didn't make sense to me. A stretch of ocean lit blue from underneath. People hovering above the water, arms out, light bending around their hands like it was listening to them. One name flashed under a man holding the air itself still in a shape no hand should be able to hold. I had no memory that explained any of this. No story I could reach for. It wasn't déjà vu. It was just strange, the way anything is strange the first time you see it and have no shelf to put it on. Then the rest of it came back, in pieces, the way water finds the lowest point in a floor. A horn. Rain on a windshield. Headlights, too close, too fast. I pushed that away. One thing at a time. So. Not a dream. Not something I would wake from properly and laugh about later. A different body. A different mother. A different sky, apparently, with things living in it that bent light and stood on water. And me, in the middle of it, with lungs that couldn't survive a flight of stairs. In my old life, I hadn't had anything special either. No money behind me, no family name that opened doors. Just a stubborn need to stop being invisible, and an exam I'd been chasing for years—the kind that tests everything a government could ask of a person, on the theory that if you can master all of it, you can be trusted with any of it. I'd buried myself in it. History, economics, the slow grinding logic of how a country governs itself. I hadn't passed. Death had gotten there first. Now, somehow, I'd landed in a body too weak to climb stairs, in a world I didn't recognize at all. For a heartbeat, nothing happened. Then a thin line of light traced itself at the edge of my vision, coalescing into a translucent rectangle. **[STELLAR ARCHIVE — BOOT SEQUENCE…]** **[Body compatibility: 23%]** **[Recommendation: Stop making demands on this chassis.]** \"What is this?,\" I muttered. \"Hmm?\" Ma glanced back. \"Nothing, Ma,\" I said quickly. \"Just… talking to myself.\" Her expression tightened, but she turned away. *Is this a system?* The interface flickered as if responding. Words rearranged themselves with the flat politeness of official instructions: **[Yes, I am but a bigger and better version of what you are thinking about]** **[Primary Directive: Guide user toward survivable threshold.]** **[Secondary Directive: Grade user's life choices, Harshly.]** I'd seen systems in games that showered you with starter packs and glowing arrows. This one felt more like a bored examiner flipping through my answer sheet. \"Can you show me something?\" I asked it silently. \"Anything?\" There was a pause. **[Access denied.]** **[Core functions locked until user demonstrates minimal narrative stability.]** \"What does that even mean?\" I breathed. The Archive did not elaborate. Another line appeared instead: **[Unlock condition — Stage 1: Enter Institute of Inter world studies, General Track.]** I went still. The Institute. Here, the Institute exam seemed to be the only door not locked from both sides. And my system would not even fully boot until I walked through it. \"You're joking,\" I whispered to the hovering text. No reply. Of course. On the screen, the segment shifted. Clean uniforms, training grounds, an anchor reading out dates in a bright, rehearsed voice. The Institute's name kept flashing under the footage. \"Ma,\" I said. She glanced back. \"Yes?\" \"What is this Institute that keeps flashing in the news?\" I asked, just to be sure. She turned and looked at me, shocked, as if I had asked something obvious. \"The Institute of Interworld Studies,\" she began. \"It is a school for higher education, which was established ninety years ago to research and nurture the changes that occurred in the world with that fog.\" Fog. Changes. People standing on water. She gave me a brief summary. \"Fog? Changes?\" I asked. \"Did you forget? Your history teacher in middle school must have told you about it,\" she finished. \"Do we have any… papers? Books? About the Institute exam?\" I changed the topic. Her face shut like a door. \"Arin.\" Her voice dropped, careful and tired at once. \"You know we're not—those coaching centres charge more in a month than I earn in two. We are not that kind of family.\" \"I'm not asking for coaching,\" I said, as gently as I could manage. \"Just the syllabus. Old papers. Anything.\" She didn't answer right away. Her hands had gone still over the stove. \"You used to ask me that,\" she said finally, quietly, like the words cost her something to let out. \"Before. When you could still get out of bed on your own. You'd come home from the market with torn‑up pages someone had thrown out—half a page of some old guidebook, nothing useful—and you'd read them under the lamp until I made you sleep. You begged me for money for a proper book, once. I told you we didn't have it.\" She stopped. \"I still don't have it.\" I hadn't asked. He had. Whatever was left of the boy whose body this was, whoever he'd been before I woke up wearing his ribs and his cough—he had already wanted this. Already understood, in a way I was only now catching up to, that this exam was the only door in this house that wasn't locked from both sides. That changed something in me. Not because I needed a reason. Because now I had two of them, stacked on top of each other. \"There's the library,\" she said, before I could speak again. \"City Central. Near the old university. They have machines you can use for free, but getting in costs something, and you—\" She didn't finish \"you can't even walk to the corner without coughing yourself sick.\" She didn't have to. I heard it anyway. \"The library,\" I repeated. She nodded, reluctant. \"Big building. I've passed it on the bus. Never gone in. People like us don't really—\" She stopped herself again, straightened her back like she was annoyed at her own sentence. \"Forget it. Sleep. We'll talk later.\" I let her think I'd dropped it. Inside my head, old habits were already lining themselves up like soldiers. If there's a library, there are books. If there's free access, there's a syllabus. If there's a syllabus, there's a path. There is always, somehow, a path. I watched her move around the room—the way her shoulders dropped when she thought I wasn't looking, the extra second her hand rested on the doorframe before she made herself walk out to face another shift. I owed her more than dying quietly in this bed while she pretended she wasn't scared. I owed some version of myself—both of us, maybe, whoever we'd each been before this—more than letting one exam decide what I was worth without putting up a fight. The Archive's window pulsed faintly at the edge of my vision. **[Warning: Current body structure fails multiple basic stability checks.]** **[Attempting library run in this condition is statistically unwise.]** \"Yeah?\" I whispered. \"So was deciding to prepare for UPSC with no backup plan. Look how that turned out.\" I swung my legs over the side of the bed. My feet hit the cold floor. The room tilted. Black crept in at the edges of my sight. I waited it out, counting, breathing as steady as these lungs would allow. Then I stood. Every joint complained. My chest burned. Sweat broke out cold along my spine. I took one step, one hand braced against the wall. Then another. I had no money, no backing, no power. The only thing however I has was the mulish discipline that had once made me read the same Laxmikanth three times over, just to be sure."},{"t":"2. No Entry","k":"Chronicles · chapter","u":"chronicles/c2.html","s":"*I am a new author please point out any inconsistencies* Cough. *\"HAH—\"* Cough. *\"HAH—\"* Cough. **[Health condition worsening with each step. Immediate medical attention rec—]** \"Shut up.\" I said it without looking at the translucent window. Didn't need to. I could feel it hovering at the edge of my vision like a disappointed parent. \"If you care so much—\" I stopped mid-sentence as another cough tore through me, \"—then do something *useful.*\" **[I am an Archive. Not a hospital.]** \"Then stop sending me hospital reports.\" Silence from the window. A small victory. I gripped the bridge railing like it owed me something and kept walking. The sky above Vasantpur had turned the colour of old bruises — black at the edges, dark grey at the centre, with the particular heaviness that meant rain in the next hour. The air tasted oily. It always did here. The wind carried the smell of industrial dye from the textile blocks on the east side, the kind of smell that clung to clothes and hair and eventually stopped registering because it never left. This was Vasantpur. Fully-fledged factory district, home to daily wage workers, machine operators, dye mixers, and one thirteen-year-old idiot attempting a forty-minute walk on lungs that had the structural durability of wet paper. I paused at the midpoint of the bridge and made the mistake of looking up. On the other side of the river — *that* Vasantpur. The commercial district. Casinos with holo-facades cycling through their jackpot animations. Guild buildings with actual stonework facades and doormen in pressed uniforms. Markets where the stalls had roofs. Mansions tucked behind security barriers. And there, rising above all of it like an argument made out of glass and sandstone — the library. \"...Hah.\" It looked further than it had from the map. Everything does, when your legs are shaking. **[For the record,]** the Archive said, **[I advised against this.]** \"For the record,\" I said back, \"you advise against everything.\" **[I advised against getting out of bed this morning. You currently appear to be proving my point.]** I opened my mouth to answer. And then the vertigo hit. It didn't arrive gradually. It arrived the way a wall arrives when you walk into it — sudden, total, and humiliating. The bridge tilted. The sky and the river swapped positions. My knees found the ground before I gave them permission. *Cold stone. Good. Focus on that.* I pressed my palm flat against the footpath and breathed — or tried to. My lungs were having a meeting about whether to cooperate and hadn't reached a consensus. \"You are going to get yourself killed, boy.\" The voice came from my left. I turned my head very slowly, because turning it quickly had not worked out for me in the last thirty seconds. A car. Black. Long. The kind of vehicle that didn't drive through this part of Vasantpur by accident. The chassis was lacquered to a shine that reflected the grey sky like a mirror, and embossed along the side, in letters subtle enough to be tasteful and expensive enough to be intimidating, was a single word: *VOYAGE.* The back door was open. An old man sat inside, watching me with the expression of someone who had seen many things and found most of them less interesting than this. \"Hop in,\" he said. I stared at him. He stared back. **[I would like to flag,]** the Archive said, **[that accepting transportation from unknown individuals is statistically—]** \"Where are you headed?\" the old man asked. \"The library,\" I said. A pause. His gaze moved — unhurried, methodical — from my face, to my clothes, to my hand still braced against the footpath. His expression did not change, exactly. But something shifted behind his eyes. Assessment completed. \"In that condition?\" he said. \"Yes.\" He looked at me for another moment. Then he laughed. Not a polite laugh. A genuine one — the kind that comes from somewhere deep and takes a moment to finish. \"Pfft— you— the way you just said that—\" He pressed a hand against his chest like he needed to contain it. **[Look at yourself,]** the Archive said, which I ignored, because I already knew what I looked like. I had checked the rearview mirror the moment I sat down. A pale, rigid face, jaw set, posture so forcibly straight it looked architectural. And ears, apparently, the colour of a traffic light. **[Your ears are redder than a tomato.]** \"SHUT UP.\" The old man laughed harder. He dropped me at the bottom of the library steps without asking my name or giving his. Just a brief look in the rearview mirror as I climbed out — still amused, still assessing — and then the Voyage was gone, silent as it had arrived. *Who was that.* **[Unknown. Vehicle registration is blocked behind a private Guild license.]** *Of course it is.* I turned to face the library. It was bigger up close. The lower floors were old — faded sandstone, wide steps worn smooth by decades of use, thick columns that had clearly been built when people believed buildings should look like they intended to remain. Above that, modernity had happened: glass and steel climbing another ten stories, wrapped in a lattice of blue-lit circuitry that pulsed at irregular intervals, like a heartbeat that hadn't quite settled into rhythm. Holo-banners rotated slowly above the main entrance. **CITY CENTRAL PUBLIC LIBRARY** *Knowledge Access Initiative — Open Terminals Available* *New Section: Basic Fog Phenomenology & Foreign Civilizations (Public Tier)* I straightened my shirt. Patched at the elbow. Washed colourless. The shoes were scuffed at both toes and I'd stopped being able to tell what colour they'd originally been. *It doesn't matter what I'm wearing.* I climbed the steps. At the top, a security gate hummed with the quiet authority of something that had turned away a lot of people. To the right of it, a guard stood with arms folded and smart-glasses that projected data I couldn't see. Sidearm at his belt. A baton with a charge-crackle that suggested it had been used before and hadn't minded. He scanned my civic card without being asked. The lenses flickered as text scrolled across them. \"Arin Valen.\" His voice was flat. Professional. \"Local resident. No ability certification. No Blessings. No institutional affiliation.\" He looked up. The look lasted exactly long enough. \"Purpose?\" \"Study materials,\" I said. \"Public terminals.\" \"Entry fee is twenty rupees. Three hours. After that, pay again or leave.\" Twenty rupees. In my previous life, that was a cheap lunch in Delhi. Here, it was four days of my mother's medicine budget. It was the difference between this week's groceries and next week's. It was — in the most practical possible terms — not a number I had. \"I don't have that much,\" I said. The guard's expression didn't change. \"This isn't a shelter,\" he said. \"Come back when you can pay. Move along.\" He turned slightly. The universal signal for *conversation concluded.* Something hot and ugly moved through my chest. I noted it, set it aside, and kept my voice level. *Arguing won't work. He doesn't make the rules.* *But he might know who does.* \"I'm not asking for charity,\" I said. \"Do you have a labour exchange program?\" The guard turned back. Slowly. Like he wasn't sure he'd heard correctly. \"What?\" \"Cleaning. Shelving. Sorting. Inventory.\" I kept my tone factual, not pleading — pleading changes the frame of the conversation and this was not a conversation I could afford to lose on framing. \"Reading access in exchange for work. Some institutions run arrangements like this. I'm asking if this one does.\" The guard looked at me for a long moment. Then he looked at my hands. Then my frame. Then he did the arithmetic behind his eyes that people always did when they looked at this body, and I knew what sum he arrived at before he opened his mouth. \"You're not exactly moving-boxes weight,\" he said. Not unkind. Just tired. \"I know,\" I said. \"I'm suggesting cleaning. Shelving. Sorting. Things that don't require me to lift anything heavier than a book. Which is what I came here for anyway.\" \"And you won't steal anything,\" the guard said, in the tone of someone who has heard that sentence before. \"No. I won't.\" He looked at me. I looked back. Somewhere above us, the holo-banner cycled through its rotation. \"Even if that kind of scheme existed,\" he said finally, \"it wouldn't be up to me. And we don't just let in every—\" he stopped, chose his words, \"—every applicant who shows up without the fee and promises to work hard. I'd have fifty people at this gate by next week.\" It wasn't cruel. It was just the particular exhaustion of someone who applied rules he didn't write to people he didn't know. I opened my mouth, not yet certain what I'd say. And then something in my chest *lurched.* Not a cough. Something deeper. A sudden, total wrongness — like my heartbeat had skipped a line and couldn't find where it had left off. The steps beneath me tilted. The guard said something I couldn't parse. The sky went very bright for a second, then very dark. I gripped the gate. **[Cardiac arrhythmia detected,]** the Archive said, and for once the tone was not sarcastic. **[Sit down now, This is not optional.]** My legs agreed with the Archive. The stone steps came up to meet me and I stopped fighting the descent. I sat — hard, ungraceful, one hand still locked around the gate bar — and put my head down and breathed. Small breaths. Count them. One. Two. \"Hey.\" The guard's voice, from above me, had changed register. \"Kid.\" Three. Four. Footsteps. Someone else. Different shoes — softer soles. Coming from inside the gate. \"What happened?\" A woman's voice. Calm but sharp, the voice of someone who asks questions expecting to be answered. \"He just—\" the guard started. \"I can see what he just did,\" the woman said. A pause. \"How long has he been standing here?\" \"Few minutes. I told him—\" \"Mmm.\" The sound of someone making a decision. \"Open the gate.\" \"He doesn't have the entry—\" \"Open it anyway.\" She said Silence"},{"t":"3. While He Slept","k":"Chronicles · chapter","u":"chronicles/c3.html","s":"**[Archive POV]** The room was small. One window, facing the building's inner courtyard. One ceiling fan, slightly off-balance, ticking on every third rotation. A stove in the corner. A chair. A bed, narrow, pushed against the wall — and on it, a boy who was not moving. The Stellar Archive hovered above him. Not visibly. Not in any way the woman who entered the room shortly after could perceive. It existed in the space between the system and the world, present the way a thought is present — without weight, without form, without anything that could be touched or seen or questioned. It had been there since the steps. It had watched the woman from the library call for a transit. Had watched the transit come. Had watched the boy loaded into it with the particular careful inefficiency of people who were not sure how fragile he was and were afraid to find out. Had watched him brought here, up three flights of stairs, by a neighbour whose name was not in any file the Archive held. It had logged all of this. It was still logging now. The doctor arrived twenty minutes after the mother. She had come home to find her son already in his bed — the neighbour again, a woman with a strong back and no patience for ceremony, who had explained in four sentences what had happened and left. The mother had stood in the doorway of the room for a long moment, looking at him. Then she had called the doctor. He was a small man. Methodical. He set his case on the floor by the bed and opened it with the ease of someone who had performed this motion ten thousand times, in rooms not unlike this one, with families arranged in postures not unlike hers — the particular stillness of someone waiting for the frame to settle before they could respond to what was inside it. He ran the diagnostics reader across the boy's chest. The Archive read the results before he did. *Left lobe: 31% functional capacity. Progressive scarring across the lower lobes, consistent with prolonged environmental exposure — industrial particulates, inadequate air filtration. Right lobe: compensating. Strain markers elevated. Compensation unsustainable at current rate.* The doctor studied his screen for a moment. Then he looked at the mother. \"The left lobe,\" he said quietly, \"is operating at roughly thirty percent.\" She didn't move. \"The right lobe has been compensating.\" A pause. \"It won't continue to do so indefinitely.\" The Archive noted: her hands. They had been loose at her sides. They were not loose anymore. \"How long,\" she said. The doctor took a breath. \"With rest. Proper rest. No exertion. Adequate nutrition—\" \"How long.\" He stopped. Rearranged. \"A year,\" he said. \"Possibly more.\" The fan ticked. Three rotations. Four. \"Possibly more,\" she repeated. Her voice was flat. Not empty — the Archive had enough language data to distinguish between the two. Empty is the absence of something. Flat is what happens when something is present and being held very carefully so it doesn't spill. \"Yes,\" Doc-saab said. \"At this age, the body still has—\" \"How much more.\" He stopped again. The Archive processed his pause: 2.3 seconds. Longer than a standard conversational gap. Within the range associated with responses where the speaker is aware they do not have the information being requested. \"It depends on many factors,\" he said. \"Living conditions. Rest. Whether the right lobe holds.\" He paused once more. \"I have seen cases where—\" \"Yes,\" she said. One word. The word that means: *I understand what you are not saying and I am choosing to take it from here.* The doctor closed his case. \"Mrs. Valen.\" His voice had dropped further, into the register the Archive classified as *pastoral* — the professional acknowledgement that the clinical portion of the visit was concluded and something else was now required. \"He's a resilient boy. The fact that he was ambulatory at all, given the state of—\" \"I know,\" she said. A silence. \"Thank you, Doctor.\" The door. The footsteps descending the stairwell, growing smaller. Then: nothing. Just the room. The fan. The boy. His mother. She stood at the door for a moment after it closed. The Archive observed: her shoulders. They had been held at a specific angle throughout the doctor's visit — back, level, braced. The kind of posture that is maintained, not natural. When the sound of the doctor's footsteps disappeared from the lower stairwell, her shoulders dropped approximately two centimetres. Not a collapse. An exhale. The private version of something she had not allowed herself while he was present. She crossed the room. At the edge of the bed she stopped and looked at her son. The Archive did not have a category for the expression on her face. It ran a classification pass: *Grief: partial match. Exhaustion: partial match. Anger: negligible. Fear: elevated. Something else—* The classification returned: *insufficient data.* She reached out and touched his forehead with the back of her fingers. Checked the temperature the way she'd done before he could remember — before this life, certainly, and perhaps before the habits needed a reason. Her hand stayed there. Longer than a temperature check required. The boy's face did not change. He was very far under. She pulled her dupatta tighter across her shoulders. Sat in the chair. Folded her hands in her lap. And that was all. No crying. No words. She simply sat with what she had been given and held it in both hands and stayed. The Archive observed this for a long time. It ran no classifications. At some point — forty-seven minutes later, by the Archive's log — the mother stood. She moved to the kitchen. Water. The mechanical rhythm of hands doing small tasks. The room was quiet now except for the boy's breathing. The Archive hovered above him. It had been designed as an evaluative system. Classification, tracking, ranking — these were its functions. It did not give. It measured and withheld and released according to protocols established at its creation. Karmic coins accrued through narrative impact: problems solved, expectations exceeded, change created in the world. The boy had created no change today. He had walked across a bridge on lungs that should not have carried him that far. He had asked a guard a question the guard had no good answer to. He had fallen on library steps having accomplished nothing, obtained nothing, moved nothing in the world from one state to another. Technically. The Archive reviewed its own protocols. Then it reviewed the day. The railing gripped like a lifeline. The twenty-rupee gap. *I'm not asking for charity — do you have a labour exchange program?* The diagnostics reader results it had seen before the doctor did and said nothing. The Archive processed this for a long time. Then, without ceremony, it generated a notification. It hung in the air above the sleeping boy — visible to no one in the room, unreachable by the unconscious figure it was addressed to, undetectable by the woman in the kitchen. **[ 1,000 Karmic Coins Obtained ]** The Archive remained still. Then, in the same silence, in the same empty room, it added one more line. No brackets. No formatting. The kind of line that is not a system output — the kind that happens when something with rules reaches the edge of them and takes one step past, just once, just here, and knows it while it does it: *[this will be the first and last time.]* The notification stayed. The boy breathed. In the kitchen, water ran. After a moment, the notification folded inward and disappeared. The Archive settled back into observation mode. The room returned to what it was: small, dim, a ceiling fan ticking slightly off-rhythm, a boy sleeping in a narrow bed against the wall. Nothing had changed. Somewhere in his account, a number had. He would not know why for a long time. Perhaps he never would."},{"t":"4. Wheel of Fortune","k":"Chronicles · chapter","u":"chronicles/c4.html","s":"I woke up to sunlight and a sarcastic window. **[Good morning. Or afternoon. Hard to say, given that you slept for fourteen hours like a man who had somewhere to be and decided against it.]** \"What time—\" **[2 PM. Your mother left for work an hour ago. She checked on you twice. You slept through both checks, which is impressive given that she is not a quiet person.]** I sat up slowly. My ribs ached. My head felt packed with wet cloth. Otherwise — functional. Technically. **[Body status: Critical. Lung capacity: 31%. General physical condition: a generous F minus.]** \"That's not a real grade.\" **[I made it real just now, You're welcome.]** I swung my legs off the bed and sat on the edge, waiting for the vertigo to pass. It passed faster than yesterday. Small mercy. On the table beside the stove was a covered bowl — dal, still faintly warm, and a folded note tucked beneath it. My mother's handwriting. Three words: *Eat before anything.* I ate. She came back at noon to check on me before the afternoon shift. I heard her key in the lock and straightened instinctively — old habit, the kind that doesn't ask permission. She stopped in the doorway when she saw me sitting upright and awake, spoon in hand, bowl mostly empty. Something crossed her face. Relief dressed up as irritation. \"You're up.\" \"I'm up.\" She crossed the room, pressed the back of her hand to my forehead — the same check as last night, same hand, same practiced motion. Whatever she found satisfied her enough that she moved to the stove and began heating water without comment. We sat in the particular silence that families build over years — not uncomfortable, just dense, carrying more than words would. Then: \"Why did you go to the library?\" Not *why did you go out.* Not *why didn't you tell me.* Straight to it. \"The Institute examination,\" I said. \"I needed study materials.\" She turned from the stove. Looked at me with the expression she used when she was deciding how many things to say out of all the things she was thinking. \"Arin.\" Her voice was level. \"You know what the doctor—\" \"I know what the doctor said.\" She was quiet. The water began to heat. \"The examination is in seven months,\" I said. \"If I pass it — when I pass it — things change. The Academy provides housing, stipend, Medical facilities.\" I held her gaze. \"Better ones.\" She turned back to the stove. Her shoulders did the thing they did — that small, private adjustment. \"You could have asked me first,\" she said. \"You would have said no.\" A pause. Then, quietly: \"Yes, I would have.\" She poured the tea, set a cup in front of me. Kept one for herself. Drank it standing up the way she always did when she didn't have time to sit, which was most days. At the door, dupatta pulled tight, she stopped. \"Eat the rest of the dal,\" she said. \"And don't go anywhere.\" \"I won't.\" She gave me the look that meant she didn't believe me but was choosing to. Then she left. The lock clicked behind her. The room settled into quiet. **[Touching,]** the Archive said. **[Ready to proceed?]** I put down the cup. \"Talk.\" **[Chant 'Stat Window.']** \"I'm not chanting anything.\" **[It doesn't have to be out loud, Think it clearly.]** *Stat Window.* The air in front of me folded open like a page, and there it was: **[ STAT WINDOW ]** ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━ Name: Arin Valen                                                                     Age: 13 Race: Human                                                                       Title: None Health Status: ☠ Terminal Pulmonary Degeneration ☠ Chronic Oxygen Deficiency ☠ Frail Constitution ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━ BLOODLINE ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━ None ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━ BLESSINGS ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━ None ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━ SKILLS ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━ None ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━ SPECIAL TRAITS ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━ [Fading Breath] Every physical action rapidly exhausts the body. [Unbroken Will] Mental resistance greatly exceeds physical limitations. ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━ KARMIC POINTS ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━ [**Karmic Coins: 1,000**] I stared at the last line for a long moment. \"What is that?\" **[It is what it says]** \"One thousand.\" **[Correct.]** \"I had zero yesterday?.\" **[Also correct.]** \"What happened.\" The Archive paused. Shorter than usual. **[You accrued them.]** \"How? I walked across a bridge and fell down.\" **[Karmic Points are generated by actions that produce meaningful effects on the trajectory of this world's events.]** A beat. **[The calculation is more complex than it appears. Don't overthink it.]** I looked at the number. Then: \"What do I do with them?\" **[Two primary functions. First — the Shop.]** A second panel opened beside the first. **[ SHOP ]** *Bloodlines | Skills | Blessings | Stat Boosts* **Minimum Entry Price: 50,000 Karmic Points** **Access Denied (Minimum Narrative Authority Required: 1)** I read it twice. \"Fifty thousand.\" **[Yes.]** \"I have one thousand.\" **[Yes.]** \"So the shop is useless to me.\" **[At present. Correct.]** I closed that panel and breathed through my nose and said nothing for a moment, because some things deserve a moment of silence out of respect for how badly they've gone. \"So I have a thousand points and nowhere to spend them.\" **[Not exactly,]** the Archive said. **[There is one other function.]** Another pause. This one different in texture — if a translucent window could be said to have a texture. **[The Wheel of Fortune. One hundred Karmic Points per spin.]** A new panel appeared. A wheel, segmented, rotating slowly. \"What does it give?\" **[Variable. Skills, Arts Fragments. Most recipients don't have access to this function.]** I looked at it. \"Most?\" **[You are the only current recipient with access to this function,]** the Archive said. Its tone was — as close to neutral as it ever managed to be, which meant it was carefully not being something else. **[It is not standard.]** I didn't ask why. Not yet. \"Show me what it's given others.\" **[It hasn't. You are the first.]** I sat with that for a moment. Then: \"Fine, Spin it.\""},{"t":"5. Null Craft","k":"Chronicles · chapter","u":"chronicles/c5.html","s":"**[To put it simply, Garbage]** the Archive delivered its verdict on the first result. I looked at what I've got **{Null Craft}** **Description: A Method to craft empty spaces in external environments, Temporary enclosed geometric spaces.** **Limitations: 1.) Spaces crafted are unstable and only remain 20 seconds at max before vanishing.** **Method: Concentrate and Think of space before you, mold it with your intent into the shape you want** **Mastery: 0%** **( 1/5 )** **[EDIT]** The word sat there like it had done something wrong. [ EDIT] Not glowing. Not pulsing. Just a faint little prompt under a very unimpressive art, waiting for someone to make a worse decision than the one that had created them. \"Explain,\" I said. **[Clarify the scope of your confusion,]** the Archive replied. **[Is it the existence of the button, your urge to press it, or your life choices leading up to this moment?]** \"All three, But start with the button.\" **[The EDIT function is a restricted interface for modifying fragmentary arts, skills, and techniques obtained through the Wheel of Fortune.]** A pause. **[It is experimental].** \"Experimental as in—you know what you're doing but there may be side effects,\" I said, \"or experimental as in no one signed off on this and we are already too far in?\" **[The second one,]** the Archive said**. [Obviously.]** I looked back at the panel. Null Crafting Art — Incomplete (Low Tier) The description read like a bug report someone had turned into a scripture. Null Crafting: temporary spatial constructs that collapsed after twenty seconds. I got the feeling that this was somehow built to fail. I focused on the art. The panel expanded slightly, text sharpening. \"Walk me through how this is supposed to work,\" I said. **[Null Crafting takes ambient spatial null—]** the Archive began. \"In small words,\" I said. \"Pretend I'm a bureaucrat.\" **[.......]** There was a pause, long enough to be petty. **[The method allows the user to create empty shapes out of space itself,]** it said finally. **[Bridges, barriers, steps, supports. Anything you think is simple enough. They form quickly and vanish quickly. They are meant as temporary tools.]** \"Tools I can't use because I will pass out before I build a staircase,\" I said. \"And even if I did, it would vanish while I was on it.\" **[Correct. Hence: garbage.]** I thought about that. About UPSC mock tests that had questions built to waste time, about trap options that existed solely to see if you'd read the syllabus or just chewed the guidebook. \"What does EDIT do, precisely?\" I asked. \"What are the limits?\" **[It allows the user to make conceptual adjustments to aspects of the art: description, constraints, progression logic.]** The Archive hesitated. **[Within reason.]** \"What is 'within reason'?\" **[Undefined.]** I looked at the faint button again. In my previous life, there had been a kind of question I liked: not the ones that tested whether you'd memorized facts, but the ones where the examiner had clearly been clever and left a method hidden in the wording. If you saw it, the question unfolded. If you didn't, you drowned in your own handwriting. Both these arts felt like that. Broken on purpose. I tapped EDIT. The window did not explode. It did, however, change. The text blurred, then fractured into lines. Each line gained a faint bracket at its end, like [] next to a poorly filled field in an online form. **{Null Craft}** **Description: A Method to craft empty spaces in external environments, Temporary enclosed geometric spaces[]** **Limitations: 1.) Spaces crafted are unstable and only remain 20 seconds at max before vanishing[]** **Method: Concentrate and Think of space before you, mold it with your intent into the shape you want[]** **Mastery: 0% (Cannot be Modified)** **(1 / 5 )** **[EDIT]** Warning: Excessive or incoherent modifications may cause severe Karmic Backlash **[So,]** the Archive said, **[you have limited opportunities to break it further.]** The words mattered. In games, tags and categories were not flavour. They were how the system decided what stacked with what, which passives applied, what quests recognized . Since the art itself did not require an energy pool. That was good. I had no energy pool. What I did have was a body that needed a support more than it needed stairs. \"Range,\" I murmured. **[Yes?]** the Archive said. \"Does effect of the art only apply outside the body '?\" **[Currently, yes. That is what 'external environment means.]** \"Currently,\" I repeated. My ribs ached when I breathed too deeply. My left lobe was thirty-one percent functioning. The right one was doing overtime. What if, instead of building steps in air, the art built support around the parts of me that were failing? Not permanent. Not healing. Just… a temporary structure for a process which happens in moments. In my mind the crude idea of a bridge pulled inward, wrapped around a lung like a virtual structure. A mesh of space holding the air instead of damaged tissue steady. Temporary. Twenty seconds hell even Ten seconds would suffice But ten seconds were a heartbeat, a breath, a cycle. What if the point were not used to make something that lasted, but something that was easy to rebuild? I edited the first line adding **[and internal]** in front of external. **[Cost of Editing: 300 Karmic Coins, Proceed?]**"},{"t":"6. Measuring the Boundaries of Emptiness","k":"Chronicles · chapter","u":"chronicles/c6.html","s":"**[Proceed?]** I ignored, scanning for more possibilities Then I choose the Method line and edited- I erased **[Concentrate]** and just let it remain at **[Think]**, making the process easier **[Net Expenditure: 600 Karmic Coins, Proceed?]** I saw the Limitations part I changed **[Unstable] to [Stable]** **[Net Expenditure: 810 Karmic coins, Proceed?]** I bit my lower lip in frustration. *Should I undo one of my modifications?* *But each one of them is crucial.* *if unstable space Bursts inside my body then?* Then a detail clicked on me. \"If I tighten the existing limitations or add the new ones, will I get a discount?\" I asked **[....Yes]** the Archive confirmed Silence stretched between both of us... \"You hid this on purpose didn't you?\" I whispered holding back my rage. **[I have no recollection of such events]** the screen held its ground I held its gaze for a while and then sighed. \"Fuck you\" I grunted while swearing to take revenge one day. **[Love you too host]** **I** switched my attention back to the editing panel, then my thought moved I decreased the time limit from 20 to 12 seconds **[Net Expenditure: 707 Karmic Coins]** the updated gross expenditure appeared. *Tch, Almost had it* I decreased the time limit from 12 seconds to 11 seconds. The gross updated once again. **[Net Expenditure: 700 Karmic Coins, Proceed?]** I let out a breath I didn't knew I was holding. \"Proceed\" I said to the window hovering in front of me. **[Are you sure? The Transaction is Non- Refundable]** \"Proceed\" I delivered the final decision. **[Transaction Successful]** simultaneously with the message the edited window appeared. **{Null Craft}** **Description: A Method to craft empty spaces in external and internal environments, Temporary enclosed geometric spaces.** **Limitations: Spaces crafted are stable and only remain 11 seconds at max before vanishing.** **Method: Think of space before you, mold it with your intent into the shape you want** **Mastery: 0%** **(1 / 5)** **[Total Balance: 300 Karmic Coins]** It wasn't a healing art. Temporary spatial constructs. Temporary internal support frames reinforcing existing organ structure. Limited external constructs permitted. Nothing exploded. **[You have 300 more coins left]** the archive reminded \"I am trying to save\" I defended **[Noted in quotable Non-Sense which sounds wise #1]** Archive window pulsed slightly \"Okay, Now\" I sat straight in the lotus position **[You are not attempting this unsupervised]** the Archive said. \"I am literally supervised by an omnipresent sarcastic rectangle,\" I replied [....] Archive was at a loss of words I adjusted myself on the bed. My ribs protested. My lungs made their opinion known. I closed my eyes. Visualization. UPSC guides had entire sections on it for stress reduction before exam day — imagine the hall, imagine your seat, imagine the paper. This was not that, but the mechanics weren't so different. I pictured my left lung. Not anatomically correct; just feeling of a missing part in my chest. A damaged, frayed balloon inside my chest. Around it, I imagined a structure similar to lungs starting from the damaged part. Not solid. Not metal. Lines in space, tracing a cage that did not squeeze but held. A cradle made of absence and a network of bronchioles and alveolis. Inhale. The air scraped in. Pain flared, familiar and ignored. I tried to feel where the breath went — down, spreading, catching at the bottom. Around that trajectory, I imagined the frame. Four lines. Eight. A structure same as lungs. Three breaths, the instructions had said. *One.* The first breath was always the hardest. My body did not trust that there would be enough air. *Two*. On the second breath, something changed. It was not dramatic. No glowing aura, no shiver of power. Just a faint sense of… a place, where there had been only pain and emptiness before. Like running your hand along a wall in the dark and finding an edge you didn't know was there. *Three.* On the third breath, my chest tightened, as if the frame I'd imagined had briefly become. The blood as if finding a new passage to go rushed Exhale. The sensation snapped. The faint contour vanished. My lungs returned to their previous status: underperforming and upset about it. I opened my eyes. \"Anything?\" I asked. **[One successful initiation. No measurable change to Vital Capacity.]** the Archive logged. I let my head fall back against the wall. \"How many repetitions before anything shifts?\" **[For a healthy trainee, perhaps several weeks.]** The Archive paused. **[For you, data is insufficient. Your body is … atypical.]** \"Inspiring.\" I closed my eyes again. Frame. Breath. Count. After ten cycles, my chest burned in a new way. Not the tearing, panicked burn of overexertion, but a deep ache, as if muscles that had never been used properly were being asked to remember they existed. **[You will pass out.]** \"Then I'll pass out,\" I said. The world had already taken my first attempt at a future halfway across a zebra crossing in the rain. A few practice breaths were not going to scare me. I kept going. Somewhere around the twentieth cycle, my arms started trembling. By thirty, sweat had broken out along my spine. I lost count once, almost inhaled too sharply, had to cough it out and start again. Each time, I rebuilt the frame in my mind, the lines a little clearer. The ache spread. My ribcage felt like it had been used as an exam hall bench for fifty years — worn, but still here. **[Idiot]** the Archive said. There was a reluctant pause. **[Vital Capacity: 31% → 32%.]** Another beat. **[Temporary. The effect may not hold.]** I smiled, small and mean. \"One percent,\" I said. **[One percent,]** the Archive agreed. **[You have also increased local strain markers. Your body will demand compensation later.]** \"Later problem,\" I said. \"Current problem just became fractionally less fatal.\" The Null Frame panel pulsed. Warning: Do not exceed safe repetition count. \"Let me guess,\" I said. \"Rest?\" **[Rest,]** the Archive confirmed. **[Ideally, under the supervision of competent medical staff.]** \"I have a chair,\" I said. \"And a sarcastic archive. That will have to do.\" I let the window fade back to the edge of my vision. The room settled into focus again. The stove. The empty bowl. The note. Eat before anything. I had eaten. Then I had edited an art and poked holes in the boundary between space and my lungs. Close enough. The light outside the window had shifted to evening. The city noises changed register: worker shifts swapping, vendors closing, hoverbikes whining a little more desperately as the first drops of rain started to tick against the glass. My eyelids felt heavy. Sleep tugged at the edges of my thoughts, the way it had on the bridge, but less violently this time. More like a professor tapping the desk with a pen. \"Stay awake until she comes back,\" I told myself. **[Your mother is scheduled to return between 8 PM and 9 PM, based on previous patterns,]** the Archive said. **[If you collapse before then, I will wake you.]** \"You can do that?\" **[I can be annoying at will.]** \"Comforting.\" I drifted. Not fully asleep. The way you doze during boring coaching lectures — the words washing over you while some stubborn part of your brain keeps scribbling notes. Time passed. The fan ticked. Somewhere in the building, someone argued with someone else about unpaid bills. A baby cried, then quieted. Rain thickened, turned the city into a muffled, wetly glowing thing. and then, the lock clicked-"},{"t":"7. A Debt which cannot be Paid","k":"Chronicles · chapter","u":"chronicles/c7.html","s":"*Please leave your reviews to help me grow* The lock clicked. I straightened too fast. My chest objected. The frame I'd been half‑holding as a reflex shattered, leaving a sharp, momentary pain in its place. \"Arin?\" my mother called softly. \"You awake?\" \"Yeah,\" I said. My voice came out rougher than I'd intended. She stepped inside, shook rain from the edge of her dupatta, and shut the door behind her. She looked more tired than she had that morning — the kind of tired that settled around the eyes and didn't leave even when you slept. Her gaze went to me, automatically. Checked the rise and fall of my chest, the colour of my face, the way I was sitting. \"You look…\" She searched for a word, abandoned the attempt. \"You didn't go out?\" \"No,\" I said. Technically true. I had gone inward. \"Good.\" She moved to the stove, relit the burner, set water to boil. The rituals of the end of a shift. Then, after a moment, she reached into the bag hanging from her shoulder. \"I brought something,\" she said. My attention sharpened. She pulled out a thin, battered book. The cover was creased, the edges softened by many hands. The title was half‑worn but still legible under the strip of tape holding the binding together. **INSTITUTE OF INTER WORLD STUDIES GENERAL TRACK** FOUNDATION PRACTICE COMPENDIUM (Second‑ Edition) My throat tightened. \"Where did you get that?\" I asked. \"A stall near the factory,\" she said, not quite meeting my eyes. \"Old textbooks, manuals. A man who collects whatever students leave behind when they drop out or… move on.\" \"How much?\" I said. \"Not much,\" she said quickly. \"He was clearing space. Wanted to get rid of the old syllabus copies.\" \"Ma.\" She sighed. \"Fifty,\" she admitted. \"Rupees. He gave a discount because some pages are missing.\" Fifty rupees. Two and a half entry fees to the library. A week of small compromises in groceries and medicine. \"You shouldn't have,\" I said, automatically. \"You asked for syllabus,\" she said briskly. \"Coaching we can't do. Book, we can try. It's not complete. But…\" She placed it on the table beside the stove as if it was something fragile. \"You always said a path is a path.\" I stared at the book. The cover showed the Academy's stylised emblem — the rising tower. Inside, pages bulged where notes had been scribbled and erased, where someone had underlined a section on \"Basic Fog Incursion Phenomena\" and circled dates for practice tests. Some other candidate's ghost clung to the margins, in faint pencil. \"Thank you,\" I said. She shrugged, uncomfortable. \"It's nothing, Just paper.\" She busied herself with pouring tea. \"Don't study too much tonight. Your body—\" \"—needs rest,\" I finished with her. She glanced back. The corner of her mouth twitched. \"Exactly.\" We drank in companionable silence. The book sat between us, its presence heavier than its weight. \"Ma,\" I said, after a while. \"Hmm?\" \"If I pass,\" I said. \"When I pass, The Institute has better medical facilities.\" \"You already told me that,\" she said. \"This morning.\" \"I know,\" I said. \"I just… wanted to say it again.\" She looked at me for a long moment. \"You think books will fix everything,\" she said softly. \"They won't,\" I said. \"But they might fix enough.\" She reached out and smoothed a wrinkle in the book's cover with her thumb. \"When your father left,\" she said, \"people told me to remarry. Find someone with a stable job. They said a woman alone, with a sick child… that was not a life.\" Her mouth twisted. \"I told them a library card was a better investment.\" \"You didn't have a library card,\" I said. \"I had the idea of one,\" she said. \"Sometimes ideas are just credit you have not been allowed to spend yet.\" She stood. \"Sleep,\" she ordered. \"You look like you lost a fight with a staircase.\" \"Bridge, actually,\" I said. She snorted. \"That too.\" When she turned away to wash the cups, I let my hand drift to my chest. Focused. Felt for the outline of the frame. It was easier this time. Not painless. Not stable. But easier. The Archive's window hovered just at the edge of vision, numbers and warnings and irritations flickering. **[Null Craft– Progress: 3%]** **[Body Status: Still Critical.]** I cracked open the compendium with my free hand. The pages smelled of old paper and the faint chemical tang of cheap ink. Diagrams of structures I have never seen in either life , lists of exam sections, sample question formats. UPSC had Paper I: General Studies, Paper II: Aptitude, Essay, Optionals. This world had its own versions. Different content. Same shape. \"Two exams,\" I murmured. \"One for the world, one for the body.\" **[Only one of them has an answer key,]** the Archive said. \"Which one?\" I asked. A pause. **[Trick question,]** it said. **[Neither.]** I smiled. \"Then we will write our own,\" I said. Outside, the rain thickened. Inside, under the ticking fan and the thin light of the old holo‑panel, I traced lines on a page with one hand and lines in my lungs with the other, and for the first time since waking in this life, the future felt less like a wall and more like a question worth attempting."},{"t":"8. Labour Exchange Program","k":"Chronicles · chapter","u":"chronicles/c8.html","s":"Its been a month since then, **[Null Craft Mastery: 86%]** , the Archive reported My feet, when I swung them off the bed, no longer felt like they were borrowing strength from the floor. When I stood, the room tilted––but only a little. [**Interesting**] the Archive murmured. [**Vertigo incidents reduced by 72 percent over the last ten days. You are, statistically less suicidal than before**.] I rolled my shoulders. The ache across my ribcage had settled into something solid, like muscles that had finally remembered their job. The Virtual lung . By now, I didn't have to think through the whole sequence. On the inhale, the Null Craft caught automatically, a network of bronchioles, alveolis and a covering coming into existence flaring into place around my ruined left lung like a second, transparent organ. Inhale—The New Virtual lung replaces the old structure line by line preventing the full momentary collapse which may cause internal building. Exhale—the frame held for the count of three, then dissolved strip by strip, making room for the next one. It wasn't healing, It was habit. My body had learned the pattern the way it once learned how to hold a pen through six hours of UPSC mains without cramping: badly at first, then mechanically, then instinctively. **[Risk of collapsing on public stairs marginally less humiliating.]** the Archive chimed in before adding- **[You are still medically fragile, Do not confuse improved statistics with immortality.]** \"Noted,\" I muttered, reaching for my clothes. The grey T-shirt was as washed-out as ever, the shoulders a little less baggy now that I'd put on some weight. Black trousers, knees thinned from wear. They were still the best I had. I smoothed the front automatically, then caught myself and snorted. The Academy wasn't marking dress code for the written exam. From the stove, the smell of reheated dal drifted over. Ma moved around the cramped room with practiced efficiency, tying her hair back, checking the gas, counting coins in her palm. \"Ma,\" I said. She glanced up. \"Hmm?\" \"I'm going to the library today.\" The silence that followed wasn't loud. It was heavy. She set the coins down carefully, as if they might explode. \"Why?\" The word came out flat, almost level. Her voice only dropped like that when she was balancing fear and anger on the same line. \"I finished the compendium,\" I said. \"Cover to cover. I need more material. Past papers. Reference texts.\" I hesitated, then added, \"If I stay on the same book, I'm just circling. The exam won't.\" Her eyebrows rose for a heartbeat before they settled again, as if her face had betrayed her thoughts and hurried to fix it. \"It's only been a month,\" she said under her breath. **[A month of not dying]** the Archive offered helpfully in my peripheral vision. I ignored it. I held her gaze instead. \"I can walk now,\" I said. \"Without the world flipping every ten steps. I'll be careful. You can drop me there. I'll call someone from the staff if I feel—\" I broke off, searching for the right word. **[Like you did last time?]** the Archive supplied. \"—if I feel unsteady,\" I finished. She watched me for a long moment. I saw her remembering: the call from the library staff, the rush back, the doctor, the prognosis that had come with an expiration date instead of guarantees. Then she let out a breath. \"Get ready,\" she said. \"I'll drop you on the way to the factory.\" **[Parental resistance: bypassed.]** [Outcome: conditional approval.] Shut up, I thought at it, and reached for my shoes. The city smelled of wet dust and old dye. Last night's rain had rinsed some of the chemical taste from the air, but the factories had already started repopulating it. We took the bus this time. Not a Voyage-branded luxury capsule, just a rattling public line that wheezed over potholes and announced its stops two beats too late. Ma stood, one hand looped around the overhead rail, the other braced lightly on my shoulder whenever the driver braked too sharply. \"You sit,\" she'd said at the stop, in the tone that ended arguments. So I did. Every few minutes, I checked in with my body. Old habit. Inhale—the frame rose cleanly, wrapping my left lung, forcing the breath to distribute more evenly. Exhale—the frame dissolved, leaving a fading imprint like a muscle memory. The Archive muttered tiny updates in my peripheral vision: Oxygen saturation, heart rate, instability markers. By the time the bus rolled to a stop near City Central Public Library, my legs ached with that now-familiar deep soreness. But when I stood and stepped down onto the pavement, the world stayed in its proper orientation. Sky above, ground below, library ahead––sandstone and glass and blue-lit circuitry humming softly under the mid-morning light. \"It looks bigger in daylight,\" I said. Ma followed my gaze. Her mouth compressed. \"Call me if you feel anything wrong,\" she said. \"Anything. Do you hear me?\" \"Yes.\" \"And don't argue with the staff. If they say enough, it's enough.\" \"Yes.\" \"And no running.\" I stared at her. \"Have you ever seen me run?\" She snorted despite herself, the sound half a laugh, half a sigh. \"Exactly. Don't start today.\" She squeezed my shoulder once, hard. \"Go. I'll be back after my shift.\" She hesitated as if she wanted to say more, then turned and walked toward the factory bus stop, shoulders squared against the day. I stood at the bottom of the library steps for a moment. No spin. No black at the edge of my vision. Just the steady thud of my heart and the faint hum of the security gate above. **[Progress]**, the Archive noted. **[Do try not to spectacularly erase it.]** The security guard at the top of the steps looked different in daylight too. Less like a barrier, more like a man whose feet hurt. He glanced up as I approached, his smart-glasses flickering once as they registered my civic card before I even tapped it. \"You again?,\" he said. His tone wasn't hostile, just surprised. The last time he'd seen me, I'd been sliding toward unconsciousness on his steps. \"Me again,\" I agreed. He studied me openly, taking in the same grey T-shirt, the same black trousers, the way I was standing upright without clinging to the railing like a drowning man. \"You shouldn't be here,\" he said, but it sounded more like a reflex than a decision. \"I need to be here,\" I said. \"I want to read.\" \"It's twenty rupees for three hours,\" he began. \"I know,\" I said. \"I also know I don't have it.\" His mouth tightened. \"Then we are back where we were.\" \"In a way,\" I said. \"But I'm not asking the same question.\" He frowned. \"What do you mean?\" \"Last time,\" I said carefully, \"I asked if there was a labour exchange program. Cleaning, shelving, sorting, in return for access. You said it wasn't your decision.\" \"I remember,\" he said slowly. \"I'm asking you now,\" I continued, \"to take me to whoever it is that can decide, Please.\" For a moment, he just looked at me. Then his gaze flicked, almost involuntarily, to my hands. They were steadier than last time, fingers curled but not white-knuckled. Sweat dampened the back of my neck, but I was still vertical. **[Last syncope event on these premises: 29 days, 17 hours ago]** Archive threw out it's logs helpfully or so it seemed. \"Delete that,\" I muttered. \"I heard you\", the guard said. \"Not you,\" I corrected quickly. \"I mean. Yes. I mean—\" He held up a hand. \"Stop. You're worse at lying than you are at walking.\" He sighed, the sound long-suffering. \"Look,\" he said. \"If you pass out again on my watch, the admin will have my salary for breakfast.\" \" Come inside before you do something dramatic on the stairs.\" He tapped his wristband against the side of the gate. The hum shifted pitch. The barrier flickered and parted. \"Three minutes,\" he warned as we stepped through. \"If they say no, you walk out. Slowly. No heroics.\" \"Deal,\" I said. Inside, the air was cooler, filtered. The noise of the city dulled to a background murmur. Rows of shelves marched into the distance, their spines forming uneven lines of text and color. Light spilled down from skylights and hidden fixtures, turning dust motes into slow, lazy stars. A woman at the front desk glanced up as we passed, giving the guard a questioning look. \"Rao-ji inside?\" he asked. \"In his cave,\" she said dryly. \"Where else.\" The guard grimaced. He then led me down a short corridor to a corner office whose glass walls were half-obscured by tall stacks of books. A small sign on the door read: CHIEF LIBRARIAN – R. RAO The guard knocked once. \"Come,\" a voice called. Male, older, threaded with a dry patience. **[Get Ready]** \"I know\" I took in a deep breath The guard opened the door-"},{"t":"9. Unpaid Labou-I mean Volunteer","k":"Chronicles · chapter","u":"chronicles/c9.html","s":"\"Sir,\" he said. \"This is the boy who collapsed on the steps last month. Says he wants to talk to you about… labour exchange.\" There was a brief silence while the man behind the desk took us in. R. Rao was perhaps in his late fifties. His hair was more silver than black, combed back with the stubbornness. His skin was the particular shade of brown that turned to leather in the sun. Lines radiated from the corners of his eyes—not from frowning, but from squinting at text for decades. Half his desk was occupied by a teetering pile of returned books. The other half occupied by two cups and a single framed photograph turned face-down. His gaze did the same slow, methodical sweep the old man in the Voyage car had done: face, clothes, posture. \"Come in,\" he said. \"Both of you.\" We stepped inside. The guard closed the door behind us. \"So,\" Rao said, eyes on me. \"You have a proposal, it seems.\" I took a breath. Frame. Hold. Release. \"My name is Arin Valen,\" I said. \"Local resident. Thirteen.\" \"So the card says,\" Rao replied. \"I want to sit for the Institute written examination,\" I continued. \"General Track.\" One of his eyebrows twitched, almost imperceptibly. \"Ambitious,\" he said mildly. \"I've been studying,\" I said. \"For a month. Intensively. My mother bought me the Institute of Interworld Studies General Track Foundation Practice Compendium, second edition. I've gone through it twice, solved all available practice questions, corrected my errors. I've made my own notes.\" Rao's gaze sharpened. \"You have the book with you?\" he asked. I nodded and carefully slid the compendium from my cloth bag. The cover was more creased than when Ma had brought it home; the tape on the spine had been reinforced with another strip. Color-coded slips of paper stuck out from the sides like flags. I placed it on his desk. He opened it, flipped through the pages with the casual familiarity of someone who knew its contents by smell. His fingers paused on the margins, where my cramped handwriting filled every available blank space. \"You redid the aptitude section.\" he murmured while scanning \"Yes,\" I said. \"My first scores were weak on pattern recognition and time management. I adjusted.\" Rao closed the book and tapped the cover. \"You understand,\" he said slowly, \"that this is only a foundation, The real exam will not be so forgiving.\" \"I know,\" I said. \"That's why I'm here. I can't afford coaching centers. I can't afford the entry fee for terminal access more than once a month, What I can do is work.\" I glanced around the office. Even in here, books sat in untidy stacks, on the floor, on the windowsill, on a chair that had clearly not seen an actual human occupant in years. \"I'm not strong enough to move crates,\" I said bluntly. \"You can see that. But I can clean tables. I can wipe terminals. I can shelve books left on reading desks. I know how to follow a catalogue. I've spent most of my life in front of books—or wanting to.\" Rao leaned back in his chair, studying me. The guard shifted his weight near the door, the leather of his belt creaking softly. \"In exchange,\" I said, \"I'm asking for access. Not money. Just the right to sit in a corner and read. If possible… paper and pens. Scrap would do.\" **[You want to be paid in stationary?]** the Archive said. **[You have hit a new economic low.]** Rao drummed his fingers once on the desk. \"You fainted on my steps,\" he said. \"The last time you came here without the fee.\" \"Yes,\" I said. \"That was… poorly planned.\" \"Your body is not designed for labour,\" he observed. \"I'm aware,\" I said. \"That's why I've been working on that too.\" Rao's eyes narrowed slightly. \"Explain.\" I hesitated. The Null Frame wasn't something I was advertising to strangers. I chose my words carefully. \"My lungs were worse,\" I said. \"A month ago. Walking from our room to the bridge was enough to almost kill me. Since then, I've been… training. Carefully. Breathing exercises. Structural support. My condition is still bad. But it's better than it was.\" Rao considered that in silence. \"How many hours do you think you can work without collapsing?\" he asked finally. \"Three,\" I said. \"With breaks. Light work.\" The Archive flashed a disapproving warning icon, but said nothing. \"And how many hours do you expect to study?\" Rao asked. \"As many as you'll let me,\" I said honestly. \"I don't have time to waste. The exam is in six months and three weeks.\" The guard made a soft, incredulous noise. Rao heard it and ignored it. \"Sit,\" he said. I perched on the chair opposite his desk, pulling my bag close to my feet. Rao looked at the guard. \"You can go,\" he said. \"I'll page you if I need you.\" \"Sir, if he—\" \"I am capable of calling for a medic if something happens to him,\" Rao said calmly. \"Go and guard the door from the ravages of unsupervised ten year olds.\" The guard's mouth twitched. He gave me a last measuring look, then left, closing the door properly this time. Rao turned back to me. \"Arin,\" he said. \"There is no official labour exchange program in this library. You understand that.\" My chest tightened. \"Yes,\" I said quietly. \"That means,\" he went on, \"that if I agree to your proposal, it will be… informal. Subject to my mood. My assessment. My patience.\" \"I understand,\" I said. One corner of his mouth curved. \"I cannot give you a salary,\" he said. \"If I do it for you, fifty others will appear at this gate with stories worse than yours. And my budget does not stretch far enough to properly pay even the staff I already have.\" \"I'm not asking for money,\" I said quickly. \"No,\" he said. \"You are asking for something more valuable.\" He tapped the compendium again. \"Books,\" he said. \"Time, Space, Quietude.\" He took a breath. \"Here is what I can offer,\" he said. \"You come Five days a week. Morning or afternoon, I don't care which, but you will tell me your schedule and stick to it. Each shift, you will spend the first two hours cleaning reading tables, collecting abandoned books, and returning them to their proper sections. You will do this properly. If I find a book out of place, I will know exactly who to blame.\" \"Yes,\" I said. My fingers had curled into fists on my knees. I forced them to relax. \"In return,\" he continued, \"you may stay for studying until closing, if your body permits. You will have access to general-tier stacks. No restricted archives. No backroom collections. I will arrange for scrap paper and usable pens from surplus. You will not, under any circumstances, charge anyone for your 'help' or pretend to be staff. If a visitor asks you a question, you direct them to the front desk.\" \"That's… more than fair,\" I said. \"You will also,\" he said, voice flattening, \"not die in my library. I do not have the energy to argue with doctors and inspectors and your mother. If you feel dizzy, short of breath, or anything that suggests your lungs are attempting rebellion, you will sit down immediately and inform the nearest adult that you are about to cause trouble.\" The Archive underlined that line three times. \"Agreed,\" I said. Rao watched me a moment longer, then nodded once. \"Good,\" he said. \"We understand one another.\" He extended his hand across the desk. I stared at it for half a second, then reached out and shook it. His grip was dry, firm, the kind you got from decades of lifting hardcovers and paperbacks instead of weights. \"Welcome to City Central Public Library, provisional… what shall we call you?\" he mused. \"Junior attendant? Apprentice? Table-wiper-in-chief?\" **[Unpaid Academic Laborer ]** the Archive suggested. \"Volunteer,\" I said. The word felt strange and right at the same time. \"I'll be a volunteer.\" \"Very well,\" Rao said. \"Volunteer it is.\" He released my hand and leaned back. \"Go to the front desk,\" he said. \"Tell Megha I've given you permission. She'll show you the cleaning supplies and explain the shelving system. Do not breathe on the rare manuscripts.\" \"I won't,\" I said, standing carefully. My knees held. The frame around my lung hummed quietly, an invisible brace. \"Arin,\" Rao added as I reached the door. I turned. He picked up the compendium again, weighed it in his hand. \"You finished this in a month,\" he said. It wasn't quite a question. \"Yes.\" He turned it over, then set it down beside his terminal. \"Leave it,\" he said. \"I'll keep it here. Less to carry. And if I find any suitable papers, I may… add them.\" My throat tightened. \"Thank you,\" I said. \"Don't thank me yet,\" he replied. \"Pass the exam. Then we'll talk.\" At the front desk, Megha raised an eyebrow when I relayed Rao's instructions, but a quick message on her terminal confirmed it. She handed me a rag, a spray bottle that smelled faintly of citrus and questionable decisions, and a plastic tub on wheels for abandoned books. \"Tables first,\" she said briskly. \"Start with Reading Hall A. Wipe, clear, sort into the tub. Don't try to shelve yet; you'll only mess it up. When the tub is full, bring it back here and I'll show you the labels.\" \"Got it,\" I said. The Archive let out a long, theatrical sigh. **[Congratulations,]** it said. **[You have acquired a new role.]** A system tag flickered briefly in the corner of my vision: **[Role Unlocked: Library Volunteer (Unpaid Laborer)** **Perks: Access to knowledge, minimal respect, cleaning-related muscle development.]** I smiled. Inhale. Frame. Eleven seconds of borrowed structure wrapped my failing lung. Exhale. Release. Another frame already rising to take its place. I pushed the tub toward Reading Hall A, the wheels squeaking a rhythm that my breath matched instinctively. Two exams, I thought. One with question papers and answer keys. One with dust, tables, and the slow rearrangement of my own body. For the first time in either life, I felt like I had, if not an advantage, then at least a foothold. Let's get to work."},{"t":"10. Six Months","k":"Chronicles · chapter","u":"chronicles/c10.html","s":"The library smelled different at six in the morning. Less like people, more like dust and old paper slowly waking up. \"Tables,\" said Megha, like always. \"Tables,\" I repeated. Reading Hall A was a graveyard of yesterday's ambitions. Open books abandoned mid-paragraph. Empty cups with the brown ring of last tea clinging to their sides. Scribbled notes on loose sheets that might once have been important and were now just… paper. I put the tub down, picked up the rag, and began. Wipe. Stack. Clear. Breathe. By now, the sequence moved through me without needing supervision. On the inhale, the Null Frame rose automatically around my left lung, a quiet scaffold of structured absence holding frayed tissue in place. On the exhale, it dissolved just as automatically. **[Null Craft – Mastery: 99%],** the Archive noted in the corner of my vision. **[Natural progress for any idiot who insists on abusing a fragment art as a respiratory aid.]** I wiped, stacked, cleared. By eight, the first wave of aspirants arrived. They came in clusters—study groups formed in coaching centers, wearing different brands of the same uniform: decent shoes, holo-tablets, noise-cancelling earpieces hooked around their necks like status symbols. Their bags looked heavier than my entire wardrobe. \"Bro, my dad said if I clear General Track for Engineering and Logistics on first attempt, he'll sponsor a Europe-tour package during vacation before opening,\" one of them said once, sliding into a seat as I wiped the neighboring table. \"Please,\" the other scoffed. \"My mom said if I get top hundred, she'll talk to our guild contact for an internal recommendation. Guardian stream, General track.\" Their voices blurred into background noise as I moved past, tub rattling softly. I wasn't jealous, Not exactly. Envy is too simple a word for the feeling of watching people start a race from ten meters ahead, with better shoes and fresh lungs, while you're still negotiating with your own ribcage. Besides, I reminded myself, I had something they didn't. I had already failed once. UPSC Mains. Rain. Headlights. The memory was a scar I could poke when I needed perspective. These kids had pressure. I had… precedent. \"Focus,\" I muttered under my breath. **[Focus]** the Archive agreed. **[On cleaning, Your true calling.]** I rolled my eyes and wiped another table. The ache in my arms came first—muscles protesting repeated motion after years of being used only for lifting spoons. Then the heaviness in my chest **[Vital Capacity: 44% → 45%]**, the Archive logged one day **[Coughing frequency: down 56% compared to baseline month.]** I leaned against a pillar for a moment. \"You okay, kid?\" Megha called from the front desk. \"Break,\" I said. \"Then more tables.\" I sat on a chair by the wall, watching the reading hall fill. Students slid into habitual seats. Some spread out across entire tables like minor nobility surveying their fiefdoms. Others hunched protectively over borrowed screens. On good days, when my hands were steady and my lungs weren't chewing on rust, I'd pick up a crumpled revision sheet someone had left behind—topics highlighted in neon, questions partially solved. I'd smooth it out carefully and tuck it between the pages of my own compendium. On bad days, I just breathed and counted the seconds between coughs. Those days grew fewer. Lunch was the best part of the day. Not because of the food—though that mattered—but because it came with my mother. She'd appear at the security gate around one-thirty, dupatta pulled tight against the outside dust, tiffin in hand. The guard would wave her through with a nod now; they knew each other by shift-timing association. \"Ma's here,\" Megha would call across the lobby, and I'd emerge from between two shelves, dust on my fingers, shirt damp at the back. \"Sit,\" Ma would say \"You vanish into these books and forget you have a stomach.\" Rice. Dal. Sometimes a bit of sabzi if the mess had been generous. Once in a rare while. \"You look less bone today,\" she'd say, watching me eat. \"More… person.\" It was not a medical term, but it wasn't wrong. **[Weight: +10.3 kg since baseline, the Archive confirmed. Caloric absorption improving. Cough intensity trending downward.]** I ate slowly, letting each mouthful sit for a moment before swallowing. Some days, when the tiffin was almost empty, a shadow would fall across our corner. \"Mrs. Valen,\" Rao's voice would say. \"You brought the boy fuel again, I see.\" Ma would stand up so fast her chair scraped. \"Namaste, sir.\" \"At ease,\" he'd say, amusement tucked into the corners of his mouth. \"This is not a parade ground.\" He'd set down a small container on the table. Banana slices. Apple wedges. Once, I swear, two pieces of an actual orange, glowing like small suns. \"My wife has decided we are both too old to finish all the fruit she sends,\" he'd say. \"It would be a tragedy to waste it.\" \"It's too much,\" Ma would protest. \"It is arithmetic,\" Rao would reply. \"If two old people cannot finish a kilo of fruit, but two old people and one stubborn boy can, the solution is obvious.\" He'd leave before either of us could argue further. Afternoons belonged to paper. After cleaning duty, I'd move to a corner table in Reading Hall B with my compendium, the \"found\" sheets, and—more recently—an almost-new notebook that had somehow appeared in my bag. I found it wedged inside my bag **[I see the spirits of surplus stationary have taken a liking to you]** the Archive observed. \"These just… show up,\" I said, turning the pen over. \"Conveniently at the start of a new topic.\" **[Yes]** it said. **[Mysterious.]** I glanced toward the glass wall of Rao's office. He was bent over a terminal, frowning at a report. \"Very mysterious,\" I agreed. I opened the notebook. Unlike my previous life, I didn't copy everything. Years of UPSC coaching had taught me the trap of writing Laxmikanth into five different registers and calling it progress. Now, my notes were about structure. Left page: topics and subtopics. Right page: questions. Ambassador Studies & Delegation – Outline Fog Phenomenology and Energy Graph Analysis – Basic and Applied Foreign Civilizations – Political structures, cultural norms Geopolitics – Resource lines, guild alliances, Resource control Economics – Cross-Continental trade, tariffs, reparations Ethics & Protocol – First-contact doctrine, treaties, negotiation frameworks Aptitude – Data interpretation, logic, decision-making under constraint Old habits die harder than people. I made a table for the week: Days on one axis, topics on the other. Morning: cleaning and passive listening to other students' group discussions. Afternoon: one major topic plus practice questions. Evening: revision and mapwork—both literal maps and mind-maps. **[You are treating your life as a multi-paper exam]**, the Archive said. \"It worked better than treating it as a story,\" I replied. Sometimes, in the late afternoon, Rao would wander past my table with the aimless precision of someone who definitely hadn't come there by accident. Evenings belonged to revision. By six, the study groups started thinning. Parents called. Coaching sessions resumed. I'd stack my notes, close my compendium, and spend an hour just… going over. No writing. Just tracing lines in my head. UPSC had taught me that revision isn't just rereading; it's rebuilding. You take apart a chapter and put it back together in your own language. While my mind rearranged information, my body rearranged breath. One evening, as I was halfway through silently reciting the key points of Gate Ethics, the Archive cleared its metaphorical throat. **[Null Craft – Mastery: 100%].** Passive condition satisfied: Cycle of Breath-Frame-Release now integrated into autonomic patterns. I blinked. \"So the frame just… runs?\" I asked internally. **[Within limits]**, the Archive replied. **[Your body has adopted the pattern. When strain markers rise above a certain threshold, the art triggers without conscious input.]** \"So now I can climb library stairs with ease?\" I suggested. **[Those remain your natural predator]**, it replied. Still, numbers didn't lie. **[Vital Capacity: 48%.]** [Cough frequency: down 59%.] [Weight: +11.2 kg since baseline.] Even Ma had noticed. \"You cough less now,\" she said one night as we sat in our room, fan ticking above. \"Not scaring the neighbours as often.\" \"Building local goodwill,\" I said. \"Very important for Ambassador track.\" She shook her head. \"Your jokes are getting worse. That's how I know you feel better.\" I smiled into my cup of tea. Time doesn't speed up when you're busy. It just stops warning you that it's leaving. Days blurred, Morning: arrive with Megha. Clean. Listen. Late morning: breathe. Survive. Afternoon: notes. \"Found\" mock papers slid between compendium pages that were, somehow, always the next topics I needed. \"How strange,\" I said, making sure to say it loud enough that Rao, passing by, could hear and not-smile. Evening: revision. Silent recitations. Occasional whispered arguments with the Archive about whether Ambassador Studies would actually take a half-dead thirteen-year-old with no visible importance. Night: home. Dinner. Ma's running commentary on her day at the factory. My filtered version of my day at the library. Six months passed like that. And then the Notification came."},{"t":"11. Notification","k":"Chronicles · chapter","u":"chronicles/c11.html","s":"*Please join the discord to point out my mistake and give your suggestions* *discord.gg/XD4VFunFCz* One evening, as I was closing my notebook, a notification slid into view without the usual dramatic fanfare. **Institute Written Examination – Application Window Open** Tracks Available: – Guardian Studies – Fog Phenomenology & Research – Combat Arts & Field Operations – Engineering & Support Logistics – Ambassador Studies & Delegation **[If you pick Combat, I will personally shut down,]** the Archive said. I stared at the list. Guardian Studies: \"No, My body would revolt at the first aid and evacuation course.\" Combat Arts: \"That was a fantasy that belonged to a different boy in a different body.\" Engineering & Support: \"Tempting, but not where my instincts pulled.\" Fog Phenomenology & Research: \"Interesting, but…I am not that kind of person\" Ambassador Studies & Delegation. Diplomacy, Protocol, Negotiation between worlds, Understanding cultures well enough to stand in the gap when things tried to fall apart. In my previous life, International Relations had been my secret favourite section in GS. Not because I liked reading about wars, but because I liked reading about the people who'd tried to stop them and almost, sometimes, succeeded. \"I'll be indoors more,\" I murmured. \"Less dust, More sitting.\" **[You are choosing your track based on seating arrangements?]**, the Archive observed. \"And on the fact that I can already think like an underpaid bureaucrat,\" I said. \"Seems… appropriate.\" The cursor blinked next to Ambassador Studies & Delegation. I clicked. A new line appeared. **Selection Criteria:** – Written Examination (General Track – mandatory) – Track Aptitude Section (Ambassadorial Focus) – Interview with Panel (Institute + Guild Representatives) My pulse stuttered, then steadied. \"So,\" I said softly. \"Two papers and an interview between me and a bed that isn't falling apart.\" **[And medical facilities]** the Archive added. **[And stipends. And exposure to environments that will almost certainly try to kill you more creatively than your lungs ever did.]** \"Motivating,\" I said dryly. The application window folded inward, leaving behind a simple confirmation. Application Recorded. Countdown to Written Examination: 27 days. Twenty-seven days. I flipped open my notebook to the master schedule. The pages were already filled with color-coded blocks, arrows, and corrections. I pulled out a pen—the same model Rao's \"surplus\" kept producing—and began a new grid. (Weeks left= four.) (Days per week in library= Five.) (Topics per day= two major, one minor.) (Mock papers: as many as mysteriously appear.) My hand stilled on the page for a moment. Then I wrote, in the top corner, in small, stubborn letters: When I pass. Not if. When. Outside, the city hummed. Inside, the library lights dimmed slightly as the evening cycle began. Breath. Frame. Release. Six months ago, those three words had been a struggle. Now, they were just how I existed. \"Let's write our own answer key, then,\" I whispered. The Archive said nothing. For once, it didn't need to. And just like that, the days went by."},{"t":"12. The Day - I","k":"Chronicles · chapter","u":"chronicles/c12.html","s":"*discord.gg/XD4VFunFCz* For a while, there was only darkness, the ticking ceiling fan, and the soft, measured drag of my own breath. then the first ray of the sun spilled through my window Then the Stellar Archive lit up at the edge of my vision. **[Institute Written Examination – Day 0.]** **[Body Status: Functional. Anxiety Levels: Predictably Elevated.]** \"Shut up,\" I whispered. I lay there for one more breath. My heart was already tapping out a map of the exam hall that didn't exist yet. On the narrow table by the stove, Ma's old, cracked phone chimed the hour. 5:00 AM. I swung my legs off the bed and sat up. Rao had given me two days leave. \"Nothing productive happens in your head in the last forty-eight hours before an exam except panic,\" he'd said, leaning back in his chair two days earlier. Megha had nodded in agreement. \"Sir, that sounds like personal experience,\" I'd said. \"Decades of it,\" he'd replied. \"Go home. Revise lightly. Sleep. If I see you in this building before the exam, I'll have you thrown out for loitering.\" So I'd stayed home. Revised with a light hand instead of tearing through new material. Now, on the morning that counted, the room felt smaller than it had ever been. Ma was already up. She moved around the stove with the controlled urgency. \"Brush, then tea,\" she said without turning. By six, I was dressed. Grey T-shirt, the newer one with fewer repairs. Dark trousers that had been ironed into a temporary truce with the concept of straight lines. The shoes I'd cleaned last night until the original color rediscovered itself. Ma set up my collar anyway. \"I am going to write, not attend a wedding,\" I said. \"You're going to change your life,\" she snapped back. \"Let me fix your collar.\" She stepped back, surveyed me with a critical eye, and made a small, satisfied sound. On the table, she'd packed a small bag: water bottle, tiffin, an extra handkerchief, and the exam admit card folded inside a transparent sleeve like it was a relic. She handed the card to me. \"Check,\" she said. **Name: Arin Valen.** Track: Ambassador Studies & Delegation – Candidate (Written). Center: Vasantpur Municipal Complex – Block C. Reporting time: 8:00 AM. Exam: 9:00 AM – 12:00 PM, 1:00 PM – 4:00 PM. \"If we leave now, we'll reach by seven-thirty,\" Ma said. \"I don't trust buses on days that matter.\" \"We barely trust them on days that don't,\" I said. She ignored that and picked up her dupatta. \"Let's go,\" she said. The Archive slid a tiny update into view. **[Distance to Center: 7.1 km.]** **[Recommended: Public Transit + Short Walk. Probability of \"dramatic collapse en route\": 18%.]** \"We're taking the bus,\" I told it silently. \"Not swimming the river.\" **[If you faint today, I will disown you as a Host.]** \"Motivating,\" I murmured, and followed my mother. The streets were different at dawn. Less noise We boarded a bus that smell of rust. Ma kept one hand on the rail, one lightly on my shoulder whenever the driver braked too hard. I sat by the window. The municipal complex rose out of the morning haze like someone had taken all the seriousness in the city and poured it into one concrete block. High walls. Security scanners at the gate. Temporary banners hung across the entrance: **INSTITUTE WRITTEN EXAMINATION CENTER – VASANTPUR** MUNICIPAL COMPLEX. Clusters of parents. Guild representatives in understated expensive clothing. Kids my age and older, some in proper uniforms, some in whatever passed for clean at their homes. Ma tightened her hand on my shoulder. \"All these people…\" she murmured. **[All these competitors]** the Archive corrected. We joined the slowly forming line at the main gate. Ahead of us, a boy in a neat blazer leaned against his mother's side, scrolling lazily through a holo-tablet. Next to them, another kid grinned at a man in an expensive-insignia overcoat. \"Dad, I'm telling you, I'm going to get into Combat Arts,\" he said. \"Let's clear the written before you start killing Fog-beasts,\" his father said dryly. \"But yes. If you get Combat, I'll upgrade your gear. Full-set armour, not this old stuff.\" Behind us, two girls whispered to each other, both clutching thin tablets. \"What if you get Gate Research?\" the other teased. \"You'll end up married to data in a basement.\" \"Better than some of the boys in our class,\" she shot back. Ma watched them, her face carefully neutral. She had nothing to offer me except a tiffin and a hand on my back. Which, in a world full of representative cards and guild contacts, somehow felt like both not enough and more than most people ever get. \"You can still come home,\" she said quietly, as if she'd heard my thoughts. \"If it doesn't work. We'll… manage.\" I looked at her. \"If it doesn't work,\" I said, \"we'll try again. And if that doesn't work, we'll find something else. But today, I'm not planning for 'if it doesn't work.'\" \"Fine,\" she said. Check-in was predictably humiliating. One line for metal detection. Another for biometrics. A third for hall assignments. Each step accompanied by officials with tired eyes and voices worn smooth by repetition. \"Admit card.\" \"Thumb on the scanner.\" \"Hall C-2. Ground floor.\" Ma walked with me until the \"Parents Stop Here\" line painted on the concrete. Beyond it, the corridor led into exam halls. This side of the line, parents paced, prayed, argued with each other about cut-offs and quotas. Ma said. \"I'll wait at the tea stall outside.\" \"You don't have to wait all day,\" I said. \"It's six hours, Ma. You have work—\" \"I told the supervisor I have a medical thing,\" she said Her hand came up, smoothed my hair automatically, then dropped. \"Write properly,\" she said. I nodded, swallowed my saliva and crossed the gate The exam hall was big enough to host a minor war. Rows of single desks stretched from door to far wall, each with a candidate number stuck to the front. A clock hung high near the ceiling. \"Find your roll number,\" an invigilator barked. I did. C-2-173. Middle row, slightly to the left. I sat. Placed my pen parallel to the admit card. Around me, the atmosphere thickened. The low buzz of whispering aspirants faded as more invigilators entered, carrying sachel-cases that rattled faintly with question papers. **[Heart Rate: +18% above resting.]** **[This is not a life-and-death battle. You may relax your fight-or-flight response.]** \"Exams killed me last time,\" I reminded it. The chief invigilator took position by the desk at the front. \"Phones and devices are to remain in the lockers. Holo-wear off. Any active interface during the exam will result in disqualification.\" Several kids with expensive earpieces shifted uncomfortably going out. **[I will not assist,]** it said. **[You are on your own in there.]** \"Good,\" I thought. \"This part is mine.\" The papers were distributed. Face down, a thin rectangle of doom. \"Do not turn the paper until instructed,\" the invigilator barked. The seconds stretched. Then: \"Begin.\" I flipped over.."},{"t":"13. The Day- II","k":"Chronicles · chapter","u":"chronicles/c13.html","s":"I scanned the heading **PAPER - I** **Institut d'Études Intermondiales** **(Institute of Inter World Studies)** **Subject: Aptitude, Reasoning, Current Affairs, Ethics and Fog Phenomenology** **Time: 3 Hours** **Maximum Marks: 200** First, my eyes ran down the sections, Mapping the terrain. Simple data interpretation. Ethics scenarios about intervention and non-intervention with foreign civilizations approached inside the Fog. Second, my mind, trained by years of UPSC pattern drills, began triaging. Easy, moderate, time-sink. Attempt order. Time allocation. Answers flowed—not flawlessly, but steadily. Where I didn't know, I built. Structured guesses. Logical extensions from known data. Where memory faltered, I wrote around it, framing what I knew and clearly marking what I didn't, the way good examiners appreciated even in failure. Around me, the tension shifted texture over the hours. At the start, it was—pens hovering, brows furrowed. Halfway through, it turned into calculation—eyes flicking to the clock, pages turning faster. In the last thirty minutes, it became pure, distilled panic in a bottle. \"Last fifteen minutes,\" the invigilator announced. I was on the final ethics question. I outlined the competing interests. Stability versus monopoly. Short-term gain versus long-term strategic dependence. Proposed a middle-path: limited technology sharing under strict treaty conditions, multilateral oversight. The pen moved more easily than my hand remembered it ever had. \"Stop writing,\" the invigilator commanded. Hands lifted, pens hovering an inch above paper. I exhaled. Paper One: done. The break felt unreal. We shuffled out into the corridor. My legs complained as blood remembered there were other parts of me besides my fingers. In the courtyard, parents waited at the perimeter. Vendors had materialized near the gates, selling tea, water, quick snacks. Ma waved frantically when she saw me. She just thrust a tiffin into my hands without asking anything. \"Eat,\" she ordered. We sat on a bench nearby and I opened it. Flatbread, rolled tight around spiced potatoes. Easy to eat without cutlery. I glanced around. A boy nearby groaned dramatically to his friend. \"Bro, that cross fog civilization contact question? Totally from last year's coaching module. Sir literally solved the same pattern in class. Easy fifty marks.\" Another shook his head. \"I think they twisted the tariff question. You guys are all saying 'increase tariffs'; I think the answer was to lower them to encourage regulated trade.\" A girl paced circles around her father. \"I missed one entire data set Dad. I hope it to be fine\" Her father murmured something soothing about cut-off ranges. I finished eating, drank water, and leaned back against the wall. My chest expanded more easily than it had six months ago. The cough that came after was small, almost polite. \"Second paper?\" Ma asked. \"Ambassadorial aptitude,\" I said. \"Case studies, scenario analysis, probably some language and culture.\" She frowned. \"You studied, no?\" \"For this?\" I said. \"I've been studying for this since I got out of the bed.\" The Archive pinged **[Overconfidence noted for future grading.]** I closed the tiffin and handed it back. \"I have to go in,\" I said. \"They'll start calling us.\" She gripped my forearm. \"Write calmly,\" she said. \"Don't try to be clever. Just be yourself.\" I smiled, turned, and walked back toward the hall. **PAPER- II** **Institut d'Études Intermondiales** **(Institute of Inter World Studies)** **AMBASSADORIAL AND DIPLOMATIC APTITUDE** **Subject: Polity, International and Extraterrestial Relations, Economics** **Geography** **Time: 3 Hours** **Max Marks: 500** Where Paper One had been broad, testing whether you could swim in shallow water, this one checked if you could survive being dropped in the ocean. Scenarios, fragments of diplomatic correspondence to complete. A question about designing seating for a multi-civilization summit so that no party felt insulted by position. Another about responding to an accidental cultural breach that risked turning a negotiation sour. Halfway through. **[You appear to be enjoying this.]** \"Of course, This is the part I'm good at.\" **[Good enough to pass?]** \"Ask me in three hours.\" The tension in the hall was different this time. Less shared. More individual. In Paper One, most people knew whether a question was doable. Here, answers lived in grey zones. At one point, the boy two rows ahead of me put his head down on the desk for a full thirty seconds, then snapped back up and scribbled furiously as if outrunning his own doubt. \"Last ten minutes,\" the invigilator announced. My hand cramped. I flexed it discreetly, finished the final advisory note, and re-checked my answers. \"Stop writing.\" Pens down. Silence. The rustle of papers being collected. That was it. Six hours of proving to a faceless panel that I wasn't just a statistic with damaged lungs. Stepping out of the hall at four-fifteen felt like exiting a different weather system. The light outside had shifted to late afternoon gold. \"It was so easy, man,\" one kid was saying loudly. \"If you did the coaching mock series, half the questions were straight lifts.\" \"Shut up,\" his friend hissed. \"Why are you saying that so happily? Now cut-off will go higher.\" Near the gate, a girl clutched her bag strap and tried not to cry. \"I blanked on the second case study,\" she told her mother. \"I knew it. We did the same thing in class. But when I saw the names, my brain just… froze.\" Her mother wrapped an arm around her shoulders. \"You wrote something,\" she said firmly. \"You don't know. Sometimes they like different angles.\" Two boys argued about a specific answer. \"The tariff question— I told you, lowering is better for long-term soft power.\" \"And I told you, you're overthinking. They want stability, not idealism.\" Off to one side, a quiet girl who had sat three rows to my right in the hall said to her father, \"I think I did okay. Not perfect. But… okay. If they want perfect, they'll pick the guild kids.\" Her father's jaw tightened. \"If they want perfect, they can hire machines,\" he said. \"They built this exam for people like you. Don't reduce yourself before they even mark the paper.\" Ma stood near the tea stall, neck craned, hand shielding her eyes from the sun. When she spotted me, her shoulders dropped in visible relief. \"How was it?\" she asked. I considered my answer. \"Honest paper,\" I said finally. \"Not generous. Not cruel. I did what I could.\" She searched my face like she was looking for something between the words. \"That's… good?\" she ventured. \"It's… real,\" I said. She nodded slowly. \"Come,\" she said. We didn't go to the library that evening. We went home first, so I could wash the residue of exam tension off my skin. But by late evening, I found myself standing at the library's familiar sandstone steps anyway. Ma had insisted on coming. \"I'll go to the factory tomorrow,\" she'd told her supervisor over the phone. \"Today, I have somewhere else to be.\" Rao was at his desk. He looked up when we approached. \"Arin,\" he said. \" and Mrs. Valen.\" Ma folded her hands. \"Namaste, sir.\" He inclined his head. \"Well?\" he asked, eyes on me. \"Well,\" I echoed. \"We did our respective jobs.\" Ma shot me a warning glance. Rao's mouth twitched. \"Good,\" he said. \"An exam's first duty is to be written. The second is to be forgotten until results.\" He reached into a drawer and pulled out a small packet wrapped in old newspaper. \"Here,\" he said, tossing it lightly. I caught it, barely. Inside: laddoos. Two, slightly misshapen, sticking to each other. \"My wife's fault,\" he said. \"She keeps sending them 'just in case someone clears something.' Today seemed like a just-in-case day.\" Ma looked mortified. \"We can't—\" \"You can,\" he interrupted. \"Think of it as… carbohydrate support for future study.\" He studied my face more closely. \"You're not coughing,\" he observed. \"Not as much,\" I said. \"Good,\" he said. \"Interview boards dislike candidates who collapse mid-answer.\" \"You're assuming I'll clear written,\" I said. He shrugged. \"I am assuming nothing. I am merely preparing my blood pressure for possible futures.\" He leaned back. \"Go home,\" he said. \"Do something that is not reading for one evening. Come back tomorrow. The shelves have missed you.\" Ma stood up and I followed behind her. We stood at the Bus Stop as the sky darkened. \"Arin\" my mother quietly said \"Ma?\" I replied \"Even if you do not pass, remember-\" she turned to face me \"I am proud of you\" I didn't say anything, just nodded and for the first time felt in both of my lives. *It was worth it*"},{"t":"14. Stage One Clear","k":"Chronicles · chapter","u":"chronicles/c14.html","s":"*Please Join the Discord: discord.gg/XD4VFunFCz* Results did not arrive for the next few days The Institute apparently seemed to follow the principle that suspense strengthens character. Days stretched. I went back to my cleaning schedule, my notes, my unofficial oral exams with Rao. Ma pretended not to check the Academy portal every night. The Archive, traitor that it was, helpfully tracked the days since exam. **[Days Since Written: 5.]** **[Days Since Written: 9.]** **[Days Since Written: 14.]** At the fifteen-day mark, as I was shelving a stack of foreign civilization texts, a new notification cut through the usual background hum of Archive messages. **[Institute Written Examination – Results Released.]** I froze. **[Candidate: Arin Valen]** **[General Track – Status: 170/200- Passed]** **[Ambassador Studies & Delegation Aptitude – Status:390/500- Passed .]** **[Overall Standing: Within Interview Cut-off Range.]** **[Next Step: Panel Interview Scheduling – Notification Pending.]** For a moment, the words blurred. Passed. Not topped. Not ranked. Not yet. Just one simple, heavy word. Passed **[Host]** the Archive said quietly. No sarcasm, no commentary. \"Yeah?\" I whispered. **[Good Job]** \"Valen?\" Rao's voice floated over from behind me. I turned. He stood there with a thin print-out in his hand. The Academy must have sent official lists to institutional centers; of course the library was on that distribution tree. He looked at the paper, then at me. \"Arin Valen,\" he read. \"General Track: pass, Ambassadorial aptitude: pass. Called for interview.\" He lowered the sheet. \"Well,\" he smiled. \"It seems my blood pressure preparations were not wasted.\" Ma would be at the home right now, thinking about what to make for dinner. As I turned to run home \"Valen\" I paused. \"You wrote 'when I pass' in that notebook,\" he said. \"I saw it, Terrible handwriting.\" Heat rushed to my face. \"You weren't supposed to—\" \"Next time,\" he said, \"leave more space after the 'when.' Life has a habit of adding things you didn't plan for.\" He waved a hand, as if dismissing me for a trivial errand. \"Go,\" he repeated. I went. Outside, the city hummed the same as it always had. But under it, something else had shifted. Telling my mother I had passed was harder than answering any ethics case study. With the system notification still hovering faintly at the edge of my vision, I took the stairs to our room two at a time. That alone would have been a miracle six months ago. Now, the Null Frame rose and fell without complaint, a quiet harness around my lungs as if it had been waiting for this climb. The door was unlatched. I pushed it open. Ma sat on the floor by the stove, sorting pulses into a steel container. The fan ticked overhead in its usual off-beat rhythm. \"Ma,\" I said. She didn't look up immediately. \"You're home early,\" she said. \"Rao-sir threw you out again?\" \"No,\" I said. \"He… dismissed me. Officially. For the day.\" That got her attention. She straightened, palms dusty with flour, eyes narrowing. \"Why? Are you ill?\" \"Not ill,\" I said quickly. \"Just… read this.\" I held out my report card. She wiped her hands on her dupatta, took the card. **INSTITUTE WRITTEN EXAMINATION – RESULTS** CANDIDATE: ARIN VALEN GENERAL TRACK – STATUS: PASSED AMBASSADOR STUDIES & DELEGATION APTITUDE – STATUS: PASSED SHORTLISTED FOR INTERVIEW Her lips moved as she read, the words forming silently before they reached comprehension. Then she Froze. The fan ticked. Outside, someone shouted the price for vegetables. Somewhere in the building, a child laughed. Ma blinked once. Twice. \"Passed,\" she said, as if testing whether the word would hold. \"Yes,\" I said. \"For sure,\" she added, like there might be a second page that said \"just kidding.\" I pointed at the screen. \"Unless they've started issuing emotional damage pranks through the civic network, yes. It's real.\" She stared at the report card a moment longer. Then she exhaled slowly, like air had been trapped in her chest for years and had only now been granted an exit permit. \"I told the supervisor you would,\" she said. \"You what?\" She sniffed, looking offended. \"He said, 'Madam, results takes time; why you are worrying so much?' I told him, 'My son will pass. You keep giving me extra shifts, and when he gets into the Institute, I'll take all my sick leave at once.'\" \"That is not how sick leave works,\" I said. \"Fine,\" she said. \"I'll fall ill properly that time. Happy?\" The corner of my mouth twitched. She wiped at her face with the edge of her dupatta, pretending it was sweat. \"Interview also,\" she said. \"There is interview, no?\" \"Yes.\" \"You will pass that also,\" she said, with the tone of someone filing a complaint directly to the universe. Then, more quietly, \"You have to. I don't have a plan for… not.\" I sat down opposite her. \"Ma,\" I said. \"We didn't have a plan for 'not' last time either. I still survived.\" She glared, then let out an unwilling laugh that sounded like it had to fight past too many other emotions. \"You tell him?\" she asked after a moment. \"Rao?\" I nodded. \"He had a printed list before I even saw the notification. I think the Academy sends him results faster than they send them to candidates.\" \"Of course,\" she murmured. \"He's a librarian. Knowledge respects seniority.\" \"Is that how it works?\" I asked. \"Don't argue with me when I'm proud of you,\" she said sharply. She reached across, caught my chin in her hand, and turned my face side to side like she was checking if passing an exam had done anything to my bone structure. \"You still look the same,\" she said. \"Just more… there.\" \"More… what?\" \"More you,\" she said, waving a hand as if that explained everything. Then she let go. \"Wash up,\" she said briskly. \"We'll go to the library. You'll say thank you properly. Then you'll start preparing to talk to important people without sounding like a smart-mouthed idiot.\" \"That's… specific,\" I said. \"Yes,\" she said. The first thing Rao did when he saw me back in the library was not congratulate me. \"Valen,\" he said, glancing up from a stack of returns. \"I thought I dismissed you for the day.\" Ma hovered behind me, suddenly shy in the cool, filtered air. She folded and unfolded the edge of her dupatta. \"Sir,\" she said. \"He… passed.\" \"So the paper says,\" Rao replied. He studied me for a long moment. His expression didn't change much, but something in his gaze did—like he'd taken me down from a shelf labeled \"hypothetical\" and moved me to \"currently in circulation.\" \"Well done,\" he said at last. \"You have successfully convinced a panel of overworked examiners that you can read and think under pressure.\" Ma made a small outraged noise. \"Sir, it is not just—\" \"It is exactly that, Mrs. Valen,\" Rao said mildly. \"And it is not small.\" He shifted his attention back to me. \"Do you know what happens next?\" he asked. \"Interview,\" I said. \"Mixed representation from Institute and guilds.\" \"And?\" he prompted. \"And they decide whether I'm worth giving a bed and three meals a day,\" I said. \"Or whether I should continue my current diet of dust and humility.\" \"That is the practical translation, yes,\" he said. \"But what does the interview actually measure?\" \"Knowledge?\" I ventured. \"Awareness of current affairs. Understanding of politics. Ability to reason through scenarios.\" \"That is what the written measured,\" he said. \"The interview measures whether you can do any of that while inhabiting a body and a social space at the same time.\" I frowned. \"Meaning?\" \"Meaning,\" he said, \"you will not be a disembodied answer sheet in that room. You will be a breathing, visibly fragile adolescent claiming a seat in a track usually filled with polished guild-trained candidates. They will watch what you say, but more importantly, how you say it. How you sit. Where your eyes go. When you choose to remain silent.\" Ma bristled. \"Fragile? He is much stronger now.\" \"I know,\" Rao said calmly. \"But the panel's eyes will still see ribs before resolve, unless he teaches them otherwise.\" He turned fully to me. \"Written exams are about what you know,\" he said. \"Interviews are about what you are.\" \"That seems unfair,\" I said. \"Of course it is,\" he said. \"That is why you must walk in having already decided who you are. Or they will decide it for you.\" The Archive stirred in the back of my mind. **[Interview: Narrative Assessment Module.] [Risk: High. Opportunity: Higher.]** \"Any practical advice?\" I asked, trying not to show how my palms had just started sweating. \"Yes,\" Rao said. \"When the schedule comes, bring it to me. We will see how much time the Institute has given you to become presentable.\" He looked at Ma. \"Mrs. Valen,\" he said. \"Your son has done well. But if you praise him too much now, he will float into the interview room and forget to sit down.\" Ma's eyes narrowed. \"As if he listens.\" \"Then perhaps he will listen to this,\" Rao said, addressing me again. \"Passing written proves you can serve. Interview proves you can represent. Those are not the same skill.\" \"I'll… learn,\" I said. \"You have been learning since the day you walked into my library and fainted on the steps,\" he said dryly. \"Go home. Wait for the scheduling notice. The scheduling notice arrived three days later, without fanfare and with the efficiency of a bureaucratic guillotine. I was at the library, midway through wiping down Reading Hall B tables out of habit more than obligation, when the Archive interrupted my internal rant about someone's abandoned tea cup ring. **[Institute Interview Scheduling Notification – Received.]**"},{"t":"15. Interview - I","k":"Chronicles · chapter","u":"chronicles/c15.html","s":"I set the rag down carefully. The Archive expanded the text. **[Candidate: Arin Valen.]** **[Track: Ambassador Studies & Delegation.]** **[Interview Date: 12 days from today.]** **[Location: Parliament Annex – Central Conclave Hall, Delhi]** **[Panel Composition:** **1.)Institute Senior Instructor (Ambassador Studies)** **2.)Guild Representative (Rotational),** **3.)External Observer (Rotational).]** **[Dress Code: Formal, modest.]** Twelve days. Not enough to become someone else. Enough, maybe, to become a sharper version of myself. I took a breath, let the Frame catch it. Rao sir appeared at the end of the hall like a librarian-shaped coincidence. \"You have that look,\" he said. \"Scheduling?\" I nodded and relayed the essentials. \"Twelve days,\" he repeated. \"Hm. They are in a hurry this year. Probably budget cycles.\" \"That's what you're concerned about?\" I asked. \"I am concerned about many things,\" he said. \"But I can only do something about some of them. Come.\" He led me to his office and gestured to the chair opposite his desk. \"Sit,\" he said. \"How you sit in here is how you will sit in there.\" I sat, at once hyper-aware of my spine, shoulders, and where my hands were supposed to go. \"Stop perching like a frightened bird,\" he said. \"You are not on trial, Yet.\" \"I feel like it,\" I said. \"Good,\" he replied. \"A little fear keeps arrogance in check. Now. We have twelve days. You will still clean, because routine anchors the mind. You will still read. But we add one more paper to your schedule.\" \"Which paper?\" I asked. \"Paper Zero,\" he said. \"Being Arin Valen without apologizing for it.\" Interview prep was nothing like exam prep. You cannot make mind-maps of yourself. Or rather, you can, but it looks suspiciously like narcissism. Still, old habits die hard. I opened a fresh notebook—courtesy of the library's mysterious surplus spirits—and wrote at the top: **INTERVIEW – AMBASSADOR STUDIES** Who am I? What do I know? Why me? \"These are ridiculous questions,\" I told the Archive. **[They are the ones they will ask, in different words,]** it replied. **[Better you embarrass yourself here than in front of the panel.]** So I tried. Who am I? – Thirteen. – Human. – Weak lungs, improving. – Former UPSC aspirant from another life. – Current library volunteer. – Applicant for Ambassador Studies. It felt both too much and not enough. What do I know? – Fog and Energy graphs basics, phenomenology, Institute structure. – Foreign civilization structures from compendium + extra texts. – Geopolitics – Negotiation case studies. – How to clean tables efficiently. -- How to survive a sarcastic rectangle \"Maybe skip the tables in the interview,\" I muttered. **[Do not underestimate the diplomatic value of cleaning up other people's messes,]** the Archive said. **[Bureaucracies are built on that skill.]** Why me? That question sat there, heavy, not interested in bullet points. I left it blank for the moment. Instead, I moved to practical drills. In the evenings, after the library closed, I sat at our lone table at home, back straight, feet flat, and practiced saying my own name like it belonged to someone who deserved a chair. \"Good morning, respected panel,\" I said to the cracked plaster wall. \"My name is Arin Valen.\" Ma watched from the stove, eyebrows raised. \"You're talking to the ceiling now,\" she observed. \"I'm rehearsing,\" I said. \"For what?\" \"For talking to people who are not the ceiling.\" She snorted. \"Don't say 'respected panel,'\" she said. \"Say 'sir' and 'madam' like a normal person.\" \"What if they identify differently?\" I asked. She threw a spoon at me. At the library, Rao added his own refinements. \"Again,\" he said for the fifth time that afternoon, as I sat in his office chair, palms resting on my thighs. \"Good morning,\" I said. \"My name is Arin Valen. I'm from Vasantpur. I'm applying for Ambassador Studies because—\" \"Stop,\" he said. \"You are talking to the floor. The panel's faces are not near your shoes.\" \"I don't want to accidentally stare at their eyes and forget my name,\" I said. \"Then practice looking between their eyebrows,\" he said. \"It gives the illusion of eye-contact without your brain panicking.\" \"That's a trick,\" I said, intrigued. \"Everything in diplomacy is a trick,\" he said. \"Again.\" I tried. \"Good morning,\" I said, focusing on an imaginary point just above his nose. \"My name is Arin Valen. I'm from Vasantpur. I'm applying for Ambassador Studies because I believe my strengths lie in understanding perspectives, not in fighting on frontlines.\" \"Better,\" he said. \"Still too rehearsed.You must know your first three sentences well enough to improvise them.\" Content prep was easier. It was familiar territory. Rao assigned me \"homework.\" \"Every day until the interview,\" he said, \"you will brief me on one current issue. Three minutes. No notes. I will interrupt.\" \"Of course you will,\" I muttered. Issues piled up quickly. Day one: A fluctuation in the energy graphs near the Northern Approach that had forced three guilds into temporary cooperation. Day two: A minor trade dispute between two foreign polities over resource extraction rights. Day three: A controversy about whether the Institute should allocate more seats to non-blessed candidates. I stood in front of his desk, hands clasped behind my back like a very short, underfed diplomat, and delivered summaries. \"The Northern Approach fluctuation has highlighted both the strengths and weaknesses of current inter-guild protocols,\" I said one afternoon. \"On one hand, rapid cooperation prevented civilian casualties. On the other, the underlying rivalry for —\" \"What would you recommend if you were in that meeting?\" Rao cut in. \"I'm not in that—\" \"Imagine,\" he said. \"You are. Go on.\" I hesitated, then answered. I tried again. \"The crisis showed we can work together,\" I said. \"If we turn that into a rule now, we won't have to gamble on it next time. And if everyone knows they'll get a fair share, they'll be less tempted to sabotage each other.\" \"Better,\" he said. \"Remember: you are not there to show them how much you know. You are there to show them that giving you more information would be a good investment.\" Etiquette was trickier. There were no official manuals for \"How Not to Insult a Foreign Delegation in 30 Seconds,\" but the Ambassador Studies section in the compendium had a few pages on posture, greeting, and introductions. I read them carefully. \"No sudden gestures,\" I murmured. \"No pointing. No crossing legs toward elders. Keep voice level. Don't interrupt. Acknowledge questions before answering. Do not attempt jokes unless you are absolutely sure they will land.\" At home, Ma watched me practice sitting and standing like I wasn't about to fall over. \"You're walking straight,\" she remarked one evening as I paced the length of the room, practicing the distance from door to chair. \"I have a spine now,\" I said. \"You always had a spine,\" she said. She grew quiet, then added, \"Don't let them make you feel small. Even if they're big people.\" \"I am small,\" I said. \"Height wise, Lung wise.\" \"You know what I mean,\" she snapped. \"You've met enough 'big people'. They sit on chairs and breathe air like everyone else.\" \"Some of them breathe better,\" I said. She pointed a spoon at me. \"You are not doing this inside my house,\" she warned Twelve days shrank quickly. On Eleventh day's evening, I was standing on the railway platform, my hands on the adjacent arms rubbing to give me warmth in the chilled evening of November. Ma stood beside me, then I heard footsteps which stopped just beside me. \"Valen,\" Rao said. \"Sir.\" He handed me a printed sheet. The Academy's emblem glared from the top. **INTERVIEW ALLOTMENT – AMBASSADOR STUDIES** CANDIDATE: ARIN VALEN DATE: 30th November, 2031 TIME: 09:40 A.M – 10:10 A.M PANEL: – Senior Instructor: Diksha Sen (Institut d'Études Intermondiales) – Guild Representative: Mikhail Orlov Volkonskaya (Zarya Consortium) – External Observer: Kenzo Hayato (Japanese Ambassador)"},{"t":"16. Interview - II","k":"Chronicles · chapter","u":"chronicles/c16.html","s":"*Please leave your Advices and suggestions, for advance communication join the discord: discord.gg/XD4VFunFCz* Delhi did not smell the way I remembered. In my first life, it had been dust, exhaust, street food, and sweat—compressed ambition under a humid sky. In this one, the sky above the capital held the faint metallic texture of barrier fields, layered over the same old exhaust and ambition. **[Location: Delhi – Central Administrative Sector.]** **[Warning: Probability of bureaucratic nonsense: extremely high.]** \"Comforting,\" I muttered. Rao adjusted the strap of the worn leather bag on his shoulder. He wore his usual shirt and trousers, ironed into reluctant obedience, and a slightly better pair of shoes than usual. \"Stop staring at the buildings like a tourist,\" he demanded. Ahead of us, the Central Annex of Parliament rose from the stone like someone had taken all the seriousness in Delhi and built a monument with it. Sandstone and glass, old and new, stitched together by blue circuitry veins. Flags fluttered from the frontage—Institute crest, national emblem, and a guild banner I recognized from too many news segments. It bore a stylized sun over a fractured horizon. Zarya Consortium. Russian-heritage guild. One of the top three in the world when it came to cross-civilizational logistics and \"special operations,\" which was polite language for \"we go places others die trying to reach.\" \"Zarya's here,\" I said, trying to keep my voice neutral. \"Mm,\" Rao said. \"Their chairman is on the Ambassadorial oversight panel this year.\" We joined the line of candidates snaking toward the Annex security gate. Ma had hugged me so hard before I left Vasantpur that the Archive had logged a brief oxygen spike. \"Don't argue with them,\" she'd said. \"Don't argue with yourself either. And if you faint, make sure you fall forward. At least then they'll remember your face.\" \"Such faith,\" I'd told her. Now, that faith sat beside me in the form of one aging librarian who had absolutely no obligation to accompany a factory worker's son and had done it anyway. \"Valen,\" he said, \"Once you cross that gate, I cannot help you. My job is to make sure you arrive at it. Upright.\" He fixed me with his examiner look. \"You will go in,\" he said. \"You will make an honest attempt to show them who you are. If they fail to understand, that is their exam to retake, not yours.\" Somewhere, the Archive scribbled that down under \"quotable nonsense that sounds wise.\" The security guard held up a hand. \"Next,\" he called. \"Candidate card.\" I stepped forward, handed over my civic ID and printed interview call letter. The scanner flickered. My name and track appeared briefly on his holo-glasses. \"Ambassador Studies,\" he read. His tone didn't change, but something in the way he nodded did. \"Second floor,\" he said. \"Hall A-3. Panels running in order. Follow the signs.\" He handed my card back. Rao stopped at the glass line marked \"Candidates Only.\" \"Go,\" he said. \"See you after,\" I said. The waiting area outside Hall A-3 was a battleground of different kind. Some candidates sat rigid, backs straight, eyes closed, lips moving silently around prepared answers. Others flipped through last-minute notes on tablets, scrolling faster than any human brain could assimilate. A few just stared at the floor, perhaps hoping it would open and swallow them before their name was called. \"C-17?\" an usher called. A girl near the front stood, wiped her palms against her skirt, and disappeared into the hall. \"C-18?\" Another boy. Another disappearance. My own number—C-23—approached at the speed of inevitability. **[Heart Rate: +24% above baseline, the Archive noted. Breath Pattern: stable]** \"Let me handle the panic,\" it said. \"You handle the sentences.\" \"I thought you refused to assist in exams,\" I thought back. **[Interviews are not exams,]** it replied. **[They are theatre. Different clause in my contract.]** \"Good,\" I whispered. \"C-23?\" the usher called. I stood. The walk from the waiting bench to the interview room door felt like the longest twenty steps in my life so far, which was impressive considering I'd once done an entire bridge on failing lungs. The door slid open with efficient quiet. Inside, the room was colder. A long table at the far end. Three people seated behind it. On the left, a woman in Institute's formal—a dark blazer with the Ambassador Studies sigil at the lapel, hair pulled back, eyes sharp and assessing. In the middle, a man with the neutral, polished look of someone who had spent a lot of time in rooms like this and had learned how to take up exactly as much space as he wanted and no more. On the right, a broad-shouldered, black-haired man with grey strands peeking in between, who wore a suit that fit like it had never known the word \"off-the-rack.\" His beard was trimmed short, his gaze heavy. A Zarya sun pin glowed at his collar. The pressure in the room shifted a fraction when my eyes met his. **[Chairman]**, the Archive whispered. **[Probability: 93%].** Of course. \"Good morning,\" I said, focusing on the space between their eyebrows because Rao had told me to. \"My name is Arin Valen.\" \"Sit, Mr. Valen,\" the woman on the left said. Her voice was clear, clipped. \"I'm Instructor Diksha Sen, Ambassador Studies. This is Mr. Kenzo Hayato , external observer . And this is Chairman Mikhail Orlov Volkonskaya of the Zarya Consortium.\" \"Good morning,\" I repeated, sitting carefully. Feet flat. Back straight. Hands resting lightly on my thighs. \"Your file is… interesting,\" Mr. Hayato said, glancing at his terminal. \"Vasantpur resident. No special ability certification. You did not even attend Middle school properly and yet you have High written scores.\" There was no judgment in his tone. Yet. \"Thank you, sir,\" I said, because that seemed safer than \"My lungs and I appreciate your interest.\" \"So,\" Instructor Sen said, folding her hands. \"Why Ambassador Studies?\" This was one of the questions I had rehearsed the most. Which, as it turned out, was exactly the problem. I inhaled. \"Ma'am,\" I began, \"Ambassador Studies sits at the intersection of geopolitics, culture, and ethics. I believe that in a post-Fog world, the primary battleground is no longer just physical territory, but narrative space. Whichever civilization can control the framing of interactions—\" \"Stop,\" a small, sane part of my brain said. \"—will have disproportionate influence over treaty structures and long-term resource allocation,\" I finished. Instructor Sen blinked once. \"So,\" she said, \"you want to control narratives?\" \"Not control,\" I said quickly. \"Facilitate. Mediate between competing narratives to arrive at stable equilibria that—\" \"—that you deem acceptable,\" Chairman Orlov finished for me, his accent wrapping itself around the words. His eyes were on me now. They were not friendly. They weren't hostile either. They were weighing. I realized, belatedly, that I sounded less like a thirteen-year-old from a factory district and more like a tired think-tank report. \"Next question,\" Mr. Hayato said smoothly, perhaps for my sake. \"Mr. Valen,\" he said, \"there has been a debate recently about whether the Institute should reserve additional seats for candidates without blessings or guild backing. You have neither. Do you think such reservations are fair?\" I knew this one. I'd thought through it with the Archive two nights ago. \"Yes, sir,\" I said. \"I believe they are fair as transitional measures to correct existing structural imbalances in access to training and opportunities. Without them, the Academy risks becoming an echo chamber for guild narratives, which weakens—\" \"You are answering like a manifesto,\" Instructor Sen cut in, not unkindly. \"Try again. One sentence. No jargon.\" I swallowed. \"Yes, ma'am,\" I said. \"I think it's fair to give people like me a chance to catch up, so that when we stand next to guild candidates, the only difference between us is what we do, not where we started.\" That landed better. I saw something soften, just a fraction, at the corner of her mouth. Chairman Orlov's gaze did not soften. \"And yet,\" he said, his voice heavy, \"if we fill this Institute with weak bodies for the sake of fairness, who will stand at the Frontlines when things that come through that fog do not care about fairness?\" There was no good one-line answer to that. \"Those who can,\" I said softly. \"This track doesn't send people to hold the front line with their bodies, sir. It sends them to hold it with their words. A healthy fool in this chair would do more damage than a sick person who understands consequences.\" My hands had clenched on my thighs without my noticing. Instructor Sen's eyes sharpened. \"So you,\" she said, \"are not a fool.\" \"I try not to be, ma'am,\" I said. \"Sometimes try is not enough,\" she said. \"Next question. Describe a time you changed your mind about something important.\" My rehearsed answer was ready. \"I once believed that merit alone was enough to—\" \"Boring,\" Chairman Orlov said flatly. I blinked. \"Sir?\" \"Every candidate we have seen says some version of that,\" he said. \"Merit. Hard work. Gated world, blah blah. Tell me something that is not in your notes.\" My mouth went dry. \"I—\" The room tilted, just a little. Not from health this time. From the realization that I had walked in here wearing a version of myself I thought they wanted to see: composed, clever, shaped by exam prep and Rao's drills. And they were already peeling it off. \"Permission to… get some air, sir?\" I heard myself say. Three sets of eyebrows went up. Behind them, three slightly different calculations flickered. \"You want a break?\" Mr. Hayato said. \"Yes, sir,\" I said. \"For… one minute. I won't open any notes. I just… need to reset.\" It was a risk. Panels were not fond of disruptions. Instructor Sen looked at the others. \"We are not in a rush. Let the boy breathe\" she said quietly. Chairman Orlov studied me for a moment longer, then inclined his head, once. \"One minute,\" he said. \"Do not run away.\""},{"t":"17. Interview - III","k":"Chronicles · chapter","u":"chronicles/c17.html","s":"The door clicked softly behind Arin as he stepped out, leaving the interview hall suddenly larger and much quieter than it had been a while ago. For a few seconds, none of the three people at the panel's table moved. Instructor Diksha Sen was the first to exhale. She set her pen down, \"Unusual,\" she said. \"Most of them try to bleed to death on the carpet before admitting they need a pause.\" Kenzo Hayato's mouth curved, almost but not quite into a smile. The Japanese Ambassador sat with his back straight and his hands loosely folded. \"He asked before he shattered,\" Hayato said. \"That, in itself, is data.\" Mikhail Orlov Volkonsky did not immediately respond. The Zarya Consortium representative leaned back in his chair, large hands folded over a chest still thick with the kind of muscle that did not come from office gyms. \"Data,\" Orlov repeated, his accent flattening the word. \"What I see is a child who treats an interview like a stage and then asks for an intermission when he forgets his lines.\" Sen's eyes flicked to him. \"You think that's all this is?\" \"I think,\" Orlov said, \"we already heard the real problem when he opened his mouth.\" He reached for the sheet in front of him, the compact dossier the Institute had prepared for each candidate. Civic ID. Medical summary. Academic history. A single line under \"Affiliations\": None. No guild, no sponsorship, no blessing certification. \"Listen again in your head,\" he went on, and his voice shifted into a dry imitation of Arin's earlier answer. \"'Ambassador Studies sits at the intersection of geopolitics, culture, and ethics. Primary battleground is narrative space.'\" He snorted. \"He's thirteen. Not a policy brief.\" Hayato glanced down at his own notes, where the same words sat underlined twice. He did not deny the accuracy of the impression. \"He has read too much and lived too little,\" Orlov said. \"He speaks in downloaded phrases. The children now—\" He broke off, searching for the word, then found it. \"Perform. Always performing. Every room is an audition. Sen turned her pen once between her fingers. \"That is not entirely their fault,\" she said. \"We built the rooms. We set the auditions.\" \"We built training grounds,\" Orlov retorted. \"They turned them into theatres.\" He tapped the dossier with one blunt finger. \"Look at his answers. On reservations, on fairness. He knows the arguments. He arranges them nicely, like plastic soldiers on a shelf. But when I ask him something that isn't in his notes—'tell me a time you changed your mind'—he freezes like someone turned off the teleprompter.\" \"Because you cut him off mid-setup,\" Hayato pointed out mildly. \"You have a talent for kicking away ladders, Orlov-san.\" \"If a boy can only stand on a ladder, he should not be on this track,\" Orlov said. \"We are not recruiting parrots. We are recruiting people who will stand between this country and whatever walks out of that Fog. Sen's expression did not change, but the tip of her pen paused above the paper. \"Every year it gets worse. These candidates—they've all watched the same tutorials, read the same 'How to Impress a Panel' guides.\" He lifted his hands in a small, irritated gesture. \"They talk about 'leadership' and 'narrative' and 'impact metrics.' Ask them what frightens them and you will get a line about 'failing my potential' as if they are pitching to investors, not talking to human beings.\" Hayato's eyes warmed with a trace of rueful amusement. \"In my country,\" he said, \"we have the same problem. Different words. Same performance.\" Orlov grunted. \"There you are,\" he said. \"This is what they think we want. A polished mask. A rehearsed humility. 'I believe in fairness; I believe in narrative equilibria.'\" He mimicked Arin's cadence just enough to make the resemblance sting. \"But when I press—when I say, 'You are weak; what then?'—you saw it. The script ran out.\" Sen's gaze dropped briefly to the medical note in Arin's file: terminal pulmonary degeneration, stabilised, functional capacity improved but fragile. Body unsuitable for strenuous frontline operations without risk. \"The script ran out,\" she agreed quietly. \"The boy did not.\" Orlov looked at her, irritation pausing long enough to let interest through. \"Explain,\" he said. \"He could have defended himself with anger,\" Sen said. \"Or collapsed into self-pity. He did neither. When you talked about weak bodies filling the Institute, he did not cry about fairness. He said, 'Those who can will stand at the Frontlines. This track holds the line with words.'\" She tapped her notes. \"Then he added, 'A healthy fool would do more damage than a sick person who understands consequences.' That was not from any coaching manual I've seen.\" \"That line was… unexpected,\" Hayato conceded. \"Not elegant. But honest.\" \"Even fools can learn a good sentence,\" he said, but the edge of denial had dulled. \"Fools don't usually distinguish between front line and negotiating table,\" Hayato said. \"He does, He knows he is not a spear. He is not trying to pretend otherwise. That already puts him ahead of half the guild progeny we see.\" There was a small silence. Somewhere outside the hall, the muffled murmur of other candidates and ushers drifted past, a reminder that time was still moving. Sen glanced at the small clock projected in the corner of her tablet. \"Forty seconds,\" she said. \"He will be back soon.\" Orlov snorted. \"If he has not run away.\" \"Do you honestly think he will?\" Hayato asked. Orlov hesitated. The image rose unbidden—thin shoulders held too straight in a borrowed shirt, the stubborn line of a jaw that had heard too many versions of \"no\" and learned to keep walking anyway. The way the boy had asked for a minute had not been the plea of someone trying to escape; it had been more like a sailor requesting a breath before diving again. \"No,\" he admitted grudgingly. \"He will come back. That is not my complaint.\" \"Then what is?\" Sen asked. He looked down at his own notes. Under \"Strengths\" someone—probably the Institute staffer who'd compiled the initial file—had written: exceptional theoretical grasp; adaptive reasoning; high persistence index despite health constraints. Under \"Concerns\": physical fragility; tendency to over-intellectualise; possible overreliance on rehearsed frameworks. \"The boy has spine,\" Orlov said. \"I am not blind. I have commanded people with less. But spirit is not rare. We can find spirit in any district if we push hard enough. What is becoming rare is the ability to exist unperformed.\" Sen's brow creased. \"Unperformed?\" she repeated. \"Yes,\" Orlov said. \"To be what you are without asking, 'How does this look?' All these children really think about is the gaze. Their parents'. Their guild's. Social feeds. Rankings. They learn to know how to appear long before they learn how to be.\" He spread his hands slightly. \"So we ask, 'Why this track?' And instead of an answer, we get a branding statement.\" Hayato's gaze turned inward, as if replaying the earlier exchange in his own mind. \"'Primary battleground is narrative space,'\" he said softly. \"Yes. That did sound like a branding statement.\" Sen did not argue. \"I am not surprised,\" Orlov said. \"I am tired.\" It came out sharper than he intended. For a moment, the room held not an old soldier in a polished suit but a man who had watched too many young faces disappear behind armour, physical or otherwise. Hayato saw the flicker and, as always, pretended not to. \"Let us be precise,\" Hayato said instead. \"What did today's performance tell us? Not about 'this generation' in the abstract. About this boy.\" Sen straightened, grateful for the shift back into clear lines. \"His knowledge is solid,\" she said. \"Not exceptional in raw volume—we have seen guild candidates who can recite resource treaties clauses in their sleep—but his connections between topics are better than average. When I asked him to remove jargon, he did so on the second attempt, not the fifth.\" \"He listens,\" Hayato added. \"When corrected, he adjusts without becoming defensive. \"He also folds under an unprepared question,\" Orlov said. \"You saw his face when I asked about changing his mind. He reached for the safe story—'I used to believe merit was enough'—the one every poor candidate keeps in their pocket. When I took it away, he froze.\" \"Or he realised it would come out false,\" Sen said quietly. \"And stopped.\" Orlov's eyes sharpened. \"You give him much credit.\" \"I give him the credit his file demands,\" she replied. \"Look.\" She tapped another section of the dossier, the one summarising the medical reports from Vasantpur: left lung functional at thirty-one percent a few months prior, right lobe overcompensating, prognosis 'one year, possibly more' with rest and minimal exertion. Then, the later update from the local physician: structural strain decreased; capacity improved; daily ambulation now possible; cause attributed to 'unorthodox but effective breathing regimen.' \"I have seen no thirteen year old who would design a training routine for an organ that is trying to kill them,\" Sen said. \"Fewer would stick to it. He did. That tells me his mind has changed about something quite important already. It used to think, 'I am dying.' Now it thinks, 'I might be late, but I can still arrive.'\" Hayato's gaze softened. \"And yet, when asked for a story, he reached for theory,\" he said. \"That mismatch is… interesting.\" \"Interesting is a polite word,\" Orlov muttered. He leaned back further, chair creaking softly. \"I understand your point,\" he said grudgingly. \"But do you understand mine? This… boy, and others like him, they have been taught that what matters is how they present their suffering, not how they survive it. So when asked, 'Who are you?' they do not think of bridges in the rain or lungs on fire. They think, 'What is the correct narrative?' It makes them brittle.\" He looked towards the door again. \"What happens when the other side does not care for their narrative?\" Sen followed his gaze. \"If he were applying for Guardian command or Combat,\" she said, \"I would agree. We do not send brittle bodies or brittle selves to the front line. But Ambassador Studies is another kind of front. Here, we need people who can see the performance, because they will swim in it every day.\" \"Seeing performance is not the same as being trapped in your own,\" Orlov said. \"True,\" Hayato said. \"Which is why this one minute is useful. You forced him off the prepared path. Now we will see what he brings back. A new mask, or a person.\" Orlov gave him a sidelong look. \"You think a single minute will do that?\" \"I have sat in enough negotiation rooms to know that the ten seconds after a person loses their script are often the only honest ones you get,\" Hayato replied. \"He did something rare for his age group. He admitted he needed to reset instead of faking through. That tells me he has at least some instinct for not lying to himself.\" \"Or he just did not want to embarrass himself in front of us,\" Orlov said. Hayato smiled faintly. \"Those are not mutually exclusive.\" The clock on Sen's tablet chimed softly. \"One minute,\" she said. \"Time.\" As if summoned by the word, the door handle moved. All three panelists fell silent. Whatever else they thought about the boy, each of them knew the small ceremony of these seconds mattered. Candidates might remember only fragments of what they themselves said in interviews; they almost always remembered how they were watched. The door opened."},{"t":"18. Interview - IV","k":"Chronicles · chapter","u":"chronicles/c18.html","s":"Arin stepped back into the room. His face was still pale, but the set of his shoulders had changed. Slightly looser, as if he had exhaled something he'd been holding since the beginning of the test. . He sat. This time, his hands did not clamp down immediately on his thighs. They rested there, fingers interlaced, knuckles not quite as white. \"Apologies for the interruption, sir, ma'am,\" he said. His voice was steadier than when he had left. \"Thank you for the minute.\" Sen inclined her head. \"Are you able to continue?\" \"Yes, ma'am,\" he said. There was less performance in it now. Orlov watched him in silence for a long heartbeat. \"Very well,\" Orlov said at last. \"Let us try again.\" He leaned forward, forearms on the table, the old soldier coming back to the surface. \"Forget merit. Forget narrative. Forget whatever your coaching manuals told you we want to hear.\" His gaze held Arin's, steady and unblinking. \"Tell us about a time you changed your mind. One specific moment. No theory. No slogans. Just you.\" Beside him, Sen clicked her pen open, ready. Hayato's fingers tightened slightly around his folded hands, the only sign of anticipation he allowed himself. He drew in a breath, slow enough that the Frame could catch and steady it around his left lung. \"Seven months ago,\" he said, \"a doctor told my mother I had about a year.\" Instructor Sen's pen paused. Mr. Hayato's gaze flicked down, checking the file, then back up to his face. Orlov did not move at all. \"He told her in the next room,\" Arin said. \"I heard it through the wall. And then I tasted it, later, when she made me drink my medicine.\" A small, wry curve touched the corner of his mouth. \"Bitter herbs. Cheap tablets. Chalky aftertaste that sticks to your tongue like dust. You can't swallow it all the way down. It just sits there, reminding you.\" \"At that time, I believed two things very strongly,\" he said. \"One, that I was going to die soon. Two, that the world owed me a little softness on the way out.\" Instructor Sen's eyes shifted, almost imperceptibly, to the medical note in his file, as if re-reading the prognosis that sat there like an old bruise. \"My mother didn't cry in front of me,\" Arin continued. \"She sat by the bed. Checked my temperature. Went to the kitchen. Came back. The only place she let anything escape was—\" he hesitated, searching for the right words \"—her shoulders, when she thought I was asleep. They dropped for two seconds. Then she put them back.\" He smiled, small and genuine, as if the memory hurt and warmed him at the same time. \"A few days later,\" he said, \"she came home with a book.\" \"A book?,\" Mr. Hayato murmured. \"Yes, sir,\" Arin said. \"Institute of Inter World Studies General Track Foundation Practice Compendium, second edition. Tape on the spine, pages missing at the back, someone else's notes in the margins. She put it on the table like it was something fragile.\" He took another breath. \"Fifty rupees,\" he said quietly. \"That's what she paid. I did the math. That's… medicine we didn't buy. Groceries we didn't buy. She won't say it that way, but I've seen her negotiate with vegetable sellers. She doesn't throw money. If she spent fifty on a broken book, it means she cut something else.\" Sen's fingers tightened around the pen. For the first time, there was a faint tension at the line of her jaw. \"I remember thinking,\" Arin said, \"'This is unfair.' Not the book. Everything. That there are families who never have to choose between dal and textbooks, and mine does, and now on top of it I have an expiry date.\" The admission came out almost light. \"Then I started studying,\" he went on. \"Partly because I was angry. Partly because there was nothing else to do in a twelve-by-ten room except count cracks in the ceiling. But also because—\" He exhaled. \"Because if she could cut groceries for me, the least I could do was not waste the pages.\" He lifted his gaze fully to them now. \"That was the first shift,\" he said. \"From 'The world owes me' to 'I owe this person in front of me.'\" He let that sit for a heartbeat, then added, almost conversationally: \"And then I realized she wasn't the only one.\" Mr. Hayato's head tilted a fraction. \"When I used to traverse between library in a bus,\" Arin said, choosing the word carefully, \"I saw a lot of people trying to fight their way out of small rooms. There was a boy whose mother worked as domestic help. She couldn't promise him coaching, or a college seat. All she could give him was a place to sleep between shifts.\" He swallowed, the movement visible at his throat. He looked down, and when he looked up again there was that same quiet, genuine smile the panel had not seen on him before. \"I remember thinking, 'I'm not even the most unfortunate person in this line,'\" he said. \"My mother could still buy me a book, something in my head… turned.\" He spread his fingers slightly on his knees, as if putting the shape of it into the air. \"I stopped asking, 'Why is this happening to me?'\" he said. \"I started asking, 'If this is happening anyway, what can I make out of it that isn't just bitterness?'\" Silence settled again, denser now. Even the air-conditioning hum seemed to recede. \"So when you ask, sir,\" Arin finished, looking at Orlov, \"about a time I changed my mind… I think it was then. When I stopped treating my life as something the world was doing to me, and started treating it as something I'm responsible for, even if the parameters are terrible.\" There was no flourish at the end. No manifesto. Just a boy sitting very straight in a too-big chair. Orlov's face did not change, but Hayato's fingers tightened slightly where they were folded. Instructor Sen had forgotten, for a few seconds, to take notes. The genuine smile lingered at the corners of Arin's mouth, almost apologetic and yet utterly unperformed. Orlov broke the silence. \"And you think,\" he said slowly, \"that telling us about dying and tiffin boxes will help you bargain with entities that see our planet as a resource node?\" The question landed like a lead weight. Arin shook his head once. \"No, sir,\" he said. \"I don't think anyone on the other side of that fog is interested in my mother's grocery budget.\" His tone remained calm, almost gentle. \"But those experiences are important for me,\" he added, \"to become the person who sits at that table and doesn't forget what he's representing.\" Instructor Sen's eyes narrowed, just a fraction. \"Explain,\" she said. \"Diplomacy is not about making everyone happy,\" Arin said. \"It's an art of negotiation where both sides can go home and keep face in front of their people while getting something that actually matters.\" He glanced briefly at Mr. Hayato, then back. \"If I'm sitting in a room with a foreign civilization,\" he continued, \"what matters on paper is tonnage, energy units, security guarantees. But what decides whether a treaty survives the first crisis is something smaller and messier—the emotions of ordinary people who will live under that agreement.\" He lifted one hand slightly, then let it rest again. \"I'm… glad,\" he said, \"that I've finally learned how those feel from the inside. Powerlessness. Humiliation. Gratitude when someone offers even a tiny bit of dignity. I don't think that makes me a better negotiator overnight. But I think it makes me less likely to treat people as numbers on a briefing slide.\" Hayato's expression shifted, almost imperceptibly, into something like approval. \"Knowledge of such emotions,\" Arin finished, \"isn't a bargaining chip I throw on the table. It's… the shape of the person doing the bargaining.\" Orlov's gaze had gone very still. \"How would that shape your choices?\" he asked. \"Give me something concrete, not philosophy.\" Arin nodded. \"If a civilization comes to the table clearly holding the lower hand,\" he said, \"The most safe answer would be, 'Good. Press the advantage. Snatch as much as possible while you can.'\" Instructor Sen knew that was true; she had heard versions of it in mock interviews often enough. \"I'd do something different,\" Arin said. \"Go on,\" Orlov said. \"I'd push for terms that are still in our favour—our duty is to protect our people first—but I'd build in pockets of leniency where their delegation can save face,\" Arin replied. \"Concessions that don't break our security, but matter symbolically for them.\" \"Symbolically?\" Sen repeated. \"Maybe it's the way the agreement is worded,\" he said. \"Maybe it's a shared project we label as cooperation instead of aid. Something that lets them go home and say, 'We didn't crawl,' even if everyone in the room knows who had leverage.\" He met Orlov's eyes. \"That way, they owe us twice,\" he added quietly. \"Once for the material terms. Once for the fact that we didn't humiliate them when we could have. That second debt… becomes a favour you can call in later, when the tables have turned or when you need a partner instead of a subject.\" He did not sound triumphant. If anything, he sounded faintly tired. \"I've been on the other side of that feeling,\" he said. \"Of someone with power choosing not to grind my face into the floor just because they could. You don't forget it. You think of them differently after.\" For the first time since the interview began, Orlov's eyes warmed by a degree. \"And if your leniency makes them greedy?\" he asked, voice softening in tone but hardening in content. \"If they look at your kindness and say, 'These humans are weak; why negotiate at all when we can demand more?'\" The air in the room shifted. It was not visible. There was no crackle of energy, no glow. But all at once the space between the table and Arin's chair thickened, as if someone had quietly increased the gravity in that narrow passage The Archive flickered at the edge of his vision. **[External hostile aura detected.]** [Classification tentative High tier combatant.] His lungs stuttered. The Null Frame around his left lobe flickered, strained; for an instant, his breath scraped like it had in the days before he learned to build it. **[Heart Rate +42 above baseline.]** **[Blood pressure spike. Muscular tremor imminent.]** Across the table, Instructor Sen's spine snapped straighter. Her hand tightened on the pen. \"Chairman Orlov,\" she began, a warning coiled in his name. Mr. Hayato's pupils had narrowed, the only sign that he, too, could feel the invisible weight pressing down on the boy. His fingers shifted under the table, the faintest hint of a warding gesture never completed. But Orlov did not look at them. His attention was fixed entirely on Arin. \"What if,\" he said again, voice low, \"the other side looks at your mercy and decides there is no point in negotiating? That you are soft. That they should squeeze harder. Demand more resources. More access. What then, boy?\" His body reacted before his mind could. His knees buckled. The chair scraped; he slipped forward, catching himself too late. He hit the floor on one knee, palm slamming down to stop himself from collapsing completely. The world narrowed to the space between his head and the table's edge. **[Frame collapse warning..]**, the Archive blared. His hand shook against the carpet. His lungs burned; black edged his vision, creeping in like spilled ink. \"Enough,\" Instructor Sen snapped, the word sharp. \"Chairman—\" \"Orlov-san,\" Hayato said at the same time, voice still mild but threaded with steel. Neither of them had time to rise. Because Arin, trembling, did something that made all three of them stop. He looked up. Not at the floor, not at the nearest exit. At Orlov. Straight into the older man's eyes. His own eyes were wide, pupils blown from strain. His face had gone several shades paler. But the line of his gaze did not break. **[Unbroken Will]**, the Archive noted distantly, to itself. **[Field test: ongoing.****]** Very slowly, muscles shaking, Arin dragged his back foot under him. His free hand found the edge of the chair. The invisible weight pushed back. His chest screamed; his lungs felt like paper being crumpled in a giant fist. But the Null Frame caught, flickered, caught again—a stuttering scaffolding around failing tissue that should not have been able to bear this much pressure. He rose anyway. First to a crouch, then to a half-stand, then finally upright, one hand braced on the chair, the other still hanging at his side. His legs shook. His breaths came short and ugly. But he was on his feet. Sen's pen had stopped moving entirely. Hayato's brows had drawn together, the faintest shock cracking his composure. Orlov's killing intent did not vanish, but it pulled back a fraction, turning from crushing weight to heavy presence. Enough to let the boy breathe. Not enough to erase what had just happened. Arin swallowed once. His throat worked around air that felt like it had to fight for every inch. He did not wipe the sweat gathering at his temple. He simply stood there, looking at the man who had just tried to fold him. \"When they demand more?\" he said, voice hoarse but steady. \"When they decide there's no point in talking because they think I'm weak?\" He straightened another millimetre, as if the sentence itself were a brace. \"Then I'd teach them,\" Arin continued, each word clear, \"I am not here to beg.\" The sentence dropped into the space between them like a marker placed firmly on a map. Not shouted. Just stated, quietly, by someone whose body was visibly losing a battle his will refused to concede. For the first time, something like surprise broke cleanly across Orlov's face. Not at the words—he had heard variations of defiance before—but at the fact that they were coming from a boy whose lungs should have folded long before his knees did, and who had stood up under his killing intent where even veteran soldiers faltered. The killing intent withdrew completely, like a tide going out. The room's pressure normalized so suddenly that Sen had to stop herself from exhaling aloud. Mr. Hayato's shoulders eased by a fraction. Arin did not sit immediately. He held Orlov's gaze for one more heartbeat, making sure the words had landed where they were meant to. But just then- He threw up, blood **[Cardiac Arrhythmia, sit down take deep breaths-]** Before archive could complete however, Arin was falling forward. The last thing Arin saw was Instructor Sen running towards him and Orlov already near him. Silence."},{"t":"19. The Letter","k":"Chronicles · chapter","u":"chronicles/c19.html","s":"The first thing I saw when I woke up was the ceiling. Different ceiling. White, too clean, with soft panels instead of yellowed plaster. No fan. Just a vent humming somewhere above, pushing air that smelled of antiseptic and filtered recycled something. I tried to sit up. My ribs filed a complaint. The Null Frame flared reflexively, then eased when it realized we weren't in the interview room anymore. **[Body Status: Fragile but not immediately dying.]** the Archive reported. **[Congratulations on surviving yet another poor decision.]** \"Water,\" I croaked. A hand slipped behind my neck, lifting my head gently. A plastic cup touched my lips. \"Slowly,\" a voice said. Male, brisk, professional. I drank. Coolness slid down my throat, bringing back scraps of memory with it. Orlov's pressure. My knees. The taste of blood. The floor rushing up. \"Hospital?\" I managed. \"Infirmary,\" the doctor corrected. \"Parliament Annex, medical wing. Close enough.\" He checked the displays hovering near the bed. \"Pressure-induced micro-ruptures in already compromised lung tissue,\" he said. \"Not the worst I've seen. Not the best either. You'll live. Annoyingly.\" \"Panel?\" I asked. He snorted. \"They're not allowed in here. Interview's over. Go home. Wait for the result like everyone else.\" Of course. I closed my eyes. \"Your librarian friend was here,\" the doctor added. \"Refused to leave until I told him you'd stabilized. The older gentleman with the sharp tongue?\" \"That sounds like him,\" I said. He moved away, footsteps soft on the padded floor. The Archive hovered closer. **[That was reckless,]** it said. **[Even by your standards.]** \"Was it worth it?\" I whispered. **[You stayed conscious longer than you should have,]** it said. **[You did not break eye-contact. You did not beg. For someone with your medical file, that counts as… notable.]** \"Notable doesn't equal selected,\" I said. **[No,]** it agreed. **[But it equals remembered.]** I wasn't sure if that was comforting. Rao arrived an hour later. The infirmary door slammed open hard enough to make the nurse jump. \"Where is he?\" he demanded. \"Sir,\" I said weakly, lifting a hand. He moved towards and then sat on the chair near my bed \"The doctor said they'll discharge you by evening,\" he said. \"And then we return at night.\" \"How long was I out?\" I asked. \"Only a few hours,\" he said. He sat on the edge of the bed, hand resting on my forearm. \"Did you… answer well?\" he asked quietly. \"Not at first,\" I admitted. \"I tried to be someone else. Then I failed at that too. Then I talked like myself. Then Orlov tried to crush me. Then I redecorated their floor.\" He closed her eyes briefly. \"You and floors,\" he muttered. \"Always meeting each other at important times.\" I laughed, then coughed, then regretted both. \"I'm sorry,\" I said. \"For what?\" he asked sharply. \"For worrying you.\" he was quiet for a moment. He sighed \"At least this time you were falling in the right direction,\" he remarked. We went back to Vasantpur that night. The train rattled, lights dimmed. I dozed against the window, feeling the slight ache in my chest with every breath, the way Null Frame now wrapped not just around old scars but fresh bruises too. Rao sat opposite, arms folded, eyes closed. He looked like he was sleeping. but he wasn't. He opened his eyes. \"You understand,\" he said, \"that whatever happens now is no longer in your hands.\" \"I never liked that,\" I said. \"Then focus on what is,\" he replied. \"Let the Institute take its time proving whether or not it is worthy of you.\" \"That's not how the power dynamic works,\" I said. \"In my library,\" he said, \"it does.\" Days turned into weeks. Life slid, with uncomfortable ease, back into its old pattern. Morning cleaning. Afternoon study. Evening revision. Night listening to Ma talk about factory gossip. The only difference was the silence. No new notifications from the Institute. No letters. No calls. The Archive checked the portal once a day, unprompted. **[Interview Results: Pending.]** After two weeks, the notification changed to: **[Interview Results: Processed. Candidates will be contacted individually.]** \"Individually\" turned out to mean \"everyone but you, apparently.\" \"Maybe the letter got lost,\" Ma said once, more out of loyalty than conviction. \"The Academy does not lose letters,\" Rao said. \"They misplace entire lives, but paper? Never.\" After four weeks, even the Archive stopped volunteering updates. \"Statistically,\" I said one afternoon, wiping down a table, \"the absence of a result is a result.\" **[It could also be bureaucracy. Or incompetence. Or someone forgetting to click 'send.'] it said** \"Comforting,\" I said. \"How are you?\" Megha asked from the front desk. \"Disappointingly stable,\" I said. She snorted. \"You'll get in,\" she said. \"People who faint that spectacularly in front of important men never get forgotten. Even if they want to.\" \"Maybe they decided they don't want a boy who decorates their floors,\" I said. \"Then they're stupid,\" she replied, and went back to her cataloguing. At the one-month mark, I stopped checking the portal. Maybe I had failed. Maybe Orlov's pressure test had been more than my body could afford. Maybe the panel had decided that sending a boy with half a lung to talk to dangerous entities was a liability they couldn't justify. I was tired. Not of trying. Of hoping. **[If you want to be sad about this, schedule it like anything else. Half an hour. Then move on.]** Archive said one evening. \"That's not how feelings work,\" I said. **[It worked for you with mock test results,]** it said. **[You would sulk, eat something sweet, and then plan the next attempt.]** \"I like how you remember the sweets,\" I said. **[I remember everything,]** it replied. **[Including the part where you swore you'd rather die than give up. You've done the first. Don't do the second.]** \"I'm not giving up,\" I said. \"I'm just… accepting that maybe this door doesn't open for me.\" **[And if another door opens?]** \"I'll check if there's an exam at the end,\" I said. The door that opened was Rao's office door. Literally. It was a slow afternoon. I was in Reading Hall A, alone except for a couple of college students pretending to study and actually scrolling their feeds. The tub of abandoned books was half-full. I was enjoying the rare silence. \"Arin!\" Megha's voice cut across the hall. \"Rao-ji is calling you. Office. Now.\" \"Did I mis-shelve something?\" I called back. \"Probably,\" she said. \" Her tone was… odd. I parked the tub, wiped my hands on my trouser legs, and headed to the office. The glass wall was fogged. Someone had activated the privacy setting. That almost never happened. I knocked. \"Come,\" Rao's voice said. The door slid open. The room was full. Megha stood to one side, arms folded, trying and failing to look casual. The security guard from the library entrance—smart-glasses, worn patience, the first man I'd ever asked about labour exchange—leaned against a shelf, hands behind his back like he wasn't sure what he was doing here. And my mother sat on the chair opposite Rao's desk, dupatta smoothed so much it looked like it might disintegrate. Her eyes were wide, not with fear this time, but with something wired and brittle. \"Ma?\" I said. \"What are you doing here? You have shift—\" \"They let me come,\" she said. \"Rao-sir sent a message. Said library emergency.\" \"I'm not on fire,\" I said. \"Not that kind of emergency,\" Megha muttered. On the desk, between Rao and my mother, lay a large cream envelope with too many stamps and too much importance. My heart did something unhelpful. Rao gestured to the remaining chair. \"Sit, Valen,\" he said. I sat. The envelope stared at me. \"So,\" I said, because someone had to break the silence. \"Either I've been banned from the library for reshelving sins, or—\" \"Open the letter, boy,\" the security guard said, sounding uncharacteristically impatient. I glanced at him. \"You know what it says?\" I asked. \"I know who brought it,\" he said. \"Courier in a suit so stiff even the scanners stood up straighter. Didn't even want to leave it at the front desk. Said 'hand-delivery only'.\" \"Hand delivered to whom?\" I asked. \"To you,\" Rao said. \"But since your presence seemed… unreliable, I took custody.\" He slid the envelope toward me. My fingers felt clumsy as I picked it up. The paper was thick, the kind that crinkled softly instead of tearing. The address was handwritten. To Mr. Arin Valen c/o City Central Public Library Vasantpur A different symbol in the top left corner: a stylized map with radiating lines. Underneath, in small print: Institut d'Études Intermondiales, Lyon. France. My brain took a moment to process the idea that a place I had previously known only from maps and UPSC geography questions was now sending me mail. \"Rao Sir,\" I said slowly. \"Is this some elaborate prank?\" \"If it is, it is a very well-funded one,\" he said. \"Open it.\" I broke the seal. Inside, there were two things: a formal letter on heavy paper, and a second, thinner sheet that looked suspiciously like a flight ticket. I unfolded the letter."},{"t":"20. The Letter - II","k":"Chronicles · chapter","u":"chronicles/c20.html","s":"*Join the discord and help me grow, thanks for your support* *discord.gg/XD4VFunFCz* *Novel will be updated every Month with multiple chapters (At least 15 chapters). If I am a bit late then that will mean I had semester exmas, Enjoy* **Dear Mr. Valen,** **On the recommendation of Chairman Mikhail Orlov Volkonskaya** (Zarya Consortium) , we are pleased to offer you admission to the Institut d'Études Intermondiales (Institute of Inter-World Studies), Lyon, France, under the Inter-Civilizational Ambassadorial Fellowship Programme. **You have been selected for this cohort on the basis of:** **– Your performance in the Academy General Track written examination and Ambassadorial Aptitude segment;** – Your demonstrated resilience and ethical reasoning under pressure; – Your explicit preference for Ambassadorial studies in a year when many candidates sought Combat tracks and failed to qualify. **Due to an under-utilization of allocated Ambassadorial positions worldwide and a shortfall in Combat Study admissions, consortium partners have agreed to reassign certain seats to diplomatic training.** **You are one of the few candidates to have genuinely prioritized Ambassadorial training as a first choice rather than as a fallback. On that basis, and following Chairman Orlov's personal recommendation, your candidacy has been forwarded for international placement.** **If you choose to accept, your travel to France and tuition at the Institute will be fully sponsored under the joint Academy–Zarya Ambassadorial Initiative. Boarding and medical care will be provided in Lyon.** The words started to blur. I forced my eyes to focus. **You are requested to report to the departure terminal at Delhi International Gateport on 31st December 2031 for transport to Lyon on the same day. Detailed itinerary attached.** **Please confirm your acceptance within 24 hours via the enclosed contact codes.** **We look forward to your contribution to the future of inter-world diplomacy.** **Sincerely,** Director, Institut d'Études Intermondiales (Lyon) cc: Academy Ambassadorial Council, Zarya Consortium Liaison Office, Moscow. My hand trembled. Two days. France. Fully sponsored. International placement. All the words I had seen so many times in brochures and scholarship announcements in my first life, always attached to other people's names. Ma whispered. \"What does it say?\" I realized I'd gone silent halfway through. I swallowed. \"They… want me,\" I said slowly. \"For an institute in France. For Ambassador Studies. On a fellowship.\" Ma stared. \"As in… out of country?\" she said faintly. \"Yes.\" \"For… how long?\" \"Years,\" I said. \"Probably.\" She sat back hard. Megha let out a low whistle. \"Look at you,\" she said. \"Local boy going foreign.\" The security guard shook his head, a slow, impressed motion. \"The courier said something about 'global intake',\" he said. \"I thought he was just being dramatic.\" Rao watched me over steepled fingers. \"Well,\" he said. \"It appears the Academy has found you… inconvenient to categorize.\" \"Inconvenient,\" I repeated. \"They had too few Combat candidates good enough to risk on the front lines,\" he said. \"Seats went unused. At the same time, the world is waking up to the fact that talking is cheaper than rebuilding cities. Orlov has been pushing for more investment in diplomats. This year, someone listened.\" He nodded at the letter. \"You, inconveniently, are exactly what he argued for,\" he went on. \"Someone who wanted this path, not as consolation, but as a first choice. Someone who doesn't flinch away when large men try to squash him like a bug.\" \"I flinched,\" I protested. \"My knees flinched.\" \"Your eyes didn't,\" he said. Ma found her voice. \"Two days,\" she said. \"How can they expect… how can they just… take you like that?\" \"It's an offer, Ma,\" I said. \"Not an abduction.\" She shot me a look. \"You think you have a choice?\" she demanded. \"After all this? You're going.\" \"Are you sure?\" I asked softly. She hesitated. Something in her eyes shifted. \"I don't want… you to go,\" she said, each word dragged out. \"But I want you there more than I want you here, dying slowly in front of that fan.\" The fan in our room ticked in my mind at that exact moment, as if remembering its role in my origin story. \"You'll be… alone,\" she said. \"I've been alone in rooms full of people for years,\" I said. \"This'll just be a different kind of alone.\" She made a choked sound that might have been a laugh or a sob. The Archive pinged me. **[This solves the 'medical facilities' problem,]** it said. **[France has decent hospitals.]** \"Is that your way of saying congratulations?\" I thought. **[No,]** it said. **[It is my way of saying you will not escape my commentary by crossing borders.]** Rao cleared his throat. \"We will need to prepare,\" he said. \"Passports. Clearances. A suitcase. Possibly two shirts that aren't patched.\" \"Three,\" Megha said. \"We can't send him to France with only one spare.\" \"I have no idea how to pack for another life,\" I said. \"Same way you packed for this one,\" Rao said. \"Books. Stubbornness. Maybe a warmer jacket.\" The room went very quiet for a moment. Then the security guard, who had been watching all this with the expression of someone who couldn't decide if he was in a dream or in a bureaucratic mistake, stepped forward. \"Valen,\" he said gruffly. \"When you first came to this library, you didn't even have twenty rupees.\" \"I remember,\" I said. \"I still don't, most days.\" He nodded at the letter. \"Next time you come back through that gate,\" he said, \"I expect you to have more than twenty. In whatever currency they're using there.\" \"I'll try,\" I said. \"You better,\" he said. \"I want to say I once turned away an international ambassador for lack of entry fee. Makes for a good story.\" \"I'll make sure you can tell it,\" I said. The library around me was the same. Shelves. Dust motes. The faint hum of terminal screens. The city outside was still Vasantpur, still smelling of dye and heat. But my personal map had changed. **[You are leaving]**, the Archive said. **[Again.]** \"Looks like,\" I said. **[You failed to get into Mussoorie in your last life,]** it said. **[Now you are being exported to France. Not bad.]** \"In fairness, my Willpower is an S,\" I assigned the grade to myself. **[Yes,]** it said. **[And it is about to be tested in new languages.]** I traced the edge of the letter with my finger. \"What do you think they want from me?\" I asked it. **[The same thing every system wants,]** it said. **[Someone who will try to fix things instead of just noting how broken they are.]** \"That sounds like your job,\" I said. **[I am an Archive,]** it replied. **[I record. You live. Don't mix up the roles.]** I folded the letter carefully and tucked it back into the envelope. Two days. France. Institute of Inter-World Studies. I had chased an exam across a bridge on failing lungs. I had walked into a panel room and bled on the floor. And somehow, in the rearrangement of vacancies and recommendations and guild politics, a cracked, underweight boy from Vasantpur had been considered… worth exporting. I let out a breath I hadn't realized I'd been holding. \"Fine,\" I said, to the shelves, to Rao's office, to the city, to myself. \"Let's see what the rest of the world looks like.\" The Null Frame wrapped my lungs like a promise. Stage Two: passed. Stage Three: boarding. *With this mass release ends, To stay connected join the discord: discord.gg/XD4VFunFCz*"},{"t":"21. Desi Dude going Foreign","k":"Chronicles · chapter","u":"chronicles/c21.html","s":"*Please join the discord discord.gg/XD4VFunFCz* Rao’s car waited downstairs before the fan had even finished its first crooked rotation of the morning. Ma checked my bag for the fourth time. “Passport?” “Here.” “Letter?” “Here.” “Medicine?” “In the front pocket.” “Admit card?” “Ma,” I said. “I’m changing continents, not sitting for an exam. There is no admit card.” She clicked her tongue. “They should have one. Then I’d know where to shout if something goes wrong.” We stepped out into air that smelled of early traffic and last night’s damp. Rao leaned against the car, arms folded, looking for all the world like this was just another library drop-off. “Valen,” he said. “Mrs. Valen.” Ma folded her hands automatically. “Rao-saab.” “In,” he said. “The world will not wait for your nerves to settle.” After three hours we were in front of Delhi Airport “In my day, we took trains and were grateful,” Rao muttered. Inside, the terminal was all glass, light, and announcements in three languages. We stood in a small island of our own while the crowd flowed around us: business travellers with uncreased shirts, families juggling children and bags, workers carrying their whole lives in one faded suitcase. “Security first,” Rao said. “Then check-in.” We moved through it in stages. Trays. Scanners. A polite stranger telling Ma to remove her bangles. The arch humming as I walked through, the panel flickering green. At the international departures hall, Rao stopped. Beyond this point, only passengers and authorised staff. He looked at Ma. “They have a procedure,” he said. “Parents can escort minors up to the aircraft door if they register. You should go with him. I will wait here.” Ma’s fingers tightened on her purse. “I don’t know airport rules,” she said. “What if I do something wrong?” “He knows the rules better than the staff,” Rao said dryly. “He’ll drag you through them. My presence will only make everyone self-conscious.” He turned to me. “You,” he said, “will walk your mother to the plane. And you will leave her in a state that does not require me to deal with a factory uprising in your absence.” “I’ll do my best,” I said. His eyes rested on me for a moment longer than necessary. “The Institute will take you far,” he said. “Remember who pushed you to the library steps.” “The guard?” I asked. He snorted. “Out,” he said. “Before I change my mind and lock you in the stacks.” He clapped my shoulder once—brief, solid—and stepped back. “Go,” he said. The staff at the check-in counter wore the same professional patience I’d seen on invigilators. Their eyes skimmed my documents, my age, the Institute’s sponsorship codes. A small flag lit up on their panel. “Institute group?” the woman asked. “Yes,” I said. “Good,” she said. “We’ve already checked in the others. You’ll be escorted to the lounge and boarded together.” The others. My stomach did something that felt very much like an exam-hall flutter. We got Ma an escort pass. “Don’t lose this,” she muttered at it. We walked down the departure corridor to a quieter gate area than the general hall. A sign over the door read: **INTERNATIONAL – GATE 12** “Institute of Inter-World Studies group?” an agent asked as we approached. “Yes,” I said. “Your mother can accompany you until the aircraft door,” she said in accented Hindi. “After that, our cabin crew will take over.” Ma nodded. Her eyes flicked around, taking in the polished floors, the big glass windows, the view of the plane waiting outside: long body, wide wings, the Institute’s logo discreetly painted near the nose. “It’s very big,” she whispered. “It’s medium,” I said. “The big ones carry people who already know how to do what I’m going to learn.” She didn’t answer that. We had maybe twenty minutes before boarding. We sat in a corner row of chairs near the window. “Eat something,” Ma said, immediately producing a small parcel from her bag like a magician. Two rotis, some dry sabzi, carefully wrapped. “Plane food will be strange.” “Airlines have entire teams for this, Ma,” I said. “They won’t serve me bolts.” She gave me a look. I ate. Silence stretched between mouthfuls, dense with all the things we’d already said and the ones we couldn’t. “When you feel…” she began, then stopped, searching for a word big enough. “When you feel… too small there, remember you’ve already survived worse rooms than any classroom they have.” The factory. The doctor’s pronouncement. The interview hall. “I’ll remember,” I said. Boarding was called. “Institute group,” the agent called. “Please line up.” We stood. At the gate, Ma’s escort pass was scanned along with my boarding pass. We walked the jet bridge together, a metal tunnel smelling of cold air and the ghosts of other departures. At the aircraft door, a senior attendant greeted us. “Welcome,” she said. “You’re with the Institute group? Seats are in the forward cabin. We’ll take him from here, ma’am, once you say your goodbyes.” Ma’s hand clenched on my arm. “Two minutes,” she told the attendant. “Of course,” the woman said, stepping aside. Ma turned to me. Up close, I could see every line at the corners of her eyes, carved there by years of squinting at bad light and worse decisions. “You will call,” she said. “From France. If you can’t, you will send something that can be held in a hand.” “Yes,” I said. “And if…” She stopped. Swallowed. “If they are unkind, you remember: you are not there by their charity. You are there because you made a path when there wasn’t one.” “I know,” I said. She took my face between her hands, thumb rough against my cheek. “Study well,” she whispered. “Live long.” Her forehead touched mine for a heartbeat. Then she stepped back before either of us could make a scene. “Go,” she said roughly. “Before I decide France is not ready for you.” She handed me to the attendant like I was something valuable and breakable. Then she turned and walked back up the jet bridge without looking back. The empty space she left at my side felt larger than the cabin. The forward cabin was… not economy. Instead of tight rows, there were wide seats arranged two-by-two, each in its own shell, with more legroom than my entire bed back home. Soft lighting. Real pillows. Screens the size of small televisions. Twelve kids were already there. I registered them the way I used to scan an exam hall: quickly, systematically. A tall boy with dark skin and a sharp undercut, wearing a blazer with a Guardian guild emblem half-hidden under his boarding pass lanyard. A girl with close-cropped curly hair and a scar along her left cheekbone, reading a slim book in French. She sat like a coiled spring, scanning the cabin between paragraphs. A boy whose clothing screamed Central Guild City: layered neutrals, discreet but expensive watch, shoes too clean. He looked bored, which meant he was terrified. A girl in a simple salwar kameez with “Dhaka Gate School” embroidered on the sleeve, fingers tracing a pattern on the armrest in rhythm with her breath. The rest blurred into a collage of different geographies and the same expression: a mix of hope, exhaustion, and the dawning realization that this was no longer hypothetical. An attendant guided me to an empty seat near the window. “Seat 2A,” she said. “You’re all in this cabin. It’s reserved for the Institute’s new admissions.” “Reserved?” I repeated, trying not to imagine the price tag. She smiled. I sat. The seat tried to swallow me whole. As the remaining parents were gently extracted and sent back up the jet bridge, the cabin settled. The tension eased, a little. Familiar pattern: new class, jockeying for position in the most harmless way available—sarcasm. The cabin door closed with a soft thump. The inertia pushed me deeper into the seat and the humming of engines reached my ears \"Well then, Open Archive\" I muttered"},{"t":"22. Seed Forging Art","k":"Chronicles · chapter","u":"chronicles/c22.html","s":"**[ STAT WINDOW ]** ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━ **Name: Arin Valen             Age: 13 (Almost 14 )** Race: Human                     Title: None **Health Status:** ☠ Terminal Pulmonary Degeneration [Stagnated] ☠ Chronic Oxygen Deficiency [Improved] ☠ Frail Constitution [Improved] **━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━** BLOODLINE ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━ **None** **━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━** SKILLS ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━ **Null Craft (1/5)** **━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━** SPECIAL TRAITS ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━ **[Fading Breath]** Every physical action rapidly exhausts the body. **[Unbroken Will]** Mental resistance greatly exceeds physical limitations. **━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━** KARMIC POINTS ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━ **Karmic Points:[ 1000 (clear written) + 4000 ( clear interview) + 300 ] = 5,300** \"5,300 Karmic coins?\" My jaw dropped **[Getting Admission in Institute of Inter World Studies shifts Host's Narrative Impact to a global scale].** I wanted to save these coins forever but man, Old habits die hard. “Open the Wheel,” I thought. The wheel appeared in front of me simple and colourful  divided in four segments instead of three now. **1. Art** **2. Skill** **3. Artifact** **4. ????** '???? wasn't there before' I noted **[Minors should not participate in gambl-]** However before it could finish I spun the will The wheel turned. It spun longer than last time Finally, it stopped on Skill **[Reward: Omnilingual]** **[Description]** You naturally understand the spoken and the unspoken, You can comprehend and speak all languages whether in words or in thoughts. **[Limitations]** Only effective when within 5 meter radius of the speaker. **(0/5)** **[Edit]** [**Balance: 5,200 Karmic Coins]** \"Ok this is certainly not bad\" I remarked **[Agreed]** “Once more,” I said. The wheel spun again. This time, it landed on Art. **[Reward: Seed-Forging Art – Incomplete]** **[Description: A method of forming ‘seeds’ within the body—condensed from elemental energy, similar to energy cores.]** [Limitations: Seeds can only hold non-terrestrial elemental energies. Earth-origin elemental flows cannot be contained.] **[Seeds will exhaust 3 days after being created]** [Method: Breathing in an environment full of a certain type of energy will automatically create seeds of that type.] **Mastery: 0%** (0/5). **[Edit]** **[Balance: 5,100 Karmic coins]** I stared. “Seeds,” I said. “Inside my body.” **[Please do not say it like that,]** the Archive said. **[You are making my log uncomfortable.]** “Why the limitation?” I asked. “No Earth elements?” **[You are, ironically, ‘over-subscribed’ to Earth already,] it said. [Your damaged lungs, your frail constitution, all highly terrestrial problems. The art is built to store foreign or cosmic energies. Think of it as… diversity.]** I considered the description. “Any immediate use?” I asked. **[Not yet,]** it said. **[You are not currently bathing in alien elemental fields, unless this airline has drastically updated their service offerings.]** I snorted softly. **[ Again?]** the Archive prompted. I checked my balance. **[5,100 Karmic coins]** \"Yes\" I signalled But to my surprise it landed on the very small part covered by ???? Suddenly the world came to a halt. My eyes blurred and my hair stood. I tried to jump out of my seat but as if some invisible chains were wrapped around me and I felt a searing sensation in my chest as if someone has peeled a big chunk of myself  and filled it with something..........sinister. The Archive Window appeared in front of me, devoid of any playfulness and pitch black displaying in bold letters **[CURSE OF DESPAIR OBTAINED]** **Description:** You are someone who is bound to face hardships but that doesn’t only make you stronger but reveal something darker about you. Whenever you face any hardship and are pushed to boundaries your darker nature intensifies, It feeds on your sanity and magnifies the one negative emotion predominant in your character. With that, I lost consciousness. \"Sir?\" A hand shook me gently awake. I opened my eyes to find the flight attendant looking down at me, worried. \"Sir, are you all right?\" she asked. \"You've been asleep for ten hours straight.\" I checked myself over — nothing obviously wrong — then asked, as seriously as I could manage, \"Can I get some water and food?\" She blinked, clearly not expecting that particular answer, then nodded and went to fetch some. I stretched, wincing at the crooked angle I'd apparently slept in, and straightened myself in the seat. \"What was that?\" I asked quietly. \"Hey. You there?\" A moment passed before the translucent rectangle finally appeared. **[This is why elders forbid minors from gambling,]** the Archive said. \"That's not the important part right now, is it?\" I said. **[........]** it kept it's silence *Seriously?* I wanted to say — but the attendant chose that exact moment to return. \"Here. This is for you, sir.\" She set the tray down in front of me. I ate. **[You look like a bear tearing apart its prey,]** the Archive mused. \"Shuddup,\" I said, glaring at it through a full mouth. Once I'd properly demolished the meal, I sat back and turned to the window. Below, city lights scattered like loose LEDs, the terrain sliding past at a speed that didn't feel real. \"When do we reach Lyon?\" I asked. **[Two hours,]** the Archive said. I sat with that for a while, letting my thoughts settle into order. Then I said, \"Open Seed Forging Art.\" The translucent window opened in front of me My current focus now was to become stronger. So I started editing the seed forging Art. The interface changed **[Seed-Forging Art - Incomplete.]** **[Description:] A method for forming ‘seeds’ within the body—condensed from elemental energy, similar to energy cores.[]** **[Limitations:****]** **Seeds can only hold non-terrestrial elemental energies. Earth-origin elemental flows cannot be contained.[]** **[Seeds will exhaust 3 days after being created]**[] **[Method:]** **Breathing in an environment full of a certain type of energy will automatically create seeds of that type.[]** **Mastery: 0%** (0 / 5) \"Non- Terrestial elemental energies, so that means I can contain fire of the Sun?\" I questioned excitedly. **[Contextually: yes, Practically: Do not even try ]** Archive advised \"Tch!\" I ran my mind. The interface in front of me changed according to my thoughts **[Description]** Form ===> Transform **[200 Karmic Coins]** Seeds ===> Cells **[600 Karmic Coins]** **[Condensed from elemental energy]** ===> **[into antennas capable of absorbing Stellar energy subconsciously.]** **[1,500 Karmic Coins]** **[Cost of Editing: 2,300 Karmic coins]** *Better* \"Now let us look at the method\" I saw **[This should be illegal]** Archive objected weakly **[Method]** **[Breathing in an Environment Full of Certain type of energy will create seeds of that type]**==>**[Any type of physical activity will start the process of transformation]** **[Cost of Editing: 5,000]** **[Total Cost: 7,300]** I clicked my tongue **[You became greedy there]** Archive pointed out I added the limitation **[Limitation]** **[Addition]: Cell transformation process will only happen in Night and the transformation will speed up in the powers of two the more intense physical activity is performed.** **[Net: 7,300 – 3,500 (Nocturnal + Intense physical exertion condition) = 3,800]** I smiled with satisfaction while the Archive evaluated **[Splendid, you totally covered the subconscious pulling of stellar energy by the cells with the excuse of making art seem like it is just for preparation of pulling stellar energy while masterfully speeding up the process with the excuse of Nocturnal time period and physical exertion]** Archive observed. I checked the changes closely **[Total Cost: 3,800]** **[Proceed? Y\\N]** . \"Yes\" I said **[Transaction Successful: 5,000- 3,800 = 1,200 Karmic Coins]** I looked at the final preview **[Seed-Forging Art]** ---> **[Art of Stellar Vessel]** **[Description:]** A method for transforming ‘cells’ within the body— into antennas capable of absorbing Stellar energy subconsciously **[Limitations:]** Cell transformation process will only happen in Nocturnal period and the transformation will speed up in the powers of two the more intense physical activity is performed. **[Method:]** Any type of physical activity will start the process of transformation **Mastery: 0%** (1 / 5) **[****Remaining Balance: 1,200 Karmic Coins]** I closed my eyes and felt slight tremor in my body, feeling the changes which were occuring. **[What will you spend remaining coins on?]** the Archive asked \"Null Craft\" I said while thinking. The land was no longer visible as clouds embraced the earth like a shroud. The interface for Null Craft opened **Null Craft** **[Description]: A Method to craft empty spaces in external and internal environments, Temporary enclosed geometric spaces[]** **[Limitations]: Spaces crafted are stable and only remain 11 seconds at max before vanishing[]** **[Method]: Think of space before you, mold it with your intent into the shape you want[]** **Mastery: 100%** **(1 / 5)** **[Edit]** I sat for half an hour thinking and making changes. After an hour the result was in front of me."},{"t":"23. Welcome to Lyon","k":"Chronicles · chapter","u":"chronicles/c23.html","s":"*Join Discord For Illustrations: discord.gg/XD4VFunFCz* **Null Craft** **[Description]: A Method to craft empty spaces or solid spatial shapes in external and internal environments.** **[Limitations]: Spaces crafted are stable and remain as long as the practitioner wants. but when in manifested form these spatial constructs use mental stamina as the fuel. The cost of maintaining the shape will eat away at mental strength compounding with every passing minute.** **[Method]: Mold it with your intent into the shape you want** **Mastery: 0%** **(2 / 5)** **[Edit]** **[Karmic Coins Remaining: 50]** **[You can only make 3 more changes in this Art]** the Archive reminded me \"Hmmm\" Sometime later the lights dimmed to a dark gold, inertia pressed me gently into the seat, and the engine settled into a steady hum. The captain's announcement came in three languages. Fast French first, then measured English, then a Hindi carefully enunciated. Seatbelts. Descent. Welcome to Lyon. My hands found the belt automatically. The sudden lightness in my stomach as the plane dropped through clouds made my lungs tighten, then adjust. **[Twelve percent elevation in heart rate,**] the Archive said. It was Archive’s way of asking “Are you nervous?” \"A lot,\" I admitted. The wheels met the runway with a double impact that travelled up through the frame of the plane and settled somewhere in my stomach. The cabin lights brightened. People stood too early, were told to sit again, pretended they had meant to stand up only to stretch. I waited until the aisle had cleared, then stood and followed the slow shuffle toward the exit. The air at the top of the external stairs hit like a different kind of exam hall. Cold, sharp, clean. It slid into my lungs in a way Vasantpur air never had — less resistance, more bite. For a moment I just stood there, one hand on the rail, letting my body recalibrate to the idea that oxygen could arrive without the taste of dye and exhaust. **[Oxygen saturation slightly improved,]** the Archive said, almost surprised**. [External particulate load: low.]** \"So this is what non‑industrial air feels like,\" I whispered. I noticed some people coming towards us in a Dark Formal uniform. They led us towards the rope line An Institute representative waited near the rope line, dark coat buttoned tight, the Institute of Inter‑World Studies emblem hanging from a lanyard at her chest. She took our passports one by one, added a sheaf of printed forms with barcodes and sponsorship codes, and fed the whole stack to the officer at the counter with the efficiency of someone who had done this many times. When my turn came, the officer glanced from passport to screen to me and back again. He stamped the passport, returned it with the faintest of nods, and spoke to the representative. The words slid through my Omnilingual interface with bureaucratic clarity: “This one looks young. Are you sure he's cleared for the programme?” \"That's the point of the programme,\" the representative said lightly. \"We start when they're still young enough to change.\" **[Accurate,]** the Archive muttered Beyond the glass doors, Lyon waited in sodium‑orange and white. The air was colder there, the wind more direct. Our breath clouded in front of us as we followed the liaison out to the line of vehicles waiting under a covered bay. There were three buses. Long, low, painted a non‑threatening grey that didn't fool anyone paying attention. The Institute of Inter‑World Studies emblem glowed discreetly on the sides — the radiating‑lines map from the letterhead, rendered in pale blue. A man in an Institute jacket stood beside a folding table at the head of the bay, tablet in one hand, tray of narrow bands in the other. His hair was thinning, his back was straight in the particular way that said I have shouted at recruits in worse weather than this. \"Line up by track,\" he called, his French‑accented English clipped but clear. \"Guardian and Combat to my left. Research and Phenomenology in the centre. Engineering and Logistics next. Ambassadorial and support to the right.\" We drifted to our assigned sides with the resigned shuffle of people who had been segmented by exam choices for most of their lives. Coloured bands waited on the tray. Red, thick and obvious, for Guardian and Combat. Blue for Research. Green for Engineering and Logistics. White, edged in a fine thread of silver, for Ambassadorial. **[Visual classification markers,]** the Archive said. **[Useful for logistics. Also for prejudice.]** I joined the knot of white‑track candidates. There were seven of us. The Dhaka Gate School girl was there, backpack hugged close, eyes moving over everything with quick, precise interest. The Central City boy with the too‑clean shoes hesitated a heartbeat between red and white, then stepped to our side, jaw clenched hard enough to creak. The man with the tablet checked our names off his list, then took a white band and snapped it gently around my wrist. The material was cool, too light to be metal but too deliberate to be plastic. It tightened once, settling into place without pinching. A tiny emblem pulsed at the centre — the same stylised map — before dimming to a soft, steady glow. \"White band,\" the man said. \"Ambassadorial track. Do not remove it unless instructed. It carries your identification and access permissions within Institute grounds.\" **[You have been officially colour‑coded,]** the Archive remarked. I rubbed my thumb along the edge of the band. It did not spark or sing or unlock hidden stats. It just sat there, a quiet circle of authority around my wrist. The Guardian candidates collected their red bands with the casual pride of people who had grown up being told their bodies were weapons. The Research kids compared blue bands and immediately began arguing about energy phenotypes. The Engineering group checked their green bands and fell into a conversation about support grids. \"Board by colour,\" the man called. \"Red first. Then blue and green. White last.\" Inside, the seats were wide and the lighting was kind to tired faces. I took a window seat halfway down. The Dhaka girl slid into the seat across the aisle, pressed her hands briefly over her band as if confirming it still existed. No one spoke for a long stretch of road. Streetlights thinned, then disappeared. Trees appeared instead, black shapes on blacker hills, and the road began to climb. Ahead, a band of light stretched horizontally across the dark: a perimeter. When the Institute finally appeared, it did not rise; it unfolded. First came the wall. Tall, pale stone mixed with darker bands of metal, the kind of architecture that cared more about function than charm. Embedded along the top were slender towers — not quite watchtowers, not quite antennae — each crowned with a cluster of sensors and, if I was any judge, weapons that were only pretending to sleep. The bus rolled toward a wide gate. Energy shimmered faintly where the arch met the ground, a translucent curtain that parted just enough to let us through. Something scanned us as we passed — a prickling across the skin, a momentary tightening around the Null Frame as if it were being weighed and filed. **[Layered defensive grid,]** the Archive said, grudgingly impressed. **[Whoever designed this expected worse than teenagers.]** Inside the wall, the world widened abruptly. Buildings rose in rings. Closest to us were the training domes, their curved roofs catching hidden light. Beyond them, residential wings stepped up the hillside, balconies stacked like careful folders. Further in still, a cluster of taller structures gathered around a central tower whose glass skin reflected stars and security lights alike. Even in the dark, the layout made its point clear: this was not a campus built to impress parents; it was a fortress built to withstand things that did not care about brochures. Five thousand candidates, the brochure had said. Seeing the scale of the place now, the number felt conservative. \"This is a city,\" I breathed. **[A city with its own jurisdiction,]** the Archive corrected. **[Population: approximately five thousand candidates, one thousand faculty and staff, plus rotational Gate specialists.]** The bus slowed at an inner checkpoint. Metallic arcs swept over us with a soft buzz, followed by the click of mechanical locks disengaging. Guards in darker uniforms than the outer staff checked the driver's codes, the representative's credentials, and gave our bands a cursory, practised glance. No one looked surprised to see seven exhausted teenagers arriving at midnight. We pulled into a covered bay and stopped. The doors opened; cold air rolled in, carrying the scent of stone, cleaning agents, and the faint metallic tang of recent energy work. A man waited at the base of the steps. His uniform was a shade darker than the others', his shoulders broader, his face lined in the way that suggested he had once shouted orders on actual battlefields and had not yet fully forgiven the world for making him trade them for corridors. \"Welcome to the Institute of Inter‑World Studies,\" he said. His voice was level, carrying without needing to rise. \"From this point onward, you are under Institute's jurisdiction. You will move when told, stop when told, and ask your questions when time permits.\" He let silence sit on that for a beat. \"Look at your bands,\" he went on. \"White track — Ambassadorial. Your dormitory cluster is North Wing Three. Your provisional identification and access levels are keyed to those bands. Lose them, and you will spend an unpleasant week explaining yourselves to administrative security.\" I glanced down at the bracelet. The soft light along its edge pulsed once, as if agreeing with the threat. \"You will meet your track coordinator in the morning,\" he finished. \"Tonight, you will follow the junior warden to your quarters. You will not wander. You will not explore the domes. **[Tragic,]** the Archive said. **[They insist on delaying your inevitable bad decisions.]** \"Later,\" I told it. \"First I need a bed that isn't held together by habit.\" I stepped down from the bus. The stone under my shoes was cold, the air colder, my lungs working a little harder in air that was somehow both thin and clean. Above us, beyond the ring of bay lights, the sky over Lyon — or whatever lay beyond Lyon — stretched vast and dark, punctured by five narrow columns of starlight falling through the open panels of a distant martial dome and the heaven touching Central tower with a thick river of pure plasma flowing in between it's structure. Behind high walls and layered shields, five thousand candidates slept, or studied, or trained. I adjusted the strap of my bag, felt the weight of the white band on my wrist, and followed the others into the fortress‑city that was now, for better or worse, my home for next several years."},{"t":"24. A Walk","k":"Chronicles · chapter","u":"chronicles/c24.html","s":"The first thing I noticed was that my lungs hurt less than they should have. The second thing was that the ceiling above me was aggressively respectable. No yellowed plaster. No water stains. Just clean white, broken by the neat grid of dim yellow light. The dorm room at the Institut d'Études Intermondiales was still smaller than any prestige brochure would admit, but it had been built by people who believed in use rather than luxury. That alone felt like an upgrade. I lay flat on my back and waited for a certain sarcastic fellow A translucent window slid into the edge of my vision. **[Sleep duration: 4 hours, 47 minutes.]** Short pause. **[Sleep quality: 31%.]** “That’s not how you say good morning,” I muttered. **[Body vitals: Heart rate elevated. Oxygen saturation borderline. Muscular fatigue high.]** The bunk above mine creaked as someone turned over. Voices drifted through the thin wall from the corridor—water running, a door slamming, the muffled curse of someone who had discovered that Institute-standard toiletries did not include caffeine. “You let me sleep less than four hours,” I said quietly. “On the first morning.” **[You elected to lie down at 02:08. Lights-out was at 22:00. Your suboptimal life choices are not under my direct control.]** “Fair” I pushed myself upright. My arms complained but held. The beds here had actual mattresses, which made it easier to sit up and harder to pretend I’d been through worse. The dorm room was a narrow rectangle—two bunk beds, four lockers, a single long desk under the window. On the opposite bed, my roommate’s blanket was a mound of breathing irritation. I swung my legs over the side of the bed and let my feet hover for a second before landing on the  floor. I dressed in a bit more presentable clothes. Pale, sharper cheekbones, hazel eyes that had seen one life and were trying to focus on the sequel. Dark hair that refused to do anything cinematic. Thirteen, on paper. More than that in all the places paper didn’t measure. I fixed my collar. “Enough for breakfast?” **[Enough to not be seen as a beggar]** the archive judged The corridor outside was already busy. I checked the time. 07:48 A.M Mess hall breakfast window closed at 08:30, according to my roommate. I closed the door and started walking. The main dining hall for western wing was a whole separate building, a broad rectangle of glass and steel. The wooden doors were polished dark brown. Inside the air smelled like steam, spice and sugar. Students were standing in a line taking the food from the long counter with different types of dishes. The seating was random, yet I could see groups of students already formed. Marking corners as their territory for the years to come. I took my food focusing on a more protein based diet. Corn soup, Rice and tofu. “Good morning,” the serving auntie said without looking up. I took my tray and scanned the room. Some kids clustered by guild, crest metal flashing at their collars like small. Some arguing like the rest of the world didn’t exist. A duo sitting in the middle corner scrolling social media on their  tablets. I’d spent a first life reading rooms like this—coaching centers, hostels, government libraries—measuring who had what and how they wore it. Here, the variables had—but the math looked familiar. I took a seat near the back, at a table half-occupied by others with blank collars. No one looked up. One boy was revising something on a thin tablet, thumb. Another chewed mechanically, gaze fixed on the far wall. I tasted the food, “Mmm” It was…good. Not the likes of what you get in a luxury hotel but better than what I could have afforded back in the Vasantpur I ate slowly, giving my stomach time to remember that we were no longer living on library tiffins and factory leftovers. **[Caloric load: moderate. Nutrient density: acceptable. Sugar content: lower than average.]** The archive noted. I finished my food, returned the tray, and stepped back out into the corridor. Morning still had on its clean, indecisive light. Classes hadn’t started yet. The schedule pinned outside the hall listed orientation sessions, faculty introductions and other briefings. Most people were out of the dorms on the road, parks, some people were exploring the academic building. I walked aimlessly while checking the shops. The Institute had been designed like someone had taken four different philosophies of architecture, argued about them for ten years, and then decided to keep everything. The campus map they’d handed us last night was still folded in my pocket, but I didn’t need it. Yet. I took one of the side paths off the main spine. Martial domes rose ahead situated in North near Combat Track students —white curves of composite material, ribs of reinforced metal, sliding doors retracted halfway to show the glimpse of mats, equipment racks, and the faint glitter of conjured energy where someone had already started showing off. I saw a group of students with armor and arsenal outside the martial dome laughing and gulping water down. Seeing their smooth and perfect physique I once again checked my loose t-shirt and trousers. I moved past. Dormitory wings came next : rows of identical windows, small balconies, laundry lines already trying to colonize railings. Some balconies had plants. The sun hid behind a large spread of dark clouds I was now at what people would call Eastern Block. The shops were more about food and Fruits rather than armour, Weapons etc. There was also a vast hemispherical dome but pitch black with several rotating dish antennas, the kind I only saw in movies in my previous life The crowd was less but there were several logistic support centers and electronics shops here. I could also see some couples here and there but anyways continued to go forward. There were total of four Hospitals, each assigned to every block. The sky had turned grey and rain threatened to spill at any moment. The band on my wrist buzzed. I tapped on it. *The orientation classes for freshers is scheduled to be tomorrow.* Reporting Time :9:00 AM Venue: Central Auditorium, Central Tower *Uniforms would be delivered by today evening to all new students* “And here I was worried about what I should wear”I muttered relieved **[Affirmative, you only have 5 pair of clothes, 3 of which are for casual wear]** I checked the time it was now 12 in the noon. I checked my Institute account, there was an amount displayed there in Euros. Entry stipend: small. Enough for necessities that weren’t real necessities if you looked at them the wrong way. Not enough for luxury. Across the lane, a boy in a lavish jacket tried on three different versions of the same hoodie while his friend and the shop attendant gave feedback. It wasn’t surprising. Back in Vasantpur, the gap had been between kids whose parents could pay for coaching centres and kids whose mothers packed tiffins inside factory mess. Here, the lines had shifted to guild sponsorships and House obligations. Wealth was never subtle if you’d trained your eyes. I moved on returning to my hideout. *[ STAT WINDOW ]* *━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━* *Name: Arin Valen Age: 13 (Almost 14 )* *Race: Human Title: None* *Health Status:* *☠ Terminal Pulmonary Degeneration [Stagnated]* *☠ Chronic Oxygen Deficiency [Improved]* *☠ Frail Constitution [Improved]* *━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━* *BLOODLINE* *━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━* *None* *━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━* *SKILLS* *━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━* *Null Craft (2/5)* *Omnilingual (0/5)* *━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━* *ARTS* *━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━* *Art of Stellar Vessel (1/5)* *━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━* *SPECIAL TRAITS* *━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━* *[Fading Breath]* *Every physical action rapidly exhausts the body.* *[Unbroken Will]* *Mental resistance greatly exceeds physical limitations.* *[Curse of Despair]* *━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━* *KARMIC POINTS* *━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━* *[Karmic Coins: 50]*"},{"t":"25. First Session","k":"Chronicles · chapter","u":"chronicles/c25.html","s":"By the time night came, my body had decided I’d made too many decisions. The sun went down over the outer walls in a smear of orange and violet. A package waited for me on my bed when I arrived. Which contained 2 pair of a dark blue almost military like uniform with silver strips. Lights came on. Corridor traffic shifted from formal to casual. Laughter moved to doorways, arguments to common rooms, fatigue to mattresses. The Archive slid a panel into my vision. **[Recommended activity: Rest.]** “No,” I said. **[Clarification requested.]** “Training.” A pause. **[You intend to compound fatigue with physical exertion.]** “Yes.” **[Is this a revenge plot against your own cells?]** “Consider it… applied cultivation.” The map in my pocket unfolded easily now. The training domes near the outer wall had been tagged with numbers and icons. Some were reserved for formal martial classes, some for Guardian simulation scenarios, some for Combat squads. One, according to the schedule, was open after midnight for self-practice. Dome 7, Near Western Ring. Perfect. I headed there. The Institute at night had its own personality. leaving only a few late-working faculty and students with too many ambitions and not enough sleep. Security lights traced gentle lines along the paths. The outer wall loomed—stone and metal and energy barrier hummed faintly, keeping Fog and other inconveniences on the right side of reality. Dome 7 sat near the outer perimeter, just far enough from the main buildings that you had to intend to reach it. The door slid open when I tapped my student band against the panel. Inside, silence. The dome was empty. The dome was a wide, circular space. Padded floor, neutral color. Equipment racks hugged the wall—practice weapons, weighted bars, target dummies. The ceiling was glassed, segments retracted to reveal the night sky. Stars. Real, not through a screen. **[Environmental** **analysis: Open sky. Ambient energy level: elevated.]** “Yes,” I whispered. The Stellar Vessel Art lived in a corner of my Stat Window now. An Archive-granted gacha pull that had been both absurd and precise. Requirements: Night sky. Open space. Desperate host. Benefits: Don’t know yet. I had checked the sign-in register at the dormitory. No one had logged the dome past nine. Either the senior students had more sense than I did, or they had other training arrangements. Either way, the space was mine — chalk circle in the center of packed earth, equipment racks along the walls, the overhead panels open to a sky so dark it made Lyon's city-glow look almost modest. I set my bag down near the entrance column. The cold was immediate. January through five open ceiling panels is January applied directly, I pulled on my training jacket, a cheap piece which can be repeatedly washed and even if color goes out, it will still remain wearable, and started with walking. Rounds of the central ground, steady pace, the ache in my lungs its usual familiar company. After four rounds of walking I did some low squats. Then I walked to the center of the deserted dome. The floor had a faint grid pattern, barely visible underfoot. I found the exact middle  and settled into the pushup stance The sky watched through the glass. I exhaled and went down, knees wobbled and stiff muscles cracked. A slight rebounding pain shot up in my forearms. I applied my strength and. “ONE” I squeaked while a slight tremble followed right after. I rose, this single action took half of my energy away. My body protested, my legs wanted me to give up trying to repair this naturally trash body, but someone who had lived his life on a tickling time clock, this was nothing I lowered again, my back pressing against my spine, and in one go went up. “TWO” I exhaled through my mouth. **[Maximum limit for physical activity reached] the Archive hovered, observing me.** I remained in the stance for a minute and slowly descended till my chest almost touched the ground, but when I tried but my strength failed me. My strength suddenly left me and the ground rose to embrace me. But then a hand came out of nowhere The hand appeared at my sternum. Not a grab. Not a catch. Placed there with precision — palm flat, fingers lightly spread, the way you'd set something fragile on a surface you didn't want to scratch yet it contained absolute strength. I pushed through. “THREE” I muttered. I turned my head to see the source of this strength. She was crouched beside me. Heels flat on the ground. The hand at my chest was steady — no tension in it, just the quiet fact of support. I stared. I had, in this life and the previous one, a fairly calibrated sense of how to look at people. Noting without fixating. Her hair was the color of snow where it hadn't been touched — the pure kind, the morning kind, the kind that a footprint ruins before you've had time to appreciate it. It fell in one clean line, unbothered by the dome's cold drafts. Her eyes, when they met mine, were the color of blueberry She was not looking at me the way most people looked at someone in training contexts — calculating, sorting, slotting somewhere. She was looking at me the way you'd look at a bird attempting to cross a very large floor. *Mildly interesting. Possibly in some difficulty.* She removed her hand. Stood — not in the quick, sequential way, but all at once, the way a folded thing opens when you remove the crease. She had two wooden swords at her hip, practice blades worn dark at the grip by hours of use. She wore a white tracksuit with black lines and black shorts, There was a golden band at her wrist. She walked to the center of the chalk circle and drew her swords. I stopped doing push-ups. I picked up my mat, moved to the edge of the ground, and sat down. My legs needed the rest and the rest of me, some emotional support. But still I stared at her. Her lonely yet strong figure under the moon, as if fighting a war no one else but only she could fight. What she was doing was more like watching water when someone had removed a frame. You saw one position. Then another. The gap between them did not exist in any visible form. This looked her casual pace. Even at this — this held-back, restrained, warm-up pace — the wooden swords generated wind. Not the exaggerated kind. The real kind. The kind that reached me at the edge of the ground with the specific weight of a displaced atmosphere. The kind that made the training banners along the wall stir and sway. The kind that, at actual speed, would have taken a person off their feet from fifteen meters without touching them. **[I cannot classify her skill level against your current benchmarks,]** the Archive said, in a subdued voice. **[The reference points do not overlap sufficiently.]** \"Yeah,\" I said. I watched for another three minutes Then I went back to the push-ups, because watching something extraordinary is not the same as doing something and my strength stat was still a diplomatic way of saying approximately zero. She moved. I exercised. At some point, I felt a pinching pain in my body starting from some parts to the whole body **[Cell Transformation in Progress] the Archive observed.** After what was my fourteenth round. I decided to pack up. I turned my head and saw that I was alone in the whole place. **[She left when you were on your ninth round]** I didn’t think of it much and left the place."},{"t":"26. Orientation","k":"Chronicles · chapter","u":"chronicles/c26.html","s":"The Institute had many ways to remind you you were small. Last night, it had used stars and a martial dome with an open ceiling. Today, it used chairs. Rows of them. Hundreds. All perfectly aligned in a descending arc in front of a stage that looked like it had been carved out of some retired war room. I arrived late. Not missing the speech late. Just late enough that every sane seat was gone, and the Archive had added a faintly judgemental tint to my Stat Window. The sound in the hall was the sound of a hive trying to learn manners. Students in the same dark blue uniforms, striped now with clean track colors—red flashes for Guardian and Combat, blue for Research, green for Engineering and Logistics, white for Ambassadorial—tested out their bands, their voices. Some of them sat straight, already rehearsing important person posture. I scanned for empty chairs. First row: full, obviously. Second: half full, but the remaining spaces were wedged between clusters of candidates who already knew each other well enough to lean in close and whisper. Third to eighth: occupied by every variety of early, prepared, and annoyingly awake student. Last row, against the back wall: three empty seats. Two of them marked by bags and one at the far corner. I took the corner. The metal of the chair was cold. The view from here turned the hall into layers: heads, bands, track stripes, the pale stone of the front wall, and above it all the Institute crest—radiating lines over a stylized world map—etched into glass. On the stage, two lecterns flanked a central emblem inlaid into the floor. Thin lines, crossing like constellations, traced an eight‑pointed pattern. At its center, a small crown and orbit motif caught the light—simple, but deliberate. The noise level dropped gradually, the way it always does when enough people realize something official might be starting. Conversations folded themselves down as the hall lights shifted. A side door at the edge of the stage opened. He walked out like the room already belonged to him and he was just taking inventory. Tall, shoulders set with the quiet precision of someone who had spent enough time in training domes to know exactly how much space his body occupied. His uniform had the same dark blue base as ours, but the stripes at his collar and sleeves were a deeper gold like the color of band of that girl last night His wrist carried it more clearly. “The Institute really enjoys labelling people,” I said under my breath. **[Institution enjoy order. You enjoy complaining about it.] Archive mocked me.** He took the central lectern. For a moment, he let his gaze move over us, row by row, like he was counting not bodies but variables. Then he smiled. Small. Precise. “Welcome to the Institut d’études Intermondiales,” he said. His voice carried cleanly. “I’m Rian Sol Virel,” he went on. “Guardian track, House Aster, Third year. Some of you have already looked up my name in the Institute’s hierarchy lists. Some of you will pretend you haven’t. That will change.” House  Aster. That was new. I filed it. “The first thing you need to understand,” Rian said, “is that this place is not a school that wants you to feel comfortable. It’s a fortress that wants you to become precise.” He lifted his wrist so the band caught the overhead light. “Look at your bands.” Across the hall, sleeves shifted as people raised their hands. Red. Blue. Green. White. A few bands glowed brighter than others, edged in faint metallic halos—silver, gold. I glanced at mine. White. Ambassadorial. A clean ring. “That color on your wrist,” Rian said, as if he’d heard the thought, “is your worth here. Track color. Quotas attached. It tells the Institute, at a glance, how much trust you’ve been given and how much you are expected to earn.” He let the silence sit for a heartbeat. “Those people whom you once admired, who once were called heroes, who on their life held back whatever was beyond that fog-“ he continued “Every one of them sat where you sit now,” Rian continued. “Every one of them wore a band. Some started at the bottom of the hierarchy. Some started near the top. Legacy isn’t about where you begin, it’s about how much you can endure long enough to become.” His eyes moved again. I felt, rather than saw, the moment they swept the last row. Hierarchy exists for one reason, he said. “Not to decorate egos, but to keep chaos from eating purpose. Track colors say what you train for and margins you have.” His band glowed briefly, gold ring bright. “Silver fascia,” he said, “Faculty, researchers, House Masters or the people who agreed to burn their lives to nurture you . Gold marks apex hierarchy—students already carrying responsibilities heavier than most faculty had at their age. Crown quota—candidates whose seats exist because someone with jurisdiction over nations and consortiums demanded they be trained, fast.” I tried not to look at my wrist. I failed. “You don’t earn yellow,” Rian said. “You’re born with it. But you still have to justify it. The Crown doesn’t like wasted investments. The Institute likes them even less.” Rian’s gaze swept back to the front rows. “If your band is plain,” he said, “good. You’re baseline. You have room to climb. If your band is edged in silver, you’ve been given less room to fail. If it’s gold, you’re carrying not just your own story but someone else’s budget.” A ripple of low laughter passed through the hall. It cut the tension without erasing it. “Hierarchy here will press you,” he finished. “If you’re lower, you’re expected to climb. If you’re higher, you’re expected to justify every millimetre of height. That is how you honor the legacy of this place. That is how you become more than a name on a list.” He stepped back from the lectern. Applause started unevenly—front rows first, then spreading backward, gathering speed like gossip. I clapped because not clapping made you stand out, and standing out in a room full of people with sharper lungs and better bands seemed statistically unwise. The side door opened again. This arrival did not come with orbit energy. It came with vector. She walked out like she had already calculated the hall’s dimensions and decided exactly how much of them she needed. Same dark blue uniform. Different details. Narrow red stripes ran along her shoulders and the edges of her sleeves—not Guardian track red, but a deeper, more formal tone. Her band was silver at the core, edged with a scarlet line. The Archive pinged. She moved to the second lectern. Adjusted the mic with precise fingers. Looked at us once, the way a doctor looks at test results before writing a prescription. “Good morning,” she said. Her accent was British, English exact. “I’m Olivia Silev. Second‑year, Student Council, Discipline Committee. My job is to make sure you understand what you’re wearing and why you don’t get to pretend ignorance later.” The hall settled. Whatever noise remained turned into the quiet shuffle of people sitting straighter. “We’ll start with the obvious,” Olivia said. “Bands.” She raised her wrist. The silver‑scarlet ring glinted under the lights. “Red bands for Guardian and Combat. Blue for Research and Phenomenology. Green for Engineering and Logistics. White for Ambassadorial and support. Those colours are not fashion choices. They are your primary directions” Panels unfolded in my vision: four simple diagrams, colours labelled. “Red,” she said, “means you have chosen to learn how to stand at the edge of the Fog and not die. Blue means you will spend years understanding why the Fog behaves the way it does, and how reality fractures. Green means you will build and maintain the structures that make everyone else’s heroics survivable. White means you will sit across tables from entities who can erase cities and attempt, with words, not to let them.” Her gaze skimmed the white‑band section. I felt it land on me for half a second. My lungs caught, then remembered how to function. “Track colours can change,” Olivia said. “It’s rare, but possible. If you demonstrate aptitude in something your current track cannot use, the Institute will move you. Do not cling to color for ego. Cling to function.” The diagram shifted. Rings appeared around the base colours—plain, silver, yellow, gold. “Now Miscellanous,” she said. “Plain bands indicate standard intake. You passed the exam. You are here. Congratulations. Work.” “Silver color indicates higher positions like Senior Guardians, lead researchers, logistics heads, ambassadors already carrying live responsibilities. You will know who they are long before you wish you didn’t.” “Gold color is apex student hierarchy, Crown quota seats.” She let the words sit. “You may have seen them,” she said. “Bands clad in full gold. Those seats exist because the Crown, consortiums, or allied governments designated a certain number of candidates to be trained with priority access to Institute resources. They represent districts, alliances, lines of responsibility you will never be allowed to misplace.” “If you don’t wear golden band,” Olivia continued, “do not waste energy resenting those who do. The Fog does not care what color of band you have. It eats without discrimination. Your job, regardless of color, is to become solid enough that when something tries to rewrite physics in front of you, you are part of the structure that holds.” She paused. The hall was quiet enough that you could hear someone’s cough three rows ahead echo against the back wall. “Bands can change,” she said. “Upwards and downwards. If you perform consistently” Olivia’s tone adjusted—still firm, but less surgical. “So what do you do with all this?” she asked. “Simple things. You show up to your classes. You train when scheduled. You rest when medically necessary instead of when boredom tells you to.” “You ask for help before you’re drowning,” she said. “You push hard enough to grow, not hard enough to break. You remember that the Institute is not interested in how tragic your backstory is. It is interested in whether you can carry the weight it will put on your shoulders.” Her hand moved. The diagrams folded back, replaced by four new sigils arranged around the crest. “And because this place does not run on individuals alone,” she continued, “you will have to join one of any four Houses.” The hall shifted. People sat up a little. Gossip had reached the dorms about Houses; this was the first official mention. “House Aster,” Olivia said, indicating a sigil shaped like a starburst over a shield, “focuses on Guardian operations and strategic command. Combat, defence, front‑line decision‑making. If your red band makes you feel too quiet, Aster will give it volume.” “House Leviathan,” a deep, swirling emblem like a coiled sea‑beast, “handles field deployment and heavy combat applications. Domes, Gate sorties, direct engagement with Fog events and rescue operations. They collect scars, disciplinary notes, and songs in approximately equal measure.” “House Meridian,” a rising line over a horizon, “centres on research, scouting, phenomenology, and analytical support. They are the ones who keep everyone else from walking blindly into fracture zones because the readings looked ‘interesting’.” “House Forge,” a stylised hammer over an orbit, “specialises in engineering, logistics, and innovation. Constructs, grids, the support systems that make the Institute more than walls and bravado. If something in this fortress works, there is probably a Forge student behind it.” The sigils rotated slowly. “You will each join one House,” Olivia said. “Not today, but soon. The Archive will generate preliminary recommendations based on your exams, your track, and your medical and psychological files. You will express preferences. Faculty will review. Final House assignment is a decision, not a vote.” House point columns appeared next to each sigil, currently empty. “Houses earn points,” she said. “Academic performance. Simulations. combat trial results. Innovation challenges. Inter‑House events. You will contribute, whether by being brilliant in class or by not crashing a training dome during exercises.” Some red‑band students shifted guiltily. I mentally promised the Archive I would try not to crash anything. It mentally promised to log it when I did. “At the end of each term,” Olivia said, “Houses are ranked. Higher ranks receive certain privilages. Lower ranks receive reminders.” **.** “Participate,” Olivia continued. “Show up. Don’t throw matches. Don’t injure your peers for pride. Use competitions to sharpen skills, not to prove that your ego has sharper teeth than theirs.” She checked a slate near her lectern. “Orientation will drown you in information,” she said. “You won’t retain all of it. That’s fine. Retain the core. The corners of her mouth tugged up a fraction. It was not quite a smile. More like a mildly amused threat. “Speaking of being dragged,” she added, “you have your first classes in less than twenty minutes. Schedules should have been delivered to your bands. Routes are mapped. If you get lost between here and your lecture hall, we will assume the Fog has eaten your sense of direction and act accordingly.” A soft ripple of laughter broke. “Last note,” Olivia said. “You are now under Institute jurisdiction. That means halls, domes, dorms, mess, and outer rings. If your band denies access, respect it. If you try to override it, disciplinary protocols will apply. Some of them are designed by Forge students with vividly creative minds.” Her band pulsed once, scarlet. “Welcome,” she finished. “Work hard. Do not make us regret the budget.” She stepped back. Rian returned to the centre just long enough to give the hall a small, precise nod. “Whatever story you wrote to get here,” he said, “this place is where you find out if it can survive contact with reality. Don’t be boring.” The Institute crest dimmed slightly overhead. A soft chime rolled through the hall—a dismissal note, administrative and absolute. Chairs scraped. Bands winked as people moved. Voices surged back, now rearranged around new words: House Aster, Leviathan, Meridian, Forge. I stayed seated for a moment. **[Schedule loaded.]** the Archive announced. “Show me,” I thought. A tab unfolded. **[ Subject: Foundation of Eidolon Theory** Instructor: Dr. Serin Ilias. Location: Central Academic Wing, Lecture Hall 3.] The Archive said [**Recommendation: Reduced walking pace. Avoid stairs where possible**. **Secondary recommendation: Do not attempt to impress classmates by collapsing dramatically.]** “No guarantees,” I muttered. I pushed myself up from the last‑row chair. “Let’s go shall we?”"},{"t":"27. First Class","k":"Chronicles · chapter","u":"chronicles/c27.html","s":"*I have started thinking more and putting more effort in chapters, You can see better writing now i guess* The lecture hall was big, very big. The walls were decorated with traditional drawings. The rows of seats were ascending in height from the front of the class to the back. A big screen covered the entirety of the front wall. A curved table for the faculty use at the front corner. Student of every track was present there. I sat in somewhere middle, on my left were a group of girls gossiping, on my right sat a guy with rotten mood. His hair and eyes were black. He was so handsome, that if he would have been on normal Earth in my days, he would definitely have gotten a lead role in a super hit movie. “Ca- can I sit beside you?” A soft voice came from right that made me turn my head. There stood a cute girl with long yellow hair and green eyes, looking at the guy in my right. The guy in my right didn’t even bother to look at her and ignored her with passion. *You’ve got to be kidding me* I silently thought. She stood there for quiet sometime, my patience finally reached its limit and I thought of helping her out of goodwill. “That seat is empty so you can take it even without asking” I said. But at that her smile disappeared replaced by a disgusted expression and with a fluent English she said “I wasn’t talking to you!” My jaw dropped and the guy’s lips twitched. *He was about to laugh wasn’t he?* I looked like a clown but then the lights dimmed “Bonjour” a feminine voice boomed in the full room. I looked forward to see a woman with brown hair and simple black suit adjusting the mic around her mouth. “We have seats for sitting, of course nothing can be done if you have already decided otherwise” the woman calmly continued “I am Serin Illias your Instructor for the subject ‘Foundations ofEidolon Theory’ and the lead researcher in Fog Phenomenology” she introduced herself. “And you, will be the people who will go in that fog and pull the humanity stuck there from the jaws of extinction” She then turned around and pressed a button on a small remote. The screen came alive, with big letters in the centre ‘FOUR PATHS’ Professor started “Before we go over what fog is since you all have already learnt a bit about that in your previous educational institutions we will go through the changes which happened in the world due to it” she kept going “When first phenomenas regarding the fog started, with that humans started showing peculiar things. Some could shatter boulders with fists saying they could feel a great flow flowing through the universe. We called them ‘Elites’ Some were able to lift vehicles without touching them, see events taking place in next cities while drinking tea at their house. We called them ‘Mentalists’ There were also some who could turn into whole other beings and utilize their abilities. Like turning the skin into the scale of dragons, spitting fire etc. We called them ‘Bloodbounds’. And there were also some who could do more than one thing at the same time, We called them Hybrids.”  She let that settle. “These four were the paths that laid the foundation for present human society to become capable of preserving itself against what was beyond that fog”. Everyone gulped at that. She again changed the slide “Now the fog, As the history suggests, it first appeared ninety years ago, it is also true if you said that it was as if the weather had become cloudy and humid but the only difference was that the clouds were falling to ground instead of flying through the air” She pressed the button again and a video opened, it was an old video clip. “We are now flying over copenhagen” a male voice began. “This is an unprecedent event, Whole Copenhagen is swallowed in a mysterious mist” the camera went live. A very big chunk of cloud could be seen sitting on land covering the whole city. Dr. Seris paused the video “That is where all of this started, at that time no one knew a thing and then what happened?” She asked turning. “The Denmark Disaster” a girl on the front bench muttered under her breath but the silence was so dense that anyone could hear it. **[Isn’t this when-]** Archive appeared like a ghost in the corner of my vision “Yes, It disappeared. forever” **[That’s why you couldn’t find it on international map]**\nArchive seemed to understand something. “We lost Copenhagen, Forever” Dr. Seris continued. “When the fog receded, it was gone, land stitched to such perfection that made it seem it never existed”. She sighed “But it was just the starting, because after that, these events started happening with terrifying consistency.” She then changed the slides again, this time it was a mathematical equation and some terminologies which I remembered from basic fog phenomenology section. “We tried every possible set of equipment, recorded and tested every measure of human and superhuman intelligence”. She recalled as if reminiscing about some nostalgic times. “Then we discovered that, these outbreak happen not randomly but in a pattern” She turned around. “This is the equation which could predict the outbreak, if the required variables attain value up to a certain point, then we can predict the probability of outbreak happening. This is called ‘the Serilian Pattern’ which was discovered by me 30 years ago” She didn’t turn towards student this time. But I could still sense a smile on her face. My eyes widened. *Now that’s new* **[Classification of the individual ‘Seris illias’ updated from ‘unknown’ to ‘extremely important’]** Archive noted**.** “How do we calculate variables professor?” a boy with blue striped uniform asked. At that she turned around. “There are some signs which could be easily felt even without equipment, these are called ‘Symptoms’ and are also the variables in this scenario. But the problem comes when these symptoms appear in multiple places. She continued  “ That’s when we use Serilian equation to find the epicenter of the event.” **“**But we aren’t helpless” She changed the slides again. “Out of 27 Fog events, we have lost 14 and made it out 13 times. ” She played another video. This time it was an interview between a woman and a man. “So what happened inside the fog?” the male anchor asked. “At first, I didn’t knew where I was” she said. “The first thing I saw was that the sky was red and that there was a countdown projected on the sky” She paused then again. “It was all chaos, blood and…” she stopped. “Anything after that?” the male anchor asked trying to ease her. “A-at last there…was a scoreboard” she stammered. “Scoreboard?” the male anchor frowned. “It was as if, we were a performance for someone” She continued “We were on 3rd place out of 5 on that list” the woman said. “Did you also see what other names were written on the list?” the male anchor asked “I couldn’t, they were blurred and after that I only remember opening my eyes here under the blue sky” she admitted. Professor Serin paused the video once again and turned around. “We took many such interviews but every group of people said something different.” She took a deep breath, “However, there were two details which were same in every case. The countdown and the scoreboard.” “That was a lot to digest in one day” I casually pointed out while my legs kept moving towards my dorm. Apparently Professor Seris dismissed our classes saying that we looked awful to attend any more classes and that she cannot see such a nasty expression on her cute student’s faces so she dismissed us for the day. **[What will you do now?]** Archive asked me. “I am gonna orchestrate a revenge plot against my cells”I said. **[……..]** The night was young, stars covered the sky like it owed them something. I slid inside the martial dome 7 sneakily with my cheap tracksuit. **[Ambient Energy levels: High]** [Total Cell Transformation Completed: 0.9%]. “Let’s get to work” *Next chapter will be more focused on training. Let me know*"},{"t":"28. Ghost?","k":"Chronicles · chapter","u":"chronicles/c28.html","s":"**[Day of Torture: 21, Total Cellular Transformation Completed: 23.7%]** The numbers hung in front of me in the form of a Translucent Window. [**Evaluation of Unbroken Will complete: Rating Improved]** Archive hovered in front of me. **[I think you must have been a jailer in your previous life since the sheer atrocities you have been committing with your body lately cannot be described with mere words]** “I…HAH…cannot…HA-help…..” I tried to say while sucking air through my mouth and nose together. I grabbed the water bottle from my bag and gulped down every last drop of it. The chill of air vanished, steam rose from my body. If torture had a language then my body must have been screaming by now. My body pained, I tried to sit straight but my body felt like lead. I heard a low hiss, I turned my head to see the same girl with snow white hair and shining berry blue eyes walking. I closed my eyes. It was always like that, She came around midnight, trained an hour and left without saying ever a single word. Sometimes she would come in front of me and demonstrate a certain stance for the exercise I was performing without saying anything. I would also correct myself through observation. If I am doing it right she would observe for a minute and then return to the middle of the ground for her own practice. We never talked but after two weeks we had developed a natural recognition for each other and didn’t mind other’s presence. But the footsteps never stopped, instead I felt a shadow covering my eyes from the starlight. I opened my eyes and saw two blueberries staring into my soul. I sat up because those eyes commanded my full attention. Her golden band reflected the moonlight. **[Classification of Snow White Girl: Extremely Important Person]** I gulped and said “What’s up?” “…………..” “…………..” **[…………]** One minute, two minute, three minute…..my face became redder with every minute. **[And the worst pickup line in history award goes to….]** Archive was about to say when she picked out a notepad and a pen from somewhere and started scribbling**.** After five second she turned it towards me. *Why?* Was what was written in the notepad in a devastatingly beautiful handwriting. **[Her handwriting makes your handwriting look like vomit]** Archive remarked. I ignored it. Instead looked towards her and said. “To survive, if you mean why I come here with this trash body of mine to exercise” Her eyes opened a fraction wider She started scribbling again, but before she could finish however the meaning arrived to me instantly. “It means to not be squashed so badly that I need to be transported to cremation oven instead of a hospital bed, at least” I said with sincerely. That made her smile She started scribbling again “I know, but at least this is better than sitting and crying over it” the meaning arrived directly to me. This time her jaw dropped and she stopped writing and started staring at me with her wide eyes. In her hands she had the notepad with a written sentence. *But your form and sequence is terrible* **[Omnilingual field test: Ongoing]** This time she put away her notepad and put her hand above her hips. *Are you a psychic or something?* The meaning arrived to me directly. “Nah, just a depressed teenager” I smiled as the conversation flowed naturally between us Her lips opened a bit and meaning arrived directly in the form of a small laugh. *Heh* Then she looked around and then straight into my eyes. *Tomorrow be here sharp at 10 p.m .*The meaning arrived to me “Huh?” I wanted to ask but she vanished like wind. **[Is this what they refer to ‘Must have been the wind’?]** “I guess we will get to know tomorrow” My day started with running to Mess for breakfast then towards the central lecture hall. “WHY THE HELL ARE ALL OF MY CLASSES IN THE CENTRAL HALL!” I complained while my legs carried me towards the lecture hall at top speed. **[ It is because someone thought that it was better to argue with fellow roommates on the cover of polity textbook rather than getting some rest]** the Archive reminded me. I attended the lectures, ate lunch, did some self study in the library and ran towards mess for dinner at 9:00 PM The door of martial dome opened with a slow hiss. I looked at my band, 9:55 PM. I looked here and there, the empty stadium provided me no answers. I reached at the center of the stadium and my watch buzzed. 10:00 PM the watch said. Suddenly the air changed and I felt a prickling sensation on my back. I slowly turned around and saw her. She stood there, behind me. Her snow like hair made in a high ponytail. Today she wore a black tracksuit and long black leggings. She was approx three heads taller than me. I had to talk to her while looking up at her. She didn’t waste any time and pointed towards the track. *25 Rounds, No break.* The meaning arrived to me instantaneously. “W-wait. I-“ before however I could object I saw her hands heading towards the swords at her hips. And as any sane person my survival instincts kicked in. She ran behind me hitting her wooden sword on the ground just behind me. “Eek” I regretted my all decisions upto this point. **[Training Regimen difficulty updated]** **[Current Difficulty: Asian]** **[Warning, the current events are being performed by professionals do not try this at home]** Archive began delivering commentary to an audience invisible to me while I prayed for my life. I crawled back to my dorm, dead both in my mind and spirit. The dorm hallway was dark, moonlight scattered through windows working for now retired lights. I crawled one step at a time, my back was hunched and my posture looked like that of a corpse. I knocked three times on the door of my room. After some time the door opened. Robert opened the door clearly in sleep paralysis. He opened his eyes a bit to see the person standing in front of him. At the exact moment light flashed revealing my ominous face with sweat and blood, looking like a corpse deciding to wake up on the day of rain. “……………….” “……………….” “……………….” “……………….” “Uh-Robert I can exp-“ “AHHHHHHHHHHHHHHHHHHHHHHH” Robert screamed falling on his hips. “H-hey, Robert it’s me Arin” I tried to explain but the light outside flickered again momentarily highlighting sweat and blood. Robert took out a cross from somewhere and started praying “I will say of the lord, he is my refuge-“ he began. I held out my hand and grabbed his cross “Hey it’s me Arin-“ but when I grabbed the cross in his hand. I saw his face going through all shades of color finally stopping at white. He fainted in my hands while saying “My lord I am coming to thee”. I looked at him, laying in my hands unconsciously, when finally someone turned on the light."},{"t":"29. The Weight of his Name","k":"Chronicles · chapter","u":"chronicles/c29.html","s":"*If you feel confused tell me, I will edit the chapter* **[COME ON SOLDIER, PUSH  THROUGH!]**the Archive motivated me “Twelve” I calmly said while rising, though my trembling arms betrayed me. *Enough!*The meaning arrived in my mind. I sat up straight. The snow white girl handed me my water bottle. I chugged it down like energy drinks from my previous life. **[Day of Torture: 40, Total Cellular transformation Completed: 56.7%]** the archive noted **[Evaluation of the Updated Training Regimen: Extremely Effective]** I looked at the snow white girl who was now training in the middle of the ground, the displaced air flowed like a cooler I hadn’t paid for. She still looked unreachable. Whenever she moved, her image blurred and instantaneously appeared somewhere else creating a torrent of wind. **[Stop staring or keep doing if you want more ‘GUIDANCE’]** The word guidance made a chill go down my spine. For the past two weeks what I have been going through is definitely not training. “But come to think of it, only because of her strictness I could make such a progress” I said while eyeing the numbers. While I talked to the Archive, I saw her coming towards me. One second she was at the other end of the stadium and other second in front of me. **[Definitely ‘Must have been the wind’]** the Archive commented in pure disbelief. *You haven’t left yet?*  She meant to ask. “Tomorrow is a weekend, so I thought of stretching the session a bit longer today” I said. *Children should go to bed early.* She meant. “Look who’s preaching” I said with a smirk. She glared at me. *You are a general admission candidate right?* *“*Yes” I said She sat on the edge of the long wall separating ring and spectator seats. *Why did you apply to come here? There must have been several other good options to live a good life.* She looked at me. “Well most of the credit goes to the fact that I didn’t had long to live if I hadn’t come here” I said. She kept quiet letting me speak. “And a part of me was greedy” I admitted shyly. *Greedy?* “I wanted to explore the world” I said while looking at the stars. “But never knew it could be so beautiful” I added. *Beautiful?  Say that after you survive a Fog event* She seemed to say. “Will see about that” I countered. *Tell me about your preparation days*  She asked “About my preparation huh?” We talked for quiet sometime, mostly me reciting the story of my library days that how Rao would appear always at the exact time I needed his guidance like a coincidence. How Megha would sometime order extra tea to assist a tiny young boy to study more. How the spirits of surplus stationary would bless me whenever I would be in a turmoil. I didn’t mention my home only that I lived with my mother who was a daily wage worker in a dye factory. Then how I had gone to the written exam center with nothing but expectations and a tiffin. *What about the interview?* She asked “The interview was the most memorable part, as far as I remember, the panel were Mrs. Diksha Sen representing academy, Mr. Kenzo Hayato as External Observer and Mr. Mikhail Orlov Volkonskaya of the Zarya Consortium.” I said. Her expression changed, first time as of what I have seen. *Did he do something?* She asked. “He showered me with his killing intent” I continued “Asking if the others tried to take advantage of me what would I do? If others thought that I was a bug they can take advantage of, then what I would do?” I recounted. *What did you do?* *“*I said that if they tried to take advantage of me or my fellow humans” I stared in her eyes and declared in the same way as I had done on the interview room “ I’d teach them that I didn’t come to beg”. **[Her POV]** I saw the boy packing up and go, but I decided to stay. I gazed up at the sky and some old memories of December resurfaced. Moscow was covered in thick snow, but nevertheless looked as energetic as ever. The sky was dark with clouds threatening of downpour at any moment. The houses and streets were adorned with colorful lights and happiness. In the center stood the Royal castle, a mix of grey and red narrating it’s own myth. In one of its rooms, there stood a dining table set up with delicacies common people would never even see in a life time. Servants stood behind chairs each. The sounds of glasses and plates along with chitter and chatter filled the room with liveliness. “And do you know after that- “  said one of my cousins. “Oh my, Is that so?” my mother curiously asked auntie who was sitting beside her. “Big sister tell me more stories” my younger brother tugged at my friend’s sleeve. But everyone quietened down the moment he stepped inside the dining hall. Mikhail Orlov Volkonskaya, my grandfather, the chairman to one of the top guilds of the world. The  Zarya Consortium and also someone from whom anyone would want words of recognition. He was a man of sheer discipline and tradition. A man of no shortcuts and someone who hated nonsense to the core. He sat at the head of the table. Servants set up his plate, filled his glass. My younger brother who was very young didn’t know much etiquette so he asked. “Grandpa, I want to hear a story” my younger brother said with expectations. Everyone stopped, my mother glared at my younger brother and reprimanded him. “Grandpa is very busy I will get you a story book later” She silenced him. “No, I want to hear it from grandpa!” my little brother pouted. At that innocent display of disobedience my grandfather smiled. He set his fork and knife aside and said. “I may not be able to tell you a good fantasy story, However I can tell you about my experience while interviewing candidates for Institute of Inter World in India. Do you want to hear?” He asked. “Yes” my little brother descended from his chair and ran towards grandfather’s chair. Grandfather took him in his arms and told him lots of stories about interviews and his travels. We all listened together, for half an hour and in between that somewhere, my brother fell asleep. A servant came forward and carefully carried him back to his room. Other servants replaced our food with warm food that had cooled off. “Were there no candidates that caught your eye?, your holiness” my cousin asked. “No” a straight answer. We were about to start our meal “But there was one-” he continued “Who glared back” he calmly recalled his eyes in a trance. My father choked on a piece of food and my big brother accidently lost the hold of his fork. “Who dared to-“ my cousin was about to stand but grandfather’s next words came as a greater shock to all of us. “He came from a nameless district from an insignificant city, his mother was a daily worker. He didn’t even attend a middle school, yet he sat with confidence in an oversized coat with such a small stature.” He kept going. “His left lung was almost destroyed, medical reports said he had almost an year to live. At first he made a lot of mistakes but corrected them in the first attempt when pointed out.” Grandfather looked up, everyone was listening to him with full concentration “I-…let out my killing intent on him to see his true nature underneath all of his wordplay.” We all held our breaths, even those old battle hardened commanders trembled under grandfather’s  killing intent. “W-what did you get?” my big brother asked, scared but curious. Grandfather said in a simple unceremonious tone “A dragon in lizard’s skin”. I hit my fork two times on the plate that made everyone turn their faces to me. Then I made some handsigns. *What was his name?* Grandfather opened his mouth “Arin Valen” “Achoo, Present Sir” I shouted at my name being called out for attendance while running in a mandatory physical fitness drill. **[Someone’s missing you? Cause you have been sneezing a lot today] Archive suggested.** “Maybe mother? I will call her later in the evening” **[Karmic Coins Obtained: 550] Notification popped up.** “What was that for?” **[No Idea Sherlock]**"},{"t":"30. Am I going to die?","k":"Chronicles · chapter","u":"chronicles/c30.html","s":"“Did you feel some change in your breathing?” nurse asked me. “Yes, lately I am able to breathe in more air” I said. I mean I am able to breathe more for approx 10 months now, but for doctor it is 20 days since they injected the stem cells in my chest. For three hours it is compulsory for me to go to medical wing where they would put me in a comfortable sleeping capsule and tell me to sleep. Well it is a respected rule to not sleep in between classes but you can’t help if someone requests you right? When nurse comes to fetch me from class everyday I can feel the gazes of hate and jealousy from my ‘precious’ fellow classmates when they would be having to attend two more lectures for attendance and here I just have to sleep to get that. I turned towards them and with a smirk conveyed my message ‘This is what you call skill issue losers’. I was being transported to northern medical wing which may be due to having most advanced medical technologies known to mankind. The van advanced smoothly reaching the northern ward of campus in just 5 minutes which would have taken me 30 minutes by foot. The guards opened the gates and the van slid inside. No matter how many times I looked, the northern ward always seemed like an another world to me. There were fountains, road stretched like a red carpet and instead of 3-4 dorm buildings there were mansions. Apparently I once asked the nurse about it and she told me that for students of combat and children of royalty or sponsoring houses were given not dorms but personalized housing with personal training space and equipments along with personnel. “Privilege I guess” I muttered. **[Of course since they are the pillars of humanity and others can be disposed if needed]** Archive commented. I was told to lay down in the incubation capsule. I laid down, the capsule was closed. Inside the dim cold blue light turned on. I closed my eyes letting the terminal fatigue swallow me. **[Day of Torture 62, Total cellular transformation completed: 74.9%]** *You are going early today? ,* meaning arrived in my mind. “Since I have exams after this weekend” I said while packing my bag. I hung the bag on my shoulders and started walking towards my dorm. **[Left lobe functionality 76%, Classification updated: From Coughing doll to Human Being]** “Eh? I wasn’t th-” I turned around instinctively and imagined a wall in front of me. Clang! Something hit the invisible wall and fell down. I took out a handkerchief and picked up the thing which fell down, It was a hatchet. [**If you hadn’t turned around-]** “I would have been severely injured” I finished. I saw in the direction from which the hatchet came but all I saw was darkness and a streetlight flickering. “I have a feeling that this is just the starting” I murmured and ran to my dorm. “Did you do what I ordered?” said a boy wearing a bathing robe, a glass of wine in his right hand facing towards the window from where the whole campus could be seen. The room was lavish, where each accessory costed in millions. A maid stood at the corner of the room and another boy at the opposite edge of the room kneeling. His navy blue coat had Red Strips. He trembled a bit his head low facing towards the ground. “I couldn’t sir” he said in a low voice. The other boy stopped shaking his glass of wine. The boy who was kneeling saw a pair of feet in front of him just after he blinked. “And what is your excuse?” a voice reached his ears, calm but dripping with rage. The kneeling boy began stammering. “I-I threw a-a hatchet b-but-..” his voice trailed off. “BUT?” “I-It hit s-something like an in-invisible wal-” but he couldn’t complete his sentence. ‘SLAP’ A voice filled the entire room. The kneeling boy whose face was down for a while now was wedged in the side wall, unconscious. “Incompetent” The boy in the bathing robe cursed. He then walked back to the window “Cassandra” “Yes, young master” the maid at the corner of the room replied. “I want that bug cleaned as soon as possible” he ordered while enjoying the view from the window . “As you wish young master” the maid smiled. “Nataliya can only be mine” he whispered to himself. “ACHOO” I sneezed loudly **[Seriously, just two months in and already playing with the heart of ladies huh?]** Archive eyed me suspiciously. “Who will like a tiny boy with pale skin and sunken eyes?” I doubted. **[We never know, rich people have fantasies anyway]** That made the hairs of my neck stand up. The lecture ended and I stood up. “Time to go home” The Friday menu always made me drool. I had lunch and started studying for the exams. I didn’t go to train today and instead made my way up to the terrace. “The campus sure is beautiful I looked from above” I admired standing near the edge. The beauty was there, the wind was there, the peace was there and above all there was no sound. “Wait, why is there no sou-” before even I could finish something pushed me from behind. The push was definitely not accidental, it was a proper push that’s all I could make out before the world spun. I grabbed the railing in panic. I was hanging in air, my eyes darted downwards. The ground was far below and I swung from the railing 13 floors above. I looked up and saw a shadowy figure. “Hey’ I don’t know who you are” I gulped “but let’s talk it out” The shadowy figure moved it’s hand and grabbed my wrist. “H-hey please don’t do this, I haven’t even dated yet” I pleaded while my mind ran at full throttle. The figure put a lot of force instantaneously. ‘CRACK’ with a sound my wrist broke and the next thing I knew was that I was falling down from a 13 storey building."},{"t":"31. One Month","k":"Chronicles · chapter","u":"chronicles/c31.html","s":"*I will post after a month now since I have exams, but I will post multiple chapters so don't worry about it and also leave your reviews it motivates me to keep writing. If you didn't like it at least comment what was missing.* **[Cassandra’s POV]** I put a bit of force and the boy’s wrist broke. ‘Poor thing’ I felt pity and pleasure at the same time thinking about how this tiny little thing will explode once it meets the ground from such height. I let his hand go and watched him fall. Thrill and ecstasy bloomed inside my heart seeing his desperate face. I saw his figure going down, my smile widening with his descent. 11,10,9,7… “Huh?” After crossing 6th floor his form shifted, no. It was more like he slid from expected trajectory towards a new trajectory mid air. “Impossible!” I exclaimed. He didn’t hit the ground, instead his form headed towards the fountain in a parabolic path. ‘SPLASH’ The water from fountain jumped when his form hit the surface of water. “Tch” I stood over the railing ready to jump down and finish him but nearby students already surrounded the fountain. “Lucky bastard, I will thoroughly finish you next time.” **[Arin’sPOV]** **‘**SHIT,SHIT,SHIT’ Wind hit my face as I descended. I looked around and saw a fountain. “Have you ever experienced a water slide?” I asked archive. **[WHAT ARE Y-] Archive tried to ask.** But I imagined, a slide. The ones they have in water parks. From the 5th floor to the fountain and after that the latest recall that I had was myself being carried out at a stretcher surrounded by nurses. After that it was all dark. I opened my eyes and saw a woman, it was all blurry so I unconsciously spoke “Mom?” “Oh my! Calling the first woman you see mom after making such a mess out of yourself is very like of you Arin” the voice was neither comforting nor cruel. I stiffened. Even if someone had asked me to identify this voice in my sleep I had have done so because… “I apologize Mrs. Serin” I tried to sit up but sharp pain shot up in my butt. She stopped me with her hand. “Either you are crazy or you want to get away with this incident like nothing happened, But maybe your luck ran out while protecting you because I am not going to let this slide” she said. Then she took out a notebook from somewhere and started interrogation. “3 Major Bones fractured in Left Arm, Right Leg and Pelvis as well as several minor bones. Minor injuries sustained in spine. 3 Operations successfully done, At least One Month of rest needed even after super regeneration capsule.” She read the report nonchalantly. She then crossed her legs “The way you reacted at least tells me that you didn’t hit your head too hard” She noted. At that moment I regretted waking up at all. . . . “Hey you there?” I whispered to air. After sometime a translucent widow appeared **[What?]** “Can’t you be a bit less rude towards your precious host?” I asked **[If you survive another fall like that, I will consider thinking about it]** **“……………”** **[……………]** **“…………..”** **[……………]** I pinched the bridge of my nose As expected we can never understand each other. . . . Morning arrived sooner than expected. Today was supposed to be my semester examination but Mrs. Serin had strictly prohibited my entrance in the exam hall saying that if something happened to me their investments would go down the drain and that she would rather see me work like an overworked corporate guy instead of me dying on her. I had one month and last night made me realize how much I need strength more than anything. I saw my legs which were in a slightly better condition. I heard a knock on the door. “Come in” I said. The nurse came inside along with breakfast. She set up the table in front of me and put down the breakfast. Oats and vegetable soup. “Here you go” she said. “Excuse me can I get -” I said. Hearing my request the nurse got confused, “I-I will ask doctor if this is possible” she ran off to call doctor. Meanwhile I looked out of the window. I recalled my conversation with Miss Serin where she said that retaking exam was not possible but not to worry since she will find other methods to torment me mentally. She also assigned me two guards when I told her that someone pushed me and the case of Hatchet which I submitted for fingerprint verification and never got the results for. Spring had arrived. Then I looked at the calendar 19th\nMarch was today’s date. “Okay, one month is all I have. Let’s make the best out of it” I understood that whoever it was will not let me live peacefully. 12th April, In a certain office of the central tower Professor Serin Illias was checking papers when suddenly the door of her cabin was violently opened. “I don’t recall giving you permission to enter, Miss Olivia” Professor Seris said while not bothering to even look up. “It is urgent” Olivia said as a matter of fact. “Wha-” before however she could respond, the door was violently opened again. “T-this is emergency” it was a man in a lab coat breathingheavily. “What happened Louis?” this time Professor Serin put aside the answer copies and sat in a serious manner. “T-the Serilian Pattern” he gulped. “The Serilian pattern has attained maximum value in two places. Professor Serin stood up “What is the expected time of starting of events ?” “They are expected to begin in a week” Louis informed. “What is the intensity probability?” She asked. “Upto 3rdgrade”Louis said with confidence “Contact the Headmaster, Olivia arrange a meeting with seniors of all tracks as well and contact all noble houses and search for the best of 3rd level in all combat personnel. “Yes professor” Olivia ran out. “Keep monitoring the symptoms and inform me as soon as any change is recorded.” She said. **[Arin’s POV]** 15th April, digital calendar showed today’s date. **Knock knock** **“**Come in” I said. I stopped pedaling and cleaned the sweat on my face with a towel. The Nurse entered carrying dinner. She didn’t even bother to gave me a strange look as this had become a routine now. Whenever she came inside the room after 7 in the evening, she found me pedaling frantically on a gym bicycle like my life depended on it. “Meet the Doctor after eating” she said and left. . . “You will be discharged tomorrow cadet Arin” Doctor declared eyeing the current reports. I nodded “You have healed completely but still take care of yourself for a month, you are not an elite or a blood bound whose body can automatically recover.” He said again. “Yes sir” I said and returned to my room. I pedaled the gym cycle till 4 in the morning and moved to bed. A pink haired woman appeared instantly beside me. “Rotating the shifts?” I asked The pink haired woman was the bodyguard assigned to me by Professor Serin. She nodded and said “Meet Professor as soon as possible after being discharged” then she disappeared. I sat on the bed. “Open Archive” **[ STAT WINDOW ]** ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━ **Name: Arin Valen Age: 14** Race: Human Title: None **Health Status:** Frail Constitution [Improved] **━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━** BLOODLINE ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━ **None** **━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━** SKILLS ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━ **Null Craft (2/5)** **Omnilingual (0/5)** **━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━** ARTS ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━ **Art of Stellar Vessel (1/5)** [Total Transformation Completed: 99.2%] **━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━** **SPECIAL TRAITS** **━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━** **[Fading Breath]** Every physical action rapidly exhausts the body. **[Unbroken Will]** Mental resistance greatly exceeds physical limitations. **[Curse of Despair: 31.6%]** **━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━** KARMIC POINTS ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━ **[Karmic Coins: 600]** “Open the wheel of Fortune” I said"},{"t":"32. Departure","k":"Chronicles · chapter","u":"chronicles/c32.html","s":"*I will try to release chapter consistently if I am able to complete them, please write the reviews. It gives me motivation* The Wheel of Fortune opened. A wheel with four different colors with 4 different sections. 1.Art 2.Skill 3.Artifact 4.??? I looked at my balance **[Karmic Coins: 600]** I took a deep breath and “Spin” . . . “So, Mr. Arin Valen” said Professor Serin sitting cross legged in front of me. Her brown hair hung like a river, her simple but alluring figure was being highlighted through her posture. I stood straight with hands behind my back in her office where everything was organized except her desk. Sunlight spilled from the window on the left from her desk. Whole room was plastered in gold with world map of the right wall and a globe on her table. Expensive gadgets lay on her desk just like that and a plate of half eaten sandwich along with an empty cup of tea. My mouth watered after seeing the sandwich due to the fact that they only served porridge and vegetable soup at the medical wing. “You missed the examination” she recounted. “Yes” “And caused quite a scene in your first year” “Yes” “So now, do you know what is going to happen to you?”she eyed my face. “Maybe I will have to attend extra lectures? Or retake examination? Or write an essay?” I said “Oh you poor lamb” she smiled devilishly. Then she gave me a paper “Read it” she commanded. I started reading, the more I read the more my palms started sweating. “T-this is-” I screamed in horror. “EXACTLY” she continued “THIS IS YOUR PUNISHMENT” she smiled. In my hand was the final list of team selected for fog event. “B-but I am onl-” “FIRST YEAR, WHO MISSED HIS EXAMS AND CAUSED A SCENE IN HIS FIRST TWO MONTHS”she finished shutting me up. “Did you know how much trouble I had to go through because of that incident?” She yelled. “At least you could’ve asked me” I said something which I regretted a moment after. Hearing my response the pen in her hand broke. Her smile vanished, the temperature of the room dropped. “Before I turn you into mincemeat, get out” her voice came out dripping with rage. I ran out as fast as I could never looking back. . . . “Toothbrush, check” “Towel, check” I crossed the word with a red pen on a thin paper piece **[Don’t forget Perfume, you reek of medicines and hospital bed]** a translucent window appeared in corner of my vision. Apparently the date of departure was today at night, told me by my bodyguard. I hung my little backpack on my shoulder. I wore the academy uniform since it was all I had which will not make me feel embarrassed outside campus. I was about to step out of the room. “Arin” a voice called me from behind. I turned around to see my two roommates looking at me as if this is the last time we will be seeing each other. Arun and Robert Arun stepped forward and stuffed a piece of sweet in my mouth “Eat it, it is Prasad sent by my mother. May Lord Narayan keep you safe from dangers” he said. “Thank you Arun” I chewed it with gratitude. Next, Robert came forward, he gave me a small crucifix and sprinkled some holy water on me and said “May Holy Spirit be with you”. “Thank you Robert” I took the baptism gratefully. Then Arun gave me a small piece of metal with a button. “Press it” he said. I pressed it and from the piece of metal unleashed a long steel needle. Like a rapier. Arun came close to me and whispered. “This is given by him” he pointed to our fourth room mate who was covered in blanket like a cocoon. “He may not say it but he wanted me to give you this believing that this may give you good luck” Arun whispered to me. I nodded and stepped out of the room. The door shut behind me. I walked upto the front gate where the pickup van waited for me. I boarded the van and it took me to the helipad. The pink haired lady accompanied me till the helipad where a different personnel was present. I was made to sit in a helicopter, I froze when I first saw a helicopter but since everyone was in a hurry I didn’t waste time. When the helicopter lifted in air I grabbed the edge of my seat on instinct. **[Location: Munich]** I closed my eyes and remembered Ma. I also recalled advices and guidelines given to me by my seniors. They also told me to be alert as delegates being killed and their heads sent as declaration of war was not common but not uncommon either. 2 Hours went by as I fell into sleep unknowingly and when I opened my eyes we were landing in Paris. “Mr. Arin” a woman in black clothes came forward after the helicopter landed. “I am Watanabe Sumire, your escort. please follow me” she walked ahead of me. I followed behind her. The facility in which we landed were guarded heavily, soldiers taking rounds with weapons in their hands. I was led through a couple of checks before sitting in a black limousine. Miss Sumire sat beside me. Car moved, scenes changing Miss Sumire was quiet, her head tilted towards the window. She didn’t talk, just sat with a straight posture gazing at the stars. We went through a deserted route, at some point of time the speed of the car was so fast that trees and other monuments blurred before even being properly constructed in my vision. I saw something moving from the corner of my eye and when I turned my head I saw Miss Sumire handing me a water bottle. I took it and drank a bit of water to ease my nervousness. She put her hand in her breast pocket and took out a nutrition bar. “Good if no meal available” she said in a low voice while putting the bar in my hand. Miss Sumire was a beauty you wouldn’t see commonly even in the institute except for the snow white lady who was in a league of her own. Her hair was the color of space blue and her eyes a light shade of purple. I took a good look at her for the first time and stared at her for a few moments without blinking in a daze. Snapping out of it only when archive appeared in front of my vision **[CONCENTRATE SOLDIER! ]** I hurriedly looked towards the window **[I understand this is biology at play, but have you ever taken a good look in the mirror?]** Archive said. “Shut up” I whispered after being hit at the sore spot. Meanwhile Miss Sumire gazed out of the window lost in her own thoughts as the car neared the destination."},{"t":"33. Fog (1)","k":"Chronicles · chapter","u":"chronicles/c33.html","s":"**[Sumire's POV]** I was told to bring the trainee ambassador from Institute of Inter World Studies. I was very excited to escort whoever it was since someone from institute of inter world studies was bound to become a person of big position if that person manages to successfully complete their studies from that institute. Even my big sister Watanabe Satsuki studied there, since she was the first born of the household, it was mandatory for her to attend the Institute alongside our cousin the imperial prince of japan. I had practiced in front of mirror about the topics I would discuss with the man I was to escort. After all he must be very knowledgeable right? Such relations when developed in future tend to benefit a lot. Even uncle Hayato said a lot of good things about that guy, he rarely says so. I waited for four hours standing in front of the helipad and when the helipad landed. \"A kid?\" I muttered seeing a tiny little boy with sunken eyes coming out of the helicopter with institute's badge on the chest of his blazer. I was disappointed, no I was angry but as instructed I had to make sure that the trainee doesn't encounter any trouble while on the way to the destination. I did as I was told. I escorted him. While in the car I could feel his gaze on me but I ignored him my anger increasing. After sometime I remembered that I didn't ask him for water or food which I was supposed to get done for him. I saw two bottles in the pouch of the seat, it had become warm and may have been two days old but he could still drink it right? I knew it was disrespectful but he wouldn't know anyway. I had a nutrition bar in my breast pocket which I always took for long journeys and gave it to him. \"Good if no meal available\" I said while giving him the last of what remained my duty. . . . **[Arin's POV]** It was still early dawn since we flew in a private jet arranged by the government itself, it took us only an hour to arrive in Berlin. I was taken to the resident hotel whose building alone made my neck hurt trying to find the height of. It was a seven star hotel. I was trying to walk in such a way that would make me look dignified while actually trying not to break anything. Even the cup of tea would alone equal my net worth here. Miss Sumire took out a black card from somewhere and after a minute threw a keycard at me. When I arrived at the floor where the room was assigned to me. I simply touched the keycard at the identification panel and the door opened. Inside the room was- \"WOOHOOOOO\" I jumped on the circular bed and it covered me in its embrace. I looked around the room, the lights were dim blue. And a sweet melodic low tune rang in the room which urged me to rest. I opened the room of the bathroom and what I saw was- \"Is this bathtub or a swimming pool?\" I freshened up and jumped in the bath tub which had the sheer size of a pool. Then I came out a minute later and did a bit of exercise for an hour **[Total Cellular Transformation completed: 99.5%]** \"Just a bit more\" I said seeing the numbers. I went to sleep after washing up again. **Knock Knock** A sound reached me who was in deep sleep. \"Huh? Bwah haffen?\" I woke up, my eyes still tired and muscles creaking like rusted gears. I went up to the door and opened it only to find Miss Sumire standing in the front. \"Why did you come so early?\" I asked clearly unaware. Her eye brows raised slightly. She showed me her mobile in which the time was 11:30 A.M We were supposed to check out at 12 noon. . . . \"I-I am extremely ashamed of my behavior\" I bowed my head to Miss Sumire who didn't say anything and just walked in the front. She was dressed in a black long coat which stopped my any attempt to evaluate her figure. **[Artifact found: Pervert eye blocker, Type: Wearable, Rank: Legendary] Archive appeared.** **\"**Tch, annoying AI**\"** I whispered **[At least not a Lecherous Skeleton]** I was about to argue back but realized that we have reached in front of an elevator. We entered and Miss Sumire pressed the button to go to terrace. We reached the terrace and there waited for us another Helicopter. \"Woah\" I exclaimed as I saw Berlin from above. **[Truly Beautiful]** even archive commented. Meanwhile we headed for our destination which was Munich. . . . \"How are your lungs boy?\" asked the man standing in front of me with a wide grin plastered on his face. \"Improving, My lungs and I appreciate your concern sir\" I said The man in front me laughed. He was none other than the external observer for my interview and Ambassador of Japan Mr Kenzo Hayato and apparently as whose assistant I was going to participate in the fog event. \"You look better the last time I remembered, now at least you can stand straight\" he joked. \"It had only been possible because you gave me a chance sir\" I remained humble, not taking part in the joke. Since he was the most senior member present. \"No, no it was only because of your own efforts-\" we continued talking till a lady came near Mr Hayato and said \"All preparations are done sir, The event is expected to start in Eight Hours\". Mr Hayato nodded and looked at me. \"Arin, if you want to quit, say it now because once we enter, we cannot come out till the event is complete\" he said in a serious tone. \"I understand, but I am not going to step back because-\" **[Mrs Serin will kill me if I go back.]** Archive read my thoughts aloud, luckily I managed to speak the lines I prepared. \"-It is my duty to lay my life to protect humanity from events like these.\" I said unwavering. He eyed me and said nothing then turned around \"Follow me\" He spoke to me about doctrines, and told me about the resources we accumulated for this cause. I saw researchers and engineers setting up machines and soldiers instructing civilians to remain inside. I also saw a grid dome forming 3 times and then disappearing. Mr Hayato also introduced me to the combat personnel. Since Institute of Inter world studies cannot send all of its personnel, many other prestigious institutes in the world sent out their students to get experience and learn. I saw a twin pair, a man and woman demonstrating a series of moves, which looked like a cluster of fire dragons dancing in the sky. When they saw Mr Hayato they stopped and bowed their heads. I also saw two girls playing with a huge boulder without even touching it **[Mentalists]** But there were three which stood out the most among the others. One girl and two boys. The girl closed her eyes and tightened her grip around her spear, the moment she came into the stance, the wind stopped and thousands of spatial cuts appeared all over the ground around her. \"Still as impressive as ever, Satsuki\" Mr Hayato said while walking towards her. The girl turned around to look towards the source of sound and her eyes shined the moment she saw Mr Hayato. \"UNCLE!\" she ran like a child and crashed softly into the embrace of Mr Hayato. \"You still didn't grow\" his words were harsh but his tone implied otherwise. She rubbed her face on his shoulder and then her eyes went over me. \"Is he the boy you mentioned?\" she asked him excitedly. Mr Hayato nodded and before he could say anything else she or Miss Satsuki's figure blurred and she was already in front of me. She was tall, taller than me and Hayato. Just like the snow white woman. She was three heads taller than me. \"What is your name?\" she asked while bowing a little trying to match my eye level. \"......….\" \"Do you know I am your senior?\" she said again \"......….\" \"Hello, Moshi Moshi?\" she waved her hand in front of me \"......….\" **[Host brain short circuited mid evaluation. Result: Temporary shock]** Archive noted . . . Here's what happened. The moment our eyes met and I saw her face properly. The water bottle slipped from my hand. \"Fuck, Being this beautiful should be a crime\" I muttered. She was almost Sumire but exponentially more beautiful and her curves were just \"Uff\". I was about to greet her but at that instant she lowered, her cleavage became a bit visible and that was it. Everything in the universe lost meaning to me, my all senses converged up to a single point and there remained only one purpose of my life. To measure the depth of grand canyon in front of me. My mind ran at full throttle, exploring countless threads of quantum possibilities and assumptions. 'Wait if I want to calculate the circumference, hey I need your help' I thought. **[Just assume a particle and integrate from limits to find the mass of the particle]** Archive said 'What should I put in the place of limits?' I thought **[Minus infinity to plus infinity]** Archive suggested. 'Sensei?' I looked at the archive in reverence and had the profound enlightenment of knowing how foolish I had been to argue with such a divine intellect. . . . The clouds moved, this time boldly and not stealthily. We all took our positions, the fog descended like a tsunami covering the whole city in mere seconds. I closed my eyes as the feeling of a tasteless and dead wind covered me. The fog had finally descended and when I opened them again, we were no longer on earth."},{"t":"34. The Romance of Four Kingdoms","k":"Chronicles · chapter","u":"chronicles/c34.html","s":"*Discord Link: https://discord.gg/XD4VFunFCz* **[LISTEN, ARIN]** archive appeared panicked, first time in a while. \"What happened\" **[NO MATTER @#$%#%$$ DON'T %$^%*YOUR FL(^&&^]** **\"**Wha-\" before however I could have understood what the archive wanted to say the fog swallowed me. I opened my eyes, only to see a red and cloudy sky. The land below us had become a prey to drought. There was no wind here. The smell that hit my nostrils was of sulphur. I saw the barrier grid already erected and that. A big clock, floating in the air. **132 Days 17 Hours 23 Minutes 36 Seconds** **[CANDIDATE AUTHOR FOUND]** a black and completely different screen appeared in front of me. **[ASSIGNING OBJECTIVE.....]** **[EVALUATING CANDIDATE PROFILE…..]** **\"**Open Archive\" I had a bad premonition so I said. Nothing happened \"Stat Window\" I yelled. Nothing happened I fell on my knees. And then the window appeared again. **[SWITCHING MODE: FROM BATTLE ROYALE TO STORY.....]** **and then everything went black.** . . . **[Serin's POV]** \"The color of Fog is also its measure of grade\" I read from the slide. \"How many types of colors have we found till now professor?\" one student asked me. \"Four: White, Orange, Cyan, Red.\" I said. \"What happens if we discover a fog event with no known color?\" Another asked. \"The possibility is low, but we will know through the values of Serilian pattern beforehand so there is no need to worr-\". I stopped in middle as my band buzzed. I went out of the class and saw an emergency message from Louis. 'If you are reading this message come to Monitoring Laboratory as soon as possible' said the message. I frowned. If the message was from Louis it had to be something serious. \"Class Dismissed\" I went inside and took my belongings leaving for the Eastern Wing. When I went outside a van was already waiting for me which only confirmed one thing. \"Something had terribly gone wrong\" I muttered to myself hurrying towards the laboratory gate. I put my hand on the scanner and gate opened. Inside everyone was in panic. Someone was running from this corner to that corner tripping two times on his laces. Someone was talking with three telephone receivers simultaneously. I kept walking finally reaching the inner part 'GEO SANCTUM' said the glowing name plate. I pushed the dark door open. The cool air of conditioner reaching me and the heavy atmosphere pressing down upon me. The people here were very few but everyone had serious expression on their faces. The sanctum was hemispherical covered with large screens showing different geographic locations with graphs of symptom reading just beside them. \"What is happening?\" I asked. Louis was the first one to turn around, his expression brightening a bit after seeing me. I put my hands on the table and saw the abnormal readings on the graph. Louis stood beside me and gave me a summary of what happened. \"Something had gone wrong with Munich Fog event-\" he started. I stiffened, the face of a young boy came in my mind. \"-the expected color of fog was white or 3rd grade. When it descended however-\" as he continued, I could feel my heartbeat getting louder. \"-the color started to change after some time of descent and the environment began to act strangely. \"What is the current color of the fog?\" I asked. Louis looked at me and said \"Black\" . . . \"You may look up\" a voice boomed. \"Huh?\" I looked up. \"Daniel and Humayu\" said a middle aged man sitting on an expensive chair with a tired expression. \"The fate of Parshara Kingdom is in your hands now. No matter the demands, make sure to come back with the alliance letter.\" He stepped down, his red thick cloak touching the ground. He reached in front of us, unsheathed his sword and placed in on my shoulder. \"May the spirit of Aezion be with you\" he said I nodded since the room didn't allow for me to ask 'What the hell?' But then everything paused and the world became black and white. A panel opened in front of me **[Re-Enacting: The Romance of the Four Kingdoms]** **OBJECTIVE: Two lovers who come from different and unresolvable backgrounds couldn't unify in the past. Unify them.** **EXTRA OBJECTIVE: ????** **REWARD: ?????** **PENALTY: Erasure.**"},{"t":"35. The Romance of Four Kingdoms (II)","k":"Chronicles · chapter","u":"chronicles/c35.html","s":"I don’t have any memories of how I found myself in this mess. The previous moment I was under a bloody sky and the next moment I was here, in an unknown world, in an unknown courtroom, in a body no historical texts bothered to remember the name of. “MAY THE SPIRIT OF AEZION WATCH OVER US” everyone yelled in unison. “Go Now” the king commanded We stood up, bowed and left the courtroom. The moment we left the courtroom we both sighed. “Arin, Is that you?” said Humayu “Hayato-san?” I asked. He nodded weakly. We both walked close together so the servants and soldiers doesn’t get suspicious. “Is this Normal?” I asked. “No, In any of the fog events I have gone to, this is the first time something like this has happened.” He said. “What happens normally?” “Normally we would be transported to a different land all together, and then fend off monsters and hostile species while allying with other races who are on good terms with us until the countdown is over” he said I processed his words for a while “Did you get something like an objective?” I finally asked. “What do you mean?” “I mean, something like a mission in a translucent panel?” I asked. He looked at me for a moment and said “No, I just opened my eyes and I was here”. I took a deep breath, meanwhile Hayato-san watched my reaction closely. “Did you see something?” he finally asked after observing me. I nodded and told him about the Objective and penalty, omitting the reward. He listened intently We reached the main gate till then. The butler stepped forward, bowed at us and asked us to follow him. The walls of castle were tall, their texture was rough with visible pores. An unknown memory surfaced within my mind. A man in a long dark robe saying “Marine concrete, made up when a certain ratio of sea metals are mixed along with infusing the energy of sea. Originating in Parshara kingdom this material thickens overtime when comes in contact with salty water”. He took us to the carriage, on the way I saw the servants looked thin, The carriage was wide, two seater, emerald colored with big thick four wheels of wood. The coachman was a young boy who descended and bowed towards us. “This is Hassan, my grandson” the old butler introduced his grandson. The escorting soldiers also stepped forward introducing themselves. We boarded the carriage and Hassan drove the carriage forward. As the carriage began to move forward, the old butler came to the window and said. “My lord, please make sure to succeed. My daughter in law is only able to eat once in two days” he said, his eyes were wide open looking at us with expectations. I couldn’t meet those eyes. “We will do best of our abilities” was the only thing I could leave him with. The carriage drove forward. Picking up speed. None of us looked back, too scared to see those eyes again. Hayato-san recovered faster than me. He looked at me, I held my clothes tighter, he put his hand on my shoulder. “This is a part of the responsibility you will have to one day shoulder. Learn from this” he encouraged me. I bit my lower lip. There were no children on the roads and the townscape looked deserted. The stalls were empty and the shops, closed. We then looked through the papers given to us. Basically Parshara kingdom used to export dye, raw metal ores and glowing stones. In return other kingdoms used to give us money. Through that money we used to buy crops and raw food materials from other kingdoms. Another memory resurfaced in my mind. This time it was a lady in the long dark robe with intricate golden patterns “The first merchant association and institute of technology were established in the olden land of Kuru or presently known as The Kingdom of Nazarana. It is the largest hub for merchants and engineers as well as mercenaries”. But lately due to the long ongoing war between the kingdom of Aurorion and Nazarana, the cost of exporting has increased many times. At first the war was expected to finish early since Aurorion kingdom’s martial might exceeded that of Nazarana kingdom. But due to some unknown reasons, Nazarana kingdom has been able to extend the war upto this day. Due to that the profits from our exported materials plummeted and today Parshara kingdom stood at the door of bankruptcy. We had also sent many requests for trade to Aurorion kingdom, but have received no responses yet. Also since Aurorion kingdom had closed off its borders recently, it was not possible to go there since it will be seen as a blasphemy to their crown. “Weird” I muttered. “Our first step is to successfully get an audience with the crown of the Nazarana kingdom” Hayato-san said while engrossed in the papers. We discussed the policies and the sum acceptable to save Parshara kingdom. The policies in the paper were a bit outdated so we used our modern knowledge to make it better. “Night already?” when I finally looked out it was the time of sunset and stars were beginning to wake up. We had crossed the border of Parshara kingdom and now were going through. “Shamshaan forest” I said while looking at the map. According to a legend, this land used to be a vast graveyard in the olden times when the law was just in name and chaos dominated the land and the sea. There were two routes for us to go. One from the Southern route via Dahan Sultanate and the other from the North via Vaeldor Principalities. We stopped near a small cluster of shorter trees and camped. The moment I went inside the carriage to retrieve the rations. “Who’s there?” I felt a gaze behind the carriage. But there was no one. I stood there for some time and then went back to the camping site. Meanwhile Hassan climbed the tree to set the rope of the tent. “You took your time” Hayato-san said. “Just stopped to gaze at the stars” “They sure are beautiful” Hayato san agreed. Hassan cooked food for all of us. We slept while soldiers took turns to watch over us. **[???’sPOV]** I gave the signal, the moment I gave the signal, scouts shot the darts through their blowgun. The darts hit the guards who were supposed to watch, they fell unconscious the moment darts hit them. I couldn’t help but smile. These guys looked rich. The jungle was our home and the night was our shelter. We began to move without making a sound. Four of ours went to kill the unconscious guards One went towards the carriage. While most of my men followed me. Slowly surrounding the sleeping guys. I raised my hand, everyone raised their swords ready to sever the heads of the sleeping guys. “Nothing personal” I muttered while raising my sword. SPLURT!... Before however I could proceed ,the boy sleeping in front of me raised his hand. It was as if he turned to get into a more comfortable sleeping position. He raised his hand causing me to pause, but the hand never came down. It remained in the air, towards me, shaped like it held something and then the sharp searing sensation came instantly throughout my chest. BLARGHHHHHH!!! I vomited blood, my vision filled with tears. Clang!, swish, Arghhhhhhhh! I heard screaming voices, but those voices were familiar and then I finally saw. Those eyes. Those green shining eyes. AUTHOR'S NOTE Join discord for illustrations: https://discord.gg/XD4VFunFCz"},{"t":"36. Gonna beat the shit out of you","k":"Chronicles · chapter","u":"chronicles/c36.html","s":"Clang! ARGHHHHH! Screams filled my surroundings, I moved my eyes and saw the bandits surrounding us falling one by one. “Seriously Arin, If you wouldn’t have alerted us. I don’t know what would have happened” Hayato-san said to me. I had already alerted them before hand. It was our good luck that these guys weren’t trained knights and that our soldiers far exceeded them in Martial power. I stood up my robe swaying with the wind. My long black hair covered my face as I tried to calm down my raging heartbeat. ‘I killed a person’ the thought disgusted me to the core, but if I hadn’t done so I would have been the one to die. I looked at the corpse of the man I killed. His eyes were still open, devoid of any light. I crouched and closed them. “My lord, the cleanup is complete” Hassan bowed while reporting. “Dig up a hole and toss these corpses there” I commanded. The remaining rats were silenced by our escorting soldiers, including the hidden ones who used blowguns. We packed up and started on our journey again since we were more than awake after such an incident. The unconscious guards were carried by the ones who were awake, tying their bodies to themselves after mounting on the horses. The carriage moved forward under the gaze of the starlight. I closed my eyes, reminiscing about the recent moments. ‘What was that?’ I thought. When our escorting soldiers ran to chase the remaining bandits, a strange glow enveloped them and their figure blurred directly appearing near the bandits and finishing them up in an inhumane speed. Another memory resurfaced within me as if to answer the question. An arena, small by no means, in that stood two people, one man and one woman. They both took the stance and a grown man blew the whistle. Immediately after the signal a strange glow different in color began to emit from both. Their figures blurred and- “My lord?” Hassan shook me awake. “Hmm? What happened?” I asked. “Please freshen up and have breakfast” he urged. I saw the outside no longer was veiled in moonlight. Sun had risen high up in the sky. “How much more time is it gonna take, I mean to reach Vaeldor Principalities? Lord Daniel?” A soldier asked me. A senior knight who was travelling by his side hit him in the head. “That’s not the way you talk to a Royal Messenger” He reprimanded. I looked at the map and answer flowed naturally as the mathematics completed in my head “Today at night we will reach Vaeldor principalities” I said while smiling as the senior knight continued to discipline the younger generation. “Halt!” a knight clad in steel armor stopped our carriage. I stepped out to show them papers and the moment I showed them the papers and the royal insignia, their back straightened. “M-my apologies” the guard quickly gave the signal to open the gates. I was about to step up the carriage. Pitter-Patter I heard a slow rhythmic pelting on the roof of the carriage. “Seriously? This is fourth time already today!” the guard of principality complained in a defeated tone. “Welcome to Vaeldor Principality or should I say the residence of the god of Rain.” The other guard mocked him. “Ughhh!” he went two steps behind and stood under a stone shelter coming out of the thick wall. I sat inside, the pelting on the roof becoming louder by the minute. “FRESh APPLES, A KILO FOR 30 COPPER COINS!” yelled a young man. The air was chilling and wet. We pulled out our blankets and covered ourselves with them. But the escorting soldiers weren’t lucky. “L-l-lord Daniel” the young recruit looked at me with pleading eyes. I tried to ignore him but I swear I saw stars twinkling in those eyes. “Someone go ahead and look if there is a hotel nearby” I said casually. “A hotel?” all of the guards looked at me in confusion. I quickly corrected my mistake. “I mean look for a place to stay” One soldier nodded and propelled ahead of us. The sky was dark and mild rain still continued. I inhaled the smell of soil which was my personal drug in every life. “1 silver for the rooms and 2 silver for dinner and breakfast included” said a young and a beautiful woman in an apron. I looked at the escorting guards, who were washed clean in the rain. “Can you also do something about the wet clothes?” I asked in perfect western dialect. Her smile widened as if she was waiting for me to ask that. “Of course sir he-“ THWACK CRASHHH! “As I thought, you still look beautiful Rurina” a young man broke the gate. He ignored us and walked towards the woman on the other side of the counter. “I am back” he said with a wide grin, his arms were thick and his stature was tall and strong. He wore a plain thin piece of cloth which barely hid his abs and comfortable loose pants. He was wet but it didn’t looked like it bothered him in the least. “What do you want?” the lady at the counter or Miss Rurina went straight to the point, her voice filled with bitterness. “Hey I was wrong, I mean I know I was at fault but you know.” He paused for a breath and continued. “I have learnt and matured while I served at the Frostgate hold” He scratched his cheek and then grabbed her hand. “Come on now I want to show you something, come with me” He began to drag her while she resisted in vain in front of his massive strength. “Let go of me!” Miss Rurina tried to protest but he kept dragging her that is until. “Hmm?” he felt an arm on his wrist. “SHE SAYS SHE DOESN’T WANT TO GO!” I said my voice breaking Miss Rurina’s pleas. The tall man turned towards me, the temperature dropped. He grabbed my collar. “You are gonna stop me?” his voice dropped mockingly. “No, I am gonna beat the shit out of you”"},{"t":"37. Gonna beat the shit out of you (II)","k":"Chronicles · chapter","u":"chronicles/c37.html","s":"**[Hayato’s  POV]** Everything has been messed up ever since we arrived here. I closed my eyes when the fog swallowed me and when I opened them I was here. In a courtroom, I have attended a few fog events and so I have studied the etiquette required for such events. But as far as I remember, in none of the events did I posses another body. I kept quiet and decided to observe. The king approached us and put his sword on the shoulder of each of us once, the touch of cold metal confirmed that this wasn’t an illusion. I moved my eyes to see my partner for the crime, the ones he called Daniel. He was a young and healthy man with long black hair and emerald eyes. Daniel had a frown, the kind when one is confused. I felt a flicker of hope, if I had been given a role of ambassador then maybe Arin too. When both of us walked out of the courtroom I decided to gamble on this possibility. “Arin, is it you?” Daniel’s eyes widened and he looked at me. “Hayato-san?” he said, his voice deep and clear. I took a deep breath, relief spreading in my heart. ‘If Arin has also possessed a different body, then maybe others too…’ I assumed. . “My lord please make sure to succeed, My daughter in law only gets to eat once in two days” said the old butler. It was the same everywhere, as an ambassador we had to make sure that we design such policies and do negotiation in such a way that the normal people of both sides can live peacefully without the either losing face. I saw Arin who didn’t dare look back. ‘He is a kid after all’ I thought. I rubbed his shoulder to calm him down but as a senior it was also my duty to teach him and this was a very good example. “This is a part of responsibility you will one day have to shoulder. Learn from it” I said. He nodded, his hands gripping his robe tighter. We departed. . SPLURT… I stabbed the unsheathed sword I had hidden under my blanket into the heart of the man who had his sword raised. He fell his eyes still wide open in shock. Blood flowed like a tap from his chest. My hands trembled. BLARGHHHHH I vomited my vision blurring as my mouth filled with a sour taste. Arin had already communicated the danger to us through drawings on the ground while having dinner. “If he hadn’t told us we would be the ones in their place” I whispered to myself. After that we were too wide awake to rest again so we decided to depart. Arin closed his eyes and as the time passed I realized he fell asleep. I too managed to catch a couple of naps in the meantime. One thing I noticed was that when the bandit was about to attack Arin, he simply raised his hand and the bandit died as if Arin had stabbed him with and invisible sword or spear. “What was that?” I pondered. I didn’t ask him since everyone had their secrets. . . I saw big walls at some distance, that means we had arrived at Vaeldor Principality according to the map. As the principality neared, sunlight began to vanish and sky began to darken with clouds. Air became chilled and blew every couple of seconds. The ground smelled of rain. Arin descended and showed them the necessary documents. The more I watched his interaction the more my chest swelled. ‘He would make one fine Ambassador’ I thought. Rain started again and I listened how the guard complained and the other mocking him by saying Vaeldor Principality as The Residence of god of Rain. It may have been a joke but I remembered it as my experience urged me to store that little detail. One more thing I observed was that there were no designs. Neither in the Parshara kingdom nor here in principality, as if the concept of art and design didn’t exist here. I told Arin “Did you see? All the building and even the walls are plain, no design, no art nothing” He nodded in agreement. We decided to rest as the younger guard requested Arin. . . The principality of Vaeldor according to the map was situated in the North, just above Aurorian Empire in the continent of Eldoris. On the left North corner there was Frostgate hold. We stopped in front of an inn, which was not big but enough to do the magic for the day. “Still no design” I looked at all the shops and buildings which were made in such a way that maximized usability and made no effort to look good. We went inside and Arin stepped forward to do the registration work. THWACK CRASHH! A young man with a thick and bulky build walked inside. He talked to the lady behind the counter and then began to drag her. Until. “SHE SAYS SHE DOESN’T WANT TO GO” Arin grabbed the wrist of the man. The air became heavier, the bulky man’s eyes narrowed and he grabbed Arin’s collar. . SHING! The senior knight beside me grabbed the hilt of his sword unsheathing it a bit. I stopped him. “We are in another country right now, if you used a sword now, it will become a big trouble for us” I said. “But-“ he tried to argue. “Don’t worry-” I continued looking forward “ I believe in him, if he is taking the trouble then he must also know how to get out of it by his own.” I said. . . . The bulky man let go of hand of the woman. The woman fell down on the floor. FWISH! The man threw a punch at Arin. Arin grabbed his arm which had grabbed his collar and jumped locking his arm with his whole body. His legs locking on the man’s neck, and then began to apply force through his whole body. The bulky man began to tremble “Ugghh” trying hard to keep his arm straight and not bend in the wrong direction. But Arin instead of brute forcing the arm turned it anti-clockwise with a jerk and- CRUNCH! “AAAAAARGHHHHHHHHHH” with a crunch the arm of the bulky man twisted a whole three hundred and sixty degrees along with Arin. ‘What moves are that?’ I observed. Arin jumped off the arm of that bulky man and delivered a knee to the nose of the bulky man whose defense dissolved completely. **CRACK!!** “Khgggh urgggg” his nose broke. But the bulky didn’t stop, instead he tried to catch Arin with his other hand. Arin avoided his hand and instead delivered a horse kick on his knee. “AAHHHH” the bulky man’s balance collapsed, at that instant. Arin kept his foot on the roof of the foot of the bulky man. Grabbed his good hand and performed a shoulder throw. **THUD!!** The bulky man fell hard on the floor becoming unconscious. “………..” “………..” “………..” Everyone present there were shocked. Arin had just incapacitated a man twice his size. “Bullying a cute girl in front of me? Not happening” Arin said at last."},{"t":"38. May god have mercy on him cause I won't","k":"Chronicles · chapter","u":"chronicles/c38.html","s":"**Knock knock knock** **\"**Coming\" Robert went to the door and opened it. \"Wha-\" Robert stopped. \"Who is at the door Robert?\" Arun asked while skimming through his economics notes. \"...…..\" \"Robert, Who is at the door?\" Arun asked again. \"...…..\" Still no response from Robert \"ROBERT?\" Arun turned his head, only to see a Robert with his jaw dropped. He stood up and went in front of the door. \"Who-...…..am I to ask\" Outside their room stood two women, One was a mature woman in a tuxedo with black hair and other one was a goddess with snow white hair and deep blueberry eyes in a long black coat and leggings. \"Is this the room of student Arin Valen?\" the black haired woman asked. \"Y-yes\" Arun replied instinctively. They both walked inside the room as if it belonged to them. The snow white haired woman made some hand signs towards the black haired woman. She nodded and asked Arun. \"Where is his bag and belongings?\" she asked. \"There in the wardrobe\" I pointed through my finger. The black haired woman went towards Arin's wardrobe and lifted it. \"Let's go your highness\" the black haired woman said. \"Huh?\" Arun caught that word and looked at the band of the tall girl with blueberry eyes. Golden band \"We have talked to your dorm supervisor\" the black haired woman said while going out. Arun couldn't say anything. The door closed. \"...….\" \"...….\" \"Arun that band..\" \"Yes\" was the only thing Arun could say. They both stood there facing the door for a minute or more. \"I get it now\" Robert broke the silence. \"Get what?\" Arun asked both them still looking at the door. \"That why he always returned so late at night\" Robert turned his towards Arun. Arun's brows knit in a frown. \"I-I didn't think he could ever-\" he tried to make out words. \"Oh my dear friend\" Robert smiled continuing. \"He just didn't wanted to tell us\" Robert came to a conclusion. \"But why would Arin out of all people?\" Arun asked. Robert's smile widened as if he had found the solution. He pointed at the door. \" If you had such a goddess as your girlfriend, would you want to show her to the world or keep her to yourself?\" Robert asked. \"...\" Arun couldn't argue anymore. \"But then why would she take his belongings?\" Arun asked. \"Its so obvious, She is missing him\" Robert pointed it out. \"And what does that have to do with his belongings?\" Arun was curious. \"Okay, did you hear what that black haired lady said?\" Robert asked. \"I have talked to your dorm supervis-\" Arun stopped, his eyes widened. \"Doesn't that mean sh-\" \"Wants to live together with him\" Robert finished the sentence. \"A live in relationship?\" Arun muttered. Meanwhile Robert went to his wardrobe and opened it, searching in deep for something. \"Fuuu, Come here\" Robert urged Arun to come to his table. Arun went there only to see a thick harbound diary. \"What is this?\" Arun asked. Robert looked towards Arun and said in a deep voice. \"Listen Arun\" he began. Arun gulped, he had never seen Robert so serious before. \"Murderers go to hell and work until their bones turn to dust before ending their sentence\" \" I am listening\" Arun said. \"Rapists are tortured for thousands of years in hell, they are castrated and inflicted pain upon trillions of times before there is salvation for them.\" he continued \"But those who betray their comrades, their homies\" his eyes darkened. \"There is no salvation for them\". He preached. The wind blew strongly, our windows banged and lights flickered as he said that. \"A-are you by any chance angry, Robert?\" Arun asked, beads of sweat flowing down from his face. Robert looked at his baseball bat and then at Arun. \"No no no, As a good friend I wish him a happy married life\" he stood up and held his baseball bat. \"I-I think you need to c-calm down Rob-\" Arun tried to say \"But as a good friend, I don't want to see my friend rot in hell forever\" Robert interrupted him as he swung his baseball bat in empty air. \"What do you mean?\" \"What I meant to say is-\" Robert looked at Arun with the same wide smile. \"May god have mercy on him cause I won't.\" . . . **\"**Why am I getting chills?\" Arin wondered while trying to sleep under an unknown sky"},{"t":"39. At the edge of Death","k":"Chronicles · chapter","u":"chronicles/c39.html","s":"\"No, no how can I take money from you good sir\" said Miss Rurina. I scratched my cheek \"Please don't do this…If you do not accept it, it will….you see trouble us\" She stood there for a minute in confusion and suddenly realized something, she reluctantly took the money and bowed. \"Please visit again sometime good sir\" she requested. Since we were travelling as a royal messenger and ambassador it wouldn't have looked good to go without paying albeit the good work. We checked out after having breakfast and also getting some packed for the journey. \"What is our next stop?\" Hayato san asked me. \"Towards the east there is a major city situated at the foothills of the great Samarian range known as Hearthglen\". I pointed out in the mirror. He glanced at the map and then began to enjoy the scenery from the window. Rain continued, a rhythmic chitter-chatter on the roof of our carriage, we have been travelling for around 5 days now. One thing I noticed was that everyone except me and Hayato-san slept very very less. Whenever I would woke up in the middle of the night I would find them sitting and talking or training. I even found Hassan sitting by the campfire reminiscing about his past. After travelling for a day and a half the chilling wet wind began to weaken and the rain stopped. Soon the trees and vegetation also decreased, revealing green marsh meadows on the other side of the border. I could see huge mountains at some distance veiled by the curtain of white cotton clouds. The sun was signing off from its duty and the sky was beginning to darken. \"I am going to take a piss\" I descended the carriage walked some distance. The wet meadows stretching as far as my vision can go. Suddenly my pant loosened and fell. \"Hey!\" I bent down to set it up again. **WHOOSH** Something went just above my head, cutting air violently. I looked around and saw some horses coming towards us fast. I ran and alerted the escorting guards. There were 10 men on horses. Each one of them armed. \"Introduce yourselves!\" the senior knight shouted while unsheathing his sword. \"There's no point in introducing ourselves to dead men\" said the man with a scar on his cheek while unmounting from his horse. Then another man unmounted from somewhere and came in front. \"You?\" I squeaked The other man was none other than the one I beat at the inn. \"Is he the one?\" the scarred man asked. He nodded. The scarred man smiled and said \"I will let you all go unharmed\" he raised his finger towards me \"Give him to us and you all can go\" he put the offer in front. \"Do you even know what you are saying? He is the Royal-\" the senior knight Sir Graham was about to say. \"This is not principality, this is an unclaimed territory under no one's jurisdiction\" he continued scratching his itching ear \"and a fitting graveyard for y'all\" he added with a grin. Till then both of the sides had pulled out weapons. But this time the situation looked dangerous. There were only 8 escort guards and they were 10 including the man I met at the inn. **SHIING!** **Clang!** Swords clashed. All the 8 escort guards clashed with 9 armed men from the other side. The senior knight Sir Graham was alone taking on two guys. **Cling! Clang!** **\"**You sure are tough for your age old man!\" laughed one of the guy fighting Sir Graham. Sir graham who was enveloped in a brown glow pushed both of them back with sheer strength. \"It seems no one is coming to save you\" the scarred man walked towards me with his great sword leisurely resting on his shoulder. \"Why are you doing this?\" I finally asked as I unsheathed my sword. \"What do you mean why, he is my younger brother\" the scarred man spoke. \"But he was the one at fault, as a big brother shouldn't you teach him that?\" I asked lifting my sword. \"Teach? My younger brother spent 5 years at the frosthold front, if he wanted to enjoy a bit after coming here what was wrong in that?\" saying that the scarred man jumped at me. **Clang!** Our swords met. I managed to hold my ground but- \"Urggh\" the force of his strike was so strong that my arms began to tremble. **CRACK!** **\"**ARGHHHHHHHHH\" I kneeled. He motioned a cutting arc with his great sword but the sword never came, instead he delivered a strong kick on my calf breaking my bone. I turned around and sliced through but he ducked and delivered a punch on my solar plexus. \"What?\" but this time it was his turn to be surprised. His punch hit something solid but he couldn't see what. I saw the opportunity and swung my sword. But all I sliced was empty air. \"Shit, He's fast\" I muttered. He moved behind, there was confusion in his eyes. The scarred man instantly appeared in front of me this time. I took the stance to swing my sword but delayed it for one second. His great sword blurred, I counted \"One\" in my mind and dissolved the wall of emptiness. I swung my weapon but instead of expected trajectory I swung my sword in a low horizontal arc. When I finally had the time to process, I saw him at a distance. His leg was bleeding. \"You….\" His eyes narrowed. I took a defensive stance. \"....\" He relaxed and held his great sword with both hands this time and took a proper stance. His eyes closed and a green glow enveloped him. My hairs stood and chills went throughout the body. I side stepped The wall of emptiness broke like a glass and a big scar formed on the ground beside me. I made the mistake of staring at it for a second. The moment I turned my head, a terrible force slammed into my skull. \"LORD DANIELLL-\"Sir Graham tried to come towards me. \"Not so fast old man\" the guy he was fighting engaged with him in swordplay again. He was bleeding from several places but was still in the condition to fight. Others did not fared well, young knights were being pushed back in terms of raw skill from experienced mercenaries. I flew for some distance before meeting the soft surface of wet grass. My head was spinning, eyes blurred, the sky was now almost fully dark. My ears were ringing and I became completely deaf from my left ear. I felt someone standing near me. Even without clear vision I could tell who he was. But then another person appeared in my frame. \"Kill him, don't worry no one will find his dead body here\" I heard the scarred man saying that to his younger brother. I tried to desperately stand up. \"Khuggghh\" But the strength of his leg intensified. \"The more you resist the more painful it will be for you\" I heard. I wanted to say something but someone grabbed my hair and forced my face upwards. I saw the man from the inn smiling. His one hand wrapped in cloth and medicines. He held a small axe in his good hand. He raised his axe and smiled. However just then a black panel appeared in front of me. I blinked once and saw it more clearly. **[Total Cellular Transformation: 100%]** **[Initiating Stellar Physique…..]**"},{"t":"40. Vessel of the Stars (I)","k":"Chronicles · chapter","u":"chronicles/c40.html","s":"**[Hayato's POV]** My eyes were revising the policy papers and data for the negotiation 14th time now, imagining different scenarios with a variety of questions from the crown. If this negotiation failed, Parshara Kingdom may not survive another year. The sky was of dark orange color now. Then I heard a scream, I looked out of the window to see Arin frantically running towards the escort guards. His screaming alerted them as they unmounted from their horses and unsheathed their swords. I too descended from the carriage and saw 10 men on the horses coming towards us. Each one of them armed except one. The scarred man at the front descended and asked the man for identification whom Arin beat at the inn. He threatened us to handover Arin or die. Escorting guards instantly clashed with them at that. Sir Graham was faring well due to his experience and vast power. He was enveloped in a mysterious brown glow, whenever the opponent's swords touched him, they never left more than a scratch. It was as if his durability has been magnified a hundred fold. The other two guys who fought him were enveloped in a blue glow, their flexibility increased, they were able to endure the sheer strength of Sir Graham's sword which had such power that whenever their swords clashed a small crater would be formed under their feet. My eyes couldn't believe what they were seeing, they were equivalent to what would be known as a first grade Elite on Earth. Other escorting knights weren't that adept. They were barely keeping up with the thugs and were being pushed back. **THWACK!** A single loud sound attracted everyone's attention. I turned my head without thinking and saw Arin flying like a thrown rag doll. The scarred man turned towards the man at the inn and blurred appearing near him, grabbed his shoulder and their image blurred appearing near Arin. I panicked and saw Hassan hidden on the opposite side of carriage covering his ears. I didn't had any power to fight but at that time my instincts won against my logic. I freed a horse and mounted over it. The horse ran, catching speed towards the man at the inn who raised his axe. The scarred man turned, his eyes bright with murderous intent. He turned fully, held his great sword and closed his eyes, the strange green glow enveloped him. My thoughts screamed at me to jump off the horse, my hairs stood up and tinnitus intensified, my body recognized the death in front of me. My palms were sweating I was about to jump but just then- **\"KEEP GOING, BE NOT AFRAID\"** a deep majestic voice of immeasurable authority sang in my head. The scarred man raised his sword, the green glow intensifying in his meditation. I was almost in front of him. He was going to swing his sword- But a small pebble, a rock half the size of a fist hit him square in the face interrupting his meditation. I turned my neck around only to see Hassan holding a slingshot while his legs protested against standing. In that short moment where his meditation broke I rushed past him and grabbed the hair of the inn man the moment his axe came down and dragged him. \"Arghhhhhh\" he shrieked in pain. It must have been just a couple of seconds that the sky and the ground exchanged places in my vision. I was violently thrown off the horse hitting the ground and skidding a bit ahead. I saw the horse crying lying by its side, bleeding profusely from the place where should have been its legs. **Squelch! Slosh!..** Sound of heels hitting the soft grass came from my right. I slowly turned my head only two see two red glowing eyes. \"It seems you wish to ascend to heaven faster, old man\" the scarred man raised his sword. The guillotine descended I closed my eyes expecting death. However **THWACK! CRUNCH!** **\"**Don't worry Hayato-san, now I will handle him\" I heard a completely unexpected voice. I opened my eyes and saw two grey eyes in front of me. \"Arin?\" I barely mustered up my voice. He nodded and turned around while a grey glow enveloped him."},{"t":"41. Vessel of Stars (II)","k":"Chronicles · chapter","u":"chronicles/c41.html","s":"**[Dara's POV]** **\"**When he saw salamander behind him he ran like crazy\" said Tanni \"Pfft- what\" one of my group members laughed while spilling his ale on the ground trying to contains his laughter. The glowing stones filled the room with a dim orange glow. Fire place kept the room warm. My fellow group members were wasted on jugs of ale and plates of crisp potato slices. I too listened to stories, our former captain or my dear friend and mentor Tanni told us maybe twentieth time, but hey in between friends the time spent matters not the bullshit going on the table. After sometime when all of them fell asleep on the table I slowly got up, took the plates and was about to move to kitchen when the door of my room opened and my younger brother entered crying. I turned my head and saw not his face but his arm which was twisted in the wrong direction, turning purple in color. **CRASH!** The plates fell from my hand, producing a loud shattering noise and waking up everyone. \"W-wha happened?\" Tanni instantly grabbed the handle of his sword. Then he saw me and turned his head, his eyes widened \"How did this happen?\" he stood up. Now everyone turned towards him and took him to the table and asked. He began to recount everything, my knuckles tightened as time passed. \"All of you get ready\" my blood was boiling. I was about to march straight to that inn and kill that son of a bitch. \"Calm down for now-\" Tanni put his hand on my shoulder. \"HOW AM I SUPPOSED TO-\" I tried to argue. \"If I heard it right, then he is not any normal bastard\" Tanni advised me. I took a deep breath and put my anger aside for a moment. Meanwhile the other guys took my younger brother inside and treated his injury. \"What do we do then?\" I asked Tanni. Tanni smiled and began to unfold the masterful plan he had come with. I had taken care of my younger brother since he was eight years old, A bit behind normal people in terms of brains but my only relative. Our father, a local mercenary died when I was just twelve and two years after that, mom ran away with another man. It was at fifteen years of age that I began to notice some weird things. I could feel the wind, not through senses but rather could see through it, could feel it becoming one with me. The leader of a famous mercenary group and my mentor Tanni found me when I fought with one of his group members over a meagre sum of 20 copper coins and absolutely overpowered him. That's when he took me as an apprentice and began to nurture my talent. \"You are blessed by spirits\" Tanni told me about my weird experience. \"Nonethless the spirit of wind\" He smiled. Soon I surpassed everyone in our group and made a name for myself in principality and lands far and wide. Money was there, friends were there, a good life was there but my brother who was to serve at the frostgate hold had to go there for 5 years as a mandatory service after turning 16. When he came back safe and sound I was overjoyed and threw a party. The life began to get on the track until today when he came back crying and an arm ruined. The plan was to silently follow them till the meadows outside the border appeared and to launch an ambush, kill them all and take care of corpses and then drag the girl to my younger brother's bed. Like that there will be no consequences since those meadows were an unclaimed territory and the wet grass will act as a good cover to hide corpses. A royal carriage would fetch a decent price even from a small kingdom like of Parshara's. This was the plan until now that is **THWACK! CRUNCH!** The feeling of lower jaw vanished and only pain remained, I lost the grip on my great sword flying for a couple of meters. I quickly stood up adjusting my posture. The blessing of wind spirit enveloped me and physical world dissolved The world slowed, feeling of senses expanding and in that field only for a fraction of second I felt a presence at the edge and the next moment that presence was in front of me. I raised my arm to block whatever was coming. **WHACK!** A kick on my left upper arm, but the force was so terrible that the sheer strength sent me flying off my feet. I hadn't even touched the ground but the presence was already upon me. I crossed my arms to block. But instead the presence grabbed my leg and slammed me into the ground. **BOOM!** **\"**khaarggghh**\"** a wet gargle escaped my mouth and stars began to dance in my vision. I sat upright my hands trembling and saw, two grey eyes. The man whom my brother should have killed already stood before me. A strange white grey glow enveloped him. I have seen red, blue, green and brown glow countless of times, but a white glow was a first. *'The blessings of a spirit king?'* the thought surfaced but was quickly rejected. If it would have been a spirit king I would still have been at least able to put up a fight, but I was overpowered by a large margin so whatever that is. Is on par with the one blessed by a spirit king. I was thinking but just then something came bouncing in my lap. I felt something warm and wet in my hands. It was the head of my younger brother. He looked scared. My mind blanked, grief and madness overran my thoughts. \"NOOOOOOOOOOOOOOOOOOOO\" I screamed. I stood up and began my final act as a big brother. Wind began to cover me. \"I'LL KILLLL YOOUUUUUUUUUUUU\" my nerves swelled as I screamed. Without caring for my body I jumped. I punched **BOOM!** His feet trembled. I gave a jab as a feint and then kneed his abdomen. He jumped backward trying to lessen the impact. *'An opening'* I thought and pushed in inside. \"Don't\" I heard Tanni scream but I believed in myself and stepped in front of him my hand blade ready to hear him apart. The moment I moved my hand blade. He looked up and smiled \"Got you\" he whispered. Before however I could understand his palms striked the sides of my head. My eardrums ruptured. \"Shit\" I cursed while my sense of direction collapsed and a moment after that I lost the feeling of my torso as my vision spun. *'I am sorry little bro, I couldn't take your revenge'*"},{"t":"42. Old Rhymes","k":"Chronicles · chapter","u":"chronicles/c42.html","s":"In front of me lay the headless corpse of the scarred man. The sword of emptiness dissolved in my hands returning to oblivion and I looked around to see the faces of enemy which had gone pale. After a while later Sir Graham came to me \"My lord we are done\" he said in a low voice. I turned just a bit to see 8 more corpses join the scarred man and his brother. \"Let us leave\" I said in a flat tone. \"Lord Daniel\" Sir Graham spoke. \"Hmm?\" I stopped \"......\" \"......\" I turned when Sir Graham didn't say anything. I looked in his eyes which were dead serious. \"Can we get an explanation for earlier?\" he finally said. All the escort guards and even Hayato-san began to look at me with expectations. I sighed \"When he was about to kill me, I heard a voice\" I lied continuing \"After that time slowed and I felt as if I am sucking air from countless noses\" I said. For some reason Hayato-san's eyes widened. \"C-could it be the blessings of a spirit king?\" one of the escorting guards began to tremble with excitement. Sir Graham moved his gaze towards the young soldier which made him freeze and then looked at me again. \"Whatever it was-\" he bowed \"Thank you for saving my men\" I was flustered by such an action. \"Eh? Please lift your head Sir Graham I only did what I should\" At my reaction everyone present there smiled. \"Halt\" said a man in a steel armor with a different badge on his chest this time. We showed them the papers and insignia of Parshara Kingdom. Hearthglen was the biggest hub for scholars. All the history texts which remained intact were stored here. Every form of knowledge was available here, It would not be wrong to say that knowledge was the currency here. \"Parshara?\" the knight guard seemed to be confused. He stayed buffering straight up for 5 minutes. Then the other guard whispered to him. \"Aren't those the ones who still worship that old god?\" he said *'Hey! I heard you'* I wanted to say but due to being short on time anyway I avoided it. We entered Hearthglen. The air was warm and dry, absolutely opposite of Vaeldor principality. The road was made up of smooth stone arranged neatly. We were welcomed by the smell of ink and paper the moment we entered. There were a variety of shops and buildings made up of bricks and cobblestone. \"Still no drawings and decorations\" I heard Hayato-san say that next to me. \"That will be 2 silver for a night stay and 1 silver for the meals included\" the receptionist grinned. I paid in advance and took the keys to our rooms, 1 room for 2 people each, that made it 5 rooms. The moon was up in the sky, lunar light colored the whole room in dim blue. I sat upright slowly, moved towards the table with soundless steps meanwhile Hassan changed position countless times in his sleep. I took a thin woolen blanket from the table and wrapped it around my face. Changed into simpler clothes and opened the window. Cold air rushed past me. I took a deep breath, held the edges of the window and put one feet on the edges ready to jump out. The time slowed and a warm flow began to seep into my nerves and muscles. I jumped onto the roof of the opposite building. The world dissolved partially. My body felt light and my senses cleared. I opened my eyes and saw far away a kid pestering his mom for a board game. I took a run and jumped as far as I could. I crossed 4 buildings in one jump landing in an alley this time. I walked out of the alley and began to explore the city. Many shops were closed and some prepared to close soon. Only a few people wandered around the street. Some people slept on the side of the road probably beggars. *Tap! Tap! Tap!...* My steps echoed on the empty footpath while I looked lazily browsed the items in the shops. *Tap! Tap! Ta!-....* My steps came to a stop. I reverse geared a couple of steps and saw an old damaged book lying on a roadside stall. The books lay on a cloth and a man with a huge brown cowboy hat dozed off by the wall. The dim yellow light from the glowing stone flickered highlighting the title of the book. '**Old Rhymes for Children**' I looked or rather stared at a certain page to which the book lay opened. *The First Doctrine* It was a small poem. I touched the rough, worn out page and turned it. The next page however was missing. \"Many pages are missing from that book\" an old and hoarse voice interrupted my thoughts. I looked up to see the dozing old man looking at me, caressing his grey and dirty beard. \"You seem to like that book quite a lot\" he smiled. *No* I wanted to say \"Yes\" the reply however came on its own before I could stop it. He extended his hands and made a sign representing 'one'. \"1 copper coin\" he said. I never wanted to buy the book, but at that time I didn't know why, but my heart urged me to find a copper piece urgently and put it in the hands of that old man. I desperately tried to find the copper coins in my pockets or anywhere but I couldn't. *'**Strange, I had some money on me while leaving the inn, I am sure'* I stopped realizing I was broke at the moment. *'I don't have money old man.' I was about to say and move on* But my legs froze and refused to move anywhere. The old man's smile widened eerily as if he knew all along. His wrinkled hands grabbed one end of the book, lifted it and put it in my hands which obeyed a foreign will. \"Take it\" he said. \"I can't, thi-\"I tried to raise my voice at the actions of the old man. \"Think of it as a loan, given by a poor old soul to a capable young soul\" he continued interrupting me. \"If however you don't like it, consider repaying later with interest to elevate the status of this poor old man\" he smiled, his blackened teeth on full display. His sunset eyes shining brighter than ever. He gave a ridiculous smile, an exaggerated smile, a tired smile. When the book touched my skin, my heart swelled and a childlike happiness settled in my heart. It was as if I had found something for which I had craved from the very beginning. He finally stood up. Tied the four corners of the cloth on which his whole daily bread depended and walked towards some unknown destination. His naked feet made no sound or as if the world itself forgot about him. I asked at last \"Can I know your name?\" The old man turned and said \"My name does not matter however, if you insist you may address me as Noizea\" The man's shape changed into a shadowy sillhoute and finally dissolved in the dark night."},{"t":"43. Knowledge and the Ignorance","k":"Chronicles · chapter","u":"chronicles/c43.html","s":"**Awaken, the fruit of my Divinity,** See far with the vision thee carry. A vision that embraces even the vices that hath been created, Only in such a vision shall you find which thee seeketh. Open your eyes, symbol of my love, The last bastion of hope, Proud child of Eldoris. \"You don't seem alright\" Hayato-san whispered to me at the breakfast table. I nodded as I struggled to keep my eyelids from being closed. I couldn't sleep last night pondering on the words of the poem. I took a bite of vegetables and grilled fish. It was spicy and sweet at the same time. I took the bowl which lay near my elbow and gulped down the green water in it. \"Who knew vegetable soup could taste so good\" I heard Hayato-san whisper to himself sitting beside me. I heard low footsteps and wooden floor creak behind me. I turned around. No one I turned back but something caught my eye. A pair of little hands and two big eyes protruding from below our table. Those eyes stared at me for couple of seconds and then changed their line of sight, I followed them and saw it eyeing the leftover food. *Ah!* A smile made onto my lips. \"Are you hungry?\" I asked. The boy looked at me again but didn't say anything. Seeing me talking to the boy Hayato san and the escorting soldiers stopped chatting. \"Didn't I tell you to not even come close to the inn?\" simultaneously with a scary voice I saw a shadow covering the place where the boy stood. I looked up to see the receptionist lady with a broom looking at the boy. I swear I saw veins popping out of her forehead. \"Why did you come here again?\" the receptionist lady asked or rather threatened the little boy. The boy didn't say anything, however he grabbed the edges of the one piece cloth he was wearing, that too was full of patches. He lowered his head. \"I will count to 3, before that run away\" she said. \"1\" Other guests stopped eating and began to turn towards the commotion. \"2\" the receptionist lifted her broom. Some looked at the boy with pity, some ignored after a glance and started eating again. \"3\" the countdown finished. The boy still didn't move. Seeing that the receptionist lady swung the broom with as much force as possible, the boy shut his eyes. But the broom never came down. \"ENOUGH!\" I raised my voice while she desperately tried to free the edge of the broom from my hand. \"Respected sir, you have only come for today.\" She gulped choosing her words carefully. \"This brat comes everyday and…..\" she stopped. \"And….?\" I caught the word. She bit into her lower lip and looked on the ground. \"And disturbs the guests\" she said. \"....\" I kept quiet letting more flow out of her. \"The guests complain as they go\" she spoke again, eyeing my reaction. \"....\" \"I-I thought….\" She stammered. I sighed. The boy still kept his head down, enduring whatever insults she threw about him. I looked at the boy first, there was no reaction, just a quiet fact of acceptance. His clothes were filled with patches, dirt was stuck in his nails and his hair was also messy, He may have been 5 or 6. His arms were thin, more than a child should have. I looked at the lady, who had stopped speaking and waited for the verdict to arrive. The guests were all looking at us now. I closed my eyes and opened them as the world partially dissolved. **\"**IS THERE A CIRCUS GOING ON HERE?**\"** my voice pressed on everyone. **CRASH!...THUD!…** Some fell to ground gasping for air. Most of them paled and stared at their plates like it was the most interesting thing they have ever seen. Meanwhile some near exit ran away. Even the receptionist fell on her knees trembling \"M-m-mercy\" she squeaked. The child just turned his head not knowing what was happening. I scanned through my gaze as the other guests started to mind their own business. **'Sheeesh, I was so cool right now!'** I desperately tried to suppress the grin from showing up on my face. I looked at the receptionist lady and helped her stand. \"No, Your little hands won't be able to lift this much\" I said to the little boy who stood in front of me with his hands spread open towards the big cloth bag full of packed food. At my words he quietly receded his hands and began to walk somewhere, we silently followed. He kept walking and walking and walking. Changing lanes, going through alleys and above the walls alike. I saw a stray cat fall from the wall just because we casually jumped over it. Just when I was about to speak he stopped. I looked at the place only to see a small hut. I looked around, the whole colony was like that, the houses were made up of mud instead of bricks and stones. The roofs were made up of hay supported by wooden columns and logs instead of stone. The air smelled of soap and wet dirt. I also saw an old lady washing clothes, one man preparing his cart on which lay polish and brush of many colors. Some kids in a one piece cloth playing on a mountain of sand, lost in their own happiness. \"So even knowledge couldn't erase discrimination\" I muttered while pondering on the fact that this city was called 'City of Wisdom'. **Creakkk!**... I heard the hinges cry as I looked towards the hut. The little boy opened the door and we saw a woman, a fairly young malnourished woman lying on a single bed. Her lips were dry, she looked older than she probably was, her cheeks were sunken and dark circles settled under her eyes giving her a tired look. The room or the house which amounted to that single room was simple, an earthen pot at the right hand of the room, a platform of dried earth on which a bowl of water lay under which burnt wood rested. A one piece old wooden table with three drawer sections and a small cupboard. On the floor lay some papers with half drawn drawings and crude writing as if someone was trying to write. The sleeping woman opened her eyes and turned her head. Her expression relaxed as she saw the little boy but then, her eyes found us. Her face went from Relaxed to confusion to horror in a span of a second."},{"t":"44. AUTHOR'S NOTE","k":"Chronicles · chapter","u":"chronicles/c44.html","s":"Yo!, I am the writer of 'Eidolon Earth: Candidate Author'. For those who have been following till now thankyou very much!. It means a lot and an announcement to make. I myself am very dissatisfied with the writing quality I have right now but from now on the chapters will have at least 2000 words. It means more description, longer, and correct grammar. Obviously chapter will be written by me but grammatical errors may be corrected by AI. neverthless I will review it everytime and chapter frequency may get less but I will upload multiple chapter at once. Please this is a request to all of you to comment at every chapter and tell me how it is and what did you like. You may not know but it will helps your bro improve. Cya till then."},{"t":"45. The Letter","k":"Chronicles · chapter","u":"chronicles/c45.html","s":"Her face went from relaxed to confused to horrified in the span of a second. \"Bholu!\" she practically shouted. The little boy ran towards his mother after being called and stopped near her hand. The frail woman raised her hand, which was trembling slightly, and started twisting his ear. The little boy grabbed his mother's hand and desperately tried to get away while his face contorted in pain. \"WHAT DID YOU STEAL?\" Her voice began to rise. Meanwhile, the rest of them standing there felt their hair rise, reminded once again of the trauma of a mother's anger. . . . \"He didn't steal anything?\" the frail woman asked in disbelief, and perhaps with some hope, even after being told so for the third time. \"Yes, we came here to deliver this packed food,\" Hayato-san said. \"W-we cannot accept such generosity,\" she tried to argue. \"If I had been in your place, I would have gratefully accepted, young lady.\" Arin turned his head to see Sir Graham standing with his arms crossed. \"Huh?\" she seemed confused. Seeing that she didn't understand the situation, Sir Graham came towards her. His heavy armor made a weighty sound with every step, his greying hair tied back in a ponytail. He looked at Hayato-san and then at Arin before continuing, \"The one who decides what the receiver receives is the giver itself.\" She stared blankly at his face. He sighed and then said, \"Look not at what is being offered, but at who offers it.\" He then looked at Arin and said, \"That young man over there is the Royal Messenger of the Kingdom of Parshara.\" Then he looked at Hayato-san. \"The old man sitting beside you is the Royal Scholar, who is responsible for preserving the very identity of Parshara — its culture and traditions.\" The woman was at a loss for words and remained seated for a minute. \"I-I…\" She looked for the right words; perhaps the situation was too much to take in. \"L-let me see too,\" came a few shrill, young voices, slicing through the serious atmosphere. They turned only to see three pairs of big eyes peeking from behind the wall of the hut. When Arin saw them, they blinked once, twice. \"Aww, they caught us,\" came the voice, and soon three little figures emerged from behind the wall. In single-piece cloth dresses stood three children, maybe five or six years old — two boys and one girl with brown pigtails. Their eyes were shining as they looked at the escort guards. One of the boys slowly came closer and poked at the steel-hard armor, his mouth drooling. \"It's… real,\" he yelped in awe. The other boy went towards Sir Graham. He stared at his sword for a second, took a deep breath, and then asked, \"Can you teach me how to wield a sword, sir?\" Sir Graham looked at him for a moment and said, \"I am afraid I cannot.\" The boy's shoulders dropped. \"But I can show you some moves, if you're interested,\" Sir Graham suggested. The boy's eyes lit up again as he followed Sir Graham out. That interaction made all of them smile. The little girl walked towards the frail woman. \"How are you, Aunty?\" she asked, eyeing the packed food on the table. At that moment, Arin felt a touch on his lap. He looked down only to see Bholu trying to put a small letter on his lap. \"What's this?\" Arin asked, taking the letter in his hand. \"BHOLU! Take it back!\" his mother screamed, but Bholu hid behind him. The letter was plain — no sigils, no symbols. Even the paper was of low quality, and cheap ink had been used. It was sealed with wax that had since hardened. \"What is this?\" Arin asked again. The frail woman replied in a low voice, \"I am… a postwoman,\" she continued. \"I travel once a week to deliver letters to Tilmis City — the one at the border of Nazarana Kingdom,\" she said. Then she bowed. \"I am ashamed that my son did something like this. Please consider his actions a child's carelessness.\" Arin listened to her and looked at the letter. \"Well, since we are already going to Nazarana, we might as well deliver this along the way,\" he told her, tucking the letter into his pocket. At that, she looked up — her jaw dropped and her eyebrows raised. She tried to stand while attempting to argue. \"No! How can I let such esteemed guests do my lowly work?\" She almost stood, but Bholu ran and hugged her — even though he could only reach her legs, he managed to stop her from standing. She glared at him, but he stood his ground. Seeing that, warmth filled Arin's chest. *Yeah, I would've done the same if I were him.* His hand went to Bholu's head. \"He's a strong kid,\" he said, smiling. Everyone agreed. The door creaked again, and Sir Graham entered. \"Looks like I missed something interesting,\" Sir Graham commented. The boy who had gone outside with him returned as well, the light in his eyes brighter than before. Arin stood up, searching for something in his pocket. \"I would have liked to know more about this place, but it looks like we need to depart now.\" Simultaneously with his words, Hayato-san stood up as well. The frail woman stood, grabbing the edge of the bed, and tried to walk them to the door, only to be stopped by Sir Graham. The children pleaded with them to stay longer, but since they couldn't afford to waste any more time here — given the worsening conditions of the Kingdom — they left. After Arin closed the door of the little hut, he signaled the guards to follow him. The world partially dissolved, and the stored energy in his cells began to course through his veins as he sprinted. Sir Graham, who was enveloped in a brown glow, ran beside him. \"May I know the reason why we are running, Lord Daniel?\" Arin simply smiled and said, \"Sometimes forcing your goodwill on someone is better than waiting for them to accept it.\" Sir Graham seemed not to understand but still followed behind him, carrying Hayato-san on his back. . . . The frail woman stood by the doorway long after the sound of boots had faded, watching the little wooden gate at the edge of the yard swing shut on its own, creaking once before it settled. Only then did her knees allow themselves to shake. She turned, and her eyes fell on Bholu, who was still watching the empty path with a satisfied little smile, as if he had personally escorted royalty out of their home. \"Bholu.\" Her voice was quiet this time — quieter than the shout that had sent him running earlier — and somehow that was worse. He turned to face her, his smile faltering. \"Do you understand what you did?\" she said. \"You put a letter into a stranger's hand. A stranger who wears armor and carries swords.\" Bholu looked at her innocently. \"They were nice,\" she agreed, and knelt slowly, wincing as her joints protested. \"This time. This time, child, we were fortunate — a good man happened to be standing in our yard. But what if he hadn't been good? What if it had been someone who took your carelessness as an insult, or worse, as an opportunity? Do you know what happens to boys who trouble the wrong stranger?\" Bholu said nothing. His lower lip trembled, and she softened, pulling him into her arms. She held him a moment longer, then let him go and pushed herself up, using the doorframe for support. Her stomach chose that exact moment to twist itself into a knot, and she pressed a hand against it, willing it to be silent. No. I will not touch what was given out of pity. The thought came proud and immediate, the way it always did, and she almost believed it. But her legs were unsteady beneath her, and the room had a way of tilting slightly whenever she stood too fast these days, and she knew — better than her pride ever would admit — that there was no strength left in her to spare on stubbornness. It was not pity. He had said so himself. The young man beside him decides what he offers, and it was the man who decided, not her. She was only the receiver. Some final piece of resistance in her gave way at that, and she turned back into the hut. The children were already crowded around the table, small hands hovering over the cloth-wrapped bundles, waiting for permission they hadn't dared to ask for out loud. She began unwrapping the packages, and the smell that rose from them — of warm rice, spiced lentils, something sweet beneath it all — made her own mouth water embarrassingly fast. She counted with her eyes before she counted with her hands. There was too much here for one sitting, too much even for one day. This would last two days, perhaps more if she was careful with it. Whoever had packed this had not simply fed them a meal; they had fed them a fortnight's worth of worry away. \"Eat,\" she told the children, setting portions before them. \"Slowly. There is enough.\" She did not serve herself yet. She watched them eat first. It was only when she began folding the empty cloth wrappings that she noticed it: a small square of fabric left forgotten on the table, tucked half beneath the food that had rested on it. She picked it up, and her fingers knew immediately that it did not belong to her — the weave was too fine, too smooth, the kind of cloth that had never known a hard day's work. A handkerchief, its corner stitched with a thread so pale it was almost silver. She turned it over once in her hands, frowning, then hurried to the door, calling out — she wasn't sure what she meant to say, only that it should be returned. But the yard was empty. The gate stood still. Whatever brown glow had carried them away had long since vanished past the bend in the road, and there was no one left to give it back to. She stood there for a moment, foolish, holding a stranger's handkerchief in the middle of an empty yard. With a sigh, she pressed it between her palms, meaning only to fold it properly before setting it aside for safekeeping — and stopped. Something inside it had clinked. Her heart gave an odd little stumble. Slowly, she unfolded the cloth, layer by careful layer, until five small silver coins slid into her open palm and caught the last of the evening light. She stared at them for a long, long moment, and then, without quite meaning to, she began to laugh — a soft, disbelieving sound, half a sob. \"Look not at what is offered,\" she murmured to no one, echoing the armored man's words, \"but at who offers it.\""},{"t":"46. Nazarana Kingdom","k":"Chronicles · chapter","u":"chronicles/c46.html","s":"\"Fine, go ahead,\" the guard said, shoving the papers back into Arin's hands. His dark blue armor shone in the warm sunlight. The huge golden gates lifted as their small carriage passed through. The walls were thick, made of smooth white stone, and guards walked the passage carved into them. They entered as the smell of spices and food hit Arin's nose. Stalls of food and merchant carts lined the way. Instead of glowing stones, there were lamp posts. The road was wide and grounded, as if carved from a single long stone. Arin could see monuments and signboards in different languages marking the routes toward different cities. Checkpoints stood along the road, each staffed by two people managing traffic. After a while, the asphalt-colored road split into different directions. They turned toward the sign that read *Tilmis*. Traffic was heavy, and when they stopped behind a large merchant cart, a boy approached the carriage with blankets draped over his torso. \"Five for thirty copper coins!\" he shouted. Arin gazed at his youthful face for a moment before the traffic moved forward again. They passed a giant wheel in the distance, and children with wide smiles and plump bodies. Arin closed his eyes, and the faces of thin, hollow-cheeked servants flashed in his mind. His breath caught for a moment before he let out a long sigh. Even the outskirts weren't empty — buildings and villages were scattered along the way. At last, they arrived at the city of Tilmis. It was livelier than any city they had visited so far. Banners bearing different symbols hung from the buildings, and the streets were thick with people. The shops were larger here, stocked with fruits they had never seen in their lives. Medicines and countless other goods filled the stalls. But their destination lay at the center of Tilmis, near the governor's residence. A small bell chimed as Arin opened the gate of the post office. The hall inside was enormous. Numerous counters lined the walls, each attended by a different clerk. Lamps spilled their light across the huge space. Staff not assigned to counters ran back and forth, carrying piles of letters and pots of ink. The hall was full. Generations sat together — a family with two children and a couple, old men and women in strange clothing. The men mostly wore dark robes with jeweled necklaces; the women wore thick blouses with full-length skirts. Small children ran about, only to be scolded moments later. The interior felt strangely cold, the opposite of the heat outside. Arin walked up to the first counter, where an old woman with glasses sat. He took the letter from his breast pocket. But before he could hand it to her — *Clack. Clack. Clack!* Someone shoved him from behind, sending him stumbling sideways. Arin grabbed the corner of the counter to keep his balance and turned to see who had done it. It was a young man, shorter than average, glancing around before speaking in a surprisingly deep voice. \"D-did my letter arrive?\" the man asked, gasping for air. Arin studied him — messy brown hair, amber eyes locked on the counter lady like an eagle's. Seeing the height difference, Arin's irritation faded, replaced by an odd sense of kinship with his own unremarkable stature back on Earth. The woman at the counter made a sour face. \"Sir, we haven't received a letter like that. If we had, I would have been informed,\" she said, maintaining her politeness. Something in the man seemed to snap. His voice climbed within seconds. \"Is it the letter, or is it that none of you know how to do your job?\" he accused, teeth clenched. The woman raised an eyebrow but kept her tone level. \"Everyone here knows how to do their job, sir. But we can't help it if a letter grows wings and flies off on its own.\" Her words dripped with mockery. The man's hand curled into a fist — a punch he might have thrown had the other party not been a woman. \"DO YOU THINK I—\" he started, but stopped when he noticed Arin quietly sliding a letter across the counter shield. The clerk noticed too, glancing sideways. Arin stiffened. \"Uh... hello?\" Silence. More silence. \"Don't mind me,\" Arin said awkwardly, trying to set the letter down on the attendant's desk. In a blink, the brown-haired man's hand shot out and snatched the letter from his grip — faster, Arin thought, than the scarred man from before. The man inspected it, then tore it open. For a moment no one spoke. His expression relaxed, and he tucked the letter into his pocket. \"I have what I wanted,\" he said, bowing his head slightly toward Arin. \"May I know your good name, sir?\" \"Daniel,\" Arin said. The man nodded, gave him a long look, and walked out of the hall. The clerk let out a sigh and turned to Arin. \"You really saved the day, good sir,\" she said with a small bow. \"It was nothing. Now, if you'll excuse me,\" Arin said, turning to leave. The bell chimed again as his feet left the marble floor and met the dirt and stone outside. \"Let's go,\" he said, sitting down across from Hayato-san, who was studying a map to guide their route. \"So this is Lupenz, huh,\" Arin muttered as the carriage passed through the city gates. Overhead, birds soared — but they were massive, each one nearly the size of the carriage. Their shadows swept across the ground below. \"This is the first time I've seen them,\" Sir Graham murmured under his breath. \"Oh! I've only heard about those — griffins, aren't they?\" one of the escort guards exclaimed, awestruck. Arin looked up at the clear blue sky, taking in creatures he had only ever read about in fantasy novels back in his previous life. The buildings here were more elaborate, and the road sloped gently upward. Threads strung between buildings crossed over the streets, hung with lanterns and banners. The architecture and city planning were far more refined. Shops stood open, and people filled the streets. Their carriage drew a bit of attention, though it faded quickly. Ahead of them stood a massive statue — a robed figure, its face hidden beneath a deep hood that concealed its eyes. Only the nose and the faint curve of a smirk were visible. Its hands were joined in front, forming an open hemisphere, cradling something within. The statue was tall, its surface webbed with faint cracks that betrayed its age. The stone had lost its luster, but the figure still carried an air of majesty. The plaque at its base read: **[ Protector of the Land of Kuru ]** **Lord Azevalgar** \"Arin, can you ask someone for directions?\" Hayato-san said. Arin nodded and stepped down from the carriage, taking in the city square as his boots echoed against the stone platform. He walked toward a jewelry shop and picked up a gemstone to examine. \"Is anyone there?\" he asked. The shop owner, a chubby man with a jolly demeanor, turned and smiled at him. \"My apologies for not noticing you, sir,\" he said, rubbing his hands together. \"I believe you're looking for a gemstone rather than a finished ornament?\" His eyes flicked to the small blue gem in Arin's palm. \"Ah, I was just wondering,\" Arin said, turning the gem over in his hand, \"where such a beautiful material might have been excavated from?\" The man's smile widened. \"As expected of a distinguished guest,\" he said, adjusting his kaftan. \"Wise men value the source more than the product itself.\" His speech had a slightly flat cadence, as though he struggled with certain sounds. \"The gentleman before me seems rather knowledgeable himself,\" Arin remarked casually. \"Of course,\" the shopkeeper said, eyebrows lifting with pride. \"I come from a place that taught the world about craftsmanship.\" \"Oh? Then would you happen to know the way to Darjan?\" Arin asked. The man pointed across the square. \"That way.\" Arin nodded. It would have been rude to leave without buying anything. \"How much for this little blue gem?\" he asked, looking up — but the shopkeeper had already turned away. Arin peered past the counter and saw the man kneeling in prayer. \"I bow for thy blessings, our lord and savior Noor-e-Zaya,\" he murmured, bowing his head. Arin called out to him once more, loud enough to be heard, but the man didn't respond. He set thirty copper coins on the shelf among the other gems and ornaments on display, then returned to the carriage. \"Toward the right street,\" he said, pointing the way the shopkeeper had shown him. The carriage rolled forward. . . . In a certain room of an expensive inn. The windows were closed and covered with cloth. A man with brown hair and amber eyes entered, locked the room from inside, lit a candle and opened the letter with nothing but only destination written on the top corner. The letter seal was violently opened before so the actual letter lay inside unguarded. The man grabbed his hair and threw the wig away revealing his dark hair. He threw the coat away and sat in a simple attire. He carefully took out the letter while he felt his heart tremble. He was overjoyed. He opened the lid and took out a piece of paper from the letter. The paper was nothing special but still the man handled it as if it was the most precious thing there is. He breathed in and started reading. **To my Love…..**"},{"t":"47. Negotiations","k":"Chronicles · chapter","u":"chronicles/c47.html","s":"\"Finally,\" said the escorting guard, stretching his arms. Arin looked out of the window. Mountains of dirt rose in the distance like crude stairs, and people in cheap clothes worked among them, small and bent under the sun. \"Pass me a glass of water,\" a woman said to a young man nearby, her accent unfamiliar, the words translating themselves inside his head a heartbeat late, the way they always did. Beside him, Hayato-san had gone soft with sleep, using Arin's shoulder as his personal pillow and snoring faintly through it. It must have been late afternoon, but sun never budged nor the griffins, circling once more over the city of Darjan. There were no walls yet — only buildings, thrown up fast and close together, as if the city hadn't decided where it ended. Then far away two massive slabs of earth came into view. It could not be considered a castle — it was a fortress. An old mammoth of stone that seemed to have rose up from the earth by some divine command. Children ran through the streets in silk too fine. A small crowd gathered around a street performer while sipping tea like it cost nothing. Paintings, bolts of cloth, crops, and artifacts Arin couldn't name were laid out along the road like pebbles — because, he realized as the carriage rolled on and the buildings gave way to mansions each larger than the royal seat back in Parshara, that's exactly what they were here. Pebbles. Wind kicked up dirt, browning the whole view. Stalls, shops, a government building — all of it fell behind them in silence as the road widened. And then the walls arrived. Real walls this time, tall enough that Arin's heartbeat picked up just watching them grow closer. He shook Hayato-san's shoulder gently. \"Did we reach?\" the older man asked, rubbing his eyes. Arin only nodded, already bracing himself for his first practical exam. . . . \"This way, distinguished guests,\" said a butler with an immaculate smile Only Arin and Hayato-san were let through. The guards were told, politely, that their arrangements would be handled elsewhere. The inner gates opened without a sound. Beyond them lay a garden maintained with a precision that took years of knowledge and practice— roses of every color, vines climbing quartz pillars carved to look like they'd grown that way naturally, two fountains murmuring in the corners, grass cut even and still wet. Attendants moved among the flowers without once glancing at the visitors. At the garden's center stood a hemispherical dome, pale blue, catching light and throwing it back when looked at too directly. Eight pillars held it up. Beneath it, Arin could see, was a library — but the stairs beside the entrance led up, not in. They climbed. At the top, lamps hung from the curved ceiling, glowing faint blue and orange, and the wind somehow found its way inside despite the glass. Red carpet swallowed their footsteps. The air smelled sweet in a way Arin couldn't place. Grimoires and old books were stacked on a table near the front. Behind that table sat a woman. They stopped, and knelt. \"We, the ambassadors of Parshara Kingdom, greet Her Majesty, Ezra Ul Nazarana,\" they said together. The woman lifted her eyes and studied them, and Arin felt himself tremble under the gaze without quite deciding to. Her black hair was pulled into a ponytail that moved like it enjoyed being touched by the wind. Her eyebrows rose, sharp, assessing. Her eyes — lavender — held on Hayato-san for a few seconds, then moved to Arin. He didn't meet them but still knew that she had stripped him bare in her mind. \"You may look up,\" she said, the familiar western language strange in her eastern accent, and gestured for them to sit in the chairs the servants had already set out. They sat. Arin's spine met the cushion at an awkward angle he corrected a second too late. Ezra set down her feather pen. A servant swept the ink and papers aside for her. She crossed her legs, her red robe protesting the movement, and turned to face them fully. A low golden table appeared between them, loaded with drinks and sweets that made Arin's mouth water. He didn't touch any of it. Ezra's eyes locked onto Hayato-san. He gave the smallest nod to Arin. Arin looked up, and swallowed. \"We have come here to request cooperation between Parshara and Naza—\" \"Cooperation?\" She cut him off. She blinked once, and her voice reached him a beat after the blink. \"Cooperation is the term used when both parties are of equal footing, Mr. Emerald,\" she said, not bothering to ask his actual name. She glanced at the paper Hayato-san had set in front of her. \"Cooperation also demands an equal exchange of benefits between both parties.\" Her eyes moved down the page faster than Arin could track. \"But here, I only smell loss.\" She flicked the dossier back across the table. Arin had nothing to say to that. *I will never tell the system window about this,* Arin thought, *if I get out of here alive.* \"Our meaning of cooperation was that we'd like to support the Nazarana kingdom in this time of crisis,\" Hayato-san said, taking the weight of the room onto himself. \"I'm listening.\" \"Parshara will provide glowing stones and raw ore exclusively, at a reduced price — an amount reserved specifically for you.\" \"Oh?\" Ezra's smirk widened. \"Care to explain how this would *benefit* me?\" Arin stayed quiet, watching Hayato-san lay down the card they'd agreed to save for last. \"When we entered Nazarana, we saw the lamp posts lighting the streets after dark. That must burn through fuel quickly.\" \"It does,\" she said, smile still climbing. \"But that doesn't explain the benefit.\" \"The benefit is that by buying raw material from Parshara cheaply, Nazarana can stockpile its own resources and redirect them elsewhere.\" \"Continue.\" \"By agreeing, you save resources for more urgent purposes — and gain an ally who—\" He stopped himself. The servants in the room went still. Even Ezra looked mildly thrown by what he was about to claim. \"An ally? Against whom would I need an ally?\" she asked, seizing the word. \"Aurorian,\" Arin said quietly. Hearing that word servants gasped, soldiers tightened their grip around the weapons. The hair on his arms rose before he understood why. A blade — curved, cold — pressed flat against his throat, close enough to open it with one motion. The sharp edges stung. Arin stopped breathing due to sudden change in situation. \"Enough,\" Ezra said, and the blade was gone as fast as it had come, as if it had never been there at all. \"Bold of you,\" she said, \"to say that name here, of all places. It's practically taboo.\" She laughed, but something underneath the laugh didn't match it. She lifted her tea. A young servant filled Arin's cup. He took it by the handle and forgot, for one second, about the saucer — which fell and shattered against the floor. He set the cup down and knelt to gather the pieces. The moment his knees touched the ground, he stopped moving. Seconds passed. Hayato-san noticed first. \"What happened? Daniel?\" Ezra's fingers tightened around her own cup. Her brows drew together, watching him. Arin shook his head, forced his hands to keep moving. \"Just counting the pieces,\" he said, and handed the broken saucer to the waiting servant. The negotiation continued for another hour but Arin drank his tea and said nothing else. Eventually Ezra passed across a long sheet, ink still wet, the terms of the day set down in writing — the sigil of Nazarana stamped in the corner, proof of an alliance now real. Hayato-san read it over once, satisfied, and rolled it closed. \"Guide them to their residence,\" Ezra told the butler, whose smile had not moved once through the entire meeting. \"E-excuse me.\" Arin raised his hand. Every eye came back to him. \"C-can I stay with Humayu?\" \".......\" Silence. No one seemed to know what to do with the question. \"I'm... scared of the dark,\" he admitted, his face going redder with every second that passed. \"Pfft- what?\" this time Ezra laughed, genuinely and not the sly smile. . . . The room they were given was small. Not cramped, exactly — just small enough to feel like a quiet joke at their expense. White marble floor, walls in thick yellow, lamps set along the wall casting everything in the same tired gold. A fireplace sat in the left wall, and a king-sized bed occupied the corner nearest the window, opposite the main door. A polished wooden table held a stack of paper and a few ink pots, set across from the bed. Above the fireplace hung a deer's head, flanked by two artificial wings. A mirror faced the door from the far wall. \"I'm glad that went well,\" Hayato-san said, cheerful, dropping onto the bed. \"Honestly, I had very little hope of pulling it off.\" Arin said nothing. \"Had to use our trump card in the end, but at least the people back home won't go hungry.\" Still nothing. \"Hey.\" Hayato-san shook him lightly by the shoulder. \"What's wrong? You've been off for a while now.\" \"I'm not feeling well,\" Arin said, and shut himself in the washroom before anything else could be asked. . . . Starlight took over where the sun had left off. Little light remained anywhere except along the walls where soldiers patrolled, the castle's hallways, and the lampposts scattered across the nation beyond. From their window, Arin could see the land stretching out below and the castle's two walls — the outer one standing as a shield, the inner one working more like a veil, hiding whatever decisions the high-born made about the lives of the people they ruled. He shook Hayato-san awake. \"W—\" Before he could finish, Arin pressed a palm over his mouth and raised a finger to his own lips. He helped him up and guided him to the window without a sound. Outside, on the ground below their room, stood a handful of cloaked figures. They pulled back from the window at once, before they could be seen. Hayato-san grabbed a sheet of paper and scrawled something, then passed it over. *What's happening?* Arin wrote back: *The alliance was never real. They played along to capture* *us.* Hayato-san's eyes went wide. He wrote again: *I too thought the negotiation went too smoothly. But what would they gain by capturing us?* *I don't know**,* Arin wrote. *When I bent down for the saucer, I heard a voice telling Her Highness we could be used as disposable pawns to get \"that.\"* *What is 'that' and did you see who it was?* Arin folded the paper into his pocket and shook his head. *No*. He looked around for finding the way to escape. *Tap... Tap... Tap...* Just then Arin heard footsteps, too light, too nimble to be heard by normal people. As his world partially dissolved. He saw the cloaked figures staring at their window. *Tap...Tap…Tap…..* From behind the door the footsteps approached closer. He turned his head again and saw a faint glow of different colors emanating from each of them as they set their eyes like a hawk waiting for both of them like play. **'A DIVERSION….WE NEED A DIVERSION...'** Arin gnashed his teeth. His heartbeat increased. He knew that he may be able to fend all of the cloaked figures, but then everyone will be alerted. Right now they must first get out of enemy's stronghold. **Tap!…..Tap!….** Footsteps stopped right in front of the door and after a few second, the doorknob turned."},{"t":"48. Escape","k":"Chronicles · chapter","u":"chronicles/c48.html","s":"The doorknob turned and the gate opened without a sound. On outer side of the door in the golden lit hallway stood three figures. The butler from before whose smile still remained concrete on his face, beside him on either side stood a pair of man and a woman clad in a cloak different from the usual attire of servants in the palace. Their plan was to capture Arin and Hayato who were fast asleep but as they opened the door, they saw Arin standing in front of them. His black hair were disheveled and his eyes struggled to remain open. Arin stepped forward before glancing at them. “What happened?” Arin asked in a tired voice as he rubbed his eyes. The butler’s smiling façade trembled a bit before again settling into the original. “Oh my, Please pardon the intrusion, this humble servant just visited to check if the hospitality of our kingdom was to the liking of our guests.” “In the middle of night?” Arin asked clearly annoyed. “Ah, of course and to deliver these blankets” he gave a small nod to the female beside him who produced two thick blankets from somewhere. “……….” “……….” Arin stared at her before asking “Where is the washroom?” “I believe there is one in your room” the butler squinted his eye. “Flush is not working” Arin replied. “………..” “………..” The butler looked inside the room and saw Hayato snoring peacefully, then turned towards the male attendant beside him “Guide him”. The male attendant stared at the butler for a couple of seconds before nodding. Arin walked out of the room along with the male attendant leaving behind three people. The butler sighed “As expected of a small kingdom” then turned towards Hayato who was snoring peacefully. Both of them approached him. “Be fast” the butler smiled but inside he felt that something was amiss. Something was wrong but he did’t know what. The female attendant stepped forward and took out a tiny glass ball with blue liquid, crushed it in between her fingers and bowed down slightly towards hayato. As she bowed down a bit suddenly the hairs of her neck stood and a shock went down her spine. However before she could understand it was too late. A hand came forth from nowhere and twisted her neck 180 degrees. **Crunch!** **THUD...**. The butler who was beside her saw her body fall lifelessly on the floor, her head turned in the wrong direction. For a few seconds he couldn’t process what he saw and then Arin appeared in front of him like a ghost. He opened his mouth to scream yet all he could muster was a tiny squeak before his consciousness was reaped. “Wake up, the work is done” Arin said as he peeked from the side of window. None of the cloak figures from before could be found outside now. Hayato turned and sat on the bed. The tension from before had passed on for now and the path for them had opened. “We managed to create a diversion-“ Hayato san continued as he approached the window and looked at the ground below. “What do we do now? Even if you offer to carry me the best we can reach unnoticed are the inner walls and that also without alerting everyone if we are lucky” he whispered. However Arin turned around and did something which Hayato couldn’t comprehend even in his wildest of dreams. He saw Arin staring at the door and then raising his leg only to pause it mid air. Then he raised his other leg while his left leg remained paused as if supported by an invisible platform. He looked like a magician walking on an invisible staircase. When Arin finally jumped on the floor from an invisible platform he turned around with a smirk only to find a Hayato with hanging jaw. “My theory is successful, I think we can escape now” Arin said while approaching him. Hayato blinked once and closed his eyes before calming down. “You really are- nevermind” he wanted to complain but this was not the time. “We can leave but still the guards might see” Arin sighed but he saw Hayato smiling. Arin followed his line of sight and saw the decoration wings and Antler above the pair of blankets bought by the butler. . . . “Did they start performing Aerial drills even at night?” a guard asked his companion while covered head to toe in steel. His gaze followed an object which looked like a bird with horns and heavy big wings. He squinted his eye suspiciously as he noticed the rather erratic movement of the bird unlike the smooth flight motion. His companion rested his hand on his shoulder “No idea mate just let it pass, nowadays the royal palace feels a lot more eerie anyway”. “I thought at first that they were rumors until the royal palace suddenly replaced its servants a few months back” the first guard said while shifting his eyes from the unidentified flying object to his patrolling path. . . . “Are you done?” the male attendant who escorted Arin till the bathroom door asked trying to enquire about his condition. Yet the only answer he got was silence. He has been standing outside for an hour now. At first he got the replies like “I am here”, “Just a bit more” but the more he waited the more uneasy he felt. He also noticed that he never heard the sound of flush even once and even Arin stopped responding now. There were no windows and only dim light from glowing stones. Arin couldn’t have escaped without alerting him that’s what the male attendant thought initially but his patience was beginning to vane now. He stood outside the toilet and kicked it open. When the dust finally settled, he saw a doll, a wooden doll in the place of Arin lying above the toilet seat burning away fading until not even the ashes remained. . . . “Why is ‘he’ so interested in Parshara kingdom?” Ezra asked the masked man standing beside her. The man beside her had pale features, slim body but as he opened his eyes Ezra felt a chill go down her spine. He said in a lifeless deep voice “The will of our master cannot be comprehended by ordinary intellect. However it is related to a treasure lost during the era of war”. This time Ezra’s eyes widened. *A treasure? Could it be a divine artifact?* She thought. She looked around the room and saw that it was filled with different faces that few months before. She gulped as she remembered that this pale man was the one who had fended off an entire battalion of Aurorian Empire’s army and made the second sword retreat with heavy injuries. She stood up “I am going to rest for today” she said as if asking for the man’s approval. The man nodded after a couple of seconds allowing her to be relieved of her duties for today. She walked alone in the hallway as she ascended the stairs one by one eventually reaching a room with a double door. She entered her room, took a shower and sat on her study table beside the bed with her diary open. Turning the pages of diary she reached for the entry two months prior. *I don’t know where these men have come from, but they gave me a choice. A choice where we could survive or even win back against the martial might of Aurorian Empire. It is just that they have two conditions which bugged me.* *First they wanted me to interfere with the trade routes of Parsharknow a kingdom forcing the country to go bankrupt.* *Second was to let them deal with all affairs regarding Aurorian, Parshara, Velmor Duchies, Dahan Sultanate and Nerith kingdom overriding the authority of crown itself.* *At that time however our chances against Aurorian empire were looking gravely thin. These men not only were successful in defending against the attacks but also successfully pushed back their army. I had no choice but to go along with them. It feels as if these men are searching for something.* *I don’t  understand their origin and why are they so fixated on ‘Parshara kingdom’ but they have agreed on leaving once their desired outcome is achieved.* *I am extremely sorry for the delay, actually I had a lot of work the following week. I will post another chapter in 2-3 days that will be flashback so that you can understand what happened and how did Arin and Hayato escaped*"},{"t":"49. Djinkya's Last Play","k":"Chronicles · chapter","u":"chronicles/c49.html","s":"*I know I deserve death but please listen I had exams and project exhibition so please forgive me. Join the discord if you wish to talk to me* *(Flashback — Hospital wing, the night before discharge)* The gym cycle's wheel was still humming down to a stop when I dropped back onto the mattress. Four hours of pedaling like my life depended on it — because, it kind of did — and my legs had gone from aching to something past aching, a place where pain stops being a sensation and starts being more like hobby. Outside the door, the corridor lights had dimmed to the shade hospitals use for \"we would like you to be asleep now.\" A cart squeaked past without stopping. Nobody expected me to be awake either. I stared at the ceiling until it stopped tilting. \"Open Archive,\" I said, once my lungs agreed to cooperate. **[ STAT WINDOW ]** **━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━** **KARMIC POINTS** **━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━** **[Karmic Coins: 600]** I looked at the number for a while. Six hundred was not nothing so functionally it might as well have been loose change I'd found in an old coat. \"Open the Wheel.\" **[You are aware it is two in the morning,]** the Archive said. **[You are aware you have surgery-adjacent injuries. You are aware that gambling has, historically, not gone well for you.]** \"I am aware of all three and have chosen to proceed regardless.\" **[Noted, for the record, as 'host was warned.']** The Wheel opened in front of me, unbothered by the hour — four wedges of color, same as always. **Art. Skill. Artifact. ????.** It had landed on Skill, twice. Art, once. The sliver marked ???? exactly once, and that one had put me in a ten-hour coma and left something called Curse of Despair sitting in my chest like a tenant who'd stopped paying rent. I did not examine why I kept coming back to a machine that had already hurt me once. That was a question for a version of me with more free time. \"Spin.\" The Wheel turned. It went longer than it needed to for four sections. Long enough that I had time to regret the decision, un-regret it, and start drafting an apology to my mother in my head, just in case. Then — click. **[Segment: Artifact]** I sat up too fast. My ribs informed me. \"That's a first,\" I said, ignoring the ribs. **[Correspondingly,]**\nthe Archive said, and something in its tone had shifted — not warmer, exactly, but more careful, the way you get careful around something you haven't personally tested yet, **[so is this.]** The air above the blanket folded, the way it always did before a reveal, except this time it kept folding — layer after layer, as if the notification itself hadn't decided how big it needed to be. **{Master Puppeteer's Set}** **[Type: Artifact — Complete. Not fragmentary. Nothing here for you to break further.]** **[Contents:]** **2x Djinkya's Understudy (Puppet)** **1x Djinkya's Cloth** **1x Djinkya's Needle** I read it twice. \"Who's Djinkya?\" **[Give me a moment,]** the Archive said. **[Cross-referencing is slower when the source material predates my classification protocols by a considerable margin.]** A pause, longer than its usual pauses. **[Archive Log — Origin Fragment, partially recovered:]** **(Djinkya, Master Puppeter and Actor…..[Additional information cannot be fetched])** **Cause of death: unknown.** **Last recorded act: one final play, written, cast, and never performed, because its author did not live to see opening night. He did not ask for more time. He asked for two understudies who would never miss a cue, a needle that would never let a seam come undone, and a curtain that could make a man vanish from the world entirely — because, in his own words, 'the best exit is the one no one sees you make.']** I sat with that for a second. \"That's — dramatic,\" I said, mostly to have something to say. **[He was a playwright,]** the Archive said. **[Drama was the job.]** The puppets came first — two of them, small enough to sit in my palms, dressed in matching threadbare coats, like they'd walked straight off a stage nobody had finished building. Glass eyes, painted just slightly too warm to be comfortable. They didn't move. I checked twice anyway. **{Djinkya's Understudy x2}** **[Description: Crafted to play any part flawlessly. When bound to a target, mimics that target's form, mannerisms, and abilities — up to the limit of the puppet's own capacity.]** **[Limitation: An understudy performs the role. It does not become the actor. Whatever the target can do, the puppet can only do as well as the puppet is capable of holding.]** \"So they copy people,\" I said. \"With a ceiling.\" **[Every actor has range,]** the Archive said. **[These two simply have less of it than the people they're impersonating. Cast accordingly.]** Next was the cloth — folded so small it looked like a handkerchief, except handkerchiefs don't usually make the hair on your arm stand up when you touch them. **{Djinkya's Cloth}** **[Description: Worn, it removes the wearer's presence from the world entirely — sight, sound, memory, record — for a limited duration.]** **[Limitation: The world does not enjoy being edited. Duration is short. Recovery after use is not instant, and the Archive cannot promise what 'presence' reconstructs to, precisely, once the world puts you back.]** \"When it puts me back,\" I repeated slowly. \"As opposed to.\" **[As opposed to nothing you need to think about tonight,]**\nthe Archive said, in the specific tone it used when it had decided a conversation was over. Last was the needle. It didn't look dangerous, which by now I recognised as the entire design philosophy of everything in this box — no case, no warning label, just a long silver needle catching the one strip of moonlight the blinds let through. **{Djinkya's Needle}** **[Description: Stitches anything to anything. The stitch does not come undone. Not with time. Not with force. Not with regret.]** **[Warning: This item is cursed. The Archive does not use that word lightly, and it is not being used lightly now.]** I picked it up carefully, the way you pick up something that has already decided it doesn't need your permission to exist. \"Cursed how?\" **[Unknown in scope. Known in principle,]** the Archive said. **[Permanence is not a small thing to hand a Thirteen-year-old with your track record for editing things until they break in interesting ways.]** \"I'm fourteen.\" **[The math does not improve your case.]** Somewhere down the corridor a door opened and a pair of shoes started walking, unhurried, in the general direction of my room. I did the only reasonable thing available to a boy lying in a hospital bed at two in the morning holding two puppets, a cursed needle, and a piece of cloth that erased people: I shoved the whole set under the blanket and lay very still, doing my best impression of someone who had never been awake in his life. The footsteps passed. A door closed somewhere else. I let out the breath I'd been holding. **[Smooth,]**\nthe Archive said. \"Shut up.\" I sat back up, unloaded the set onto my lap, and looked at the final line still hanging patiently in the corner of the window. **[Claim Cost: 600 Karmic Coins]** **[Deducted automatically. Complete artifacts do not negotiate.]** \"Wait.\" I sat up straighter, which the ribs vetoed a second time. \"Why Six hundred?\" **[You spun the Wheel. That was you asking.]** “But usually only a hundred Karmic Coins get cut” **[Since always,]**\nthe Archive said. [**You were simply too poor, previously, for it to come up.]** **[Balance: 0 Karmic Coins]** I looked at the zero. It looked back, with the specific smugness only a balance of exactly nothing can manage. \"...Why Six hundred,\" I said slowly, \"specifically for this time?\" There was a pause. The kind the Archive used right before it said something it knew it shouldn't. **[Inflation, my guy.]** \"...\" [.........] \"You cannot just say that to me right now.\" **[I can. I did. You may grieve at your own pace.]** I lay back down, needle in one hand, two puppets and a fistful of cursed cloth balanced on my chest, a balance of zero coins glowing faintly in the corner of my vision like the world's smallest tombstone. Tomorrow I'd be discharged. Tomorrow I'd have to walk into Professor Serin's office and explain — nothing, actually, since she had no idea any of this existed, which was, on reflection, a mercy I hadn't earned. Tonight, I had two of a dead man's understudies, a needle that didn't believe in mistakes, and a cloth that could make me disappear from the world if I ever needed to badly enough. Broke, exhausted, and technically richer than I'd been an hour ago in every way that couldn't be counted. I'd take it. I closed my eyes. Past the window, the sky over the medical wing was already starting to grey at the edges, and morning — and Professor Serin, and whatever paperwork disaster she'd prepared for me — was not nearly far enough away."},{"t":"50. Fugitive [1]","k":"Chronicles · chapter","u":"chronicles/c50.html","s":"**“Don’t look down. Don’t look down,”** I kept muttering under my breath as my eyes darted toward the torch-lit walls. I felt Hayato-san’s grip on the edges of the blanket tighten as his warm breath hit the nape of my neck. I quickened my steps as I felt the familiar surge of warmth flow through my body like a stream, while countless cells sucked in air from the surroundings. The cold breeze touched me as I cut through it effortlessly. I couldn’t see clearly in front of me because I was wearing a deer mount like a mask, to give the impression of a big bird. To conserve my energy, I imagined a platform made of emptiness using Null Craft and stepped onto it. The world around me slowed as I guided the stream toward my heel, pressing against the invisible platform, and... **BOOM...** With a low, muffled blast, I shot forward. **“Ugh.”** Even with such an efficient method and such abundant energy, I found it extremely hard to manage the expenditure of my mental stamina. My head throbbed and my eyes hurt each time I shot forward. *'No, not here.'* I glanced back for a moment, watching the outer wall come and go. We had escaped the jaws of danger, but the danger itself had not decreased. I knew we had at most half an hour to improve our chances of escape, as the puppets would not last more than an hour. *** **“Hmm?”** Vazir peeked through the window of his carriage, following an erratic silhouette crossing the sky over the outer walls. **“Drills? At this hour?”** he murmured to himself, pondering the possibility until the coachman’s voice reached his ears. **“We are about to arrive, Your Highness,”** the coachman relayed respectfully. Vazir hummed to signal that he had heard. He crossed his legs and leaned back slightly, resting his elbow on the edge of the window and his chin on his knuckles. Moonlight spilled through the carriage’s only opening as it moved forward past countless lampposts. His eyes drifted over the flames, barely alive on the drops of oil. *'Such a waste.'* That was his first thought whenever he considered this new practice, which had started two months ago. He had been very surprised to learn that Her Majesty had approved it. It was a clear waste of resources and would do more harm than good. **“Just what is she thinking?”** Vazir questioned Her Majesty’s strange decisions in recent times, though he never voiced it aloud. He looked down at his left hand, which bore light ink stains. A warm smile emerged on his face at the sight, then reverted to a stern, frozen expression as he heard noises in the distance. The carriage wheels halted in front of a thick iron gate. The soldier looked at the carriage and hurriedly signaled for the gate to be opened. The familiar sound of water splashing at the edge of the fountain and the smell of wet grass made his lips curl up. He straightened his spine and stretched his arms, producing a satisfying pop. **“They can’t have gone far!”** he heard someone shout as wind hit his face and horses shot past his carriage. He snapped to attention and saw five knights sprinting off somewhere. Suddenly, a bad premonition welled up within his heart. He opened the door and jumped out just as the carriage was about to turn sideways toward the inner section. As he jumped, an orange glow enveloped his figure, leaving a fiery trail behind. *'I hope everything is all right.'* It was the only thing he could pray for. **……Smash!** Vazir headed straight for the upper floor of the inner castle, his surroundings smearing into streaks of color. *Right, at the end of the lane to the stairway, up the...* he recited the familiar map in his mind, and within a couple of seconds his legs had carried him in front of the double doors. The door was open. He stepped inside. **“Sis...ter?”** Heads turned toward him, yet he couldn’t recognize any of them. At first he thought he had entered the wrong room. That was until he saw the woman on the bed. The moment his eyes landed on her, it felt as if someone had forcibly pulled the breath from his lungs. His jaw went slack, his eyes widened, and he fell to his knees. The blanket covering his sister had turned crimson. Her skin had paled like white cloth. There was no sound of breathing, only an uncomfortable silence that seemed to stretch forever. His mind refused to accept the reality in front of him. Moments ago, he had been elated at the thought of reuniting with his elder sister, eager to tell her stories of the Academy campus. Now he had found her lifeless body in her own room. That night, his sister Ezra Ul Nazarana, ruler of the Nazarana Kingdom, was murdered. **“...Who?”** he asked, his words half a sob. The unknown pale man answered without turning toward him. “...Envoys of the Parshara Kingdom.” His deep, chilling voice carried across the room as his hands moved to close the eyes of the now-deceased queen of Nazarana. **“...Why?”** Vazir asked, half in rage and half in grief, tears flowing down his cheeks without his knowledge. No matter how hard he thought, he couldn’t find a reason solid enough to explain why a nation with which they’d had almost no interaction would resort to such actions. The pale man looked into the prince’s eyes, causing him to go still, and then spoke. **“Her Majesty rejected the trade alliance that Parshara offered.”** Vazir’s brows knit as the logic finally clicked into place. Even an amateur could work out why Parshara had done it. Because of the war between Nazarana and Aurorian, the kingdoms of the western regions were facing extremely high taxes on imports and exports. It didn’t take a genius to see that... *'They want the war to end quickly.'* As the final piece fell into place, Vazir took a deep breath. **“I think I know where we will find our culprits,”** he muttered in a low voice, but the impact was like a tsunami. **“Oh?”** For the first time, he drew a reaction from the pale man in the butler’s clothes. Vazir found the man disgusting, but more than that, he wanted to avenge his sister. His gaze fell again on the light ink stains on his fingers, and a certain woman’s face appeared in his mind. His brows relaxed a little at the thought of her before he asked himself: *\"She must have received my letter by now, right?\"*"},{"t":"51. Fugitive [2]","k":"Chronicles · chapter","u":"chronicles/c51.html","s":"Nazarana was a vast kingdom of trade and commerce. Its capital, Darjan, where the crown resided, was established in the middle of four great commerce hubs: ‘Tilmis’ of the North, ‘Ganj’ of the East, ‘Somalis’ of the South, and ‘Baazar’ of the West. All others were but minor urban settlements or Noble Houses managing their respective lands. *** **[Arin’s POV]** Cold liquid makes its way down my throat, easing my throbbing head a bit. “Huff... ha... more... ha,” I demand, moving the now-empty chalice towards Hayato-san. He turns around, refills the cup, and gives it to me again. After an hour of travel, we spot a secluded barn near a farm and decide to rest for a little while. My head feels as if someone is pumping air into an already full balloon. My vision blurs, and sleep threatens to take over. I bite my lips hard to push through the terrible vertigo. I spent too much energy on Null craft. Then, stretching my limbs, I lean onto a pile of soft hay behind me, and for the first time in a while, I am allowed to think. Not as Daniel, but as Arin. I let my eyelashes meet and my thoughts drift freely. *Where did it all go wrong?... Why is this happening?...* I am unable to think. Questions come crashing down, many at a time, leaving me confused. Then I remember the screen at the start of the fog event. **[Re-Enacting: The Romance of the Four Kingdoms]** **OBJECTIVE:** Two lovers who come from different and unresolvable backgrounds couldn’t unify in the past. Unify them. **EXTRA OBJECTIVE:** ???? **REWARD:** ????? **PENALTY:** Erasure. *Wasn’t this supposed to be about reuniting two lovers?... Forget the lovers. Hell, I’ll be happy even if I manage to stay alive.* I take a deep breath to clear my mind. *Focus. No need to worry about completing the objective. First, we need to get out of the border; only then can we think about anything else.* *But then again, if I die here, does it mean that I die in real life? Does erasure as a penalty mean death?* I feel a lump in my throat, making it hard to swallow. *No. No. No. Arin, focus. There must be some way. Mother Nature always leaves a path open.* I try to divert my remaining energy to devise a plan for Hayato-san and me to escape, yet I can only see myself drowning in the unyielding flow of thoughts. *What would Arun and Robert be doing right now? Is it night already there?* Unnecessary thoughts begin to claw at my mind, so much so that I don’t even see the message in front of me. **[Curse of Despair is in effect: Host will continue to lose his sanity bit by bit while the curse is in effect.]** *I really want to see Professor Serin’s reaction. If I die here, would she be sad? Guilty? Heh, well, serves her right.* I know that harboring such thoughts is wrong. **[Sanity: -1%]** *How much time has passed there? Did Ma receive the stipend I sent her? Would I be awarded a posthumous degree if I die?* No, snap out of it! **[Sanity: -1%]** *I bet Rao still roams the reading hall under the guise of “inspection.” That old man seemed to have gotten his youth back when the guard handed him my admission letter.* **[Sanity: -1.5%]** *Would Ma be alright without me?* I am drowning. **[Sanity: -2%]** *No, would it even be right to call her Ma?* Someone, help me. **[Sanity: -3%]** *I don’t know. I may not even be her real son.* Anyone... please. **[Sanity: -5%]** *What if—?* “Arin!” a muffled voice shatters my trance. For some reason, I am covered in my own sweat. “Wh-wha?” I panic. Hayato-san puts a hand on my mouth, cutting me off. Then he motions for me to keep quiet. “Are you feeling okay? You’ve been sleeping for almost an hour now.” I nod, my eyes bloodshot and my head dizzy, but the throbbing has almost stopped. We can move. My hands creak like stubborn metal hinges. I stand up, dusting off my back. Meanwhile, Hayato-san unties the horses. We mount our respective horses, and with a loud neigh, they move towards our next destination. *** **[Vazir’s POV]** “W-we d-don’t... know” is all the man in front of me can say before he becomes unconscious again. His gray hair, tied in a ponytail, is a mess now. Eight others stand trembling, their bodies covered in various wounds. “We tried every method, sir, but they won’t budge,” says the prison guard beside me, his head down. I don’t reply. Too many problems have come at the same time. The whole nation is in a state of emergency. Messengers and aerial patrols have been dispatched to notify the governors of Ganj, Baazar, Tilmis, and Somalis, and soldiers have been instructed to make announcements wherever they reach. The pale man has also vanished somewhere. The aerial unit will take a couple of hours to reach the border and notify the guards to close all the entrances and exits. “Should I draft an execution notice, sire?” the prison guard asks me. I shake my head after a moment of thought. The guard frowns but doesn’t push me to answer. I look at the far end of the cell. There lies a kid, bruised, his body trembling in the cold of the underground prison. “Keep the guards. Take out the kid,” I say, opening the gate of the cell and preparing to leave. The guard seems surprised but immediately complies. As much as I would love to tear down the Parshara kingdom, I cannot overlook my own deductions. *The work was too clean, devoid of any mistakes,* I ponder. *Even if we assume Parshara did it, why would they do it in such a manner? It is the same as coming out in front and announcing it to the whole world. It doesn’t benefit Parshara in the least. But then again, why would they run?* I am so engrossed in my thoughts that I don’t even notice when I reach my sister’s office. I open the door and see a man with sunken cheeks and a rather lean physique sorting documents. “Chancellor Wallabh? What are you doing so late in the queen’s study?” The moment I call out his name, he shudders and tilts his head in a lightning-fast manner. His face is covered in sweat, and he freezes when he sees me enter. I start walking towards him after closing the door. He turns around bit by bit, his hands behind his waist. “Your Highness... I-I...” He is about to say something when, just then... *Click.* The door opens behind me. “Chancellor Wallabh, you are under arrest on charges of conspiring against the nation,” an indifferent voice declares."},{"t":"52. Fugitive [3]","k":"Chronicles · chapter","u":"chronicles/c52.html","s":"**[Vazir’s POV]** The words crashed down like a divine judgement. I turned around and saw a fat man who was none other than the chief accountant along with some guards and a lean man beside him staring daggers at the chancellor. **“What is the meaning of this?”** I asked turning towards the fat man. Chancellor too seemed to have seen a ghost as he opened his mouth trying to say something and only to close it right after. The lean man stepped forward, he bowed and said **“Your highness, we have found records of embezzlements from the royal treasury. The records are not in proper shape but nonetheless the proof is concrete”.** He handed me a bunch of papers which bore the official seal of the kingdom. I more I delved into the paper and its contents the more my breathing quickened. It was a record, a record of total expenditure from the royal treasury. On surface everything seemed to be alright but if someone were to calculate the numbers, they would catch it. The calculation was amiss, by a very thin margin in every column. I scanned it from top to bottom, no matter how much I wanted to refute it, the proof was right in front of me. I lifted my gaze with a twisted face. My left brow twitched involuntarily *‘Old man Wallabh? Out of all people?**’*\nunlike any other personnel I had known Wallabh since childhood. He was practically my first teacher. I could still vividly remember his big hands covering mine and teaching me to write alphabet by alphabet. I was about to question him when- **“Your highness, I swear upon my honor it wasn’t me!”**\nhe pleaded, his hands trembled. He went down on his knees, grabbed my left arm with his palms. **“Those papers, they are fabricated!”** his grip on my arm tightened. **“Enough! Guards take him”** the chief accountant commanded. **“Y-your highness, you have…no please believe me I beg of you”**\nhe came closer, now grabbing my arm like a tree branch to prevent his fall. **“Blasphemous!, this is an insult to the crown!”** the lean man yelled. Seeing the chancellor hide behind me the soldiers stopped, confused. For a moment silence settled inside the room. **“Guards!”**\nthe soldiers straightened hearing my voice. I looked towards old man Wallabh, his face was pitiful. **“Throw him in the cell”** I said. Chancellor’s face paled but the soldiers instantly complied, their steel armor clanking in unison as they held Wallabh’s arms whose grip felt like clay. He didn’t make any commotion, his eyes were wide open and jaw hanging as he couldn’t process what had happened while soldiers dragged him out of the room. I closed my eyes and sighed. **“You”** I said turning my head towards the lean man. He turned towards me and bowed **“Your highness?”** **“I haven’t seen you before, present your credentials in the office.”**\nat my words he didn’t speak which was same as affirmation. **“….And you”**\nI said turning towards chief accountant. He bowed slightly **“Your Highness?”** he asked. **“Arrange for someone to act as temporary chancellor and…”**\nI thought for a moment before continuing. **“Connect me with the governors through magician’s glass, until I go get some fresh air”.** At that both of them ran out to make the preparations. I too stepped out of the inner castle and in the garden, it reminded me of my sister. I touched the petals of roses as I walked along the edge of inner walls and at some point I slipped inside a watch tower quietly. There were 4 towers, one in a direction each and two ways to go inside a watch tower which were built within the inner walls. One way was through the wall itself, the way on the top of walls where soldiers patrolled was connected to the tower and one can descend inside the tower from the door there. One was through a trapdoor hidden in the shade of roses and leaves. I slid into the hole. Descending the ladder, insides were dark with a thick and oily smell. My eyes turned orange from its original lavender color and sparks appeared crackling in my left hand. Illuminating the room I saw spider webs and layers of dust present over the old furniture. **“Sadly no one is aware of such a nice place”** I lamented. This place hadn’t been looked after for years. It used to be my hideout when I was young. I saw a painting of myself and sister hung on the far right of the room. I stepped on the stairs in the middle of the room which led to the next floor. My steps echoed as I finally stopped in front of an old drawer. This room was clean unlike the lower room. I pulled the handle. Its joints screeched as the old drawer revealed a diary. **“I see….-“** a smile made its way up to my lips. **“You are still sharp as ever, Wallabh”** I murmured to myself. I closed my eyes and recalled the exchange that happened a few moments prior. **“Your highness, I swear upon my honor it wasn’t me!”**\nhe pleaded, his hands trembled. He went down on his knees, grabbed my left arm with his palms. His finger tapped twice on my knuckles. (Inner castle) **“Those papers, they are fabricated!”** his grip on my arm tightened. Then he made three circles on my wrist (Third Watchtower) **“Enough! Guards take him”** the chief accountant commanded. Two taps (Second Floor) and an arrow facing upwards (From below) **“Y-your highness, you have…no please believe me I beg of you”**\nhe came closer, now grabbing my arm like a tree branch to prevent his fall. Then he gave a slight pinch to my elbow (Emergency!) **“Using a game to convey such a message? I expected no less from you old man”**\nI whispered to the walls. Then opened my eyes and in the darkness which dared not shroud my gaze from the truth, I opened the diary. *** [Arin’s POV] **….Fwoosh……swish..** Arrows went past me avoiding me by a hair’s length. I tightened my grip on the rein and urged the horse to go faster. It cried and my surroundings began to blur. I turned my head to see Hayato san at some distance. He too had leaned ahead in a streamlined posture as he cut through air like a knife through butter. The curtain of starlight which had covered the sky for quite some time now was beginning to open. Dawn was breaking. I could faintly see trees. A lot of trees, it was a forest as much as I could comprehend right ahead. I turned my head and saw a few griffins above. “Hayato san, into the forest!” I screamed as I steered the horse towards the nearest opening in the forest."},{"t":"53. Guerilla Warfare","k":"Chronicles · chapter","u":"chronicles/c53.html","s":"**Authors Note: After a lot of thought I have arrived at a conclusion that from now on the chapters will be in MULTI POV form. (One POV per chapter)** **I at first tried to steer the narrative in 3rd person camera locked at the current scene form but my brain asked “Can you accept such an Outcome?” and my heart answered “No”** **For some reason I felt that this is a reasonable development, what are your thoughts?** *** **{Arin’s POV}** I steered the horse towards the nearest opening of the forest. Along with it my eyes turned gray, and the world slowed down. As I entered the forest, the sounds of birds chirping and leaves rustling entered my ear. Shade of the trees covered my figure preventing the armored soldiers from spotting me and I heard the sound wing coming closer, turned my head and my prediction proved to be right. The Aerial soldiers pursuing us abandoned the advantage of their position and came closer, flying just over the trees to spot me and Hayato san. I jumped towards a thick tree branch letting the horse run ahead and turned around. The world slowed down as and vision turned blue as if submerged in water. I saw three griffins all of them near the trees. ‘Either do it in one swoop or fast enough that no one gets to react’ I naturally reached a conclusion. The surface of the tree branch cracked and I launched towards the nearest pursuer from the vast expanse of leaves and silence. **“Whe-?”**his head turned slowly like a film set on 0.5x speed and his hands seemed to slowly raise trying to protect himself, but I was faster. **“Khugh..”**\nhe shrieked as my knee slammed onto the cap of his nose. I felt something crack where I had hit him. The force however sent him flying off the griffin and on the ground somewhere in the forest. I turned my gaze on my right where the remaining two were, however they had their guard up. I ran on air with null craft in a zig zag motion so fast that their eyes couldn’t follow and pounced on the left one while going in the direction of the right one. He couldn’t even understand anything before my horse kick shattered his ribs causing a nasty dent and the inertia, true to its word sent the rider flying in the green sea below. I turned back and saw the last one putting a metal pipe in his mouth. I ran towards him but till then he had already blown the whistle. He swung his already drawn out sword but I summer saulted and grabbed his head pushing him off the griffin. The griffins without their riders became confused and began to fly in a circle behind each other’s tails. I tried to control a griffin yet, it seemed they had some basic intelligence which allowed them to identify me and hence I got a clear rejection whenever I tried to approach them. I sighed and dashed down into the forest searching for Hayato san. I didn’t had to search for long before I found his horse after some time and him lying upside down on the ground. **“Hayato san!”** I yelled his name trying to wake him up. His face looked thinner than before. I frowned and looked around, there was nothing, just a maze of trees and leaves. **“D-Daniel?”**\nhe mumbled my name opening his eyes. I opened my mouth to say something but closed it for some reason. **“Are you ok?”** I asked in my western accent, a bit hesitant. He nodded and then asked me **“Did…we lose the pursuers?”** he questioned in a low voice. **“Yes..we lost them”** I answered. **“Where do we go now?”** he asked. I thought for a moment and then told him about the whistle. **“To my prediction If we both travel together now, we will we easily identified”** **“So…you mean we-?”** **“Yes…let’s split up and meet at Somalis in the south in a couple of days”**\nI suggested. **“But if they find-?”** **“No one will know”** I told him and then led him to the soldiers I had defeated a couple of moments ago. **“You did this?”** he seemed a bit surprised. I started taking off their armor and put it on myself. Hayato san seeing me also starting wearing the armor of one of them. After we were done I took the two off whom we wore the armor and threw them somewhere deep in the forest giving last of my responsibility to the mother nature. **“How much time will it take you to reach Somalis?”** He asked me. **“Two Days”** I gave him a vague number. **“How should I contact you?”** he asked. **“You don’t know?”** I asked, lifting my brow. He frowned and said **“I know but, don’t you think the current situation demands us to switch to a more hidden means of communication?”** **“Hmmm”**\nI nodded while looking elsewhere. **“Remain in Somalis for today and then tomorrow at night, I will set a forest fire. When you see the forest fire, start moving towards Velmor duchies”**\nI said **“Where will you remain till then?”** He asked **“I told you didn’t I? I have a good friend in ‘Ganj’. He will help me set up our escape”** I smiled. He nodded and hopped onto the back of the horse. **“Okay then, I will stay at a village near Somalis. Don’t be late”**\nhe gave a light kick to the horse and began moving towards Somalis. I saw his figure vanishing and bit my lips. *‘Neither did I had any friend in Ganj and nor did we had any means of communication’*\nI thought. I let out a long sigh, I would have really overlooked it hadn’t Hayato san talked to me in Japanese the whole tour and called me ‘Arin’ instead of ‘Daniel’. *Now, what should I do? Should I try to save him? I even left the soldiers and Hassan there to escape.* I turned my head and saw small dots in the sky, becoming bigger. Many of them, infact dozens. “Let’s first get out of this situation. Logically, I cannot save them now. Let’s take one step….at a time”."},{"t":"54. The Depth of the Rot","k":"Chronicles · chapter","u":"chronicles/c54.html","s":"**“Did something happen, Your Majesty?”** Razakh, the governor of Tilmis, asked, his projection flickering on the intricately decorated crystal glass before the king. Vazir crossed his fingers in front of his lips, took a deep breath, and prepared to deliver the news of his sister’s death. **“Ezra Ul Nazarana passed away today, past midnight,”** he said in a low but clear voice. The moment the words left his mouth, the faces of the four governors changed. He watched those changes closely, hoping to learn how deep the rot had spread. Razakh Altarum, governor of Tilmis, frowned, his brows drawing together. Panak Khulmann, governor of Baazar, looked stunned. His brows shot up and his jaw went slack. Silvaris Donna, governor of Ganj, showed little change in her expression, but her eyes narrowed and her fists clenched. Zalima Eklaire, governor of Somalis, remained quiet, which piqued his curiosity. **“How?”** Panak broke the silence at once. No one else spoke, urging Vazir to continue. **“We… don’t know for certain. But yesterday afternoon we happened to have some guests, you see…”** He told them how the room where the two delegates of the Parshara Kingdom had stayed was found empty after the queen’s passing, and what condition the late queen’s body had been in. **“War! War it is!”** Panak shot to his feet, his fists trembling, his voice breaking. **“Calm down, Panak,”** Razakh said. **“Nothing will be solved in anger. Your Highness, if you may…”** Razakh urged him to go on. He nodded. **“I understand what you feel, Panak. But there is something I must discuss with all of you in order to reach a reasonable conclusion.”** He sat up straighter. **“I think there is a hidden power manipulating this entire situation,”** he said in one breath. This time, all of them were at a loss for words. **“May I ask how Your Majesty reached such a conclusion?”** Zalima asked, her usual nonchalance gone, her expression contorted. **“It’s… let’s just say it’s intuition,”** Vazir replied vaguely. He could see confusion and denial on every face, yet he could not tell them about the diary, not until he trusted someone enough. **“What do you want to propose?”** This time it was Silvaris. She cut straight to the point, realizing he was beating around the bush. He sighed. **“I want the restrictions on the trade routes lifted, and…”** He paused. **“I want to resume importing glowing stones from the Parshara Kingdom, given the mounting cost of oil and other fuels.”** This time the reaction was explosive. Panak, who had held himself in check until now, slammed his fist onto the table in front of him. A brown glow enveloped him, and the table shattered into fine pieces. “**An insult! This is an insult to the late crown!”** he roared, his eyes bloodshot, his receding hairline in full display. A nasty scar ran diagonally down his face, making him look all the more intimidating. **“I will skin those bastards alive the moment I find them,”** he declared, and with that, his projection ended. Vazir drew a deep breath and kept his orange aura from flaring up. A surge of energy rose in his chest, then subsided. He closed his eyes and let the silence stretch. *It’s not that I don’t want to give you proof. But I’m trapped. Everywhere I go in this castle, I can feel* their *gazes.* **“He was presumptuous, yes, but he was not wrong. Your Majesty, if I may, I suggest you think it over alone.”** With that, Silvaris’s projection vanished. Only Zalima Eklaire and Razakh Altarum remained. **“Rest, Your Majesty,”** Zalima said, and she too closed her projection. Now he was alone with Razakh Altarum. He finally relaxed and spoke in a low voice, without any air of formality. **“How have you been, Grandpa?”** A smile blossomed on the old man’s aged face. **“Just living… since your grandmother passed away,”** he said, half in complaint. Vazir smiled bitterly and told him about his life so far in the Nerith Kingdom, where he had studied at the Imperial Academy. Razakh listened intently, reacting to every part of the story. When he ran out of tales, he blurted out, almost unconsciously: **“If I had arrived sooner, could I have saved her?”** He had spoken his thoughts aloud. Razakh’s eyes softened. **“What happened was not in your hands, so it should not weigh on you,”** he said warmly. He added, as if reminiscing, **“Life is like that. You never know when you may lose someone, which is why we should cherish every moment.”** After that, Razakh excused himself and shut off the projection. **“Ow!”** Vazir looked down at his thumb, which had turned red. He realized he had been rubbing his hand with it for nearly the whole conversation. He looked around and noticed the room was brighter than when the meeting began. Dawn had broken. Sunlight fell through the windows, unable to be held back by the red silk curtains. His gaze moved to the long table before him. It was made of gold and stretched from one wall to the other. Black obsidian chairs lined its edges, reflecting the sunlight and giving them a twinkling sheen. **“It’s unlike you to think so much.”** He heard a familiar voice. **“Big Sister?”** At the far end of the table sat a woman with flowing black hair, lavender eyes like his own, and a face that almost matched his. She smiled. **“I would rather start throwing punches than go through the tedious process of planning. Isn’t that what you are like?”** He rubbed his eyes. She was gone. There was no one in the room. *Heh… hehe… hahaha.* He began to laugh. His lips curled into a smile, yet tears flowed freely from his eyes, staining the table below. **“You really know me inside out, Big Sister,”** Vazir Ul Nazarana muttered in a trance. Only now did it truly strike him: *You have left me…* AUTHORS NOTE: I am a bit confused about the narrative style. Someone told me that POV's from other characters create confusion and advised me to switch to third person for them and first person from MC's point of view only. Let me know your thoughts. This chapter was for to add depth in Vazir's character."},{"t":"55. Bird in the Cage (I)","k":"Chronicles · chapter","u":"chronicles/c55.html","s":"**{Arin’s POV}** I lay on my back in the dirt with my eyes closed and my mouth slightly open, and tried very hard to look like a man who had been kicked off a griffin. It wasn’t difficult. My armor was dented where I had thrown myself against a tree. There was mud on the breastplate and a leaf stuck on my shoulder guard. I had even smeared a little blood from the first soldier’s nose on the side of my helmet, which felt like going a bit too far, but ‘*a careful liar leaves nothing to chance’.* The beating of wings grew louder. Wind pushed through the trees and the leaves around me rustled. **‘Dozens. Maybe two dozen.’** The thud of claws on the soil followed one after another. Armor clanked. Someone barked an order and boots rushed towards me. **“Here! One of ours is here!”** **“Is he alive?”** A gauntleted hand grabbed my shoulder and shook me. I let my head roll to the side, counted to three in my head, and then coughed. **“Khuh…khugh…”** **“He’s alive! Get some water!”** Hands pulled me up into a sitting position. Someone pressed a flask against the gap of my helmet, and I took a small sip while keeping my visor down. A man with a plume of grey on his helm crouched in front of me. Captain, going by the way the others stood stiff around him. **“Report, soldier,”** he asked. **“What happened here? Where are the other two from your unit?”** I had thought about this part for a long time while lying in the mud. Hayato-san was heading to Somalis, and there was a part of me that screamed at the idea of putting that word in this man’s ears. But the pursuers would sweep the south anyway, since it was the obvious road for two fugitives from Parshara. *‘Give them the road they already expect. And hope the one I walk off is the one they don’t look at.’* I lowered my voice and roughened it, dropping the western accent and trying the eastern one. **“They…went down into the forest, sir. I saw them hit the other two. One of them kicked me off my mount before I could blow the whistle.”** I coughed again, because it seemed right. **“They rode south. Towards Somalis.”** **“Both?”** **“Yes, sir.”** The captain stood and turned to his men**. “Third and fourth pack, to Somalis! Sweep the road and the villages around it! Send word to the governor of Somalis and tell her to close the gates.”** Then his gaze turned back to me and the other fallen soldier two trees away who was groaning with his hand on his nose. **“The rest, we escort the wounded to Baazar. It’s the closest garrison.”** I bowed my head and let out a breath no one could hear. *‘Baazar. West, as far from Somalis as I could hope for. And the last place two fugitives from Parshara would go.’* Which is exactly what makes it a bad place to be. There was no choice, though. **“Can you stand?”** a voice asked. The hand which had been holding me up belonged to a young man with a round face and a crooked nose, who looked like he had been broken once and set badly. He pulled my arm over his shoulders before I could answer and half-dragged me towards one of the griffins. **“Come on, you’re riding with me. Dorvan,” he said, tapping his own chest. “You?”** *‘Name. Name. The armor.*’ I remembered the name done in a neat artistry on the inside of the collar. “…Rhen,” I said. **“Rhen. Alright, Rhen, hold on tight.”** The griffin turned its head the moment I came near. One golden eye locked on me. A low sound rolled out of its throat, and wings flared. *‘Of course. Earlier too. They don’t like me.’* “**Easy, girl, easy!”** Dorvan patted its neck. We rose. The forest dropped below us in a green sea, and I clutched the leather strap with both hands, which wasn’t acting. I had jumped from towers and ridden on thin air, but I had never in my life been carried by something that actively disliked me. “**Two of them, and they dropped three of you?”** Dorvan shouted over the wind. **“Monsters. Absolute monsters.”** **“Yes,”** I said. **“Monsters.”** He was silent for a few breaths, and then his voice changed. **“Is it true? About the Queen?”** I frowned, My fingers tightening on the strap. *‘Queen? Did something happen?’* I thought. But the gears in my mind turned quickly. I made a statement. Neither confirming nor denying. **“That’s what we were told, when we were woken u urgently to find the fugitives.”**I said. After listening to me he continued. **“Her Majesty is dead and the envoys from Parshara did it. I couldn’t believe it. You know my family was in debt, Two years ago, my father’s workshop in Baazar, he made lamp fittings. The guild was going to take it. And then the Queen cut the lamp tax for small workshops. Just like that. My father cried in the kitchen when he heard.”** He laughed, a short, embarrassed laugh. **“I thought, what kind of queen cuts a tax like that? And now…”** He didn’t finish. I stared at the back of his helmet, my eyes wide in disbelief. I could not say a single word. *‘She is dead?......’* *‘She was shrewd. I knew that. She sat in front of Hayato-san and me with the lavender eyes and the red robe and did calculate profit and loss of the alliance in her head faster than we drank the water in front of us. And she was alive. And now…’* I swallowed it down. Like that Dorvan kept talking all the way till we reached Baazar. I didn’t reply. *** Baazar rose out of the plains, breathing like a living giant. We came in over the western walls in late afternoon. Smoke pipes stood out in the forests of buildings and houses above rooftops of slate and tin. Banners, hundreds of them hung like advertisements. Under us ran streets so crowded that I could see them moving like rivers of ants, and even from that height I could hear the clang of hammers and the cries of merchants. ‘Baazar. The west hub of Nazarana thrived off of merchants and tourists and buyers all around the continent.’ The garrison was built into the wall itself, with a tower for the griffins and long barracks of grey stone behind it. We landed in a yard of packed earth. Nurses took the other fallen soldier away on a stretcher. I waved them off, claiming that nothing was broken, and a harried quartermaster with ink-stained fingers thrust a wooden token into my hand. **“Wing C, room nine. Dinner is served in the room. Governor’s orders, everyone eats in their quarters tonight. Report at dawn.”** **“Orders for what, sir?”** I asked. He looked at me like I was an idiot**. “War, soldier. What else?”** Dorvan clapped my back, hard enough to rattle my armor. **“Get some rest, Rhen. I’m in room six, if you need anything.”** He grinned, and walked off whistling. I stood there for a moment, holding a wooden token and wearing a stranger’s name. *‘Dorvan,’* I thought. *‘Don’t be so kind. It makes this difficult.’* Room nine was a stone box with a long table, eight cots, and one small window. Five soldiers were already there, and the smell of lamb stew and flat bread filled the air. They glanced up when I came in. I raised a hand in greeting and sat at the end of the table, where the lamp barely reached. The helmet was the problem. A bowl was put in front of me and I looked at the spoon and thought, with some desperation, *‘How do I eat with a helmet on?’* **“Something wrong with your head, mate?”** a bearded man across the table asked, around a mouthful of bread. **“Kicked in the face,”** I mumbled. **“It’s swollen. I don’t want…you know.”**\nI gestured vaguely at my own visor. **“Not pretty.”** There was a pause, and then the man snorted. **“Ha! Fair enough. Keep it on, then. Nobody wants to look at that while eating.”** The others laughed. I raised the visor a hand’s breadth, just enough for the spoon, and kept my face in the shadow of the rim. The stew was too salty. I ate it anyway. For a while they spoke of the price of wine, a woman at the Rusty shop near the garrison, a bet gone wrong. I listened and chewed and let the voices wash over me, and then, the way water finds a crack, the talk turned. **“Did you hear the governor smashed a whole table this morning?”** **“A whole table? Gold?”** **“Gold. Broke it into pieces with one fist. My cousin is a guard in the east wing, he saw the thing. Says the governor was shaking.”** **“Can you blame him? The Queen…”** **“Shh, quiet.”** **“No one’s listening. I’m only saying, if I were that Panak, I’d be breaking more than a table.”** I kept my spoon moving. **“I heard the Supreme Commander came in on a griffin an hour after noon,”** a thin soldier with a pockmarked face said, lowering his voice. **“Straight from the capital. They say he didn’t even stop to wash.”** **“Tarkhan? Here?”** **“And that’s not all. I saw a woman at the gate last night, red coat, two curved swords. The innkeeper at the Anvil said it was the Crimson Gale herself. Sellen Orath.”** Someone whistled. “**The Crimson Gale? She’s not cheap. Who paid her?”** **“Who do you think?”** They went silent. A spoon scraped a bowl. Then the bearded man leaned in. **“Heard the gates are sealed too. Outbound only. No one leaves Baazar without the governor’s seal.”** My spoon stopped. “**Sealed? The whole city?”** **“Since noon. Merchants are screaming. Governor doesn’t care.”** **“Well. Those Parshara bastards can’t have gotten this far.”** **“No. But if they have…”** The bearded man drew a finger across his throat. **“Governor will have them before the next sunrise.”** They laughed, grimly, and the talk went back to wine. I looked down at my bowl and found that I wasn’t hungry anymore. *‘The Supreme Commander. A famous mercenary. A sealed city. In one afternoon.’* The governor had not wasted a single hour. *** I lay on a cot with my hands behind my head, staring at the dark ceiling. The others had blown out the lamp one by one and now the room was filled with deep, steady breathing. Someone snored. Moonlight fell in a thin silver line through the window. *‘Think.’* The city gates were sealed. That meant I couldn’t walk out, and a soldier of the aerial unit, whose griffins hated him, could not simply fly out either. *‘The Cloth,’* I thought. *‘It erases my presence. For a short time. At the gate, it might work.’* *‘Null Craft. Twenty seconds of empty space at a time. The wall is high. And I may not find a cover like previous time. ’* I turned over the possibilities one by one like cards, and every one of them ended in the same place: a lot of people looking at a stranger who shouldn’t be there. *‘One step at a time,’* I told myself again. It had sounded wise in the forest, but at this hour there was nothing more foolish that being the rat in a cage thinking that it was safe. Knock. Knock. I froze. I counted five breaths, and the knock came again, soft and polite. Knock. Knock."},{"t":"56. The Quiet Yard","k":"Chronicles · chapter","u":"chronicles/c56.html","s":"Knock. Knock. I did not move. Whoever it was had knocked three times now. The room breathed around me. Five soldiers, asleep, one of them snoring in a way that made the cot creak. 'If it were the governor's men, they would not knock,' I told myself. 'They would open it and hold a lamp to my face and ask me to take the helmet off, and that would be that.' Knock. Knock. I got up. I did not put the helmet back on. In the dark, in a barracks, with the visor down, I was a shape and nothing else; with it off I was a boy with a swollen face, which is easier to look past than a stranger who will not show his own. I pulled the blanket over my shoulders like a man woken from a good sleep and annoyed about it, and opened the door. A child stood in the corridor. He could not have been more than twelve. Too-thin wrists, a wool coat three sizes too large with the sleeves rolled twice, and a smell coming off him that I knew well enough — horse, hay, and the particular sourness of a boy who has been up too long. **\"...Sorry,\"** he said. And then nothing. He had clearly prepared a sentence and lost it. **\"Well?\"** I said. Unkindly. I meant to be unkind. **\"The horses, sir.\"** He looked at my boots rather than my face, which was a mercy. **\"The night man's gone to the wall. There's twenty-two of them and the feed's not down, and if it's not down by the bell the stablemaster will—\"** He stopped himself. **\"He'll not let me go home, sir. That's all.\"** **\"And the others?\"** **\"Nobody's answering, sir.\"** I looked past him down the corridor. Windows on the left, grey stone, the long row of doors with their numbers painted badly by someone in a hurry. Every one of them shut. Twenty-two horses. A garrison of two hundred men. A bell that had not rung. 'No one is answering,' I thought. 'In a building with two hundred men in it.' Somewhere under my ribs, the thing I had been carrying since Parshara sat up and paid attention. **\"The** **stablemaster** **sent you here****?****\"** I asked. **\"He said anyone. He said get anyone.\"** **\"At this hour?\"** **\"He said it hours ago, sir.\"** The boy's voice cracked and he pretended it hadn't. **\"I've been knocking since.\"** I should have said no. I want that written down plainly, because everything after this is my own fault and I would like the record to show that I knew. There is a version of that night where I closed the door, lay back down and was gone over the eastern wall before the bell. It was not a difficult version. It required nothing of me at all. But the boy was standing in a corridor in wool three sizes too large, in the cold, being ignored by two hundred sleeping men, and I had been that boy. Not in this life. In the one before. There is a particular kind of tired that comes from being small and having to ask, and it does not leave you when you grow. **\"...Lead,\"** I said. He looked up. **\"Sir?\"** **\"Lead, before I change my mind.\"** ******* The stable yard was on the northern side of the wall, tucked into the elbow where the garrison met the curtain, and the wind came down that elbow like water down a chute. Twenty-two horses. I counted them twice, because counting is what I do when I am nervous, and got twenty-two both times. The boy — his name was Ivan, and he apologized for it, which told me everything about how his month had gone — dragged a sack of feed off the cart and started on the near stalls. I took the far ones. For a while it was fine. The work was good. That surprised me. Grain, water, the specific weight of a horse's head coming down into your hand . A little hot breath in the cold. It was the first ordinary thing that had happened to me in some days, and I found I was doing it carefully. Then I noticed the quiet. Not the ordinary quiet of a walled yard at night. Not the held-breath quiet of a place where everyone is asleep. **Quiet.** I stopped with my hand on a bucket and listened — not hunting for a particular sound, but letting the whole of it arrive and sorting it. Wind in the slates. The curtain creaking. A horse shifting two stalls down. Ivan's boots, small and quick. And nothing else. No crickets. The yard backed onto the western ditch, and the ditch ran the whole length of the outer wall, and every night of my life that I could remember — this life and the other one — a ditch in autumn had sound coming out of it. Water in a place, grass above it, and something living in between. It was silent. 'Oh,' I thought, with a calm I did not feel. 'Someone has taken the crickets away.' **\"Ivan****,\"** I said, and kept my voice ordinary. **\"How long since the last** **hourly** **bell?\"** He thought about it. **\"Not since I started, sir. That's the thing. I thought I'd missed it and I—\"** **\"Go inside.\"** **\"But the feed—\"** **\"Go out, to the other stable wing****.** **Now.** **Walk, don't run, and if anyone stops you say** **that** **you were sent to check the register** **to confirm the entries****. Say it back to me.\"** **\"I was sent to check the register** **to confirm the entries for the day****.\"** **\"Go.\"** He went. He looked back twice. I did not watch him the second time, because by then I had already turned around. There was no one in the yard. I want to be precise, because later I had to be. Eleven stalls down one side, eleven down the other, a cart, a feed store with its door hanging open, a gate to the wall walk. Two lanterns. A well-cover. Me. No stablemaster. No guard. I had been feeding horses for the better part of an hour with a man beside me, and I had not once asked myself who had unlocked the far stalls. 'Not now,' I prayed, and reached for it out of sheer habit. The world half-dissolved in my eyes, my senses came alive all at once, and there was nothing there. No thin pale line at the edge of vision. No grading. No warning. The Archive was not in this world. So the part of me that had been listening for it all evening, the part that had grown used to being told things, went suddenly and completely alone. And in the place where the warning should have been, I found that I already knew. 'Behind me.' I threw myself forward. Not a step. Not a dive. I put everything I had into being somewhere other than where I was, standing open in the air behind me, and I went through it face-first into the cobbles. Behind me, the wall burst apart. Not a hole. Not a crack. The stretch of wood and stones came back as splinters and one enormous block that turned twice in the air and landed four feet from my head hard enough to jump the ground. Wind poured through. The horses screamed. I lay on my face in the dirt and my senses which had heightened to its limit caught the sound of something slicing the air reaching for my neck behind me. ***"},{"t":"57. The World and the Water","k":"Chronicles · chapter","u":"chronicles/c57.html","s":"{Arin's POV} It came. I moved before the thought arrived — weight on my left toe, heel kicked back, feet flipped under me. Then I turned, and dropped. The air blurred through where my chest had been a moment ago, and the stable came apart. Not smashed. Cut — a line through the wood and the horses. “**Kghh****”** I exhaled forcefully through my mouth. *‘I would have been severed in half had that connected’* I back flipped putting some difference between us. He came through the opening he had made. Short. Neat. Gloves indoors. A bluish glow faded off his skin like heat off stone. The air around him moved. **\"****Tarkhan****,\"** a woman's voice came from behind him. **\"You have made it a public matter** **now****.\"** My eyes widened hearing that name *‘Supreme commander* *Tarkhan**?*’ I wished for such a scenario to just be my misunderstanding. Yet it was shattered the next second. She came through next. Red coat, two curved swords worn low and crossed. Seeing her I remembered a name from the previous conversation **“****Sellen** **Orath****?”** I spoke to myself mulling and finding a way to escape. She turned her eyes towards me the moment I muttered her name and smiled. **\"The city is sealed,\"** she said. Pleasant voice. **\"You are not going anywhere.\"** She said it like the ending had already been written. Behind Tarkhan, seven men stepped onto the cobbles. I turned. I turned, those seven men stood exactly one foot from each other. Draped in deep violet from head to toe. I couldn’t see their faces as it was covered by their hood. I turned my gaze back to Sellen who took out the curved swords from her waist. **“Stay out of my hunt old man”** she warned Tarkhan as she disappeared from her spot reappearing in front of me in split of a second. *** The world dissolved partially as the time slowed, I saw her sword coming in an arc. I closed in and tried to incapacitate her with a full fledged blow on solar plexus but she kicked off the ground and her knee slammed straight into my jaw. *Crack* Excruciating pain flared and my teeth rattled. But I managed to get hold of her leg. I turned around to slam her into the ground *[Curse of Despair is Active]* *[-1% Sanity]* I had one plan: get out. I was not there to win. She cut the air where I had been. I opened a null four feet left and she was already turning before I arrived. I opened one past the gate and she cut that too — not at the hole, at the air on the far side, where anything coming through would have to be. She had read the trick before I finished using it. So I used the holes to hide behind. A null between us, me moving along the rubble behind the noise. She kicked through it. One under a trough, and I rolled; she stepped over the trough. Every move I had, she had an answer that cost her nothing. Never overcommitted, never gave me the half-second a man needs to make a mistake look like luck. Five days I had lived on other people's mistakes. Tonight nobody was making any. Then she came at me properly — not a probe, a decision. She was stronger than me. Not a little. The first exchange put me on one knee; the second took the sword out of my hand into the wall; the third put her boot in my chest and I went back into the trough hard enough to lose the last of my air. I got up. She let me, which was worse than if she had not — it meant she was not worried. I swung at her with my fist because it was the only weapon left, and she caught my wrist, turned it, and put me down again. ******* *[Sanity: -2%]* The fear did not go away. It went quiet, and under it something opened. The colour drained out of the world at the edges. My heart went slow. And the thinking — the part that had kept me alive for five days — did not stop so much as get put aside, the way you put aside a coat when the work gets heavy. I came off the ground at her. ******* She caught the first blow and it moved her. That was all — it moved her, and she looked at me with an expression I had not seen on her all night: surprise, and behind it, arithmetic. Then she stopped being careful. It was still not close. She put two cuts on me — forearm, ribs — and neither landed on a man who had moved. I had not moved. I had eaten them to stay in reach. She hit me with everything; I came back from worse positions each time. I hit her in the mouth. She broke my nose, and I got a hand in her coat and pulled her into the wall. This is the part I cannot explain. Nothing hurt. My body was a thing I was using, and the more it broke the less it argued. She needed help. The Crimson Gale needed help, and I found that funny in a way I could not have explained. ******* The Supreme Commander walked into the yard and put himself in front of me. He did not hurry. The stones under his feet came apart as he came, and the ground where I stood began to go thin and grey. I threw myself at him anyway. Null under his feet. Nothing. Null behind his head — he had already moved. My fist at his throat and he was not there and then he was, and the back of his hand took me across the yard into the gate. I got up. I put a hole in the air, went through it, came out behind him, and he had turned before I arrived — he had watched me do it four times and stopped needing to watch. I put another and another. Twenty seconds each, then gone. Six, seven, each further into the yard with fewer places left. Somewhere in the eighth my hands stopped working and my knee stopped taking orders. I fell, got up out of pure refusal, and he broke my arm. I did not feel it. That is the truth and it is not a boast. I looked down, understood the shape was wrong, and hit him with the other one. ******* He raised the sword the way a man raises an axe at a fallen tree. There was no room left behind me, and no hole I could open that would be finished before it landed. The sword was in the air in front of my face. I could see the line of it, the light on the edge, a notch three inches up from the tip, and its shadow by my head. And the world stopped. Not slowed. Stopped. The sword hung and did not move. The dust did not move. Her red coat did not. And I could see. Dots of golden light. Hundreds, then thousands, through the air in every direction, sitting in no order at all. Some close enough to touch, some specks of dust in sunlight. None of them moving. And every one joined to the others by a thin gold line. The lines crossed and met other dots and crossed again, and the whole of it hung in the stopped air like a net thrown over the world — through the stable, the wall, him, me. I did not know what they were. I do not know now. There was one behind Tarkhan, three feet from his shoulder blade. I reached. ******* The world started again. I was standing behind him. His sword finished its swing through the air where I had been and took the ground with it. He came out of it turning — a man who had hit nothing and knew what that meant — and found me behind his shoulder. His face changed. Not fear. Worse. The face of a man doing sums he could not finish. \"You moved,\" he said. I said nothing. My hands shook and something ran out of my nose. The woman had stopped dead, both swords half raised. The seven hoods turned at once, the way a flock of birds turns, looking at me. \"He moved,\" she said, very quietly. ******* The second from the end of the row lifted one hand. No sound, no wind, no flash. I was on my knees, arms pulled behind me by nothing, held flat the way a nail is held by a plank. My lungs stopped obeying me. I reached for the craft, and the door opened onto a wall. I fought the word four times and got nothing, and on the fifth understood there was nothing to fight. It is not a binding, I thought, swaying. It is a word. They said a word, and the world agreed with them. Then a sound at the far end of the yard. Boots on wet stone. A head turning. Panak came around the end of the row into the lantern light. ******* They put me on my knees in the long hall, on the blue stone, under the imperial portrait with the crown's seal below it. Panak had washed his face. Between sealing a city and taking apart a yard, he had found time to wash and put his coat on properly, and I do not know why that frightened me more than the wall. \"I did not kill her,\" I said. \"I did not kill anyone. I never went near the queen. I was in the passage with—\" My voice came apart. \"I never touched one person since I came to this country. Not one. Ask the soldiers. Ask the man who found the bodies. Please.\" Please. It came out on its own. I had promised myself I would never be a man who says it, and I said it four times. Panak listened. \"I know,\" he said. The record-keeper's brush stopped. \"I know you did not kill her,\" he said again, and there was no kindness in it. He said it the way a man says the weather. \"I knew before I left the palace. The wound was not made by a Parsharan blade. Every man here knows it, and the report I sent to the capital left that part out.\" \"Then why—\" \"Because it does not matter what you did.\" He looked up at the portrait and said the rest to it. \"I have spent my whole life kneeling to a crown that never looked down at my province. My queen is dead and the crown is laughing — laughing because I kneel on the floor of my own hall to a story about a Parsharan boy, because that is what the crown asked for, and I am doing it.\" \"This is not for the queen,\" he said. \"She is past being served. Not for the crown either. This is for my city. If I hand the crown a dead Parsharan, the war does not come here. If I am too proud to do it, two hundred thousand people pay for my pride with their throats. And I am not a great man, boy, so I do not get to be proud.\" \"Put your head down.\" My arms were held, my legs did not work, the craft was a locked door, and I had just begged a man in front of his guards — and I lifted my head and looked at him. \"I did not kill her,\" I said. Flat. Not a plea this time. I wanted it on the record. \"No,\" said Panak. \"You did not.\" Then he looked past me and said, in a voice that carried to the far end of the hall: \"Record: the prisoner did not confess. The prisoner did not confess at any point, and he is to be recorded as not having confessed.\" The brush moved."},{"t":"Aurorion Empire","k":"Chronicles · 05 Worlds and Locations","u":"chronicles/lore/chronicles--aurorion-empire.html","s":"\n  History\nThe land previously was known as the land of 'Sama'. In the Era of war there were no kingdoms established and civilizations ran amok. People lived in groups and no order existed.\n\nAt that time there was a shrine, an empty shrine at the heart of this land where now stood the royal castle after 600 years. In that empty shrine resided a blind man. He may have been blind but his ears, nose, touch and intuition were so great that he could live like a normal person. \n\nAt that time there was no order and so to protect himself from bandits. He developed a method, he firmly believed that a human was never meant to just crawl on the land and there must have been something which he can accomplish and so through arduous exercises and means compiled with his terrifying control over his senses he managed to successfully develop a method.\n\nHe writes in the text which is the only known text from that era. \n\"I pass on this method to whoever reads this, By concentrating on your breath, take in the air and feel it going through your nerves, next guide the coolness spreading in your chest towards your legs, contracting them and releasing moment after all the while holding your nerves in place, speeding up circulation in bursts. This shall ultimately give you mastery over 'Speed'.\"\n\nThis is the only letter and along with it are just his diary about the chaotic life of those days.\nHowever at the end of that diary there is an interesting entry.\n\"They are coming, many of them, they told me to close the shrine. But, How do I put it? They don't seem.....alive. Their voices are like a hiss of a snake and their touch, it is cold.\"\n\nSo the current royal family"},{"t":"Dahan Sultanate","k":"Chronicles · 05 Worlds and Locations","u":"chronicles/lore/chronicles--dahan-sultanate.html","s":"  Dahan Sultanate\n\n  status: unresolved.   Named, not developed. Do not invent detail.\n\nNamed on the map (crescent emblem) and in ch 49's list of powers. Dahan City. The arid south-west.\n\n   What is actually established\n\nThe published text mentions this place but does not characterise it. Anything beyond the name, the emblem on the map, and the settlement is   not canon  .\n\n   To develop\n\n- Government and succession\n- Relationship to Kalman's Oath (see   Nazarana Kingdom  )\n- Its stake in the Eldoris war\n- Why it is in the scenario at all — what it is  testing \n\n   Related\n\n  00 Eldoris Overview   ·   Map  \n"},{"t":"Eldoris","k":"Chronicles · 05 Worlds and Locations","u":"chronicles/lore/chronicles--eldoris.html","s":"  Eldoris\n\nSee   00 Eldoris Overview   for the full note.\n\n-   Parshara Kingdom  \n-   Nazarana Kingdom  \n-   Aurorion Empire  \n-   Dahan Sultanate  \n-   Order of Zenos  \n"},{"t":"Eldoris — Civilizational Research","k":"Chronicles · 05 Worlds and Locations","u":"chronicles/lore/chronicles--eldoris-civilizational-research.html","s":"  Eldoris — Civilizational Research (v2)\n\n  v2 supersedes v1.   The map has been read; the cosmology has been confirmed by the author; the lovers are identified. Sections marked   ESTABLISHED   are on the page or author-confirmed. Everything marked   PROPOSED   needs your ruling.\n\n---\n\n   Part 0 — Confirmed canon from the author (treat as fixed)\n\n  Fact   Status  \n --- --- \n    Aezion   is the   creator deity   of the continent of Eldoris   ESTABLISHED  \n    Azevalgar   is an   outer god   seeking to   destroy   the continent   ESTABLISHED  \n  Aezion and Azevalgar are   opposed   — they are   not   the same being   ESTABLISHED  \n  Parshara's \"old god\"   is   the creator deity —  but the people no longer remember    ESTABLISHED  \n  Aezion's divinity   shattered into four great spirits     ESTABLISHED  \n  Those four became   four jewels     ESTABLISHED  \n  The jewels   gave birth to the individual spirits of the elements   — e.g.   Noor-e-Zaya     ESTABLISHED  \n  The lovers are   Vazir   (crown of Nazarana) and the   younger princess of the Aurorion Empire     ESTABLISHED  \n  Their relationship was   secret  , and   both died before they could be together     ESTABLISHED  \n\n  This kills my v1 proposal outright.   I suggested Aezion and Azevalgar might be the same being. They are enemies, and that distinction is the spine of the world:   one made the continent, the other is unmaking it from outside.  \n\n---\n\n   Part 1 — The cosmology, now that it's fixed\n\n    1.1 The chain of descent\n\n   \nAEZION (creator)\n   │  divinity consolidated into ONE jewel\n   │  → broke into FIVE when the priest wrote the hymns\n   │    (rain ran the ink; the words were pronounced differently)\n   ▼\nFOUR GREAT SPIRITS  ← greater than a Spirit King\n   │  (the four misheard readings)\n   ▼\nELEMENT SPIRITS  (Noor-e-Zaya and others)\n   │\n   ▼\nSPIRIT KINGS  (Tarkhan — Dispersion; Panak — earth)\n   │\n   ▼\nTHE BLESSED — ordinary glow-coloured fighters\n   \n\n  The fifth piece is Aezion's actual power, intact, hidden somewhere.   Full account:   The Shattering  .\n\n  ESTABLISHED on the page:   glow colour marks a blessing. Ch 41 lists  \"red, blue, green and brown\"  as the common glows a fighter will have seen; a   white-grey   glow is rare enough to be mistaken for a Spirit King's. Ch 54 gives Panak a   brown   glow and Vazir an   orange   one. Ch 57 explains Tarkhan's as  \"the blessing of the Spirit King of Water, and the quality of it was Dispersion.\" \n\nFive rungs, not three:   blessed → Spirit King → element spirit → great spirit → Aezion.  \n\n    1.2 What \"Noor-e-Zaya\" tells us\n\nA shopkeeper in Tilmis kneels mid-transaction and murmurs  \"I bow for thy blessings, our lord and savior Noor-e-Zaya\"  (ch 46). He is   not   praying to Aezion. He is praying to an   element spirit   — one of the jewels' children.\n\n  PROPOSED — the most important structural implication:  \n\nEvery blessing on the continent descends from Aezion consolidated into one jewel, broken into five when the priest's hymns were smeared by rain. So   every blessing is a fragment of the creator.   A blessed man carries a piece of the god who made the world, and does not know it.\n\nThat makes the power system a form of   unwitting relic-worship  , and makes Azevalgar's strategy obvious and terrifying: he does not need to defeat an army. He needs to   collect or corrupt the fragments.   Every blessing taken is a piece of Aezion removed from the board.\n\nIt also means the spirits are not gods. They are   debris   — and people pray to debris because the original is not there any more. That is exactly why Parshara \"does not remember,\" and why a border guard can sneer at an old god.\n\n    1.3 The four jewels and Nazarana's four cities\n\n  PROPOSED — the strongest single idea in this document.  \n\nNazarana is governed from   four   cities over   four   branches of business. The creator shattered into   four   great spirits, which became   four   jewels.\n\n  City   Branch   Proposed jewel / element  \n --- --- --- \n    Tilmis   (north)   scholarship, post, the World History Tree   the record —  what is remembered   \n    Ganj   (east)   ores, minerals, appraisal   earth —  what is weighed   \n    Baazar   (west)   the largest market   — the one that circulates  \n    Somalis   (south)   information, mercenaries   — what is known  \n\nNow read what your vault already states, with new eyes:\n\n   \"Kalman's Oath when broken will curse that lineage forever and no one from that lineage shall receive the blessings of spirits for years to come.\" \n\n  Why would breaking an oath cost a lineage its spirit blessings — unless the spirits themselves are the oath-keepers?  \n\n  PROPOSED:   Kalman's Oath is sworn   on the four jewels  , and enforced  by the four great spirits , not by law. It is not a legal fiction with a curse attached. It is a   contract with held collateral  : the jewels physically rest in the four cities, and a broken oath means the spirits withdraw.\n\nThree consequences, all free:\n\n1.   The governors' private armies have a purpose.   They are not vanity. Each garrisons the jewel in their own city, so no three can move against the fourth without losing a spirit.\n2.   The oath is breakable — by seizing a jewel.   Not by lawyering it. That is a heist, not a coup, and a heist is a better plot.\n3.   Azevalgar's route into the continent is the jewels.   He does not need an army to destroy Eldoris; he needs four thefts. And the conspiracy that approached the Nazarana crown (ch 48) was offering precisely the help that would get him close.\n\n  It also explains why the crown has no army of its own   — it does not need one. It needs the jewels to stay where they are. The crown's entire power is the  continued existence of four objects.  That is a magnificent amount of fragility for a state.\n\n    1.4 The seal is already broken, and the text says so\n\nCh 46, in Tilmis, the city of records:\n\n   \"The statue was tall, its surface webbed with faint cracks that betrayed its age.   The stone had lost its luster  , but the figure still carried an air of majesty. The plaque at its base read:   Protector of the Land of Kuru   Lord Azevalgar\" \n\n  Azevalgar — the outer god who wants to destroy the continent — is the protector deity of Nazarana's founding oath, and the one the oath is \"overseen by.\"  \n\nYour vault says it plainly:  \"The Kalman's oath is said to be overseen by Azevalgar (Protector of Kuru). Even the pale men seem to know about Kalman's oath.\" \n\n  This is the best thing in your worldbuilding and I do not think it has been used yet.  \n\nNazarana's entire constitutional order — the thing that stops four merchant armies eating each other — is guaranteed by   the enemy of the world.   The oath that keeps Kuru intact is administered by the god trying to end Eldoris.\n\nWhich means:\n\n- The oath has   never   been a protection. It is a leash, and Nazarana has held the wrong end of it for six hundred years.\n-  \"No one from that lineage shall receive the blessings of spirits\"  is not a punishment Azevalgar invented.   It is what Azevalgar does.   He takes fragments of Aezion. That is the mechanism.\n- The   pale men knowing about Kalman's Oath   is not trivia. They are his agents, and they know the instrument because their master wrote it.\n-   His statue stands in Tilmis, the city that records everything, and nobody questions it.   The most important fact in the setting is a public monument in the most literate city on the continent, and the population walks past it.\n\n  One line of dialogue fixes the arc's stakes:   someone asks why the Protector of Kuru is a cracked, lustreless statue — and the answer is that it was never a statue of a protector. It is a   receipt  .\n\n    1.5 The exemption — confirmed\n\n  ESTABLISHED (author).   The oath binds the crown, the four governors and their lineages. It does   not   bind the   Order of Zenos  , because   Azevalgar created the Order.  \n\nSo Zenos can betray a governor, or the crown, with   no backlash whatsoever   — no curse, no lost blessings, nothing.\n\nThat makes the four governors' position exactly as bad as it looks:\n\n  They are protected from   They are exposed to  \n --- --- \n  Each other     Zenos — completely and costlessly    \n  The crown   —  \n\nThe oath froze the four houses in place for six hundred years, and then a party that exists outside the oath was given the freedom of the board.   They are locked in a cage with a guard who works for the escapee.  \n\nRead   Kalman's Oath   for the full mechanic, including the two consequences the prose should carry:  a governor can be punished for trying to leave but never for what he does , and  the only defection available is joining the organisation that is hunting him .\n\n---\n\n   Part 2 — The map, read and checked against the prose\n\n  File:    Eldoris continent.png , 1536×1024. (Author's copy at  Desktop/Killer Petals/ .)\n\n  Title cartouche:    THE CONTINENT OF EIDORIS — In the Age of Veiled Skies \n\n  Seas:   FROSTWARD SEA (north) · THE VEILED SEA (east) · SUNSET OCEAN (west) · SOUTHERN EXPANSE (south)\n\n  Legend:   Major Kingdom · Minor Kingdom · Sultanate / Empire · Free City / Republic · Fortress / Stronghold · Major City · City / Town · Road / Trade Route · Border\n  Scale:   0–500 km. Compass rose, north up.\n\n    2.1 Regions and where they sit\n\n  Region   Position   Emblem  \n --- --- --- \n    Frostgate Hold     far north-west, on the Frostward coast   snowflake  \n    Vaeldor Principalities     north, inland behind the northern range   shield  \n    Svitavar / Sylvaris Enclave     north-central   circular seal  \n    Nerith Kingdom     north-east   shield  \n    Aurorion Empire     west-central, large   gold double-headed eagle  \n    Parshara Kingdom     south-west,   on the west coast     —  \n    Dahan Sultanate     south-central / west   crescent  \n    Nazarana Kingdom     east-central, large   white tree  \n    Velmor Duchy     west/south-west of Nazarana   —  \n    Galdwn Marches     south of Nazarana   —  \n    Lorven Republic     south, coastal   shield  \n    Eldrith Kingdom     south-east   circular crest  \n\n  Samarian Mountains   run north–south through the centre, forming   Aurorion's eastern border and separating it from Nazarana.  \n\n    2.2 Map versus prose — agreement\n\nFour checks, four passes:\n\n-   Vaeldor is north, just above Aurorion   — ch 37:  \"The principality of Vaeldor according to the map was situated in the North, just above Aurorian Empire.\"  ✓\n-   Frostgate Hold is at the north-west corner   — ch 37:  \"On the left North corner there was Frostgate hold.\"  ✓\n-   Hearthglen sits at the foothills of the Samarian range   — ch 39:  \"a major city situated at the foothills of the great Samarian range known as Hearthglen.\"  ✓\n-   Parshara is on the western coast   — required by the marine concrete: a sea-metal industry needs a coast. ✓\n\n  This is a well-built map.   The geography does real work: Parshara's concrete needs the sea, Aurorion's mountains make it defensible, Nazarana sits astride the eastern trade.\n\n    2.3 Map versus prose — problems\n\n  1.  Tilmis ,  Ganj ,  Baazar ,  Somalis  are not on the map.  \nNazarana's entire constitutional structure — four cities, four governors, four jewels — is invisible. The map shows one large blue region with a tree. Either the four cities need founding, or the region should be drawn as four sub-regions, which is truthful to how it actually works.\n\n  2. Name spellings drift between map, vault and manuscript.  \n\n  Map   Vault note   Manuscript  \n --- --- --- \n   VELMOR Duchy     Velmor Duchies    \"Velmor Duchies\" (ch 48)  \n   GALDWN March    —   \"galdwin marshes\" (author)  \n   PARSHARA KINGDOM     Parshara Kingdom    ✓ consistent  \n   Svitavar / Sylvaris Enclave    —   \"Silvaris\" only as a   person   (ch 54)  \n   AURORION EMPIRE     Aurorion Empire    \"Aurorion\" ×4, \"Aurorian\"/\"Auroria\" elsewhere  \n\n   Silvaris Donna  is the governor of Ganj, and the north holds a  Silvaris/Svitavar Enclave .   Almost certainly an accidental collision — one of them should change.\n\n  3. Four regions exist on the map and nowhere in the manuscript:    Lorven Republic ,  Eldrith Kingdom ,  Svitavar/Sylvaris Enclave , and  Frostgate Hold  as a  region  (in text it appears only as a hold/fortress). Not contradictions — but four polities with emblems and no role, and if they never appear the map promises more than the book delivers.\n\n  4. The map carries no shrine, temple or holy site.   A continent whose religion is fragment-worship shows no fragments.\n\n---\n\n   Part 3 — The civilizations, with analogues\n\n    3.1 Nazarana — the Land of Kuru\n\n  ESTABLISHED.   Four cities, four governors, four branches of business, no nobles, private armies, Kalman's Oath, moustaches as male fashion, silk over-garment with a breast emblem and buttoned ankle trousers, forests north before Somalis, farms mainly in the east.\n\n  In the text (ch 46, Tilmis):   golden gates, smooth white stone walls, roads that look  \"carved from a single long stone,\"  lamp posts rather than glowing stones, monuments in many languages, checkpoints, a giant wheel,  \"children with wide smiles and plump bodies.\"  The post office is enormous,  \"numerous counters lined the walls, each attended by a different clerk,\"  and  \"the interior felt strangely cold, the opposite of the heat outside.\" \n\n  The governing line:   Arin sees the plump children and  \"the faces of thin, hollow-cheeked servants flashed in his mind.\" \n\n  ESTABLISHED (ch 54) — the four governors, named:  \n\n  Governor   City   Glow  \n --- --- --- \n    Razakh Altarum     Tilmis   —  \n    Panak Khulmann     Baazar     brown   (earth)  \n    Silvaris Donna     Ganj   —  \n    Zalima Eklaire     Somalis   —  \n\nThey attend council by   projection  , not in person —  \"With that, Silvaris's projection vanished.\"  Vazir's own aura is   orange  .\n\n  This corrects v1.   I proposed a physical meeting hall for the four governors. The text already has remote projection, which is better: every council scene has a body count of ghosts, and   Vazir is alone in a room with four armed men who are not there.   Keep it.\n\nIt also sharpens his line:  \"Everywhere I go in this castle, I can feel their gazes.\"  He cannot tell which shadows are projections.\n\n     Analogue: the Hanseatic League\n\n  Nazarana   Hanseatic League  \n --- --- \n    Four   governors of   four   cities     Four quarters  : Wendish, Saxon, Prussian-Livonian, Westphalian  \n  Merchants, not nobles   Merchant councils, no princely authority  \n  Private armies per city   Member cities raised their own contingents; no federal army  \n  One branch of business per city   Each quarter dominated by particular trades  \n   \"The things you cannot find anywhere will be found here\"    The four  Kontore : London, Bruges, Bergen, Novgorod  \n\n  Why the League failed, and what it means for you:   no central treasury, no navy, no way to compel a member that refused. It did not lose a war — it became   obsolete.  \n\n  PROPOSED flaw:   Nazarana's crown has   no army and no tax  . Its only power is an oath guaranteed by a hostile god. The state therefore ends the moment someone proves the oath is void — and per §1.4, someone already has, because Azevalgar's statue is cracked and lustreless in the open.\n\n  What to borrow — the  waqf .   Ottoman bazaar buildings were religious endowments; rents funded hospices and schools, and the property could not be sold, seized, or taxed. Make   Baazar's market sacred property   and Baazar becomes the continent's neutral ground  by law rather than arms  — and it gains a missing institution: a clerical class of accountants who administer the endowment. A market run by priests of the ledger is very nasty and fits a book whose cosmology is bureaucratic.\n\n  What to borrow — the  barīd .   The Islamic postal service was also the intelligence service; postmasters doubled as informants and reported prices, trials, weather and the conduct of officials to the capital.\n\nYou have already split this in two:   Tilmis owns the post, Somalis owns information.   Those are the two halves of one organ, in two rival cities, neither able to work without the other, both reading the other's mail, and the crown dependent on both. An espionage triangle that costs one sentence to establish.\n\n  What to borrow — Timbuktu.   A desert-edge caravan stop that became a scholarly capital through   private family libraries   rather than a state archive, with   books as a trade good  . Gives Tilmis a concrete economy: it does not own knowledge, it owns  copies , and the copying is the business.\n\n  What to borrow — the Sogdians.   A trading diaspora whose language became the continent's lingua franca, with colonies in every foreign city. Gives Somalis a reason to exist beyond thuggery: they are  everywhere , they speak  everything , and the information network is the by-product of being the only people who can talk to both sides.\n\n---\n\n    3.2 Parshara\n\n  ESTABLISHED.   Under Nazarana's sanctions. Has a Royal Messenger and a Royal Scholar (Arin and Hayato's assumed posts). Insignia checked at borders. Its people starve —  \"My daughter in law is only able to eat once in two days.\"  A Hearthglen guard sneers:  \"Aren't those the ones who still worship that old god?\" \n\n  Two new facts from the prose, both large:  \n\n  1. Marine concrete.    \"Made up when a certain ratio of sea metals are mixed along with infusing the energy of sea. Originating in Parshara kingdom this material thickens overtime when comes in contact with water.\"  The castle walls Arin sees have  \"visible pores\"  — that is the material.\n\n  2. Glowing stones.   Ch 54, Vazir to the council:  \"I want to resume importing   glowing stones from the Parshara Kingdom  , given the mounting cost of oil and other fuels.\" \n\n  This changes the sanctions completely.   Parshara is not merely a food producer under blockade.   Parshara is the continent's energy supplier.   Glowing stones are a fuel, Nazarana has been cut off, and the council's objection is economic before it is political. Nazarana lights its streets with lamp posts  because  the stones stopped coming.\n\nSo the blockade is not \"keep the enemy poor.\" It is   \"deny the enemy the market for the thing we cannot do without, so we can buy it cheaply later\"   — and the four governors profit from the shortage. A real sanctions dynamic, and it makes the war legible.\n\n     Analogue: Roman marine concrete + Zoroastrian fire\n\n  The concrete is Roman, not Persian.    Opus caementicium  — pozzolanic concrete with volcanic ash —   sets and strengthens in seawater   and self-heals cracks through growth of aluminium tobermorite and phillipsite. It is why Roman harbours outlived modern piers by two thousand years.\n\n  PROPOSED political consequence:   if Parshara's concrete strengthens in water and repairs itself,   Parshara's harbours cannot be besieged.   Not \"are difficult\" —  cannot . Every breakwater, sea-gate and quay on the continent is Parsharan work, and nobody else can maintain it. That is why there are sanctions instead of an invasion: you cannot storm a country whose walls heal, and you cannot afford to lose the only people who can pour a breakwater.\n\n  Give the recipe a lock.   Real pozzolana came from one district. Proposed: the \"sea metals\" are a specific sand from one Parsharan shore, and \"the energy of sea\" is not metaphor — it needs a spirit-blessed worker. The recipe is thus   simultaneously a mineral monopoly and a religious one  , and cannot be copied without both.\n\n  For the old god:   Zoroastrianism grades sacred fire by how many fires made it.\n\n-   Ātash Dādgāh   — household fire; may go out.\n-   Ātash Ādarān   — embers from the hearths of   four estates   (priest, warrior, farmer, artisan); kept burning continuously.\n-   Ātash Bahrām   — embers from   1,001 fires  , one from each trade in society, including fire struck by lightning; up to a year to consecrate.\n\n  PROPOSED — now that you've confirmed the old god is Aezion:   Parshara keeps a fire that has   never gone out  , assembled from embers carried in from every trade in the kingdom. Not a statue, not an idol.   The last surviving intact address of the creator.  \n\nOne object gives you:\n\n- A religion that is also a constitution — every profession is inside the flame, so the fire  is  the nation.\n- A war aim that makes the blockade monstrous: under sanctions, what Parshara cannot afford is   fuel  .\n- A reason the Royal Scholar's post matters — Hayato is  \"responsible for preserving the very identity of Parshara,\"  which now means keeping the fire's liturgy.\n- The reason nobody remembers Aezion: the fire is tended by a handful of people who may no longer know whose fire it is. They have fed a god for six hundred years without a name for him.\n-   A physical thing Arin can be shown once and never forget.  \n\n  And the killer detail:   if Aezion shattered into four spirits, and Parshara's fire is the last intact piece, then Azevalgar's most valuable target is not a jewel.   It is a flame that must be fed every day.  \n\n  Priestly titles:   Zoroastrian priests are  athravan , later misread as \"fire-priest\" (etymology disputed). Available if you want something archaic that isn't a direct borrowing.\n\n---\n\n    3.3 Aurorion\n\n  ESTABLISHED (vault, unusually complete).   Once called   Sama  . No kingdoms in the Era of War. At the heart of the land — where the royal castle now stands after   600 years   — stood an   empty shrine  , where lived a   blind man   whose remaining senses were so sharp he lived normally. He developed a method on the conviction that  \"a human was never meant to just crawl on the land and there must have been something which he can accomplish.\" \n\nHis words:  \"By concentrating on your breath, take in the air and feel it going through your nerves, next guide the coolness spreading in your chest towards your legs, contracting them and releasing moment after all the while holding your nerves in place, speeding up circulation in bursts. This shall ultimately give you mastery over 'Speed'.\" \n\n  The only known text from that era.   With it: a diary. The diary's final entry:\n\n   \"They are coming, many of them, they told me to close the shrine. But, How do I put it? They don't seem.....alive. Their voices are like a hiss of a snake and their touch, it is cold.\" \n\n  In the text:   Aurorion bears a double-headed eagle. Its castle walls are built in   marine concrete   (ch 35) — which, per §3.2, means   Aurorion's walls are Parsharan work.   A kingdom at war with the sanctioned kingdom, defending itself with the sanctioned kingdom's technology. Either a contradiction or a very good secret.\n\n     Analogue: pranayama, and scripture-as-title-deed\n\n  The method is real physiology with a wrong conclusion.    \"Holding your nerves in place, speeding up circulation in bursts\"  is breath-retention with vasoconstrictive control — the territory of hatha yoga manuals, Tibetan  tummo , and qigong.   Plausible but wrong.  \n\n  Use that.   Aurorion's martial tradition rests on a successful empiricist who misdiagnosed his own discovery. Every technique descended from him inherits the error, and the error is why the tradition has a ceiling.\n\n  Second analogue — scripture as title deed.   The Avesta, the Vedas, the I Ching: in each case one body of text from a lost age functions as the charter of a living institution.\n\n  PROPOSED:   the royal family rules because it holds   the only copy   of the blind man's text. Not strength, not election —   custody  . A monarchy whose legitimacy is archival, which is thematically exact for a novel about a world under review, and it creates a vulnerability that costs nothing:   burn one book and the dynasty is over.  \n\n  The diary entry is the most important object in your setting.   Six hundred years ago, at the founding moment of the greatest power on the continent, something arrived that was not alive, and   a man whose senses were superhuman could not classify it.   Not a man being frightened — a failure of category.\n\nNow that Azevalgar is confirmed as an outer god, this has a destination:   they were his, or the pale men's, or the Fog's — and the text has already shown you all three.   Whatever they were, the enemy came to the shrine   first  , before the kingdoms, and the shrine was empty even then.\n\n---\n\n    3.4 Vaeldor Principalities\n\n  ESTABLISHED.   Shield emblem, north, inland behind the range, forested. Arin's diplomatic destination.  \"Still no design\"  — buildings  \"made in such a way that maximized usability and made no effort to look good.\"  Frostgate is a frontier post where young men serve five years from sixteen (ch 41). Hearthglen's air is  \"absolutely opposite of Vaeldor principality\"  — warm and dry.\n\n  PROPOSED analogue:   the fractious German principalities, or better,   Irish/Scottish clan chiefdoms   — many small lordships, no single throne, an aesthetic of utility because nothing survives long enough to decorate, and a frontier institution that doubles as a rite of passage and a dumping ground for surplus sons. The five-year service forges a shared identity across principalities that share nothing else.\n\n  Free detail:   a garrison that turns over completely every five years cannot accumulate institutional memory. Anyone who has served longer than five years is either a criminal or a liar. One scene, free.\n\n---\n\n    3.5 Dahan Sultanate\n\n  ESTABLISHED.   Crescent emblem,  Dahan City , arid south-central/west, borders Parshara, Aurorion and Remtor. Nothing else in the text.\n\n  PROPOSED analogue:   the trans-Saharan caravan economy — camel trains of up to a thousand animals, oasis-to-oasis relay, salt-for-gold, merchants richer than the land they cross.\n\n  The strongest available idea:   Dahan's wealth is   water rights  , not gold. Whoever holds the oases holds the crossings, and the oases are shrines. That merges caravan economy with religious institution and makes Dahan the natural rival to Parshara's fire —   a nation of wells against a nation of flame, both holding something sacred that everyone else needs.  \n\n---\n\n   Part 4 — The real plot engine, already written\n\n  ESTABLISHED, ch 48.   Vazir's diary, an entry two months prior:\n\n   \"I don't know where these men have come from, but they gave me a choice. A choice where we could survive or even win back against the martial might of Aurorian Empire. It is just that they had two conditions which bugged me. \n   First they wanted me to   interfere with the trade routes of Parshara  , a kingdom forcing the country to go bankrupt. \n   Second was to let them deal with all affairs regarding   Aurorian, Parshara, Velmor Duchies, Dahan Sultanate and Nerith kingdom   overriding the authority of crown itself. \n   …I don't understand their origin and why are they so fixated on 'Parshara kingdom' but they have agreed on leaving once their desired outcome is achieved.\" \n\n  The whole arc's machinery, already on the page.   Read with the cosmology in hand:\n\n- The \"men\" are outside. No origin. They offered military salvation.\n- They demanded   (a)   bankrupt Parshara and   (b)   control of five sovereignties.\n- They are   fixated on Parshara   — where the fire is, and the only remaining intact piece of Aezion.\n- They promised to   leave   when the outcome is achieved. Not empire-building.   Collecting something, then going home.  \n\n  PROPOSED: these men are Azevalgar's.   The sanctions are not Nazarana's policy — they are the enemy god's procurement programme, executed by a desperate king who thinks he is buying a war.\n\n  And Vazir knows something is wrong   —  \"their origin,\" \"so fixated\"  — but cannot see the shape of it, because he has been told the enemy is Aurorion. He is fighting the wrong war, on the wrong continent, for the wrong god, and the diary is the only place he has written it down.\n\n  Which means Vazir is not a villain.   He is the man who signed the contract, and the arc's central tragedy is that he did it to save his people. It also makes him a perfect match for the younger princess of Aurorion:   both are being destroyed by an agreement neither understood.  \n\n---\n\n   Part 5 — The lovers, and what the objective actually asks\n\n  ESTABLISHED (author).   Vazir and the   younger princess of the Aurorion Empire  . Secret relationship.   Both died before they could be together.  \n\n  ESTABLISHED (ch 51).  \n\n   \n Re-Enacting: The Romance of the Four Kingdoms \nOBJECTIVE: Two lovers who come from different and unresolvable\n           backgrounds couldn't unify in the past. Unify them.\nEXTRA OBJECTIVE: ????\nREWARD: ?????\nPENALTY: Erasure.\n   \n\nEldoris is   not an exam   — it is a   re-enactment  . Your overview note says the scenario tests  \"what kind of author a candidate is.\"  The text says it replays a historical failure and asks whether it can be corrected.   Correct that note.  \n\n  The word that matters is \"unify,\" not \"reunite.\"   They were never together. There is nothing to restore.\n\n    5.1 The four kingdoms of the title\n\nThe scenario is   The Romance of the Four Kingdoms  , and the lovers come from   two   of them. That is the title's own problem, and a gift:\n\n  PROPOSED:   the four are   Parshara, Aurorion, Nazarana and Dahan   — the four powers on Vazir's own list, in the region where the map puts them. The others (Velmor, Galdwn, Nerith, Lorven, Eldrith) are the  field  on which the four collide.\n\nThe romance failed because it required   four   kingdoms to consent and only two people wanted it. That is why the failure was historical rather than personal — and why it cannot be fixed by putting two people in a room.\n\n    5.2 The problem the objective has, which the arc must solve\n\n  Both lovers are already dead.   So \"unify them\" has at least three readings, and the choice determines the ending:\n\n  Reading   What Arin must do   Cost  \n --- --- --- \n    A. Replayed alive   — the present Vazir and present princess  are  the roles, and this time can succeed   Bring them together in the re-enactment   Vazir is the man destroying Parshara. Uniting him with Aurorion's princess is treason on both sides.  \n    B. Posthumous   — they are dead and stay dead; Arin unites what they left   A marriage in death: joined houses, a shared shrine, a name spoken for both   He completes the objective with a  symbol , not a wedding. Cold, and very much this novel's register.  \n    C. The re-enactment is the second chance   — the fog replays the moment before their deaths, and Arin is inside it   Prevent their deaths  in the replay , knowing the original outcome   He is not fixing a relationship. He is trying to un-die two people already dead, with the countdown running.  \n\n  My read: C, with B as the twist.   C explains why there is a   countdown   at all — a re-enactment has a runtime because it is a  performance , and when the clock ends the performance ends and the original outcome stands. B is the answer to what \"success\" can mean when the people are dead, and it is the only version that lets the ending be true rather than sentimental.\n\n  But this is your call, and it is the largest single decision left in the arc.   I will not write into any of the three without your word.\n\n    5.3 What you said about the run\n\nYour instruction:  \"Taking them closer will only do good for Arin but he have to run now.\" \n\nSo Arin gets partial credit and must flee — he moves the lovers closer, earns something, and cannot finish. That is a good shape, and it has a consequence worth naming:   the objective does not fail, it is abandoned mid-progress.   If he can never return, the penalty is Erasure and the re-enactment resets. If he  can  return, the reader knows the objective stays open for a hundred chapters — a promise you have to be willing to keep.\n\n  PROPOSED:   give him a visible, literal marker of partial progress — something that measurably moved. A letter delivered. A name spoken aloud. One of them weeping. A statue's head turned. Then when he runs, the reader has watched a thing  almost  happen, which is the most painful possible outcome and the one that sells the next arc.\n\n---\n\n   Part 6 — Additions worth making\n\nOrdered by value.\n\n    6.1 Shrines, jewels, and markers\nA continent whose religion is fragment-worship should show fragments. Four jewels, four cities, one shrine in Tilmis where a shopkeeper kneels mid-transaction. The map should carry one marker; the plot should carry four.\n\n    6.2 The Book of Deeds\nA physical ledger in each capital listing what the kingdom is owed and owes — with   Kalman's Oath as the first entry  , still the first line six hundred years later. It makes the state's moral condition a number, gives you bureaucratic magic consistent with a cosmology of Editors and review, and gives Arin a scene where he reads Nazarana's ledger and finds Parshara's name on every page.\n\n    6.3 Currency as occupation\nNazarana's coin is copper and universal (a boy sells food for thirty of them, ch 46); Parshara's people eat every other day.   PROPOSED:   the coinage is Nazaranan everywhere and Parshara's mint has been idle a generation, because the metal comes from Ganj. Every transaction in the starving kingdom uses the enemy's money. Currency as occupation, felt in the hand.\n\n    6.4 Four aesthetics, four theories of permanence\nVaeldor builds for use because nothing lasts. Nazarana builds to be seen because the market rewards it. Aurorion builds one thing and fills it with a book. Parshara builds to last forever, and so builds in water.   One philosophical disagreement, showing up in the masonry.  \n\n    6.5 Articles of manufacture Arin can pick up\nThe prose is generous with light and sensation; what is missing is  objects . Each is a paragraph and each does a civilization's work:\n\n- A   pozzolanic brick  , still healing at the crack after a century in the water.\n- A   Ganj touchstone   — black, smooth, the only way to tell gold from gilded copper. The guild's real monopoly is not mining but   certification  : anyone can dig, only Ganj can say what a thing is worth.\n- A   Tilmis postmark   — an instrument, struck not written, that makes a letter real.\n- A   Baazar seal   — the physical form of the endowment: a mark meaning  this cannot be sold .\n- A   glowing stone  , and the fact that it is warm.\n- A   Frostgate service token  , issued at sixteen, returned at twenty-one, worth nothing anywhere else.\n\n    6.6 Move the Order of Zenos out of Eldoris\nYour vault files it as an Eldoris faction and the note admits the text does not support it.   It should not be an Eldoris institution.   A conspiracy  inside  the test can be solved by the test; a conspiracy that   infiltrated from outside   means the examiners are compromised — and you already have an  outer god  as antagonist. The Order belongs at world level, beside Azevalgar. Moving it costs nothing.\n\n---\n\n   Part 7 — Decisions I need from you\n\nBlocking, in order of weight.\n\n1.   Which reading of \"unify them\"?   A, B, or C. Recommendation:   C with B as the twist  , because C is the only one that justifies a countdown.\n2.   Are the four jewels physically in the four Nazarana cities?   If yes, Kalman's Oath is a contract with held collateral, the private armies make sense, and the heist structure exists.\n3.   Do the four great spirits correspond to the four element glows   (red/blue/green/brown, plus the rare white-grey), making Panak's brown earth and Tarkhan's water two of the four? Or are the great spirits a rank above the element spirits that Noor-e-Zaya belongs to?\n4.   Are \"the men\" of ch 48 Azevalgar's?   Everything in that diary reads as procurement.\n5.   Does Aurorion's concrete come from Parshara?   Ch 35 describes Aurorion's castle walls with visible pores, and your note says the material originates in Parshara. Either it is imported — a secret dependency in a war — or it is a continuity slip.\n6.    Silvaris Donna  (governor of Ganj) vs the  Silvaris/Svitavar Enclave  region.   Which gets renamed?\n7.   Do  Lorven Republic ,  Eldrith Kingdom  and the  Svitavar Enclave  appear in the planned arc?   They are on the map with emblems and absent from the prose.\n\n---\n\n   Part 8 — Method note\n\nThe map had to be read in crops: full-image vision analysis timed out five times, so it was split into a 520px overview plus north, north-west, north-east, south-west, south-east and Nazarana crops, converted to JPEG at 85–90 quality. That pipeline works and is re-runnable.\n\nRegion names were read inconsistently across passes ( Svitavar / Sylvaris ,  GALDWN / Galdon ,  Solstheim / Solinar , and one-off reads of  Toreth ,  Aethel ,  Remtor ). Those should be confirmed by eye.\n\n---\n\n   Related\n\n  00 Eldoris Overview   ·   Nazarana Kingdom   ·   Parshara Kingdom   ·   Aurorion Empire   ·   Vaeldor Principalities   ·   Dahan Sultanate   ·   Map   ·   02 Open Decisions   ·   The Editors   ·   Curse of Despair  \n"},{"t":"Map","k":"Chronicles · 05 Worlds and Locations","u":"chronicles/lore/chronicles--map.html","s":"!  Eldoris continent.png  \n   KINGDOMS\n\n  Aurorion Empire  \n  Nazarana Kingdom  \n  Parshara Kingdom  \n  Dahan Sultanate  \n  Velmor Duchies  \n  Vaeldor Principalities  \n"},{"t":"Nazarana Kingdom","k":"Chronicles · 05 Worlds and Locations","u":"chronicles/lore/chronicles--nazarana-kingdom.html","s":"    History\n- Founded at the same time as Aurorian Empire.\n- Known as 'Land of Kuru' in the era of war.\n- Founded by a Council of Merchants and overtime evolved into a hierarchial structure.\n- Governance is not directed by nobles but the governors of 4 Major cities overseeing different branches of bussiness.\n- 'Tilmis' of the North did scholarly bussiness and postal duties throughout the continent as well as recording all the major incidents on the continent in their famous 'World History Tree'.\n- 'Ganj' of the east did bussiness of ores and minerals, often famous for its accurate appraisals on rare minerals. It was also one of the great exporter of copper and granite.\n- 'Baazar' of the west didn't had any speciality yet thrived from tourism all over the world due to being the host of largest market. It is said that the things you cannot find anywhere will definitely be found here.\n- Tourists, rich men, women and merchants come here all year around to buy and sell goods.\n- Lastly comes 'Somalis' of the south, it specialized in information bussiness and mainly lived off mercenary, labour bussiness with a couple of hidden organizations which offered information for a reasonable price.\n- All governors have their private army, but due to 'Kalman's Oath' they cannot directly attack and usurp the crown since Kalman's oath gives the royal family  and their lineage absolute right over the land of Kuru, but on the other hand it is due to that the governors also have their positions solidified, since it also states that the crown cannot appoint anyone the ruler of any of the four major cities outside of the current governor's direct lineage.\n- Kalman's oath when broken will curse that lineage forever and no one from that lineage shall receive the blessings of spirits for years to come.\n- The Kalman's oath is said to be overseen by Azevalgar (Protector of Kuru). Even the pale men seems to know about Kalman's oath.\n-   The oath does NOT bind the   Order of Zenos     — Azevalgar created the Order, so the oath cannot harm them. Zenos may betray any governor or the crown with no backlash at all. Full mechanic in   Kalman's Oath  .\n\n   CURRENT SCENARIO\nThe governors are each promised separately (which they think that only each individual is promised and don't know that all of them are being toyed with) along with Kalman's Oath.\n\nSo they play along refusing to lift the sanctions and restrictions on Parshara and also instead of doing that they try to declare war against Parshara and pressure other countries to act against Parshara. To which Vazir handles saying that the news of Her Majesty dying must not be leaked outside otherwise it can backfire.\n\nVazir knows that he must leak the info somehow without being known.  and so he spends most of his time learning bread and by his sister's grave (which he does in order to make all the servants believe that he is very grief stricken).\n\n   World Building\nConcentrates on rich activities more. Hires Mercenaries more that actual soldiers. There are farms only on the east side mostly.\nThe traditional dress in a silk cloth with an Emblem on the breast pocket with comes till thighs and a tight black trouser, with buttons on the ankle end and elastic with a belt to tighten it.\nThe moustache is a trend among men, they make them feel more masculine.\n\nThere is a vast forest on the northern side before somalis. "},{"t":"Parshara Kingdom","k":"Chronicles · 05 Worlds and Locations","u":"chronicles/lore/chronicles--parshara-kingdom.html","s":"  Parshara Kingdom\n\n  status: unresolved.   Named, not developed. Do not invent detail.\n\nUnder sanctions and restrictions. The current arc blames its envoys for Ezra's death; Arin publicly doubts that.\n\n   What is actually established\n\nThe published text mentions this place but does not characterise it. Anything beyond the name, the emblem on the map, and the settlement is   not canon  .\n\n   To develop\n\n- Government and succession\n- Relationship to Kalman's Oath (see   Nazarana Kingdom  )\n- Its stake in the Eldoris war\n- Why it is in the scenario at all — what it is  testing \n\n   Related\n\n  00 Eldoris Overview   ·   Map  \n"},{"t":"Vaeldor Principalities","k":"Chronicles · 05 Worlds and Locations","u":"chronicles/lore/chronicles--vaeldor-principalities.html","s":"  Vaeldor Principalities\n\n  status: unresolved.   Named, not developed. Do not invent detail.\n\nNamed on the map (shield emblem); north-central west, forested.\n\n   What is actually established\n\nThe published text mentions this place but does not characterise it. Anything beyond the name, the emblem on the map, and the settlement is   not canon  .\n\n   To develop\n\n- Government and succession\n- Relationship to Kalman's Oath (see   Nazarana Kingdom  )\n- Its stake in the Eldoris war\n- Why it is in the scenario at all — what it is  testing \n\n   Related\n\n  00 Eldoris Overview   ·   Map  \n"},{"t":"Velmor Duchies","k":"Chronicles · 05 Worlds and Locations","u":"chronicles/lore/chronicles--velmor-duchies.html","s":"  Velmor Duchies\n\n  status: unresolved.   Named, not developed. Do not invent detail.\n\nNamed on the map and in ch 49. Settlement: Velmoor.\n\n   What is actually established\n\nThe published text mentions this place but does not characterise it. Anything beyond the name, the emblem on the map, and the settlement is   not canon  .\n\n   To develop\n\n- Government and succession\n- Relationship to Kalman's Oath (see   Nazarana Kingdom  )\n- Its stake in the Eldoris war\n- Why it is in the scenario at all — what it is  testing \n\n   Related\n\n  00 Eldoris Overview   ·   Map  \n"},{"t":"Arin Valen","k":"Chronicles · Characters","u":"chronicles/lore/chronicles--arin-valen.html","s":"  Arin Valen\n\n  status: proposed   — assembled from the published text (207 mentions) and the master bible.\n\n   Established by the published chapters\n\n-   Protagonist.   POV is first person, marked  {Arin's POV} .\n-   Age 13, becoming 14   through the published run.\n-   Reincarnated.   The story opens in a life that is over; he wakes into a world that is  \"not exactly the same\"  Earth.\n-   Family:   had a   brother   and a   father   (\"Father\" appears often, warm).   Ezra   is a close figure whose death drives the current arc — 15 mentions.\n-   Hayato   — the closest companion, 110 mentions; a Japanese-named boy, village near   Somalis  ; last published chapter has Arin thinking of  \"Hayato-san in a village near Somalis, of a fire I would not be able to light.\" \n-   Rhen   — a name Arin does not consider himself to be:  \"Not soldier. Not Rhen.\" \n-   Career premise:   the early chapters are an   examination   — aptitude, reasoning, current affairs, ethics,  Fog Phenomenology . He is being recruited by inter-world institutions.\n-   Wealth/status:   poor enough that the \"Labour Exchange Program\" and unpaid labour arc matter; he works.\n\n   Voice\n\nFirst person, dry, self-deprecating, fluent in the language of an exam he is failing. Casual profanity. He narrates his own humiliations without flinching and without self-pity —  \"I lay on my back in the dirt... and tried very hard to look like a man who had been kicked off a griffin.\" \n\nThat is unusually good comic register for the genre and should be preserved absolutely.\n\n   Inheritance and power\n\n  Undivine, External, Spiritual Energy of Shunya   →   Mythology and Rumor   (as  the False Prophet 's mirror).\n\n  Not yet confirmed on the page   whether the reader has seen Mythologise used.   UNRESOLVED   — check before writing ch 56.\n\n   The path, per the master bible\n\n1.   Mythology   — invent a myth.\n2.   Rumour   — spread it.\n3.   Belief   — it becomes real, and he can use what it makes real.\n\nCosts:   justification   (the lie must be argued for),   belief   (finite fuel), and   fragmentation   (each belief splits him further from himself). See   Personality Erosion  .\n\n   His central irony\n\nHe is a   forgery competing with an absence.     The False Prophet   grows by being mistaken for Arin, using no effort at all — it simply  is believed . Arin must work for every gram of belief he gets, and the impostor gets it free.\n\n   Physical / personality notes from the images on disk\n\n Arin Valen.png ,  Silver-Haired Crimson Imperial Portrait.png ,  The Many Moods of a Brooding Protagonist.png  — the character art suggests silver/pale hair and a brooding register.   Do not treat the images as canon   — they are reference art, and the bible does not describe him physically.   UNRESOLVED.  \n\n   Open questions\n\n- Does he keep the original Arin's memories, or only his own?\n- Is his family alive in this world, or is each one a different version?\n- What exactly did he lose to the Undivine path so far? The price should be visible by ch 55.\n\n   Related\n\n  02 Open Decisions   ·   Veil of Maya   ·   Spiritual Energy of Shunya   ·   Curse of Despair   ·   The False Prophet   ·   Hayato  \n"},{"t":"Hayato","k":"Chronicles · Characters","u":"chronicles/lore/chronicles--hayato.html","s":"  Hayato\n\n  status: proposed   — assembled from the published text (110 mentions, second-most-cited character).   Healthier ground than Arin's sheet: most of this is on the page.  \n\n   Established\n\n- Japanese-named; Arin calls him   Hayato-san   — respect, and a slightly older register.\n-   From a village near Somalis.   The newest published chapter has Arin thinking of  \"Hayato-san in a village near Somalis, of a fire I would not be able to light\"  — so Hayato is   away from home  , and Arin owes him something he cannot repay.\n- Currently   travelling with Arin  , fugitives together. They ride horses; Hayato unties them.\n- He is a   mentor-shaped peer.   His dialogue is the book's clearest thinking:\n\n   \"…ally distinguish between front line and negotiating table.\" \n   \"He knows he is not a spear. He is not trying to pretend otherwise. That already puts him above—\" \n\n   \"Those who lose their script are often the only honest ones you get.\" \n   \"He did something rare for his age group. He admitted he needed to reset instead of faking it.\" \n\n   Why he works\n\nHayato is the   only character who talks about honesty as a technique.   In a novel where the protagonist's power is literally forgery, the deuteragonist's virtue is admitting you have lost your place. That is a deliberate and well-built contrast — protect it.\n\nHe is also the character who   names what Arin is doing wrong   without being an antagonist. That is rare and valuable.\n\n   Open questions\n\n- How did they meet? During the examination, or after?\n-   Is he human?   Nothing suggests otherwise, but nothing confirms it either.\n- Does he know about Arin's pathway?\n\n   Related\n\n  Arin Valen   ·   02 Open Decisions  \n"},{"t":"Cosmology","k":"Chronicles · Cosmology and Metaphysics","u":"chronicles/lore/chronicles--cosmology.html","s":"  Cosmology\n\n  status: proposed   — imported from the master bible. Not canon until the author promotes it.\n\n   Core premise\n\n    The multiverse is a manuscript under review.  \n  Reality can be written, interpreted, divided, and rewritten.\n\nExistence is not a machine and not a dream. It is a   draft under editorial review.   Worlds are submitted, examined, found wanting or sufficient, and either permitted to stand or revised out of existence.\n\n   Structure of reality\n\n    Ordinary reality\nOne Earth, one life, cause and effect. Ordinary people are unaware they are inside a manuscript at all.\n\n    Supernatural reality\nThe three   Occult Substrates   — the media a manuscript can be written in. Fortune, weather, faith, myth, rumour and memory are not flavour; they are the ink. See   Spiritual Energy of Shunya  ,   Primordial Qi of Chaos  ,   Veil of Maya  .\n\n    Higher-order reality\nAbove the substrates sit   The Editors   and the reviewing apparatus. Reality is legible to them the way a page is legible to a reader.\n\n    The Manuscript\nFor reality to  be  a manuscript means it has an author, a version, a margin for error, and a reader. It also means it can be   plagiarised   — which is precisely what   Arin Valen   does.\n\n   The laws that follow\n\n1.   A claim must be justified.   Anything written into reality must be paid for. This is why  Mythology and Rumor  costs Arin something every time it works.\n2.   Belief is fuel.   A thing believed in becomes load-bearing, whether or not it is true. This is the engine of   The False Prophet  .\n3.   Division is a valid edit.   Reality can be split rather than rewritten — hence Divine vs Undivine as two alignments, not good vs evil.\n\n   Why the antagonists work\n\nAn Editor is not a villain. An Editor has   a standard  , and Arin fails it. The horror is procedural: he is not being hunted, he is being  reviewed .\n\n   Open questions\n\n- Does the manuscript have one author or a committee?\n- Is Earth an original or a copy? The published premise —  \"the same Earth you died. But it is not exactly the same\"  — implies a copy, which implies a forger.\n- What happens to a world after review fails?\n- Can a world refuse review? That may be what   Primordial Qi of Chaos   is for.\n\n   Related\n\n  The Editors   ·   Author Candidacy   ·   00 Stellar Archive Overview The Stellar Archive   ·   Power System Tree  \n"},{"t":"EDIT","k":"Chronicles · Cosmology and Metaphysics","u":"chronicles/lore/chronicles--edit.html","s":"  EDIT\n\nThe Archive's paid rewriting function. Arin does not merely receive rewards — he   alters their wording before accepting them.  \n\n   How it works (ch 22, in full)\n\nHe takes  Seed-Forging Art — Incomplete  and buys three changes:\n\n  Change   Cost  \n --- --- \n   Form  →  Transform    200  \n   Seeds  →  Cells    600  \n    condensed from elemental energy   →   into antennas capable of absorbing Stellar energy subconsciously     1,500  \n    Subtotal       2,300    \n\nThen he buys a   limitation   to reduce the price — cell transformation only at night, speeding up in powers of two with intense exertion. The Archive prices the restriction at   −3,500  , and he also folds in a benefit under the same clause. Total:   3,800  .\n\n   Why this is the best mechanical idea in the system\n\n  The EDIT function lets him pay to be clever.   Limitations reduce cost, so the optimal move is to design a narrow, strange, personal technique rather than a broad one. It rewards exactly the trait Arin has — unwillingness to accept the given wording — and it means his powers are  engineered , which is far more interesting than granted.\n\nIt also gives the Archive its best comedy, because it is an accountant watching a teenager commit small frauds:\n\n    This should be illegal  \n    Splendid, you totally covered the subconscious pulling of stellar energy…  \n\n   Note for the prose\n\nThe Archive   objects, prices, and complies  . It is never enthusiastic and never obstructive. Keep it as a clerk, not a conscience.\n\n   Open questions\n\n- Are there limits on what can be edited — can a reward's  existence  be rewritten?\n- Can a curse be edited? Nobody has tried.\n\n   Related\n\n  Wheel of Fortune   ·   Karmic Coins   ·   00 Stellar Archive Overview The Stellar Archive   ·   Seed Forging Art  \n"},{"t":"Fog Events","k":"Chronicles · Cosmology and Metaphysics","u":"chronicles/lore/chronicles--fog-events.html","s":"  Fog Events\n\n  status: proposed.   Named in the published text (ch 1, as  Fog Phenomenology ; ch 33, titled  Fog (1) ). Mechanism unresolved.\n\n   What they are\n\nProvisional zones. Inside a fog, reality is   under review   — legible to an editor, amendable, and not yet settled. Ordinary rules hold, but they hold  loosely .\n\nThe published text treats Fog as an academic subject:  \"Fog Phenomenology\"  is examined in the aptitude paper.   That is a superb choice   — it means the existence of fogs is common knowledge, treated as curriculum, and therefore banal. Nobody is frightened of the thing that will kill them.\n\n   Why they exist\n\n  Proposed:   a review in progress. See   Author Candidacy  . Where the manuscript is being examined, the paper goes thin — that thinness is fog.\n\n   What they do\n\n- Make worlds   amendable.   Edits land more easily inside a fog.\n- Make people   unrecorded.   The same property that lets Arin forge lets a candidate vanish.\n-   Gate promotion.   Passing review inside a fog is the proposed route to Candidacy.\n\n   Eldoris\n\n  00 Eldoris Overview 00 Eldoris Overview   is a fog world — the preliminary test, per the author. Pass and return to Earth.\n\n   Open questions\n\n- Are fogs natural, engineered, or   deliberate examination halls  ?\n- How long does one last? Days, centuries, subjective years?\n- What happens to the people inside a fog that   fails   review?\n- Can a fog be entered deliberately? If yes, by whom?\n\n   Related\n\n  The Editors   ·   Author Candidacy   ·   Cosmology  \n"},{"t":"Karmic Coins","k":"Chronicles · Cosmology and Metaphysics","u":"chronicles/lore/chronicles--karmic-coins.html","s":"  Karmic Coins\n\nThe currency of the     Wheel of Fortune     and of the     EDIT     function.\n\n   Earned (as shown in ch 22)\n\n  Source   Coins  \n --- --- \n  Written examination cleared   1,000  \n  Interview cleared   4,000  \n  Other   300  \n    Balance at ch 22       5,300    \n\n    Getting Admission in Institute of Inter World Studies shifts Host's Narrative Impact to a global scale.  \n\n  Note what the Archive is actually measuring:   not effort, not virtue —   Narrative Impact.   The system pays for story significance. That is a bureaucratic premise doing thematic work: a world under review pays its inhabitants in attention.\n\n   Spent (ch 22)\n\n- Wheel spins: 3\n- EDITing  Seed-Forging Art  into  Art of Stellar Vessel : 3,800 net\n-   Remaining: 1,200  \n\n   Why this matters\n\nArin says  \"I wanted to save these coins forever but man, old habits die hard.\"  He is a gambler with a fixed income and no discipline — and in ch 22 that habit costs him the   Curse of Despair  .\n\n   Open questions\n\n- Can coins be earned outside the institute system?\n- Do other people have balances, or is the ledger personal?\n- Can coins be transferred or stolen?\n\n   Related\n\n  Wheel of Fortune   ·   EDIT   ·   00 Stellar Archive Overview The Stellar Archive   ·   Curse of Despair  \n"},{"t":"Seed Forging Art","k":"Chronicles · Cosmology and Metaphysics","u":"chronicles/lore/chronicles--seed-forging-art.html","s":"  Seed-Forging Art\n\nArins first Art, won from the     Wheel of Fortune     in ch 22 and immediately rewritten via     EDIT    .\n\n   As received\n\n-   Method:   forming seeds within the body, condensed from elemental energy — similar to energy cores.\n-   Limitation:   seeds can only hold   non-terrestrial   elemental energies. Earth-origin flows cannot be contained.\n-   Decay:   seeds exhaust three days after creation.\n-   Method:   breathing in an environment full of a given energy creates seeds of that type.\n- Mastery: 0% (0/5)\n\nThe Archive explains the limitation with the best line in the chapter:\n\n    You are, ironically, oversubscribed to Earth already. Your damaged lungs, your frail constitution, all highly terrestrial problems.  \n\n   As rewritten —   Art of Stellar Vessel  \n\n  Changed   To  \n --- --- \n  Form   Transform  \n  Seeds     Cells    \n  Condensed from elemental energy     Antennas absorbing Stellar energy subconsciously    \n  Breathing in a charged environment     Any physical activity begins the process    \n\nPlus a purchased limitation: the transformation happens   only at night  , and speeds up in powers of two with intense exertion.\n\nNet cost   3,800   Karmic Coins. Mastery: 1/5.\n\n   Why the rewrite matters\n\nHe turned a passive, environment-dependent art into a   subconscious, always-on, exertion-powered   one — by paying for a restriction. The night-only clause is the admission ticket, and he hides an acceleration under it.\n\nThat is a design signature:   Arin does not take the deal on offer, he argues it down and re-drafts it.   It should recur every time he gains something.\n\n   Open questions\n\n- What are the five mastery tiers, and what does 5/5 look like?\n- Do cells persist forever, or decay like seeds did?\n- Does absorbing Stellar energy have a cumulative cost on his body?\n\n   Related\n\n  EDIT   ·   Wheel of Fortune   ·   Karmic Coins   ·   Arin Valen   ·   Curse of Despair  \n"},{"t":"The Editors","k":"Chronicles · Cosmology and Metaphysics","u":"chronicles/lore/chronicles--the-editors.html","s":"  The Editors\n\n  status: proposed   — imported from the master bible. Almost entirely unestablished in the published text.\n\n   Definition\n\nThe authorities who review the manuscript.   Not gods   — gods are contents of the manuscript. The Editors stand outside it, deciding whether it stands.\n\n   Purpose\n\nWhy does the manuscript require review?   Unresolved.   Two candidates:\n\n-   Quality control.   The multiverse is finite in some resource — attention, belief, stability — and worlds compete for it. Review is triage.\n-   Authorship.   Someone wants to  write , and every existing world is in the way. Review is a means of opening a slot.\n\nThe second is darker and fits the published tone better.   PROPOSED, not decided.  \n\n   Authority\n\n-   Can:   approve, reject, revise, reassign, erase.\n-   Cannot (proposed):   create from nothing. An Editor edits; it does not author. That limitation is Arin's opening — an Editor needs material, and Arin supplies forgeries.\n-   Bound by:     Author Candidacy  .\n\n   Relationship with reality\n\nThey are not  in  the multiverse. They relate to it as a reader to a page: total access, no presence. They cannot be fought — only   satisfied, evaded, or replaced.  \n\n   Internal structure\n\n-   Known Editors:   none named.\n-   Roles:   unresolved.\n-   Conflicts:   the bible's own implied tension is that different Editors hold different standards.\n-   Unknown members:   implied.\n\n   Relationship with Arin\n\n-   Initial:   he does not know they exist. He thinks he is cheating a world.\n-   Later:   he learns the world was already flagged, and his forgeries are either the evidence against it or its defence.\n-   Final:   unresolved. The published premise — the world forgets him while belief keeps him alive — suggests he ends   outside the review   rather than passing it.\n\n   Narrative function\n\nThe question they ask is the book's question:   who has the right to say what a world is?   Arin's answer is \"whoever can make people believe it.\" That is either the truth about authorship or the crime of it, and the novel should not settle which until late.\n\n   Open questions\n\n- Are the Editors people, systems, or a role anyone can be promoted into?\n- Is one of them already inside Arin's story under another name? Nothing confirms it.\n- Do they know Arin is forging — and is  passing  review worse than failing?\n\n   Related\n\n  Cosmology   ·   Author Candidacy   ·   00 Stellar Archive Overview The Stellar Archive   ·   The False Prophet  \n"},{"t":"Wheel of Fortune","k":"Chronicles · Cosmology and Metaphysics","u":"chronicles/lore/chronicles--wheel-of-fortune.html","s":"  Wheel of Fortune\n\n  status: canon   — used on the page in ch 22 and ch 32.\n\n   What it is\n\nA four-segment gamble bought with   Karmic Coins  . You spin, you get what you get.\n\n  Segment   Reward  \n --- --- \n  1     Art   — a technique or discipline  \n  2     Skill   — a discrete ability  \n  3     Artifact   — an object  \n  4      ????    — appeared in ch 22; it was   not there before    \n\n   Costs and rewards, as paid\n\n- Ch 22 balance:   5,300   coins (1,000 written exam + 4,000 interview + 300)\n- Spin 1 →   Skill: Omnilingual   (5 m radius, spoken and unspoken)\n- Spin 2 →   Art: Seed-Forging Art — Incomplete  \n- Spin 3 →    ????    →     Curse of Despair    \n- Remaining after edits: 1,200\n\n   The fourth segment\n\nThis is the one that matters. Arin notices it before he spins —  \"'???? wasn't there before,' I noted\"  — and spins anyway, because the Archive's warning was cut off mid-word.\n\n  The wheel is not safe and never was.   The presence of an unlabelled segment is the whole warning, and he read it as an opportunity. That should colour every future spin.\n\n   Editing\n\nThe Archive allows     EDIT     — buying alterations to a reward's wording before accepting it. Arin turned  Seed-Forging Art  into  Art of Stellar Vessel  this way, converting seeds into cells and breathing into any physical activity, at a net cost of 3,800 coins.\n\n  This is his most distinctive use of the system  : not drawing good rewards, but rewriting mediocre ones. It is the behaviour of a man who cannot leave a thing alone, and it is the same trait that fills Cheonma's blanks in another story entirely — here it fills the Archive's small print.\n\n   Open questions\n\n- Does segment four always produce a curse, or only sometimes?\n- Can a curse be  edited  like an Art can? (Nobody has tried. Someone should.)\n- Can the wheel be spun for someone else?\n\n   Related\n\n  00 Stellar Archive Overview The Stellar Archive   ·   Curse of Despair   ·   Arin Valen   ·   Karmic Coins  \n"},{"t":"1. Ajax Wilhelmshire","k":"Against the Heavens · chapter","u":"atth/c1.html","s":"“Ajax, are you listening?” Ajax Wilhelmshire raised his eyes. His mother stood in front of him, one hand resting on the handle of his wheelchair. Beside her stood his father, dressed in a formal tailcoat. His usually immaculate coat had already acquired a few wrinkles, courtesy of the preparations taking place throughout the mansion. “I am,” Ajax answered. His father looked unconvinced. “Then repeat what I said,” he demanded. “You're going to be busy. Mother will be busy. The servants will be busier than both of you. And I'm supposed to take care of myself.” A brief silence followed. His mother sighed. “Must you always make it sound so miserable?” she asked, guilt creeping into her voice. “I believe that's the most family-friendly definition anyone could come up with for my situation right now,” Ajax retorted. His father coughed. It was a suspiciously well-timed cough, considering the amusement visible in his eyes. Today was Ajax's little sister's birthday banquet. The entire mansion had been in a state of organised chaos since morning. Flowers were being arranged, silverware polished, decorations inspected, and an army of servants moved through the corridors carrying things Ajax dared not count. “Your sister will be occupied with her guests,” his mother continued. “Your father and I will have to receive the representatives. There may be people asking to meet you as well.” “I'll be fine,” Ajax said. “Don't overexert yourself. And if you need anything, tell someone,” his father added. Ajax nodded. His mother adjusted the collar of his shirt, although it was already perfectly straight. His father placed a hand on his shoulder. “Enjoy the evening, son.” “Hmm…” That appeared to satisfy them. His parents exchanged one final glance before leaving. Within seconds, they were discussing the seating arrangements and the arrival of some particularly important guests. Ajax watched them disappear down the corridor. Then he looked around the mansion. He had approximately an hour before the guests started arriving. An hour of freedom. Not that he had anywhere particularly interesting to go. *** The wheelchair moved with a quiet mechanical hum. Ajax travelled through the eastern corridor, passing two servants carrying a large arrangement of white flowers. “Those will look good in the main hall,” he remarked. The older servant stopped. “Yes, young master. We were just—” \"Take the tall sprays out first. The arrangement is too wide — it will not clear the doorframe otherwise.\" The servant glanced at the flowers, then at the entrance. “Oh. You're right.” Ajax continued on his way. A little later, he reached the front hall, where several servants were struggling to arrange the lighting. The wires running along the walls looked rather untidy. Ajax approached and studied their work. “Are the wires giving you trouble?” The servant turned around. “Ah! Yes, young master.” “Why not hang curtains along both walls? You could run the wires behind them. That would hide the mess without interfering with the lights.” Ajax pointed towards the walls as he explained. The servant's expression gradually changed as he followed the suggestion. “Thank you, young master!” He bowed before hurrying off to put the idea into practice. Ajax sighed and continued on his way. He wasn't doing anything particularly impressive. He simply noticed the little things that others tended to overlook. At least, more often than most people did. He soon reached the balcony overlooking the western side of the mansion. His hands slowed, and then stopped moving the wheels. Silver light fell across his face. He turned his head and looked towards the sky. Countless stars glittered above him, scattered across the darkness like a tapestry woven by the gods. Ajax moved a little closer to the railing. A familiar fascination settled in his eyes. He had once dreamed of exploring those skies. Before the accident, he too had possessed an inextinguishable desire to reach those distant heights. An accident had left his lower body completely paralysed. He could still vividly remember the pain—a kind of pain that made one forget how to scream. At first, he thought he had adjusted. The difficulties of daily life were frustrating, certainly, but he had managed to make peace with his new circumstances. It was the people around him who made that increasingly difficult. His friends gradually stopped visiting. At first, he assumed they were busy. Soon, even their replies to his letters became less frequent. At social gatherings, he began noticing the way people looked at him. Their gazes lingered for a moment before turning away, never returning. Ajax had always been lively and optimistic, someone who enjoyed a good conversation. But as his old friendships faded, he began looking for other ways to occupy himself. He read more. Studied more. Tried to become someone people would want to talk to. Perhaps, he thought, if he became interesting enough, they would come back. They didn't. Three months ago, on his seventeenth birthday, he finally understood how far apart they had grown. A few people still sent letters. Some of them were genuinely concerned, and he could feel it in their words. But there were so few of them. The representatives came, too. They delivered an overly formal speech, congratulated him, and wished him a healthy life. That was when Ajax finally accepted the reality he had spent so long trying to deny. And that was when Ajax Wilhelmshire changed. He slowly raised his left hand, gripping it tightly before extending it towards the sky. For a moment, he tried to grasp one of the stars. Of course, he caught nothing. It was an innocent gesture, perhaps. Or perhaps it was a yearning he had never managed to bury. He wanted to ask the stars a question. *What had he done to deserve such a fate? What crime had earned him this punishment*? But the distance between them was too great for any answer to reach him. Still, Ajax had made a decision long ago. Whatever the price, he would pay it if it meant he could stand again. And when that day comes, he would demand an answer—even if he had to use force. It was an impossible dream according to worldly standards yet, Ajax held onto that imaginary like a child and refusing to let go. He lowered his hand, turned his wheelchair, and hurried towards the main hall. He passed the dining room, where several people were arranging the place settings. “That's the wrong tablecloth.” A young maid froze. Ajax pointed towards the embroidered edge. “The birthday girl's table is supposed to have the silver border. The gold ones belong to the dinner tables.” “Oh! I apologise!” The maid hurried to correct her mistake. Ajax wasn't particularly fond of giving orders. He wasn't particularly fond of being treated like someone who needed constant supervision, either. Fortunately, the servants had gradually learned that he was perfectly capable of noticing things. Unfortunately, they had also learned that he noticed things they would rather he didn't. *** By the time Ajax reached the main hall, the preparations were nearly complete. The enormous chandelier overhead had been polished until it seemed almost like a second sun. Long tables stood along the sides of the room, decorated with flowers and carefully arranged silverware. Servants moved between them, making the final adjustments. And near the centre of the hall stood a boy. He looked around Ajax's age, perhaps a little younger. He had brown hair, green eyes, and a rather ordinary face. He wore the uniform of the household staff, though his collar was slightly crooked. The boy was standing in front of a decorative vase. More precisely, he was rubbing it. Ajax watched for a moment. The boy rubbed the same spot. Then again. And again. Ajax approached. The boy didn't notice him. His attention was entirely occupied by the vase. Ajax examined the object. It was a tall porcelain piece, decorated with blue patterns and a silver rim. Quite expensive, judging by the craftsmanship. There was a small black mark on its side. Ajax pointed towards it. “There's still a spot there.” The boy immediately looked down. “Where?” Ajax pointed again. “Right there.” “Oh.” The boy leaned closer and rubbed the mark with his thumb. Once. Then twice. He applied a little more pressure. Even then, the black spot refused to budge. Ajax watched silently. The boy pulled out a cloth and began polishing the area. A minute passed. Ajax glanced at the nearby servants. A few had begun watching. Three minutes. The boy changed the angle of his hand. Five minutes. He finally stopped. The mark remained. The boy stared at it. Then at his hand. Then back at the vase. A strange silence had settled over the hall. “Is it... still there?” he asked. Ajax nodded. The boy's expression became one of genuine despair. He rubbed harder. A servant nearby covered her mouth. Another turned away, his shoulders trembling. The boy looked around. Only then did he notice that nobody was working anymore. Everyone was watching him. He looked back at Ajax. The young master sat in his wheelchair, his face perfectly composed. For a moment, neither spoke. Then the boy's eyes widened. “Wait.” A servant made a strangled noise, desperately trying to contain his laughter. Ajax smiled. He had only meant to tease the boy, but seeing his expression, he couldn't help himself. The boy stared at him with wounded dignity. The servants finally burst into laughter. “I'm sorry,” the boy said, though he sounded anything but. “I thought there was something wrong with the vase.” “There was.” “What?” “A black spot.” The boy looked ready to argue. Ajax reached out and caught his hand. “It's painted.” The boy stared at him. Then he looked at the vase once more. The black mark was part of the decorative pattern. He had spent five minutes polishing a painting. The boy closed his eyes. “Can we... keep it a secret from the outside world?” Ajax laughed again. He gave the boy's hand a friendly shake and lightly patted his back with the other. “Good work.” The boy looked at him. “Thank you. I'll be sure to dedicate the next five minutes of my life to another artistic masterpiece.” “Heh…” Ajax chuckled. For the first time in a long while, the evening didn't feel quite so dull. And somewhere between their hands, Ajax felt a faint prickling sensation around his fingers. It was light enough to be mistaken for a trick of the mind. He glanced down at his left hand. Nothing appeared unusual. He ignored it. “What's your name?” he asked. The boy straightened his collar. “It's Zhen... Zhen Shuri.” “Ajax Wilhelmshire.” “I know.” “Then why didn't you say hello?” Zhen looked at him. “You were busy watching me polish a vase.” Ajax considered that. “Fair enough.” Behind them, the servants were still laughing."},{"t":"2. The Diary","k":"Against the Heavens · chapter","u":"atth/c2.html","s":"The guests started to arrive. By then, the main hall had transformed. The flowers were in place, the silverware had been inspected twice, and the musicians had begun playing a gentle tune near the western balcony on the raised platform. The enormous entrance doors remained open, allowing servants to welcome the guests as carriages arrived one after another. Ajax had been positioned near the entrance. Not officially, of course. His mother had simply suggested that he might enjoy greeting some of the guests. His father had agreed. And since refusing would only invite another discussion about his health, Ajax had accepted. He sat beside a small decorative table, dressed in a dark formal coat with silver buttons. His thick black hair swept back and his obsidian eyes set on the entrance. His wheelchair had been polished that afternoon. He suspected someone had spent more time polishing it than his shoes. **“Welcome, Sir Hemingway.”** Ajax inclined his head as an elderly man approached. **“Ah!** **Young Ajax.** **You've grown up.”** Ajax had heard that sentence at least a hundred times. **“I'm afraid I haven't had much of a choice.”** The man chuckled politely. His wife offered him a smile. **“How are you feeling these days?”** **“Quite well, thank you.”** **“That's wonderful. Your parents must be very relieved.”** **“They are.”** The conversation lasted another minute before the couple moved towards the main hall. Ajax watched them leave. The smile had been pleasant enough. It simply hadn't reached their eyes. The next guest was a merchant whose company had recently secured a trade agreement with the royal family. He spoke to Ajax for nearly five minutes, though most of those minutes were spent discussing his father's recent economic reforms. A military officer came next. Then a representative of the royal family. Then a distant relative. He answered everyone. He remembered names, exchanged greetings, and occasionally made a joke when the opportunity presented itself. Most people responded kindly. Some even seemed genuinely pleased to see him. But there was always a certain hesitation. They avoided asking about his future. And whenever his wheelchair became the subject of conversation, their expressions acquired that particular softness reserved for unfortunate things. Ajax was familiar with it. He didn't particularly dislike these people. Most were simply uncomfortable. And he had no intention of making them more uncomfortable. Once the initial wave of guests had arrived, he quietly moved towards the side of the hall. *** The Wilhelmshire family occupied a peculiar position in the republic. The country had a royal family, and the crown remained the highest authority. The governors were appointed to oversee the major administrative territories, managing their affairs, maintaining institutions, and implementing royal policy. There were five governors in total. They were not nobles in the traditional sense. They did not own the lands they administered, nor could they treat their territories as personal inheritances. Their authority came from the crown, and their responsibilities extended only as far as the royal administration permitted. They were, in a sense, limbs of the royal government. A healthy kingdom needed functioning limbs. But they were still limbs. The governor of a territory could command considerable influence, control administrative appointments, negotiate trade arrangements, and oversee regional security. Naturally, the position came with considerable privileges. The Wilhelmshires were wealthy, influential, and counted among the republic's most prominent families. Their residence was large enough to host hundreds of guests, and their social circle included big names and top shots from various countries. They were the so called upper class. Ajax understood that his father's position was one reason people were so interested in speaking with him. The other reason was considerably less flattering. A little curiosity, a little sympathy, and occasionally a little gossip. *** He was watching a group of musicians when he spotted a familiar face. Brown hair. Green eyes. A crooked collar. Zhen Shuri. The boy was carrying a tray of glasses near the far end of the hall. He looked different from earlier. His movements were slower, and his eyes seemed to wander without settling on anything for too long. There were dark circles beneath them, and his hair had become noticeably messier. He nearly walked into another servant. **“Careful!”** **“Sorry.”** Zhen adjusted the tray and continued. Ajax watched him for a moment. The boy who was fine a moment ago, now looked as though he hadn't slept properly in days. Ajax tilted his head. *Interesting.* He thought The boy who had spent five minutes polishing a painted spot seemed to have lost the energy to argue with the world. *** **“Ajax.”** His father's voice came from behind him. He turned. Governor Wilhelmshire stood beside him, accompanied by a middle-aged man wearing a dark green suit and a woman with silver ornaments in her hair. **“Come. I'd like you to meet someone.”** Ajax straightened slightly **“This is chancellor Matthew** **Ornavis****, his wife, and their daughter.”** Ajax greeted them. **“It's an honor to meet you, Sir Matthew.”** Ajax tried to kneel but was abruptly stopped by the chancellor. **“No, no what are you doing kid”** he reprimanded Ajax while simultaneously setting him back in his wheelchair**. “I've heard quite a bit about you.”** He said smiling warmly at him. Ajax wondered what exactly he had heard. **“Your father speaks highly of you,”** the minister continued. He raised an eyebrow turning towards his father, who in turn gave him a brief look and then coughed. Seeing that Sir Matthew laughed. **“Ha! You have a sense of** **humour****. Good. That's always useful in politics.”** **“I've heard it can be fatal in certain times.”** This time, even his wife smiled. They moved towards a quieter seating area near the balcony. A servant brought refreshments, and the adults began discussing matters that Ajax had heard often enough around the house. Ajax listened. He wasn't expected to contribute, but he had always found these conversations interesting. Ajax took a sip of water. The discussion continued for several minutes before chancellor Matthew turned towards him. **“So, Have you decided what you intend to do in the future?”** Ajax lowered his glass. **“I haven't made a final decision.”** **“Hmm, then I would but suggest to make that quickly”** the minister said. **“My daughter will be attending Mora’s Council of Magic in** **Lahir** **Empire, starting this winter.”** Ajax glanced towards the young girl who joined them a couple of moments ago. She appeared to be around his age. She had long blonde hair and red eyes which calmly swept across the room. **“Mora’s Council of Magic?”** Ajax asked again. His father nodded. **“It's located in the South, Situated and founded in** **Lahir** **Empire.”** **“It’s the most prestigious institutions on the continent in terms of Magic.”**Ajax observed. **“Indeed,”** Sir Matthew replied**. “The Council accepts students from several countries. It has a particularly good reputation for magical sciences, though getting admission is extremely tough.”** Ajax nodded and fell into deep thought. His father glanced at him and smiled. **“I have given Ajax’s application in Altos Institute of History and Literature sciences.”** Ajax blinked, surprised. Sir Matthew smiled and for some reason **“My, my look at the time. Now if you would excuse us”** sprinted off with his family in tow. Ajax looked at them leaving and then, turned towards his father. **“What about little Rose?”** he asked his father about his little sister**.** **“I plan to teach her myself.”** His father said. Ajax considered for a moment. He knew that his father had long decided to nurture his sister as his successor. It was not due to his biases but the fact that his sister had a natural talent in politics and negotiations. She could be said an ideal candidate. That was that but, he also knew that she would never accept the position due to Ajax himself and so this can also be said an ideal situation where he would also get to explore the world and she would learn by his father’s side. He wondered whether she knew such a side of father. **“I don’t know if I am making the right choice”** his father finally spoke, not matching him in the eyes. **“But I want the best for both of you.”** He stroked Ajax’s head affectionately and left to wrap up the remaining events. Ajax saw his father leave. The man who he had seen always confident and fearless of his choices , for the first time hesitated to make a decision. That made Ajax realize that his father was also just a human. He wasn't sure whether being sent to a foreign academy counted as a childhood experience, but he kept the thought to himself. At last he could only sigh as the world around him continued to move. *** When the last guests finally departed, the mansion descended into a different kind of chaos. Servants began clearing tables, collecting decorations, and restoring the hall to its usual appearance. The musicians packed their instruments. The guards resumed their regular patrols. And the birthday girl, having exhausted the majority of her energy, was carried upstairs by their mother. The family gathered in the drawing room afterwards. His father had removed his formal coat, while his mother sat with a cup of tea. Ajax occupied his usual place near the window. **“Did you enjoy yourself?”** his mother asked. **“Very much.”** Ajax replied. **“Good”** She seemed satisfied. His father took a deep breath and looked at him. He nodded back and then his father told his mother that he plans to send Ajax to Altos Institute of History and Literature sciences. His mother immediately started firing arguments against it and it took a long time to finally convince her. In the process Ajax himself learnt many new things as his father continued explaining the academy, the travel arrangements, and the courses available to them. Ajax listened. He asked a few questions about the institution's library, its department, and the facilities available to students with physical disabilities. His father answered what he could. Some details would need to be confirmed with the institute itself. After the discussion was over Ajax was escorted back to his room. The day had been long. He was tired. After changing into his nightclothes, he settled into bed and stared at the ceiling. Ajax wondered why he looked so exhausted. He closed his eyes. There would be time to think about it tomorrow. *** He opened his eyes. The ceiling was gone. So was his bed. Ajax found himself suspended in an endless expanse of darkness. Except it wasn't entirely dark. Stars stretched in every direction, countless points of light scattered across an immeasurable black sky. There was no ground beneath him. No walls. No sound. He looked down. His wheelchair was gone. His legs were there, or at least he could feel them, but the sensation was strangely distant. Ajax remained still. He tried to move his fingers. They responded. He looked around again. **“Am I dreaming?”** No answer. Then something appeared before him. A translucent window. It hovered in the starry expanse, its surface faintly illuminated by a pale silver light. Letters began forming across it. **[Extracting Inheritance………]** Ajax stared. A second line appeared. **[Dual Nurture Program- Phase - 1]** The window remained suspended before him. Ajax continued staring at the words. After a while, he raised an eyebrow. **“...What?”**"},{"t":"3. Beginner’s Pack","k":"Against the Heavens · chapter","u":"atth/c3.html","s":"The words did not change when he read them a second time. Ajax checked a third time, purely on principle. **[Dual Nurture Program]** hung in the dark exactly where it had been, glowing faintly. He had always considered dreams rather unpleasant. Most were scraps of the day stitched together by a mind that had forgotten restraint. Sometimes he dreamt of falling. Sometimes he dreamt of walking.Once, memorably, his father had turned into a particularly angry goose. This was nothing like those. The darkness around him wasn’t empty. Stars hung in it from every side, some blazing, some so faint he could only see them by not looking directly, and they were arranged in patterns that felt less like scattered light and more like a painting. He was standing. He had been standing for some time, he realised. Ajax looked down. There was no wheelchair. There were his legs, and his feet, planted on a floor like a perfectly still lake that reflected no stars. He could feel it: the faint cool of the surface under his heels, the weight of his own knees. Not the dull, borrowed awareness he had learned to live with. Actual feeling. He didn’t move. Hope was a dangerous habit. He had broken himself of it at considerable cost, and he had no intention of relapsing over a dream. Then, very slowly, he curled his toes. They curled. **“****Woah****….”** He exclaimed as he wobbled while trying to walk in a straight line. One step, and then another. **“Don’t wake up,”** he told whichever part of him was in charge of that. **“Please.** **Just this once.”** Nothing answered. But the dream, to its credit, did not end. He took a third step, and a fourth, and then, because no one was watching, a clumsy, ridiculous little jog that sent soft ripples of starlight spreading across the floor. **“Ha****..****haha****…****hehehe****..”** He laughed while picking up his pace. Half in amazement and half in disbelief. When he finally stopped, breathing hard, the window was waiting for him. Not impatiently. **[Conditions met.]** **[Initiating compatibility assessment.]** The window vanished, apparently regarding the question as rhetorical. For a moment nothing happened. Then two clusters of stars began to drift out of the dark. The first was black. It did not shine so much as swallow. The stars around it dimmed as it passed, as if it were drinking their light. Its points were scattered, jagged, almost quarrelsome. The second was bright. Its stars were joined by fine silver threads, tight and exact, a lattice that reminded him of the inside of a clock. It turned slowly as it approached, precise as a mechanism and about as warm as one. The two formations drifted toward each other and stopped. A new window unfolded. **Compatibility Assessment** **Inheritance** **A** **— Compatibility [89%].** **Inheritance B — Compatibility [11%].** Ajax read it twice. He had been a reasonably good student. Even he could tell which of those was a passing grade. **[Merging Inheritance A.]** **[****Phase** **one Loading…………….]** **Phase One: Initiated.** The bright cluster withdrew without any visible hard feelings, like a guest who had just realised it was at the wrong table. The black one did the opposite. It came closer. Ajax felt the stars lean over him, and for one very long second he was certain that something vast, and not at all unfriendly, had turned over in its sleep. Then it was gone, and the dark was only dark again. **“Inheritance?”** he said to the empty sky. **“Whose inheritance?** **Who is—****”** **[Beginner’s Pack: Granted]** Something took shape in front of him, and it was a diary. Not a divine relic. Not a legendary treasure. A diary: old brown leather gone soft at the corners, a metal clasp darkened with age, no title, no crest, no markings of any kind. It looked like something one might find at the bottom of a drawer, beneath spare buttons. Almost insultingly ordinary. Ajax reached for it. His fingers touched the cover. It was rough and a bit hard unlike ordinary leather. He frowned. **“Quite real for a dream.”** he held the diary in his hands. It had weight and smelled faintly of ink and old rain that perhaps waited for someone to pick it up. He undid the clasp. The first page was packed with dense, slanting script in characters he had never seen. They were not the alphabet of his own country, nor any of the foreign languages he had seen. And still the meaning settled in his mind like water finding its level, as though he had known the language once and merely forgotten. *To whoever finds this**,*\n*If you are reading this, that can only mean one of the two things. Either I have failed in my quest or that the mighty wheel of time doesn’t concern me anymore.* Ajax raised an eyebrow. An unusual way to open a diary. *I have spent too many years looking upward.* *The heavens are vast. Ordinary men call them divine, fear them as unknowable, and study them as though their secrets are inexhaustible.*\n\nThe writer left a blank space as if deciding to write ahead or not. \n\n*I too once believed that if I looked long enough, I would understand or that my efforts would be acknowledged.* *But I was wrong.* He turned the page. The writing was personal and untidy, full of corrections: sentences struck out, rewritten, struck out again. Someone arguing with himself in ink. *The first lesson I learnt was simple: the heavens do not answer, nor do they care to.* *The second was harder to accept for me: perhaps my approach was in itself fundamentally flawed.* Ajax went very still. A few hours ago, though it felt like another life, he had sat on a balcony with his hand raised to the stars and asked what crime he had committed to deserve this. They hadn’t answered him either. He swallowed, and read on more slowly. The diary went on, talking about everything. The meaning of life, fate and that if everything was predetermined then why was human effort necessary? Who gave them the authority to toy with a human life? Ajax felt that either this diary is written by a madman or a philosopher, a great one at that. But after reading the writer’s argument Ajax could feel: sadness and perhaps denial. It felt like reading a child’s diary who had become very stubborn and now cannot be lured away with chocolates or little rewards anymore.  \n\nThis writer questioned everything. Why did the body obey the mind? Why did fear interrupt a movement already begun? Why did a man hesitate, even when he knew exactly what he wanted? And why did the heavens look so orderly from far away, and so chaotic up close? Between the questions came confessions, gloriously unflattering. *I have mistaken strength for sovereignty.* *I have mistaken silence for peace.* *I have mistaken the heavens for something beyond myself.* Ajax nearly smiled. Whoever this was, he was at least honest, which put him ahead of most of the people Ajax had met at formal gatherings. Further on, the handwriting changed. It grew neater. Sharper. The hand of a man who had stopped arguing and started deciding. *Anything given for* *free,* *is a shackle. Everything sweet is poison. The heavens do not hate us. That is the trouble. They simply do not care.* *A man with no self is a river. Whatever falls into him, he carries. Do not be a river.* *Be a gate.* Below it, a line had been crossed out so violently that only one sentence survived. *To read the heavens, I must first learn to read myself.* Ajax looked at it for a long time. It wasn’t even a remarkable sentence. He had read grander ones. But the letters had been pressed so hard into the page that they left grooves, and something in the plain fury of those grooves made the words feel less like philosophy and more like a door being kicked open. He turned to the last written page. Only a few lines. *I once wanted to reach the heavens.* *To become one with that divine realm.*\n*But now I know. That can never happen, not until one of us* *yeilds**. Whatever those orthodox idiots call me, I do not care.*\n\n*I will not go down, no matter what heavens throw at me.* And beneath it, a name. Ajax sounded it out carefully. **“Ch…** **Cheonma****?”** The stars flickered. The diary shuddered in his hands, and for one instant Ajax felt something that was not a voice and not a thought. It was closer to standing at the foot of an enormous mountain and understanding, for the first time, that he had been mistaking the foothills for the world. Then it was gone.\n**[Phase One: Interrupted]**\n**[Progress: 1/3]** **[Inheritance: Dormant.]** **“Interrupted?”** Ajax said. **“By whom?** **I didn’t do anything—”** The pages slid out of his fingers like smoke. The floor went soft. The stars smeared into long, drifting streaks, and he grabbed for the diary and caught nothing at all, and then woke up."},{"t":"4. One Month","k":"Against the Heavens · chapter","u":"atth/c4.html","s":"Morning arrived without knocking on the door. Sunlight slipped through the curtains and settled across Ajax's face. Outside, the estate had already begun its daily routine. Servants crossed the courtyard, someone was arguing with a deliveryman, and the distant sound of a cart rolling over the stone path reached his room. Ajax opened his eyes. The dream remained unusually clear in his memory. The endless sky. The two formations of stars. The old diary. And that name. \"Cheonma...\" He repeated it quietly. It sounded strange. Almost like a name from an old folklore. He looked towards his bedside table. There was no diary. Of course, there wasn't. It had been a dream. Ajax pushed the thought aside and reached for the small bell beside his bed. A servant would come shortly to help him prepare for the day. There was no point wasting the morning thinking about something that had happened in his sleep. He had more practical matters to attend to. In exactly two months the winter will arrive and with that, a new term for the Altos Institute of History and Literature Sciences will start. He had to plan for his departure and also get a thorough understanding of the political standings of his father’s contacts and for the sake of connections he would build in the future there. His father had mentioned it during the banquet, but the arrangements had been discussed long before that. The academy was located in a foreign country, far from the governor's territory and the familiar comforts of their estate. It was a prestigious institution. Students from different countries, families, and backgrounds gathered there to study. Though of course it was respected only when it came to administration and engineering sciences. The curriculum was broad, covering everything from literature and archaeology, to political studies and economics. His father had already expressed his preference. Literature and Archaeology. Ajax did not object. He enjoyed reading and writing, especially poetry which was his hobby. History offered him the thrill of occasional mystery hidden between the lines of an ancient text. These were the few interests he could pursue without needing someone to assist him. Besides, his father seemed rather pleased with the idea. Ajax had learnt that arguing against a pleased father was never a good idea. He rang the bell. A servant entered a few moments later. \"Good morning, young master.\" \"Good morning. Could you bring me the documents listing the political workings and social standing of the Central Plains?\" \"Of course.\" The servant bowed and left. Ajax glanced towards the window. Two months. It was not a particularly long time, especially when there were preparations to make. He would need to learn about the institute's facilities, understand its social hierarchy, and decide what bricks to lay for his future. The Wilhelmshire family library was quiet. It was one of Ajax's favourite places in the estate, though not necessarily because of the books. The library had wide windows overlooking the eastern garden, comfortable chairs, and very few people who bothered him with unnecessary conversation. Most importantly, nobody expected him to participate in anything. Ajax sat beside a large table, a book open before him. Several sheets of paper lay scattered around it, along with a notebook and a cup of tea that had long since gone cold. He was studying the Altos Institute's preliminary curriculum. There was no entrance examination he needed to worry about. His father's position and the family's arrangements had already secured his admission. Still, he had a month before departure, and doing nothing for an entire month seemed rather wasteful. Besides, he was curious. The academy's Archaeology department had an extensive introductory programme. Students were expected to understand historical dating, ancient scripts, the classification of relics, and the basic principles of magical archaeology. The last subject particularly interested him. Magical relics were not uncommon in this world. Even ordinary households possessed a few objects that relied on stored magical energy. But the older artifacts were a different matter. Some were discovered in ruins. Others were recovered from places where the natural laws behaved strangely. And a few were so old that nobody could confidently explain how they had been made. Ajax turned a page. Introduction to Historical Artifacts and Their Classification. He read the first few paragraphs, then made a small note in his notebook. His handwriting was neat, though he occasionally paused to consider a particular word. A servant entered quietly to replace his tea. \"Would young master like something to eat?\" \"Later, thank you.\" The servant glanced at the books. \"Studying for the academy?\" \"Something like that.\" \"Surely the academy won't make you work so hard before you've even arrived.\" Ajax looked up. \"Then I'll have an advantage.\" The servant smiled. \"Of course, young master.\" She left. Ajax returned to his book. He had no particular ambition to become the academy's most diligent student. He simply liked knowing things before he needed them. It saved time. And time, unlike most things, was difficult to recover once wasted. By the time he finished the introductory chapter, the sunlight had begun to fade. He closed the book and stretched his fingers. There were still several sections left, but he could continue tomorrow. His wheelchair rolled quietly across the floor as he made his way out of the library. The rest of the day passed without anything particularly interesting happening. He had dinner with his family, listened to Elena complain about the clothes being prepared for their journey, and spent some time reviewing the academy's prospectus. Eventually, he returned to his room. It was an ordinary evening. At least, it began as one. The stars appeared again. Ajax stood in the same endless expanse. He looked down at his feet. Then around him. The vast sky was unchanged. Countless stars stretched into the distance, and the two strange formations remained suspended far above him. For several seconds, he simply stared. \"...Again?\" He had been expecting another ordinary dream. Not this one. He remembered the diary. More importantly, he remembered waking up the previous morning and finding nothing in his hands. Ajax turned around. The old brown diary was resting on a stone table behind him. He approached it slowly. It was exactly as he remembered. The worn leather cover, the darkened clasp, and the slightly uneven edges of its pages. He opened it. The first entries were still there. He turned through them, searching for the passage he had read the previous time. To read the heavens, I must first learn to read myself. He stopped at the line. Then he turned another page. The previous time, the writing had ended shortly after the name. Now there was more. Ajax leaned closer. The handwriting was familiar, though the words seemed to carry a different weight. They say the heavens decide who may ascend and who possesses the right to transcend. They say that the stars have already determined the place of every man. That some are born beneath fortunate constellations, while others are condemned to crawl beneath them. I disagree. Ajax read the lines again. The next passage was written with noticeably heavier strokes. No one will descend from the heavens to grant me permission. No divine hand will reach down and open the path before me. And if it is the heavens themselves that stand in my way, then I shall cut the heavens. Ajax's eyes narrowed. He had never read anything quite like it. The statement was not written as a boast. There was no audience to impress, no opponent to intimidate. It read like a conclusion. A man had looked at the world, considered its rules, and decided that he would not accept them merely because they existed. Ajax turned the page. For the first time, the diary opened into a clearly marked section. At the top of the page was a title. The First Step — To Feel the Qi. Below it, the writing became more organised. Before the body may be refined, before the will may be tempered, one must first perceive what already surrounds him. The world is not empty. Every breath enters a world that is already moving. Every step is taken upon something that does not cease to exist when one's eyes are closed. Yet most men remain unaware of it. Ajax read on. The writer described Qi as something that existed throughout the natural world. It moved through living things, through the air, and through the land. It was not something that could be summoned simply by wishing for it. Nor could it be seen by ordinary sight. The first task was to become aware of it. The diary contained several exercises. The first was called Stillness of the Body. Sit without forcing the breath. Do not attempt to control it. Observe its arrival and departure. Notice the movement of the chest, the warmth of the air, and the subtle changes that occur when the body rests. Do not mistake the absence of movement for the absence of activity. The second was Stillness of the Senses. Close the eyes. Listen without searching for a particular sound. Distinguish the near from the distant, the continuous from the intermittent. When the mind begins to wander, return to what is already present. The third exercise was shorter. Observe the world. The movement of leaves. The warmth of sunlight. The direction of the wind. The rise and fall of water. Do not merely look at them. Pay attention to the changes that connect one moment to the next. Ajax paused. He turned the page. There were notes on the importance of patience, followed by warnings against forcing sensations that were not there. Do not imagine Qi and then congratulate yourself for finding it. The mind is capable of deceiving itself with remarkable ease. Ajax almost smiled. That sounded like something his father would say. He continued reading. You may feel nothing for days. You may feel nothing for months. Do not become impatient. The first step is not to command Qi. It is to recognise that it exists. Ajax closed the diary halfway and looked up. The stars above him were beautiful. He had seen them during both dreams, but this time he found himself observing them differently. Their arrangement. Their distance. The faint differences in brightness. He wondered whether the writer had once stood beneath a similar sky. How long had it taken him to develop this path? How many years had he spent recording these observations? And why had the diary been given to him? There were no answers. Only another page. Ajax turned it. At the bottom, a final sentence had been written. Do not rush to become someone who commands the heavens. First, learn to notice the world that exists beneath them. The words settled in his mind. Then the diary began to fade. Ajax immediately looked down. \"Wait.\" The pages disappeared one after another. A translucent window appeared. Initial Synchronisation: In Progress. Inheritance: Cheonma's Diary. First Section: Available. Before Ajax could read anything further, the stars stretched into lines of light. The world vanished. *** Morning. Ajax opened his eyes. He remained in bed for a few moments, staring at the ceiling. Then he slowly turned his head towards the bedside table. Nothing. He looked at his hands. Nothing there either. Yet the words remained in his memory. The first step is to feel the Qi. Ajax sat quietly. He had no idea whether the diary was real or whether his mind had begun repeating an unusually elaborate dream. But the instructions were simple enough. He could try them. There was no harm in sitting quietly and observing his surroundings. After all, he had plenty of time. And if it turned out to be nothing more than a dream, he would have lost only a few minutes. Ajax reached for the bell. The day had begun. ***"},{"t":"5. The Shuri Household","k":"Against the Heavens · chapter","u":"atth/c5.html","s":"Zhen Shuri was looking at a piece of paper. It had been the same piece of paper for nearly an hour. On it were several diagrams, a few calculations, and a rough illustration of what appeared to be a mechanical device. He had drawn it three times. The first version had been too complicated. The second had been too expensive. The third was... perhaps possible. He leaned back and examined his work. \"Not bad.\" He turned the paper around. \"Actually, no. Terrible.\" The device was supposed to be a compact magical regulator. It would take an unstable energy supply and distribute it through several smaller channels, reducing the fluctuations that damaged ordinary artifacts. At least, that was the idea. The design itself was not particularly revolutionary. The principles already existed in several forms of magical engineering. What made his design interesting was its size. Most affordable regulators were inefficient and bulky. Better ones required carefully manufactured components, rare materials, or a degree of craftsmanship beyond what a commoner could easily afford. His proposed design attempted to solve the problem through a different arrangement of the internal channels. It was clever. At least, he thought so. Unfortunately, being clever did not make the components appear on his table. Zhen looked at the small collection of materials beside him. A few metal plates. Several damaged conductors. A cracked energy crystal. And a regulator component that had probably been discarded because it no longer worked. He sighed. \"Resources. My greatest enemy.\" A knock came from the door. \"Zhen?\" His father's voice. \"Come in.\" The door opened. His father entered, wearing his usual work clothes. He looked tired. \"What are you doing?\" \"Preparing something for the academy.\" His father glanced at the papers. \"Again?\" Zhen nodded. His father pulled out the chair opposite him. \"Listen, son. I know you want to attend the Central Academy.\" Zhen waited. \"But it is expensive. Even if you get in through merit, there will be travel expenses, equipment costs, and the cost of living there.\" \"I know.\" \"You cannot expect us to provide everything.\" \"I understand.\" His father looked at the diagrams. \"Is this what you've been working on?\" \"Yes.\" \"Will it help you get admitted?\" \"It might.\" His father sighed. \"Zhen, I don't want you wasting your time chasing something that may never happen.\" The words were not cruel. That almost made them worse. His father was not trying to discourage him out of spite. He was simply looking at the matter from the perspective of a man who had spent his life counting every coin. The academy was far away. Its students came from wealthy families, powerful institutions, and countries with resources his family could barely imagine. Even if Zhen managed to secure admission, how would he afford to stay there? His father stood. \"Think carefully. You have work at the estate. Keep your position. If you continue studying engineering, you can perhaps find a decent workshop willing to take you as an apprentice.\" Zhen lowered his eyes. \"All right.\" His father nodded and left. The door closed. Zhen looked at the papers again. For a while, he did not move. Then he picked up the diagram and folded it. He knew his father wasn't wrong. That was the annoying part. He had memories of the original Zhen Shuri. He knew how much his family struggled, how carefully they managed their money, and how little they could spare. He also knew what awaited him at the Central Academy. The main cast. The events that would shape the future. And the disasters that would eventually turn this world into something barely recognisable. He rubbed his forehead. \"Of all the worlds...\" He stopped himself. Complaining would not solve anything. He needed a way in. And he needed resources. Zhen looked at the broken components on his table. Perhaps he could approach a local workshop. Perhaps he could offer to repair something in exchange for materials. Or perhaps he could sell the design. He frowned. No. Selling the design without being able to demonstrate it would be difficult. He needed a working prototype. He leaned back and stared at the ceiling. \"One month.\" He had one month. It was beginning to sound like a very unpleasant amount of time. Eventually, exhaustion won. Zhen pushed the papers aside, lay down, and closed his eyes. *** The small house appeared once more. Zhen stood in the middle of the room. He immediately looked towards the table. The enormous tome was there. \"Well, look who's back.\" He approached it. The book was exactly as he remembered, its dark cover and reinforced spine unchanged. He opened it. The first pages were familiar. The introductory passages, the observations, the diagrams. He flipped through several sections. Then he noticed something. A new passage had appeared. It was not a continuation of the final entries bearing Merlin's name. Instead, it was a section placed much earlier in the tome, between two groups of notes. At the top was a simple heading. **The First Lesson — Perception.** Zhen leaned closer. *Before one attempts to understand the world, one must first learn to perceive it.* *The eye receives light, but seeing is not the same as understanding.* *The ear receives sound, but hearing is not the same as listening.* *The mind receives impressions, but impressions are not knowledge.* Zhen turned the page. There were several exercises. The first concerned vision. *Observe a single object.* *Do not immediately name it. Do not recall what you know about it.* *Observe its shape, its edges, the light falling upon it, and the shadows created by its surroundings.* *Look long enough to notice what you would normally overlook.* Zhen stared at the illustration accompanying the exercise. It depicted an ordinary tree. But the notes surrounding it were extensive. The writer had recorded the changing position of the branches, the direction of the leaves, the way sunlight passed through gaps in the canopy, and the subtle variations in colour. The second exercise concerned sound. *Listen to the world without choosing what deserves your attention.* *Separate the sounds that overlap. Notice the distance between them. Observe how they change when the source moves.* The third concerned the mind. *The mind is not merely an instrument for remembering.* *It is also an instrument for arranging what is perceived.* *Observe. Compare.* *Question.* *Do not accept the first explanation simply because it is convenient.* Zhen turned another page. There were more notes. Some described the movement of water. Others concerned the behaviour of flames, the formation of clouds, the movement of air, and the patterns of energy in natural environments. The instructions were not about memorising facts. They were about learning to notice. Zhen read a passage twice. *Magic is not made by imagination alone.* *Imagination without understanding is a picture without a world.* *First perceive. Then imagine. Then believe. And finally, understand.* He stopped. That was interesting. Very interesting. The book did not tell him how to cast a spell. It did not give him a list of techniques or a collection of ready-made magical formulae. It was teaching him how to observe the world. Zhen looked around the small house. The light entering through the window. The dust floating in the air. The faint creaking of the wooden floor beneath his feet. He had never paid much attention to any of it. Now, he found himself doing exactly that. A final note appeared at the bottom of the page. *Do not mistake perception for mastery.* *You have only begun to see.* Zhen closed the tome. He glanced towards the window. The stars outside seemed unchanged. Yet he could not shake the feeling that he had been looking at them incorrectly. Then the room began to fade. The walls dissolved. The table disappeared. The book slipped from his hands. And the dream ended. *** Zhen woke up. He stared at the ceiling. For a moment, he remained quiet. Then he sat up and looked at his hands. \"Again.\" He glanced towards the table. His diagrams were still there. The broken components were still there. And his problems had not magically disappeared. He sighed. \"Well, at least the old man is consistent.\" He rubbed his eyes. The two dreams had given him something different. Ajax had received a path that began with the body and the perception of Qi. Zhen had received a legacy that began with the senses and the understanding of the world. Neither had been handed an answer. Only a first step. Zhen looked at his folded design. Then he looked towards the window. Perhaps there was something worth trying. He stood up, opened the window, and watched the morning light fall over the neighbouring rooftops. For the first time in a long while, he did not immediately think about what he could build. He simply observed. The movement of the leaves. The direction of the wind. The distant sounds of the waking city. And somewhere beneath all of it, the world continued to move. Zhen watched for another minute. Then he remembered that he still had to go to work. \"...Right. Back to being poor.\" He hurried to get dressed."},{"t":"6. Learning to Feel","k":"Against the Heavens · chapter","u":"atth/c6.html","s":"The garden was quiet in the early morning. Ajax sat beneath the shade of an orchid tree, his wheelchair positioned beside a small stone table. Beyond the garden, the road in front of the Wilhelmshire estate stretched towards the distant city. Rows of orchid trees stood along the outer wall, their branches covered in clusters of pink blossoms. A few petals drifted through the air. Cars passed along the road at regular intervals. Inside the estate, servants moved between the flowerbeds, trimming branches, watering plants and carrying out the ordinary duties of the morning. Ajax watched them for a while. A servant stood a short distance away, waiting for instructions. \"That will be all for now.\" \"Would young master like anything else?\" \"No. You may attend to your other duties. I'll call if I need something.\" The servant bowed and left. Ajax reached for the water bottle on the table. Its curved surface contained a small crystal, held in place by a metal setting. Several runes had been inscribed around it, allowing the bottle to maintain the temperature of its contents. The water was still warm. He took a sip and placed it back. Then he closed his eyes. The instructions from Cheonma's diary returned to his mind. *Stillness of the body.* *Stillness of the senses.* *Observe the world.* Ajax settled into a comfortable position and began to regulate his breathing. He had some familiarity with Pranayama, though he had never practised it with any particular seriousness. This time, however, he paid attention to each breath, allowing it to arrive and leave without forcing it. He listened. The sound of passing cars. The rustling of leaves. The distant footsteps of servants. A bird calling somewhere beyond the wall. He tried to notice the intervals between sounds, the differences in their distance, and the subtle changes that occurred as the wind moved through the garden. Half an hour passed. Ajax opened his eyes. Nothing. He looked at his hands, then at the garden. There was no strange sensation, no sudden clarity, and certainly no mysterious energy flowing through his body. He felt exactly as he had before. \"...Well.\" He picked up the water bottle. \"That was rather uneventful.\" He took another sip. It was not particularly disappointing. The instructions had never promised immediate results. In fact, the diary had repeatedly warned against impatience. Still, he had expected something. Perhaps not a dramatic transformation, but at least a small indication that he was doing something correctly. Instead, he had spent half an hour sitting beneath a tree. Ajax considered the matter for a moment. Then he decided to try again tomorrow. There was no reason to abandon something simply because it had failed once. *** The following morning, Ajax asked one of the estate's attendants to purchase a diary for him. It was a small, dark-covered book with runes inscribed along its edges. The runes were designed to recognise their owner and prevent anyone else from opening its pages. A rather convenient feature. He examined it briefly before placing it beside his bed. That evening, he began writing. He recorded everything he could remember from Cheonma's diary. The exercises. The warnings. The passages about the heavens and the nature of transcendence. He even copied down a few sentences that had particularly caught his attention. *The first step is not to command* *Qi**. It is to* *recognise* *that it exists.* Below it, he added his own observation. *I have yet to* *recognise* *anything.* He paused. Then wrote another line. *Perhaps I am simply doing it incorrectly.* The next morning, he returned to the garden. And the morning after that. For seven days, Ajax repeated the exercises. He sat beneath the orchid trees, closed his eyes, regulated his breathing and observed the world around him. Sometimes he practised in the garden. On other days, he remained in his room, listening to the sounds beyond his window. He recorded the duration of each attempt and anything that seemed unusual. There was very little to record. His breathing became more comfortable. He found that he could remain still for longer periods without becoming distracted. He also began noticing sounds he would ordinarily ignore. But Qi? Nothing. He was beginning to suspect that his greatest achievement so far was becoming better at sitting quietly. It was a rather modest accomplishment. *** During the same week, Ajax returned to the library. The family library was extensive, containing historical records, old geographical surveys, accounts of ancient civilisations and several collections of translated manuscripts. He had originally intended to spend his remaining time studying the Central Academy's preliminary curriculum. That plan had not been abandoned. It had merely acquired a new competitor. Cheonma. Ajax searched through several historical collections, looking for the name and the strange writing he remembered from the dream. The name was difficult to trace. Different books used different spellings, and some old records appeared to describe Cheonma as a title rather than an individual name. The writing in the diary was unfamiliar, but a few characters resembled those found in certain older manuscripts. He made notes of the similarities. Nothing conclusive emerged. There were references to an ancient figure associated with martial philosophy, but the accounts were fragmented. Some described a wandering ascetic. Others spoke of a man who had rejected the authority of the heavens. Ajax found the contradictions more interesting than the stories themselves. If these accounts referred to the same person, then either the historians knew very little about him or the man had been considerably more complicated than the legends suggested. He copied the relevant passages into his private diary. One particular account caught his attention. According to an old record, Cheonma had once withdrawn into the wilderness to understand the nature of Qi. He had spent three months observing the natural world before he managed to recognise it. Three months. Ajax read the passage twice. Then he leaned back. \"...That's reassuring.\" He had spent a week without accomplishing anything. Apparently, the person who had written the instructions had needed considerably longer. It was difficult to tell whether this made the task easier or more discouraging. Ajax decided it was the former. At least he was not falling behind. *** The eighth morning began like the others. Ajax sat beneath the orchid tree, his private diary resting on the table beside him. He had repeated the exercises several times, but the results remained almost identical. He could notice the wind. He could distinguish the footsteps of a servant from those of someone walking along the road. He could recognise the changes in his own breathing. But none of these things brought him any closer to feeling Qi. He opened his diary and read through his notes. There were several pages of observations, followed by a few questions. *If* *Qi* *exists throughout the natural world, why can it not be perceived through ordinary observation?* *Does it require a particular state of the body?* *Is the inability to perceive it a limitation of the* *senses,* *or a misunderstanding of what is being perceived?* He tapped his pen against the page. The final question bothered him. Perhaps he had been approaching the matter from the wrong direction. Cheonma's instructions were clear enough. Observe the world. Do not force sensations. Do not imagine Qi and mistake the imagination for discovery. But the diary was not a collection of unquestionable truths. It was a record of someone's journey. There were sections in which Cheonma presented questions without answers. Other passages contained theories, possibilities and observations that seemed to contradict one another. Some ideas were written with certainty, while others were followed by notes expressing doubt. The man had not simply written down what he knew. He had also written down what he did not know. Ajax found that rather reassuring. If Cheonma had already understood everything, there would have been little reason to keep a diary. He looked towards the small collection of discarded magical devices that had been brought to the garden for inspection and disposal. A few of them contained small energy crystals. They were no longer useful for their original purposes, but the crystals themselves appeared intact. Ajax's gaze settled on them. An idea occurred to him. It was not particularly elegant. In fact, it went against the very spirit of the first lesson. But he considered it anyway. If Qi existed throughout the world, and the energy stored within crystals was a form of it, then perhaps he could use one as a reference. He had spent a week trying to recognise something he could not perceive. What if he first experienced it in a form that was impossible to overlook? Ajax examined the nearest device. The crystal inside was small, no larger than the tip of his thumb. It was an ordinary storage crystal, the kind used in several household appliances. He removed it from the discarded device. Then he hesitated. The diary had warned him against forcing sensations. He looked at the crystal. \"...This is probably a bad idea.\" He placed it on the table. A few moments passed. Then he picked it up again. He was not entirely convinced that the experiment would work. Nor was he certain that it was a good idea. But he had spent seven days repeating the same exercise without making progress. Perhaps a different approach was worth considering. He placed the crystal over a prepared receptacle and carefully broke its casing. The stored energy escaped. For an instant, a faint glow spread across the surface. Then the energy rushed outward. Ajax felt it. It was like an electric current passing through him, except that the sensation was not entirely physical. Something unfamiliar brushed against his senses, spreading through his awareness before he could properly understand it. His fingers tightened. The sensation disappeared almost as quickly as it had arrived. Ajax remained still. His heart was beating faster. He looked at the broken crystal. Then at his hands. \"...That was something.\" He took a moment to settle himself before writing anything. He did not want to confuse surprise with understanding. The sensation had been brief, but it was distinct. It had not felt like warmth, cold, pressure or pain. It was something else. He opened his diary. *Experiment: Exposure to stored energy crystal.* *Observation: Upon release, the energy produced a sensation distinguishable from ordinary physical sensations.* He paused. Then added: *Possible relation to* *Qi**.* He underlined the final words. It was not proof. But it was the first time he had felt something that might be Qi. *** The following days were considerably more productive. Ajax did not repeat the experiment carelessly. He began by observing the crystals before their energy was released, noting their faint glow and the subtle changes that occurred when they were used in ordinary devices. He also paid attention to the sensations left behind after the first experiment. At first, he could only recognise the energy when it was released suddenly. Gradually, however, he began noticing something else. The energy did not seem to disappear completely. There was a faint impression left in the surroundings, almost like the lingering presence of a sound after it had stopped. He could not tell whether this was genuinely Qi or merely the result of his senses remembering the previous experience. So he recorded both possibilities. Some of his notes became increasingly speculative. *Perhaps perception is not the same as sensation.* *Perhaps the body does not need to feel* *Qi* *in the same way it feels heat or pressure.* *If the world is filled with* *Qi**, then the inability to perceive it may not mean that it is absent.* *Perhaps the observer is the problem.* He stopped after writing the last sentence. Then he added: *This is only a hypothesis.* He had no intention of pretending otherwise. The recurring dreams continued as well. Every time Ajax fell asleep, he returned to the endless expanse beneath the stars. The stone table remained. The brown diary remained. And Cheonma's writing continued to reveal itself, section by section. Some nights, the diary offered more practical instructions. On others, it contained philosophical discussions that seemed to have little connection to cultivation. There were passages about the relationship between the self and the heavens, the nature of will, and the difference between possessing power and having the right to use it. Some sections ended with questions. Others contained incomplete theories. Ajax began to understand that the diary was not necessarily presenting a finished path. It was presenting the thoughts of someone who had walked one. And perhaps, in some places, even Cheonma had been uncertain. That possibility changed how Ajax read it. He stopped treating every sentence as an instruction. Instead, he began asking why it had been written. *** Two weeks passed. By then, Ajax had become more familiar with the sensation produced by the crystals. He could recognise it more readily, though he still could not properly explain what he was feeling. The distinction between external energy and the faint impressions he had begun noticing around him became clearer. He returned to the original exercises. Stillness of the body. Stillness of the senses. Observation of the world. This time, he approached them differently. He no longer expected the exercises to produce a sudden result. He simply tried to recognise what was already present. The garden became his usual place of practice. He would sit beneath the orchid tree, listening to the road and watching the movement of the leaves. Occasionally, he would look at the warm-water bottle on the table, paying attention to the crystal embedded in its surface. The device had become an unexpectedly useful object of study. It contained stored energy, and the runes around it regulated the flow to maintain the water's temperature. He could not understand the entire mechanism, but he could observe the relationship between the crystal, the runes and the changes in the water. One morning, while repeating the exercises, he noticed something. It was faint. So faint that he almost dismissed it. But there was a difference between the sensation he remembered from the released crystals and the feeling he was now beginning to recognise. The former had been sudden and overwhelming. This was quieter. It seemed to exist beneath the ordinary sensations of his body, almost hidden behind them. Ajax stopped moving. He did not try to grasp it. He did not attempt to direct it. He simply observed. For several moments, the sensation remained. Then it faded. Ajax opened his eyes. His gaze fell upon the diary. He reached for it and turned to a clean page. *Observation: There appears to be a distinction between the energy perceived outside the body and the energy present within it.* He paused. *The former is easier to* *recognise* *when stimulated. The latter is considerably more subtle.* He read the sentences again. Then he added another line. *I do not yet understand whether they are fundamentally different or merely different expressions of the same thing.* He set the pen down. For the first time, he felt that he had made some genuine progress. Not a breakthrough. Not mastery. Just progress. *** That evening, Ajax returned to his room earlier than usual. His private diary lay open on the table. Several pages were filled with observations, questions and small diagrams. Beside it was the academy prospectus. He had nearly forgotten about it. He glanced at the remaining course descriptions before closing the booklet. There was still much to prepare before leaving for the Central Academy. He would need to decide which books to take, arrange his personal belongings and make sure the necessary accommodations were prepared. And, apparently, he would need to continue studying an ancient man's rather unconventional philosophy. Ajax looked at the diary. He had initially considered the recurring dream an elaborate trick of his mind. But dreams did not usually repeat themselves with such consistency. Nor did they provide new information each time. He still had no proof that Cheonma had existed. He had no proof that the diary was anything more than a strange inheritance. And he certainly had no proof that the path described within it would lead anywhere. Yet he had begun to feel something. That was difficult to dismiss. He opened his private diary one last time and wrote: *Cheonma* *spent three months attempting to perceive* *Qi**.* *I have spent three weeks.* *I cannot say that I understand his methods, nor can I confirm that my interpretation is correct.* *However, I have begun to distinguish something that I could not perceive before.* He considered the page. Then, beneath it, he wrote: *Perhaps the purpose of this diary is not to provide answers.* *Perhaps it is to teach me how to ask better questions.* Ajax closed the book. Outside, the evening wind moved through the orchid trees. A few pink petals drifted past his window. He watched them for a while. Then he turned towards his bed. Tomorrow, he would try again. And perhaps, eventually, he would understand what Cheonma had been trying to show him. For now, learning to notice was enough."},{"t":"7. A Change of Direction","k":"Against the Heavens · chapter","u":"atth/c7.html","s":"The Shuri family was preparing to move. Or, more accurately, they were discussing whether they should move. Their belongings were few enough that everything they owned could be packed into several large trunks, a collection of cloth bundles, and two wooden boxes that had seen better days. It was not the first time they had done this. Zhen Shuri sat near the doorway, watching his father inspect a worn travel bag. His mother was sorting through their clothes, separating what they needed from what could be sold or left behind. His younger sibling was busy trying to fit a collection of small toys into a box that was clearly too small. A familiar scene. One that the original Zhen had experienced many times. His family had never been particularly settled. They travelled between towns and settlements, taking whatever work was available. Sometimes his father found employment repairing household devices. Sometimes his mother helped with cleaning, cooking or other domestic work. There were periods when they stayed in one place for several months, and others when they moved again after only a few weeks. It was an exhausting way to live. But it kept food on the table. Zhen looked at the half-packed bags. Then he spoke. \"Father, I think we should move to the central kingdom.\" His father stopped inspecting the bag. His mother looked up. Even his younger sibling paused. For a moment, nobody said anything. Then his father asked, \"The central kingdom?\" \"Yes.\" \"Why?\" Zhen had expected that question. He had been thinking about it for the past two days, trying to find a reason that would not sound suspicious. Unfortunately, the truth was not something he could simply explain. *Because this place is going to become a disaster zone.* *Because I remember something terrible happening here.* *Because one month from now, someone will contract a parasite created by Heaven itself, and that infection will become the beginning of the end.* He maintained a neutral expression. \"I heard there are more workshops and industrial facilities in the central kingdom. The demand for magical engineering work should be higher there.\" His father looked at him. \"You heard this from whom?\" \"People at the estate.\" \"Which people?\" Zhen paused. \"...People who talk.\" His father stared. Zhen stared back. His mother quietly returned to folding clothes. \"That is a remarkably detailed source,\" his father said. \"I try to be thorough.\" \"You also tried to repair a regulator with a cracked crystal last week.\" \"And it worked for nearly four seconds.\" \"Three.\" \"Four, if we count the moment before it exploded.\" His father closed his eyes. Zhen decided not to continue defending that particular achievement. After a moment, his father placed the bag on the floor. \"Listen. The central kingdom is not cheap. There are more opportunities, certainly, but there are also more people competing for them. We would have to find somewhere to stay, and I would need to secure work before we spend what little money we have.\" \"I know.\" \"Do you?\" His father did not sound angry. That made the question more difficult to answer. Zhen glanced at the wooden boxes. He knew how much effort his father put into keeping the family afloat. He remembered the careful calculations, the repairs done late at night, and the occasions when his parents quietly went without something so that the children could have it instead. He did not want to make their lives harder. But staying here was not an option he could accept. \"I can help,\" he said. His father raised an eyebrow. \"I can repair household devices. I can also work in a workshop, even if they only need someone to sort components or assist the technicians. I don't have to attend the academy immediately.\" His father remained silent. Zhen continued. \"And the central kingdom has more opportunities to study engineering. I could learn from people who actually know what they're doing.\" \"Unlike you?\" \"Precisely. I am an excellent example of what happens when someone studies without proper supervision.\" His mother gave a small laugh. His father did not. But the expression on his face softened slightly. \"Zhen, I know you want to study. I know you want something better than this.\" He looked down at the bag. \"But wanting something and being able to afford it are different matters.\" \"I understand.\" \"Then don't make promises you cannot keep.\" Zhen nodded. He had no intention of promising that everything would work out. He could not guarantee a job. He could not guarantee admission to the Central Academy. And he certainly could not guarantee that his memories of the future were accurate enough to save anyone. But he could try. \"I am not asking you to spend all our savings,\" he said. \"Just give me a chance to find work there. If I cannot find anything, we can reconsider.\" His father looked towards his mother. She had stopped folding clothes. \"What do you think?\" he asked. She considered it for a moment. \"We have moved before. And if there are more opportunities there, perhaps we should try.\" His father sighed. \"You two seem to have discussed this already.\" \"We have,\" Zhen admitted. \"When?\" \"Yesterday.\" His father looked at him. Zhen smiled faintly. \"With myself.\" His father rubbed his forehead. For several seconds, the room remained quiet. Then he picked up the travel bag again. \"Very well. We will look for work in the central kingdom.\" Zhen blinked. \"Really?\" \"Do not make me regret it.\" \"I'll do my best.\" \"Your best is not always reassuring.\" \"That's fair.\" His father shook his head, but there was no real irritation in his expression. \"Start packing. We will leave once I have arranged the remaining work here.\" Zhen nodded. \"Thank you, Father.\" His father waved a hand dismissively. \"Save your gratitude. Find us somewhere to stay.\" \"Yes, sir.\" Zhen turned towards the boxes. For the first time in several days, he felt that something had moved in the right direction. It was a small step. But he would take it. *** The following morning, Zhen woke earlier than usual. The house was still quiet. His father had gone out to speak with a local merchant, while his mother was preparing breakfast. His younger sibling was still asleep. Zhen stepped outside. The air was cool, and the morning light had only begun to spread across the neighbouring rooftops. He sat on a low wooden bench. Then he remembered the tome. *The First Lesson — Perception.* He had read it several times now. The instructions were simple. Observe without immediately naming. Look without assuming that seeing something meant understanding it. Listen without deciding beforehand which sounds mattered. At first, he had thought the exercises were almost absurdly basic. He had spent years studying engineering. He knew how to observe a mechanism, identify its components and understand the purpose of its construction. Or so he had believed. The tome seemed to suggest that he had been doing something else entirely. He looked towards a tree growing beside the house. Its branches extended unevenly, with several leaves catching the morning sunlight. *Tree.* The word appeared immediately in his mind. He stopped. That was exactly what he was supposed to avoid. He tried again. The trunk was not perfectly straight. Its surface had several shallow grooves. One branch extended farther than the others, and a smaller one grew beneath it at an awkward angle. A few leaves had curled at their edges. One was partially covered by another. The sunlight reached the upper branches first, leaving the lower portion in shadow. He continued observing. The leaves moved. Not all at once. Some shifted with the wind, while others barely moved. A thin branch swayed, returned to its original position, then swayed again. Zhen watched the movement for several minutes. Then he heard something. A cart passing along the road. He turned his attention towards the sound. The wheels produced a low, uneven rumble. One side seemed louder than the other. The sound grew gradually, reached its peak, and then faded into the distance. He could distinguish the cart's movement without seeing it. Interesting. He closed his eyes. The sounds around him became easier to separate. A bird. A distant conversation. Someone sweeping the road. The faint creaking of the wooden gate behind him. He had heard these things countless times. He had simply never paid attention to them in this way. Zhen opened his eyes. \"That old man might actually know what he's doing.\" He looked at the tree again. Then at the rooftops. Then at the sky. The world had not changed. But the way he was examining it had. He wondered how much of his previous knowledge had been built upon assumptions. How many times had he looked at a magical device and immediately decided what was wrong with it because he recognised a familiar arrangement? Perhaps he had been overlooking things. A change in the surrounding temperature. The direction of the energy flow. A slight difference in the material. A component that behaved differently from what its appearance suggested. He had often approached problems by identifying them first and examining them afterward. The tome seemed to recommend the opposite. Observe. Compare. Question. Only then should he attempt to understand. Zhen leaned back. \"All right, Merlin. Point taken.\" He returned inside. His unfinished regulator design was still on the table. He picked it up. For a while, he simply looked at it. The arrangement of channels. The damaged conductor. The position of the crystal. The small gaps between the components. He had seen the design dozens of times. Yet now he found himself examining details he had previously ignored. He noticed that one of the channels was slightly narrower than the others. He had assumed the difference was insignificant, but the calculations suggested that it might affect the distribution of energy. He marked the area. Then he examined the conductor. Its surface had several tiny irregularities. He had previously classified it as damaged and unsuitable. But perhaps the damage was not uniform. Perhaps only one section needed replacement. He picked up his pencil. A new diagram began to take shape. He did not know whether the revised arrangement would work. But he was beginning to understand something. The purpose of perception was not simply to see more. It was to make fewer assumptions about what he saw. **Three days passed.** The Shuri family made their arrangements, settled their remaining obligations and prepared to leave. Zhen spent much of his time helping his father, looking for information about the central kingdom and occasionally working on his regulator. He also continued practising the exercises from Merlin's tome. Sometimes he observed the movement of clouds. Sometimes he listened to the sounds of the market. On one occasion, he spent nearly twenty minutes watching water drip from a damaged roof. He discovered that there were surprisingly many ways to become bored. But he also discovered that the more he observed, the more details he noticed. The water did not fall at perfectly regular intervals. The drops gathered at the edge of the roof before releasing. Their size varied. Some struck the ground directly, while others landed on the leaves of a nearby plant. It was ordinary. Almost painfully ordinary. Yet he found himself wondering about the forces involved, the shape of the roof, the surface tension of the water and the way the drops changed direction. He did not need to find a grand meaning in everything. Sometimes, observing something was enough. And slowly, the habit began to influence his engineering work. He started examining discarded components instead of immediately throwing them aside. He compared their behaviour, noted their differences and recorded what he could learn from them. His progress was not dramatic. But it was real. Then, on the evening before their departure, he remembered something. *** Zhen sat beside the window. The family had finished packing. Most of their belongings were ready to be loaded onto the transport cart in the morning. His father was speaking with a neighbour outside. His mother was preparing the last meal they would have in the house. Everything was ordinary. And that was precisely what made the memory so unpleasant. He remembered the story. Not all of it. Never all of it. The original plot had been enormous, filled with characters, political conflicts, ancient histories and events that he could barely recall in the correct order. But certain things had remained in his memory. The disasters. There were six of them. Civilisation-level catastrophes, each one different from the last. They were not merely wars or conflicts between powerful individuals. They were events that threatened the existence of entire societies. The first was biological. A parasite. Zhen frowned. He remembered its appearance only vaguely. More importantly, he remembered what it could do. It was not an ordinary organism. It possessed sentience. It could connect infected organisms through a hive mind, and its capacity to evolve was terrifying. It adapted, changed and developed at a rate that made ordinary biological progression seem almost meaningless. And it was not a natural occurrence. It had been created by Heaven itself. Zhen leaned against the window frame. \"Of course it was.\" He remembered the beginning. The first calamity would strike after the story's opening events, when the Wilhelmshire family was still at the centre of the narrative. One month. Approximately one month after the beginning of the original sequence. His expression grew serious. He tried to recall the exact circumstances. Where had it appeared first? How had it spread? Was there an initial outbreak, or had it existed unnoticed before anyone recognised the danger? He could not remember. The details were frustratingly incomplete. But one thing stood out more clearly than the rest. The first person to contract it. Ajax Wilhelmshire. Zhen went still. For several seconds, he simply stared through the window. The boy with the composed expression. The young master who had tricked him into polishing a painted spot on a vase. The governor's disabled son who had seemed far too entertained by the whole incident. Ajax. He remembered that name. In the original story, Ajax was the first victim of the parasite. And he did not survive the way the original narrative had initially led readers to believe. Zhen pressed his fingers against his forehead. \"Wait a minute.\" The timeline. The Wilhelmshire estate. The first infection. He had been so occupied with his own problems that he had almost forgotten how close the first calamity was. If his memory was correct, the parasite would emerge in approximately one month. And Ajax would be the first to contract it. Zhen looked towards the packed belongings. Moving to the central kingdom suddenly seemed like a much smaller achievement. He had managed to persuade his family to relocate. He had found a way to continue his engineering studies. He had begun understanding the first lesson of Merlin's tome. But none of that answered the most important question. Could he actually prevent what was coming? He did not know how the parasite infected its first host. He did not know whether it could be detected before symptoms appeared. He did not even know whether the original story's events would unfold exactly as he remembered them. His memories were not a reliable record. They were fragments. And fragments could be dangerous. Zhen looked down at his hands. He had come to this world with knowledge of its story. But knowledge was not the same as understanding. Merlin's first lesson had made that painfully clear. Perhaps the same principle applied here. He needed to observe the world as it was, not simply force it to match the story he remembered. Still... There was one person he could not afford to ignore. Ajax Wilhelmshire. Zhen let out a slow breath. \"I hope I'm wrong.\" Outside, his father called for him. \"Zhen! Come help with the last box!\" He looked towards the doorway. \"Coming!\" He stood, but before leaving, he glanced once more at the darkening sky. The first calamity was approaching. And somewhere, in a distant estate, a young man was probably spending his evening reading an old diary beneath the light of a rune-powered lamp. Completely unaware of what awaited him. The next morning, the Shuri family left for the central kingdom."},{"t":"8. A Faint Trace","k":"Against the Heavens · chapter","u":"atth/c8.html","s":"Ten days had passed since Ajax first began noticing traces of energy without the assistance of a crystal. Ten days of experiments, observations, and considerably more patience than he had initially expected to require. His progress, however, remained difficult to measure. He could recognise the sensation now. Not consistently, and certainly not whenever he wished, but he had begun to distinguish it from the ordinary impressions produced by his surroundings. It was faint, almost imperceptible, and disappeared whenever he tried too hard to focus on it. That last part was particularly troublesome. Every attempt to grasp the sensation seemed to make it slip away. Ajax sat beneath the orchid trees in the estate garden, a small energy crystal resting in his palm. The morning was quiet. A few servants passed along the distant pathway, carrying baskets and speaking in low voices. Beyond the estate walls, the occasional sound of a carriage or automobile drifted through the air. He paid little attention to them. His eyes remained closed. He released a small amount of energy from the crystal. The sensation came almost immediately. A faint disturbance brushed against his awareness. It was familiar now, though still difficult to describe. It did not resemble warmth, pressure, or the movement of air against his skin. It was simply there. Ajax concentrated on it. And it vanished. He opened one eye. \"...Still rather uncooperative.\" He turned the crystal between his fingers. The experiment was becoming repetitive. He would stimulate his senses, recognise the sensation, attempt to follow it, and lose it the moment he tried to hold on. Sometimes he could recognise a trace left behind after the energy had dissipated. On other occasions, he could barely distinguish anything at all. Still, the results were not entirely useless. He had started to notice that the sensation produced by the crystal was not quite the same as the one he occasionally encountered during meditation. They shared certain characteristics, but there were differences. The crystal's energy was easier to perceive. It was more distinct, almost as though something had briefly announced its presence before disappearing. The other sensation was much quieter. It did not announce itself. It seemed to exist beneath the threshold of his ordinary awareness, waiting to be noticed. Ajax placed the crystal beside him. Then he resumed his meditation. *** His daily routine had changed considerably. He no longer spent as much time in the library or the estate's common areas. Whenever someone asked about him, the answer was usually the same. Young Master Ajax was reading. Or resting. Occasionally, both. His family did not object. Ajax had always enjoyed spending time with books, and his upcoming departure for the Central Academy provided a convenient explanation for his sudden interest in remaining indoors. Elena, however, was less convinced. \"You have been reading for three days.\" Ajax looked up from the book resting on his lap. \"An impressive observation.\" \"You're not even turning the pages!\" \"I am contemplating the contents.\" \"You're looking at the same page.\" \"Some passages deserve more consideration than others.\" Elena narrowed her eyes. Ajax returned to his book. She stared at him for another moment before deciding that arguing with her brother was unlikely to produce anything useful. \"You're strange.\" \"I have been told.\" She left shortly afterwards. Ajax waited until her footsteps disappeared down the corridor before closing the book. The book had, in fact, remained on the same page for nearly an hour. He glanced towards the window. The garden was visible beyond it, the orchid trees swaying gently in the breeze. There was no particular reason to continue practising outside. His room was quiet, and the occasional interruption could easily be avoided. Besides, he had begun to realise that the exercises required considerably more time than he had initially anticipated. He had no intention of wasting it. *** The recurring dreams had continued. Every night, Ajax returned to the endless expanse beneath the stars. The stone table remained where it had always been. Cheonma's brown diary rested upon it, and its pages continued to reveal themselves gradually. During the past ten days, Ajax had encountered several sections that he had previously overlooked. Some concerned the nature of Qi. Others discussed the relationship between the body, the mind, and the natural world. He had also found several passages describing sensations that Cheonma had experienced during his own early attempts at cultivation. Ajax found those particularly interesting. Cheonma's descriptions were not identical to what he had experienced. In one passage, he described Qi as a presence that could be distinguished from the ordinary sensations of the body. In another, he compared its perception to recognising something that had always existed around oneself but had previously gone unnoticed. There were references to subtle changes in the surrounding environment, too. None of the descriptions provided a precise explanation. Some were written as observations. Others were questions, followed by incomplete thoughts. Ajax had initially expected the diary to contain a clear explanation of what he was supposed to feel. Instead, it offered him several accounts of what Cheonma himself had experienced. That distinction mattered. He could not simply assume that his own sensations would be identical. Perhaps their bodies perceived Qi differently. Perhaps the circumstances were different. Or perhaps he had misunderstood something in the descriptions. He had no way of knowing yet. One passage caught his attention. *Do not mistake the first sensation for the thing itself.* *The body* *recognises* *what the mind has not yet understood.* *Observe the difference. Do not force the two to become one.* Ajax read the lines again. Then he turned the page. There was no further explanation. \"...You really do enjoy leaving things unfinished.\" The diary, naturally, offered no response. *** The following morning, Ajax began another experiment. He placed a small energy crystal on the table beside him and settled into his usual position. His private diary lay open nearby. He had already recorded the previous attempts, including the circumstances in which the sensation had appeared and the reasons it had disappeared. Today, he intended to approach the exercise differently. Rather than trying to reproduce the sensation and immediately follow it, he would attempt to remain aware of it without interfering. He closed his eyes. His breathing gradually settled. The sounds outside his room became less important. He could still hear them, but they no longer demanded his attention. He released a small amount of energy from the crystal. There. A faint impression appeared at the edge of his awareness. Ajax recognised it. He did not move towards it. He did not attempt to grasp it or force his attention upon it. He simply observed. For a moment, the sensation remained. Then it began to fade. Ajax felt the familiar urge to concentrate harder. He resisted it. The sensation grew weaker. And weaker. Until— Nothing. He opened his eyes. It had lasted longer than before. Not by much, perhaps, but enough to make him reconsider his approach. He looked at the crystal. Then at his diary. He wrote down the duration of the attempt and added a short observation. *Attempt: Maintain awareness of the sensation without deliberately pursuing it.* *Result: The sensation persisted for longer than in previous experiments.* *Possible conclusion: Excessive concentration may interfere with perception.* He paused. Then added: *Not confirmed.* Ajax put down the pen. He would need to repeat the experiment. It took several more attempts. Some failed almost immediately. Others produced the same brief impression he had experienced over the previous ten days. But gradually, something changed. Ajax began to understand that the sensation did not need to be pursued. Whenever he tried to seize it, his attention became too narrow. He would focus on the sensation itself and lose awareness of everything surrounding it. When he allowed his attention to settle, however, the faint impression sometimes remained. It was a delicate balance. He could not ignore it, but neither could he force himself to perceive it. He needed to remain aware without becoming impatient. The next attempt began like the others. The crystal released its stored energy. A familiar sensation brushed against his awareness. Ajax recognised it. His breathing remained steady. He allowed the sensation to remain in the background of his awareness, neither chasing it nor deliberately pushing it away. Seconds passed. Then more. The sensation weakened, but it did not disappear. Ajax's fingers twitched slightly. He ignored them. For the first time, he could distinguish the sensation from the memory of having felt it. It was not merely the recollection of the crystal's energy. Something was still there. A faint trace. He remained still. Then, very carefully, he began to follow it. Not with his hands. Not by moving his body. He simply directed his attention towards the sensation, attempting to understand where it seemed to originate and how it changed. It was like following a barely visible thread in the darkness. He could not see its beginning. He could not determine where it ended. But he could follow it. For several moments, the sensation remained intact. Then it disappeared. Ajax opened his eyes. He did not move immediately. His gaze rested on the crystal. Then on his hands. A quiet smile almost formed on his face, though he suppressed it before it could fully appear. \"...So that's possible.\" He reached for his diary. His hand moved a little faster than usual as he turned to a clean page. *Experiment: Maintaining the sensation during meditation.* *Result: Successful, once.* *Observation: For the first time, I was able to retain the sensation without immediately losing it through excessive concentration.* He stopped writing. There was something else. He had not merely felt the energy. He had followed it. Even if only briefly. He added another line. *The sensation appears to have a traceable direction or pattern. Further observation is required.* He read the entry twice. Then he underlined the final sentence. He had no idea whether the sensation was truly Qi, whether it originated within his body, or whether he had simply discovered another aspect of the energy released by the crystal. There were still too many uncertainties. But ten days ago, he could barely recognise anything without deliberately stimulating his senses. Now, for the first time, he had managed to maintain the sensation through meditation and follow it for several moments. It was not mastery. It was not even control. But it was something he could build upon. Ajax closed the diary and glanced towards the window. The morning light had shifted across the floor. He had been sitting there for considerably longer than he intended. \"...I suppose breakfast can wait.\" He picked up the crystal again. There was still much to learn."},{"t":"9. The Threads Within","k":"Against the Heavens · chapter","u":"atth/c9.html","s":"Two weeks passed. Ajax had stopped counting the number of attempts. At first, he had recorded every successful experiment, every failure, and every minor variation in the sensations he experienced. He had even begun noting the time of day, the condition of the energy crystals, and the duration of each meditation. Eventually, however, the records became too numerous. There was always something else to observe. A slight difference in the sensation after waking up. A change in its intensity following a meal. An unfamiliar impression while sitting near an enchanted household device. Even the subtle distinction between the energy released by a crystal and the sensation he occasionally encountered within himself. He wrote them all down. His private diary had become a rather important companion. *** The first significant change came during an ordinary morning. Ajax sat beside his bedroom window, following the familiar routine. He had a small energy crystal placed on the table. Its stored energy had been released several times already, leaving only a faint residue. He closed his eyes. He did not attempt to hold the sensation. Instead, he allowed it to appear and disappear. A brief attempt. A pause. Then another. He repeated the process. At first, there was little difference from his previous exercises. Sometimes he recognised the sensation immediately; sometimes it took several attempts. On particularly unproductive mornings, he could spend nearly an hour without noticing anything meaningful. But he continued. He had begun to suspect that his earlier approach was fundamentally mistaken. He had treated the sensation as something that needed to be caught and maintained. Every time it appeared, he would concentrate on it, trying to prevent it from disappearing. That had made the entire process unnecessarily difficult. Perhaps the problem was not the sensation. Perhaps it was the way he approached it. Instead of trying to sustain it, he began training himself to recognise it repeatedly. He would perceive it for a moment, relax his attention, and try again. He did this dozens of times. Then hundreds. The duration of each successful attempt mattered less than the consistency with which he could recognise the sensation. It was a simple adjustment. The results, however, were not. *** A few days later, Ajax noticed something unusual. He had been following the sensation for several minutes when he became aware of something beyond it. Until then, he had only been able to recognise a vague presence within his body. It was difficult to distinguish its location, and even more difficult to understand its movement. Now, something seemed to be taking shape. He stopped. Then resumed the exercise. The sensation appeared again. This time, he did not attempt to follow it immediately. He allowed his awareness to settle around it, observing its subtle changes. There. Something was moving. Or perhaps it had always been moving, and he had only just begun to notice. Ajax concentrated on the sensation. It seemed to extend through his body in countless directions. Thin. Faint. Interconnected. Like an enormous network of wires, each one running alongside another, branching and meeting at places he could not properly identify. He could not see them. It was not a visual phenomenon. Rather, his mind was beginning to interpret the sensation through a familiar image. Countless threads. Countless connections. Ajax remained still. He carefully followed one of them. For a brief moment, he could distinguish its direction before it became indistinct among the others. His eyes opened. \"...What was that?\" He looked down at himself. Nothing appeared different. He moved his fingers, then his arms. Everything seemed as it had before. Yet the sensation remained in his memory with unusual clarity. He reached for his private diary. This time, he did not begin with a written description. He picked up a pencil and started drawing. The first sketch was little more than a collection of lines. He marked the approximate locations where the sensations seemed strongest, then drew several branching connections between them. He paused. The result looked rather strange. It resembled an anatomical diagram drawn by someone who had never studied anatomy. Ajax examined it. \"...Not particularly convincing.\" He turned the page and tried again. This time, he divided the sketch into several sections, using different patterns to distinguish the sensations he had experienced. He marked the areas he could recognise with confidence and left the rest incomplete. At the bottom, he wrote: *The perceived sensation resembles an interconnected network extending throughout the body.* *The apparent connections are numerous and difficult to distinguish individually.* *This is an interpretation of the sensation, not a confirmed physical structure.* He underlined the final sentence. He had no intention of mistaking an impression for an established fact. Still, the sketch was worth keeping. *** The following days brought another change. Ajax no longer needed to sit in perfect stillness to recognise the sensation. He continued practising meditation, of course. It remained useful for observing the more subtle aspects of Qi. But the sensation itself had started appearing during ordinary activities. At first, it happened accidentally. While drinking water, he noticed a faint movement within his body. While eating breakfast, he became aware of the same interconnected sensation. Even when reading, he occasionally found himself recognising it without deliberately attempting to do so. The first few times, he stopped whatever he was doing and tried to examine it. That was a mistake. The moment he redirected all his attention towards it, the sensation became difficult to distinguish. So he changed his approach again. He continued eating. He continued reading. He allowed the sensation to remain in the background of his awareness. Gradually, it became easier. He could recognise it without interrupting whatever he was doing. It was no longer necessary to enter a state of intense concentration every time he wished to perceive it. His body was becoming accustomed to the sensation. Or, more accurately, he was becoming accustomed to perceiving something that had probably been present all along. He still could not explain exactly what he was feeling. He could not command it. He could not deliberately alter its movement. And he certainly could not claim to understand the network he had sketched. But he could feel it. That alone was a considerable improvement. *** His private diary continued to fill. One page contained observations about the differences between external energy and the sensation within his body. Another contained a list of experiments involving energy crystals. Several pages were devoted to the descriptions found in Cheonma's diary, alongside Ajax's own interpretations. There were diagrams, corrections, crossed-out sentences, and questions that had yet to receive answers. Some entries were only a few lines long. Others occupied entire pages. He had begun writing whenever he noticed something unusual, regardless of whether he was in the middle of an experiment. Consequently, his handwriting had become increasingly inconsistent. A few entries were written in haste, while others were carefully arranged. He occasionally returned to older observations and added corrections. Sometimes, he found that an assumption he had made several days earlier was no longer convincing. He would cross it out and write a new explanation beneath it. The process continued. And continued. Until one evening, Ajax reached for his diary and discovered something unexpected. There were no empty pages left. He turned the book over. Then opened it again. He checked the final page, followed by the one before it. Every page had been used. He stared at the book for a moment. \"...When did that happen?\" He had not realised how much he had written. For a while, he simply examined the pages. The first entries were brief and uncertain. They contained descriptions of sensations he could barely distinguish. The later ones were considerably more detailed. There were diagrams of the perceived network, comparisons between different sensations, and notes concerning the circumstances in which he could recognise Qi without deliberate concentration. He had also filled several pages with questions. Ajax turned to the first page again. It was strange. He had started with almost nothing. A vague sensation produced by an energy crystal. And now, two weeks later, he had filled an entire diary trying to understand it. He closed the book. Perhaps he needed another one. *** That night, Ajax returned to the familiar expanse beneath the stars. The stone table stood in the same place. Cheonma's brown diary rested upon it. Ajax approached and opened it, intending to examine the passages he had encountered during the previous nights. But something was different. There was another book on the table. It was considerably thicker than the diary. Its cover was dark, with no visible decorations. Unlike Cheonma's worn leather diary, its surface appeared almost untouched. Ajax stopped. He had not seen it there before. He looked around the empty expanse. Nothing moved. No new system window appeared. There was no voice announcing another inheritance or explaining what he had received. Only the book. Ajax approached the table. He reached out and placed his hand upon its cover. The title was written clearly across the front. **Reverse Heaven Divine Scripture.** He read it once. Then again. His gaze shifted towards Cheonma's diary. \"...Another one?\" He pulled out the chair and sat down. For a few moments, he simply looked at the unfamiliar book. Then he opened it. And began to read."},{"t":"10. Reverse Heaven Divine Scripture","k":"Against the Heavens · chapter","u":"atth/c10.html","s":"The book was heavier than it looked. Ajax rested it upon the stone table and examined its cover. **Reverse Heaven Divine Scripture.** There were no decorative inscriptions, no elaborate symbols, and no explanation of its contents. Just a title. He opened the first page. The writing was familiar. The same strange script that somehow settled into his understanding whenever he read Cheonma's diary. But unlike the earlier sections, this one began without an introduction. *Qi* *originates from Heaven.* *It descends to Earth.* *It returns to Heaven.* *Thus, the cycle continues.* Ajax read the lines twice. The next page contained a simple illustration. A circular pattern, with Heaven at the top and Earth at the bottom. Several arrows connected the two, forming an uninterrupted cycle. Below it were a few more sentences. *The cultivator follows this cycle.* *The closer the mind approaches Heaven, the purer the* *Qi**.* *The purer the* *Qi**, the greater its capacity.* *The meridians expand. The* *dantian* *deepens.* Ajax leaned back slightly. It was a familiar principle. The cultivation traditions he had encountered in books often described something similar. The mind, through discipline and understanding, gradually aligned itself with the natural order. But the next page was different. There was only one question. *Why?* Ajax paused. He turned the page. *Why should purity of mind determine the purity of* *Qi**?* Another page. *Qi* *follows its nature.* *The mind follows its nature.* *Why must one depend upon the other?* Ajax's eyes narrowed. He continued reading. The following entries were brief. *Perhaps the mind is not merely following* *Qi**.* *Perhaps it changes what it receives.* He turned another page. *The self distinguishes.* *The self interprets.* *The self imposes.* *What passes through it may no longer remain unchanged.* Ajax stopped. He read the lines again. Then slowly turned the page. There was a rough diagram. A natural stream of Qi entered a circle marked **Mind**. On the other side, the flow emerged differently. The original illustration was incomplete. Several lines had been crossed out, and a few annotations had been added beside it. One sentence remained beneath the diagram. *The mind may be a filter.* Ajax looked at it for a while. \"...Interesting.\" He continued. *** The next section contained the results of an experiment. Or, more accurately, the beginning of one. *I observed the flow.* *I observed the mind.* *I observed the difference.* There were several pages of short notes, most of them concerning the relationship between the natural flow of Qi and the state of the cultivator's mind. Some observations contradicted earlier assumptions. Others ended with questions. Then he found a passage that caught his attention. *The self is not empty.* *It desires. It rejects. It distinguishes itself from the world.* *Perhaps this is why the* *Qi* *changes.* Ajax read the lines carefully. Cheonma had not written much. But the implication was becoming clearer. The mind was not necessarily purifying Qi by becoming one with Heaven. Perhaps conventional cultivation purified Qi because the practitioner gradually abandoned the distinctions imposed by the self. And perhaps the opposite was possible. If the self could act as a filter, then strengthening its influence might produce the opposite result. Not purity. Something else. He turned the page. *I attempted to impose the self upon the natural flow.* *The* *Qi* *changed.* *It did not become pure.* *It became mine.* Ajax remained still. The next few pages contained more diagrams. The natural cycle appeared several times, but the arrows were different in each illustration. Some were crossed out. Others had been redrawn. Cheonma seemed to be examining how the flow changed when subjected to the will of the cultivator. There were no detailed instructions. No elaborate explanation of the process. Only a handful of observations. *Natural* *Qi* *follows Heaven.* *The self imposes its order.* *The flow is altered.* *The result is Demonic* *Qi**.* Ajax stared at the final line. He turned another page. It was nearly empty. Only a short passage had been written near the bottom. *I have not understood the method.* *I have only confirmed the possibility.* *The self must become the source of the imposed order.* *Until then, the change remains superficial.* Ajax looked back at the earlier diagrams. A thought occurred to him. If the mind was a filter, then the transformation was not simply a matter of directing Qi differently. It was a matter of changing the principle through which Qi was received and circulated. He did not yet understand how that could be achieved. Neither, apparently, had Cheonma. At least, not when he wrote these notes. Ajax continued reading. The final pages contained a heading. **Reverse Heaven Divine Scripture.** Beneath it was a short statement. *Heaven gives the order.* *The self rejects it.* *The* *Qi* *changes.* There was no further explanation. Ajax closed the book. For a while, he remained seated. The earlier sections of Cheonma's diary had taught him to perceive Qi. They had described the natural cycle and the importance of allowing the body to recognise what already existed within it. This scripture seemed to question the very foundation of that approach. It did not ask him to become closer to Heaven. It asked what would happen if he refused to follow its order. Ajax glanced at the circular diagram once more. Heaven. Earth. The returning cycle. Then he looked at the second illustration, where the natural flow passed through the mind and emerged altered. \"...So that's why.\" He leaned back. The explanation was incomplete. The method was vague. And the experiment, if it could even be called that, seemed to have been recorded in fragments. But one thing was clear. Cheonma had not written this as a conventional cultivation technique. He had written it as a question. A question that he had attempted to answer through experimentation. Ajax placed the scripture beside the brown diary. He looked at both books for a long moment. One had taught him to feel Qi. The other proposed that he change its natural order. He glanced towards the endless sky. \"...You certainly chose an interesting subject.\" The stars remained silent. And Ajax, having reached the end of the scripture, began reading it again."},{"t":"11. The Young Master Has Become a Hermit","k":"Against the Heavens · chapter","u":"atth/c11.html","s":"The Wilhelmshire estate had a peculiar problem. Its second young master had become difficult to find. Not because he had left the estate. Quite the opposite. Ajax Wilhelmshire was almost always in his room. He read there, rested there, ate there, and occasionally requested that certain books be brought to him. The servants assigned to his floor had become accustomed to carrying trays of food upstairs, collecting empty dishes, and delivering fresh stacks of paper. At first, nobody had considered it particularly unusual. Young Master Ajax had always been fond of books. His departure for the Central Academy was approaching, and perhaps he was simply preparing himself. But a few days became a week. Then another. And gradually, the household began to notice. *** \"Has Young Master Ajax eaten?\" The question came from the lady of the house. A servant standing near the dining room entrance bowed. \"Yes, Madam. His meal was delivered to his room a little while ago.\" \"Again?\" The servant hesitated. \"Yes, Madam.\" The lady glanced at the empty chair beside Elena. Her son had not joined them for dinner in several days. \"Did he say why?\" \"He mentioned that he was reading, Madam.\" Elena placed her spoon down. \"He's always reading.\" Her mother looked at her. \"That is hardly a crime.\" \"I didn't say it was.\" Elena folded her arms. \"He doesn't even come out to talk anymore. Yesterday, I went to his room, and he told me he was busy.\" \"What was he doing?\" \"Reading.\" \"Then perhaps he was busy.\" \"He wasn't even turning the pages!\" Her mother paused. \"...What?\" \"He was staring at the same page. I waited for almost five minutes.\" Elena looked genuinely offended. \"I think he was pretending.\" A faint smile appeared on her mother's face. \"Perhaps he was thinking.\" \"About what? The page?\" \"Elena.\" \"Fine.\" She returned to her food, though her expression remained dissatisfied. The lady of the house looked towards the empty chair again. Ajax had never been particularly sociable, but he had always made an effort to join family meals. He listened to their conversations, occasionally exchanged remarks with his father, and endured Elena's endless complaints with remarkable patience. Now, he seemed content to remain in his room. She did not wish to force him out. Still, she intended to speak with him. *** The following afternoon, his father visited. Ajax was seated beside the window, a book resting on his lap. His private diary was safely tucked away in a drawer. He looked up when the door opened. \"Father.\" \"Am I interrupting?\" \"Not particularly.\" His father glanced at the book. \"That is reassuring. I was beginning to suspect that I needed an appointment to speak with my own son.\" Ajax considered that. \"I could ask the servants to prepare a schedule.\" \"Please don't.\" His father pulled a chair closer and sat down. For a moment, he simply observed his son. Ajax appeared well enough. His complexion was normal, and there was no obvious sign of illness. He did seem somewhat more tired than usual, although that might have been the result of spending too much time indoors. \"Your mother is concerned.\" Ajax looked up. \"Is something wrong?\" \"That is what she would like to know.\" \"With me?\" \"Unless someone else has been secretly occupying your room.\" Ajax glanced around. \"I would have noticed.\" His father sighed. \"Ajax.\" \"I am listening.\" \"Why have you stopped joining us for meals?\" Ajax thought about it. There was no particularly complicated explanation. He had found that practising in his room was more convenient. Going downstairs, eating with everyone, and returning afterwards interrupted his routine. Besides, the sensation had begun appearing during ordinary activities. It was easier to observe when he did not have to divide his attention between conversations. \"I have been occupied with my studies.\" \"Your academy studies?\" \"Among other things.\" His father raised an eyebrow. \"Among other things?\" Ajax realised he had perhaps answered too quickly. \"Historical research.\" That was not entirely untrue. His father looked at him for a moment before leaning back. \"You know, there is no requirement for you to finish the entire history of the world before entering the academy.\" \"I shall try to restrain myself.\" His father shook his head, amused despite himself. Then his expression became a little more serious. \"Your mother worries that you are isolating yourself.\" Ajax looked towards the window. He had not particularly thought about it. He was still in the same house. He spoke to the servants, occasionally received Elena's visits, and met his parents whenever they came to see him. It had simply become less frequent. \"I don't intend to avoid the family.\" \"Then perhaps you should occasionally demonstrate that.\" Ajax looked back at him. There was no accusation in his father's voice. Only a quiet suggestion. \"I understand.\" His father stood. \"Good. Your mother would appreciate seeing you at dinner.\" Ajax hesitated. \"Tonight?\" \"Unless you have arranged an appointment with your book.\" \"I shall consider cancelling it.\" His father gave him a look. \"See that you do.\" *** Ajax did not attend dinner that evening. He had intended to. He genuinely had. But an observation during his afternoon meditation had led to another experiment, which had produced an unexpected result. By the time he finished recording it, dinner had already been served. He stared at the clock. Then at his diary. \"...That was unfortunate.\" He could hear Elena's voice in his imagination. *He's always reading.* Ajax closed the diary. Perhaps tomorrow. *** Meanwhile, outside the Wilhelmshire estate, the young master's behaviour had become a subject of conversation. The first rumours were relatively harmless. At a small gathering hosted by a noble family, two ladies discussed the upcoming academy term while their attendants arranged refreshments. \"Have you heard? Young Master Wilhelmshire has apparently become quite the scholar.\" \"Ajax?\" \"Yes. I was told he spends nearly all his time reading.\" \"Well, he is going to the Central Academy. Perhaps he is preparing.\" \"Perhaps. Though I heard he has stopped attending family dinners.\" The other lady raised her eyebrows. \"Really?\" \"Apparently, even his meals are sent to his room.\" \"That sounds rather excessive.\" \"Or perhaps he simply dislikes company.\" They exchanged a brief glance. Neither seemed particularly concerned. The conversation soon moved to the academy's upcoming term and the latest political appointments. But the rumour did not disappear. It merely travelled. *** A few days later, the interpretation had changed. At a gathering attended by several upper-class families, a gentleman mentioned the matter while discussing the Wilhelmshire household. \"I hear their second young master has become something of a recluse.\" \"Recluse?\" \"Apparently, he rarely leaves his room.\" \"Is he unwell?\" \"I couldn't say. There are rumours that his health has deteriorated.\" Another man took a sip from his glass. \"Wouldn't the family announce something if that were the case?\" \"Not necessarily. The Wilhelmshires have always been rather private about family matters.\" A third man, who had been listening quietly, joined the conversation. \"I heard something different. Apparently, the boy has developed an obsession with ancient records.\" \"An obsession?\" \"Old manuscripts, historical documents, forgotten civilisations. That sort of thing.\" The first gentleman laughed. \"Then perhaps he intends to become an archaeologist.\" \"At his age?\" \"Why not?\" The third man shrugged. \"Who knows? Young people have strange interests.\" The conversation continued. Nobody had any real information. That did not prevent them from discussing it. *** Not every interpretation was charitable. A few days later, the matter reached a more political gathering. One of the attendees had recently been refused an invitation to meet the young Wilhelmshire. \"I was told he was unavailable.\" \"Perhaps he was occupied.\" \"For several days?\" The gentleman gave a dismissive laugh. \"An interesting way to treat an invitation.\" A woman sitting nearby turned towards him. \"Perhaps the young man is unwell.\" \"Then his family should say so.\" \"Would you prefer them to publish a medical report?\" A few people chuckled. The gentleman did not. \"Regardless, it is rather strange. The family is preparing him for the academy, yet he seems unwilling to participate in even the simplest social gatherings.\" \"Perhaps he doesn't care for them.\" \"That would be an unfortunate trait for someone of his family.\" The woman regarded him over her cup. \"Or perhaps he is simply not interested in entertaining you.\" The conversation shifted shortly afterwards. But the remark remained. And so did the rumour. Some called him eccentric. Others called him arrogant. A few suggested that the Wilhelmshires were concealing a health problem. There were even those who wondered whether his withdrawal had something to do with internal family affairs. None of them knew the truth. And, as was often the case, the truth was considerably less interesting to them than the possibilities. *** Back at the estate, the household had reached its own conclusions. Elena had decided that her brother was becoming a hermit. The servants believed he was preparing for the academy with unusual dedication. His mother was concerned that he was spending too much time alone. His father was beginning to suspect that the books had become a more demanding companion than the rest of the family. Ajax, meanwhile, was unaware of most of these developments. He was seated at his desk, examining a page of handwritten observations. A small plate of food rested beside him. He had already finished eating. His attention was on the faint sensation he had gradually become accustomed to perceiving. It was there. Quiet. Familiar. He did not need to close his eyes or regulate his breathing to recognise it anymore. He simply continued reading. A knock came at the door. \"Young Master?\" \"Come in.\" A servant entered carrying a fresh tray. \"Madam asked me to remind you that the family will be having breakfast together tomorrow.\" Ajax looked up. \"Breakfast?\" \"Yes, Young Master.\" He glanced at the clock. Then at the servant. \"Please inform her that I will attend.\" The servant bowed. \"Certainly, Young Master.\" As the door closed, Ajax returned to his notes. He had no particular objection to breakfast. In fact, he rather enjoyed his mother's cooking. He would simply have to finish his experiment earlier. *** The following morning, Elena arrived at the dining room before everyone else. She took her usual seat and waited. Her brother arrived a few minutes later. She stared at him. Ajax looked at her. \"Good morning.\" Elena continued staring. \"...You're here.\" \"I am.\" \"Actually here.\" \"I can see that.\" She looked towards their mother, who was trying rather unsuccessfully to hide her amusement. Ajax took his seat. His father arrived shortly afterwards. For a while, breakfast proceeded normally. Elena spoke about the clothes she intended to take to the academy. Their mother asked Ajax whether he had finished preparing his belongings. Their father mentioned a few matters concerning the upcoming departure. Ajax answered when spoken to. He even joined the conversation occasionally. Nothing extraordinary happened. Yet the atmosphere seemed a little lighter. At one point, Elena leaned towards him. \"Are you coming to dinner too?\" Ajax considered the question. \"I shall try.\" \"That's what you said last time.\" \"Then I shall try more successfully.\" Elena looked unconvinced. His father laughed. His mother smiled. And for a little while, the second young master of the Wilhelmshire household was no longer a rumour, a recluse, or an eccentric scholar. He was simply their son and brother. *** That evening, however, Ajax returned to his room. His private diary lay open on the desk. He had several observations to record, and the new book he had requested from the estate library had finally arrived. He sat down. Outside, the orchid trees swayed in the evening breeze. Inside, the faint sensation continued to flow through his awareness. Ajax picked up his pen. There was still much to learn. And somewhere beyond the walls of the estate, people continued to discuss the peculiar habits of the young Wilhelmshire."},{"t":"12. The Crown's Reception","k":"Against the Heavens · chapter","u":"atth/c12.html","s":"The invitation arrived three days after the Wilhelmshire family had resumed having breakfast together. It was delivered in a dark blue envelope, bearing the royal crest in silver. Ajax noticed it before anyone else. \"That looks expensive.\" His father, who had been reading the accompanying letter, glanced at him. \"It is an invitation.\" \"I gathered that much from the envelope.\" His mother gave him a warning look. Ajax returned to his breakfast. Elena, however, had already abandoned her food. \"Is it from the palace?\" \"Yes,\" her father replied. \"Are we invited?\" \"We are.\" \"All of us?\" \"Yes, Elena.\" She sat straighter. \"What's happening?\" Her father folded the letter. \"The Crown is holding its annual reception for the departing generation.\" Ajax looked up. \"The departing generation?\" \"Those who will be entering academies, military institutions, research establishments and other professional institutes this coming term. The Crown hosts a gathering before they disperse.\" His mother added, \"It gives the young people an opportunity to meet one another. Many of them will spend years in different institutions and countries. It is easier to establish connections before everyone goes their separate ways.\" \"And their families,\" his father continued. \"Naturally, the adults have their own reasons for attending.\" Ajax nodded. \"Political connections.\" \"Among other things.\" \"Trade agreements?\" \"Sometimes.\" \"Military appointments?\" His father smiled. \"Occasionally.\" \"Marriage arrangements?\" His mother nearly dropped her cup. \"Ajax.\" \"What? It seemed like a reasonable possibility.\" Elena giggled. His father coughed into his hand. \"Your mother would appreciate it if you didn't make every conversation sound like a parliamentary investigation.\" \"I'll endeavour to improve.\" Elena leaned forward. \"Will there be people from the Central Academy?\" \"Certainly.\" \"People our age?\" \"That's rather the point.\" \"Will there be food?\" Ajax looked at his sister. \"That was rather predictable.\" Elena ignored him. His mother smiled. \"There will be a formal dinner as well.\" \"Then I'm definitely going.\" \"That was never in question,\" her father said. Ajax looked back at the invitation. He had spent the past few weeks avoiding unnecessary social gatherings. His parents had been patient about it, though he suspected that patience was beginning to wear thin. Still, an invitation from the Crown was not something one casually declined. \"When is it?\" \"Tomorrow evening.\" Ajax sighed internally. Apparently, his books would have to tolerate some competition. *** The following evening, the royal palace was illuminated from the entrance courtyard to the eastern galleries. The Crown's reception was not quite a ball, nor was it an ordinary dinner. It was something between the two. The main gallery had been opened to accommodate the guests. Long tables were arranged along the outer walls, while smaller seating arrangements occupied the central hall. Musicians played near the balconies, and servants moved between the guests with trays of refreshments. The palace's architecture was impressive without being excessively decorative. High ceilings rested upon broad pillars. The walls were covered with paintings depicting important events in the country's history, and large windows overlooked a garden filled with pale flowers. At the far end of the gallery stood a raised platform bearing the royal crest. There was no throne. This was not a coronation or a formal audience. It was a reception. And the Crown wanted its guests to feel comfortable enough to speak. Ajax entered alongside his parents and Elena. His wheelchair moved quietly across the polished floor. He glanced around. There were considerably more people than he had expected. Ministers, governors, military officers, industrialists, scholars, representatives of the royal court, and their families. Some had travelled from distant administrative territories. Others had arrived from foreign countries. The younger guests were particularly easy to identify. They stood in small groups, usually accompanied by someone older, and attempted to appear comfortable while discreetly observing everyone around them. Some already knew one another. Others were clearly trying to determine whom they should approach first. Ajax found the arrangement mildly amusing. A gathering intended to establish friendships had apparently begun with everyone calculating the usefulness of potential friends. \"Ajax.\" He turned towards his mother. \"Yes?\" \"Your father and I will be speaking with the Royal Secretary. Elena will remain with the other young ladies for a while.\" Elena immediately looked offended. \"I can speak for myself.\" \"Nobody suggested otherwise.\" \"Then why did you say it like that?\" \"Because I know you.\" Ajax looked between them. \"Should I be concerned?\" His mother smiled. \"Only if your sister decides to negotiate.\" Elena raised her chin. \"I negotiate very well.\" \"I've seen you negotiate for an additional dessert,\" Ajax replied. \"That was successful.\" \"Unfortunately.\" His father laughed. \"Come. Let's greet the Secretary.\" *** The first few minutes were spent exchanging formal greetings. Ajax recognised several faces from earlier gatherings, including Minister Edric Voss. The minister approached with his wife and daughter. \"Governor Wilhelmshire.\" \"Minister Voss.\" The two men exchanged greetings before Edric turned towards Ajax. \"Young Ajax. It's good to see you again.\" \"The pleasure is mine, Minister.\" \"I hope you've been keeping well.\" \"Quite well, thank you.\" Edric nodded. Then he turned slightly. \"Allow me to introduce my daughter properly this time. Liora.\" The young woman beside him inclined her head. She had dark, neatly arranged hair and a composed expression. Her dress was elegant without being overly elaborate, and the small silver ornament pinned near her collar bore the insignia of the Voss family. Ajax recognised her from the earlier banquet. \"It's a pleasure to meet you again,\" she said. \"Likewise.\" \"Father tells me you've chosen Archaeology and Literature.\" \"I am considering them.\" \"Considering?\" \"I haven't decided whether I should study both or focus on one.\" Liora smiled. \"That sounds like a difficult decision.\" \"Not particularly. I simply haven't found a convincing reason to choose.\" \"Then perhaps you should attend the Academy first.\" \"That is the current plan.\" She laughed softly. Edric looked pleased. \"My daughter has been interested in political history for years. She intends to study Political Studies and Historical Administration.\" Ajax glanced at her. \"Historical Administration?\" \"It's a relatively new programme,\" Liora explained. \"It studies the development of administrative institutions, their records, and the relationship between political authority and historical documentation.\" \"That sounds interesting.\" \"It is. Although Father thinks I should have chosen something more practical.\" Edric raised an eyebrow. \"I never said that.\" \"You suggested Economics.\" \"Because it is useful.\" \"Everything is useful to you if it can be included in a budget.\" Ajax considered that. \"That sounds familiar.\" Edric looked at him. \"Your father?\" Ajax nodded. \"Though I suspect the Royal Treasury has influenced him.\" Governor Wilhelmshire laughed. \"Careful, son. The Treasury has ears.\" Liora looked towards the raised platform. \"Do you think the Crown's reception is actually useful?\" Edric answered before Ajax could. \"Of course it is. You will meet people who may become important in your professional life.\" \"That isn't what I meant.\" \"Then what did you mean?\" \"Do you think people will remember one another after tonight?\" The question was simple, but Edric did not answer immediately. Ajax looked at her. Liora continued. \"Everyone here is being introduced to everyone else. Names, families, institutions, ambitions. But most of us will leave for different places. Some will enter military academies. Others will study magic, engineering, medicine, or politics. We may not meet again for years.\" \"That doesn't make the meeting useless,\" Ajax said. She turned towards him. \"Why?\" \"Because you don't need to maintain a relationship continuously for it to matter. Sometimes you only need to remember that someone exists.\" Liora considered that. \"That's a rather practical answer.\" \"I've been told that practicality is useful.\" \"By your father?\" \"Mostly.\" Edric laughed. \"Your son is becoming dangerous, Governor.\" \"He's always been dangerous when allowed to speak.\" Ajax looked at his father. \"I'll remember that.\" *** The reception grew livelier as the evening progressed. A group of young men stood near the western balcony, discussing their respective institutions. One of them wore the uniform of a military preparatory school. Another had the insignia of an engineering institute. Ajax was introduced to them by Liora. The first was named Darius Renwick, the son of a regional military commander. He was broad-shouldered, confident, and spoke with the certainty of someone who had never had to doubt his own future. The second, Lucien Ardent, came from a family involved in magical manufacturing. He was quieter, though his eyes brightened whenever the conversation turned towards technology. \"You're joining the Central Academy?\" Darius asked. \"Yes.\" \"Which department?\" \"Possibly Archaeology and Literature.\" Darius blinked. \"Both?\" \"I'm still deciding.\" \"Why not Magical Sciences? The Academy is famous for it.\" Ajax looked at him. \"Are you joining the military?\" \"Military Command Institute.\" \"Then why not study engineering?\" Darius frowned. \"Because I'm joining the military.\" \"Exactly.\" Lucien laughed. Darius looked between them. \"What's so funny?\" \"Nothing,\" Ajax replied. \"I was simply observing that we seem to have different ideas about specialisation.\" Darius folded his arms. \"I don't see the point of studying things that won't help you.\" \"That depends on what you consider helpful.\" \"Can Archaeology help you win a battle?\" Ajax thought for a moment. \"Occasionally.\" Darius raised an eyebrow. \"How?\" \"By telling you where the previous army failed.\" Lucien laughed again. Darius looked unconvinced. \"That's not the same as fighting.\" \"No. But it might help you avoid fighting somewhere you shouldn't.\" The young military student considered that. \"You're saying history teaches strategy?\" \"Among other things.\" \"Then why not study military history?\" \"Perhaps I will.\" Darius shook his head, though his expression had softened. \"You're difficult to argue with.\" \"I've been told.\" Liora took a sip from her glass. \"That seems to be a recurring problem.\" *** A little later, the group was approached by an older gentleman wearing a dark military uniform. His hair was mostly grey, and several decorations were pinned across his chest. Darius straightened immediately. \"General Halden.\" The general nodded. \"Renwick.\" He looked at Lucien, then at Liora. Finally, his gaze settled on Ajax. \"Governor Wilhelmshire's son?\" Ajax inclined his head. \"Yes, General.\" \"I remember your father. We worked together during the northern security reforms.\" Governor Wilhelmshire, who had been speaking with another minister nearby, noticed the general and approached. \"General Halden. It's been some time.\" \"Too long.\" They exchanged greetings. The general then looked towards the younger group. \"So. The next generation.\" Darius immediately began explaining his plans for the Military Command Institute. The general listened without interruption. When Darius finished, he asked: \"Why do you want to join the military?\" Darius answered confidently. \"To serve the country.\" \"That's a good answer.\" The general waited. Darius seemed uncertain whether he was expected to say more. \"Anything else?\" \"I want to become a commander.\" \"Why?\" \"To lead.\" The general nodded slowly. \"Everyone wants to lead. Very few want to be responsible for the people who follow them.\" Darius frowned. \"Isn't that the same thing?\" \"No.\" The general glanced towards the balcony. \"When you are responsible for a hundred soldiers, you make decisions that affect a hundred lives. When you command a thousand, your mistakes become larger. When you command ten thousand, even your good intentions can become disasters.\" The young man fell silent. The general looked at Ajax. \"And you? What do you intend to become?\" \"I haven't decided.\" \"That is an honest answer.\" \"I've found that making decisions before understanding the available choices is usually inefficient.\" The general laughed. \"Your father has raised a cautious son.\" Governor Wilhelmshire smiled. \"Only in certain matters.\" \"Then perhaps he has failed.\" Ajax looked at the general. \"Is that a military assessment?\" \"An old man's opinion.\" \"Those are often more difficult to challenge.\" The general laughed again. He pulled a chair closer and sat with the younger guests. \"Tell me something. What do you think makes a good commander?\" Darius answered first. \"Strength.\" Lucien said, \"Knowledge.\" Liora considered it. \"Judgement.\" Ajax remained quiet. The general looked at him. \"And you?\" Ajax took a moment. \"Knowing when not to give an order.\" The general's expression changed slightly. \"Explain.\" \"A commander may possess strength, knowledge and judgement. But if he believes every situation requires his intervention, he will eventually become the greatest obstacle to his own people.\" The general watched him for several seconds. Then he nodded. \"That's a better answer than most officers give me.\" Darius looked mildly dissatisfied. The general noticed. \"Don't misunderstand me, Renwick. Strength matters. Knowledge matters. Judgement matters. But you must learn to recognise the limits of your own judgement.\" He leaned back. \"An army does not fail only because its commander is weak. Sometimes it fails because the commander refuses to listen.\" Lucien nodded thoughtfully. Liora looked at Ajax. \"That sounds like something worth remembering.\" \"It probably is.\" The general chuckled. \"Good. At least one of you is listening.\" *** Near the centre of the hall, a woman in a deep blue evening dress was speaking with several young guests. She was introduced as Lady Yselle Maren, daughter of a senior naval administrator. She was strikingly beautiful, with dark hair arranged in an intricate braid and a calm expression that made it difficult to determine whether she was amused or simply observant. Unlike many of the other guests, she did not seem particularly interested in discussing family positions. She was discussing ships. More precisely, the lack of them. \"The northern ports are expanding,\" she said. \"But the fleet is still relying on vessels designed for older trade routes.\" Lucien looked interested. \"Are the new engines not sufficient?\" \"Not entirely. The engines are better. The hull designs are not.\" Darius frowned. \"Isn't that a naval matter?\" \"Everything is a naval matter until it becomes an economic matter.\" She turned towards Ajax. \"And you? What will you study?\" \"Archaeology and Literature, perhaps.\" \"Then perhaps you can tell me why the Royal Maritime Archive has been neglecting its oldest records.\" Ajax looked at her. \"That is a rather specific question.\" \"I've been told that scholars enjoy specific questions.\" \"Some do.\" \"Do you?\" \"Usually, when they come with documents.\" She smiled. \"Then you may be disappointed. Most of the documents are incomplete.\" \"That doesn't necessarily make them useless.\" \"Why?\" \"An incomplete record can still tell you what someone considered worth recording. Sometimes the omissions are as interesting as the contents.\" Lady Yselle studied him for a moment. \"That's an unusual way of looking at archives.\" \"Is it?\" \"Most people want to know what happened.\" Ajax glanced at the paintings on the walls. \"I think it's equally important to know who wanted people to remember it.\" The woman smiled more genuinely this time. \"Perhaps you'll be useful after all.\" Liora looked between them. \"I thought you were going to ask him about the ships.\" \"I did. We arrived at history instead.\" \"That happens quite often around him,\" Liora said. Ajax looked at her. \"I wasn't aware I had developed a reputation.\" \"Not yet.\" *** The evening continued. People moved between conversations, exchanged contact information, and made arrangements to meet again after the institutions opened. Some connections were sincere. Others were clearly calculated. A young man from a wealthy industrial family offered Lucien access to a private workshop. A daughter of a military minister exchanged correspondence details with Darius. Liora spoke with several students who intended to study political sciences. Ajax, meanwhile, found himself having more conversations than he had expected. He spoke with a historian about the preservation of magical ruins. He listened to a military officer discuss the difficulties of transporting supplies across mountainous territories. He even found himself explaining to a young woman why he preferred studying old maps to reading contemporary travel guides. \"Old maps are inaccurate,\" she argued. \"Often.\" \"Then why study them?\" \"Because they tell you what people believed the world looked like.\" \"That seems rather indirect.\" \"History usually is.\" She laughed. At some point, Elena appeared beside him. She looked pleased with herself. \"Have you made any friends?\" Ajax glanced around. \"I've spoken to several people.\" \"That isn't what I asked.\" \"Then perhaps you should ask a more precise question.\" Elena rolled her eyes. \"You're impossible.\" \"That seems to be the general consensus.\" She leaned closer. \"I met a girl from the eastern territory. She's joining the Central Academy too.\" \"That's good.\" \"And she knows a lot about magical fashion.\" Ajax stared at her. \"Magical fashion?\" \"Yes.\" \"Is that a department?\" \"No.\" \"Then what does she study?\" \"Magical Sciences.\" \"Ah.\" Elena narrowed her eyes. \"Don't sound so disappointed.\" \"I wasn't aware I had.\" \"You did.\" Ajax decided not to argue. His sister was clearly enjoying herself. He would not ruin it. The formal dinner began shortly afterwards. The tables were arranged in several sections, with the most senior officials seated near the royal platform. The younger guests were placed together in a large dining hall adjoining the main gallery. Ajax was seated beside Liora and Lucien. Darius sat opposite them. The first courses were familiar. Roasted vegetables, fresh bread, soups, and several dishes prepared with locally sourced ingredients. The atmosphere remained lively. Then the servers began bringing out the special seafood course. It was an unusual selection. The Crown had commissioned a shipment from the Veylan Maritime Exchange, a company operating along the southern coast. The order had been placed specifically for the reception, and the company had supplied a team of culinary specialists alongside the seafood. The palace's own kitchen had handled the preparation under the supervision of the royal banquet staff. The head chef had inspected the shipment when it arrived. The seals were intact. The accompanying documents were in order. The ingredients appeared fresh. There had been no reason to suspect anything unusual. The special course included silver-shell oysters, deepwater prawns, and a delicate white-flesh fish found only in the southern waters. A light sauce accompanied the dishes. The smell was pleasant. The presentation was beautiful. Liora looked at the plate. \"Have you tried this before?\" Ajax examined it. \"No.\" \"Neither have I.\" Lucien had already taken a bite. \"It's excellent.\" Darius nodded. \"Better than the usual palace menu.\" Ajax looked at him. \"That's a rather bold statement.\" \"Try it.\" Ajax picked up his fork. He tasted a small portion. The flavour was delicate, with a slight sweetness and a freshness he wasn't accustomed to. \"It is quite good.\" Liora smiled. \"You sound surprised.\" \"I had prepared a complaint.\" \"About the food?\" \"About the expense.\" She laughed. Lucien leaned forward. \"Apparently, the Crown ordered the entire shipment specifically for tonight.\" \"From the southern coast?\" Ajax asked. \"Yes. Veylan Maritime Exchange. They supplied their own specialists as well.\" Darius nodded. \"My father mentioned the company. They have been expanding their trade routes.\" Liora looked towards the adults' table. \"That explains the number of merchants here.\" \"Does it?\" Ajax asked. \"Of course. The Crown's reception is an opportunity to establish connections. A company that supplies the royal household gains a certain amount of prestige.\" Lucien added, \"And if the reception is successful, the Crown may continue ordering from them.\" Darius smiled. \"Then perhaps the company has already won.\" Ajax looked down at his plate. \"That depends on the food.\" Darius laughed. \"You're impossible.\" \"Apparently.\" The conversation moved on. Nobody noticed anything unusual about the meal. Nobody questioned the ingredients. Nobody had any reason to. Later in the evening, the royal secretary addressed the guests on behalf of the Crown. His speech was brief. He welcomed the young entrants, wished them success in their respective institutions, and reminded them that the country would eventually depend upon the work they did. He spoke of knowledge, responsibility, cooperation and the importance of maintaining connections beyond the boundaries of one's profession. There was polite applause. Afterwards, the reception gradually began to wind down. The younger guests exchanged final greetings. Some promised to meet again. Others were already discussing travel arrangements. General Halden approached Governor Wilhelmshire before the family departed. \"Your son is an interesting young man.\" Governor Wilhelmshire glanced at Ajax. \"That is one way to describe him.\" \"He listens more than he speaks.\" \"Usually.\" \"Keep that habit alive.\" The governor smiled. \"I'll try.\" The general turned towards Ajax. \"Remember something, young man. Institutions will teach you many things. Some will be useful. Others will merely be required.\" Ajax nodded. \"But don't let the institution decide what you are allowed to become.\" The general placed a hand on his shoulder. \"Good luck at the Academy.\" \"Thank you, General.\" The journey home was quiet. Elena was tired. Their mother was speaking with their father about the guests they had met. Ajax sat beside the carriage window, watching the palace lights disappear into the distance. He had enjoyed the evening more than expected. There had been interesting people. Interesting conversations. And several questions he wanted to investigate later. He glanced down at his hand. The faint sensation of Qi remained familiar. He could feel its subtle circulation, though he had no control over it. He looked back outside. Tomorrow, he would return to his books. For now, that was enough. The Crown's reception had ended. And nobody in the palace had any reason to believe that the following morning would be different from any other."},{"t":"13. The Morning After","k":"Against the Heavens · chapter","u":"atth/c13.html","s":"The first report arrived at the palace shortly before sunrise. It was initially treated as a medical emergency involving a single guest. A young minister's son had developed a high fever during the night. His family physician had been summoned, but the boy's condition had worsened rapidly. He had begun coughing. There had been blood. The physician had immediately requested assistance from the Royal Medical Service. By the time the second report arrived, the first had not yet been resolved. A military officer who had attended the reception had collapsed at his residence. Then a member of the royal administrative staff. Then two members of a merchant family. The reports were unrelated in appearance. Different households. Different physicians. Different districts. But the symptoms were disturbingly similar. *** Ajax woke with a dry throat. For a moment, he remained still. His room was quiet. The curtains were partly open, allowing the morning light to enter. He tried to swallow. It hurt. He frowned. Perhaps he had slept with his mouth open. He reached for the glass of water on his bedside table. The first sip did little to help. The second brought an unpleasant taste. Ajax paused. It was metallic. He placed the glass down. A faint pressure had settled behind his ribs, accompanied by an unusual heaviness in his limbs. He had experienced fatigue before. This felt different. He reached for the bell. A servant entered shortly afterwards. \"Good morning, Young Master.\" \"Good morning.\" The servant approached. \"Would you like breakfast?\" \"Perhaps later. Could you bring me some more water?\" \"Of course.\" She noticed his expression. \"Are you feeling unwell?\" \"A little.\" \"Should I inform Madam?\" Ajax considered saying no. Then he remembered his father's conversation with him. \"Please do.\" The servant bowed and left. Ajax leaned back against his pillow. He closed his eyes. The faint sensation of Qi was still there. But something about it felt unusual. He could not explain what. It was as though the familiar sensation had become difficult to distinguish from the discomfort spreading through his body. He tried to focus. A cough interrupted him. He covered his mouth with a handkerchief. When he lowered it, a small red stain had appeared on the white cloth. Ajax stared at it. For several seconds, he did not move. Then he pressed the bell again. *** His mother arrived first. She was followed by his father and Elena. \"Ajax?\" His mother hurried to his bedside. \"What's wrong?\" \"I don't feel particularly well.\" His father looked at the handkerchief. His expression changed. \"How long has this been happening?\" \"Only since I woke up.\" His mother touched his forehead. \"You have a fever.\" Elena stood near the doorway. \"Is he going to be alright?\" Nobody answered immediately. His father turned towards the servant. \"Call the physician. Now.\" The servant hurried away. His mother sat beside Ajax. \"Does anything hurt?\" \"My throat. And my chest feels rather heavy.\" \"Anything else?\" \"I'm tired.\" His mother looked at him carefully. \"Did you feel this way last night?\" \"No.\" \"Are you certain?\" \"Quite.\" His father moved closer. \"Ajax, did you eat anything unusual yesterday?\" Ajax thought. \"The seafood course, perhaps.\" His father looked towards his mother. \"Did anyone else eat it?\" \"Most of the guests, I believe.\" Elena looked confused. \"Why?\" Her father did not answer. He was already reaching for the communication device on the table. *** The physician arrived within minutes. He was an elderly man who had served the Wilhelmshire family for years. He examined Ajax, asked several questions, and checked his temperature. His expression grew increasingly serious. \"Has he been coughing?\" \"Only recently,\" his mother answered. \"Any difficulty breathing?\" \"Some heaviness in the chest.\" The physician nodded. He examined the handkerchief. Then he looked at Ajax again. \"I would like to conduct a few tests.\" \"Is it serious?\" his father asked. \"I cannot say yet.\" \"Then tell me what you know.\" The physician hesitated. \"I have received reports of similar symptoms from the city this morning.\" Governor Wilhelmshire went still. \"Similar?\" \"Several people have fallen ill during the night. Some are experiencing fever, weakness and respiratory distress. A few have also reported blood when coughing.\" His mother looked at Ajax. \"How many?\" \"I don't know the exact number.\" The physician closed his medical case. \"But there are enough cases that the Royal Medical Service has begun coordinating the response.\" The room fell silent. Ajax looked at the physician. \"Did they all attend the reception?\" The physician paused. \"Some did.\" That was enough to change the atmosphere. *** By midmorning, the palace had become a centre of emergency activity. The Royal Medical Service had received dozens of reports. The Crown's administrative office was contacting every family whose members had attended the previous evening's reception. The palace kitchens had been closed. The banquet staff were being questioned. The suppliers were being contacted. And the royal physicians were attempting to determine whether the illness was connected to the dinner. The first suspicion naturally fell upon the food. The special seafood shipment was brought under examination. The Veylan Maritime Exchange was contacted. Its representatives insisted that the shipment had been properly handled and accompanied by all necessary documents. The palace's head chef was questioned. He was bewildered. \"We inspected everything when it arrived.\" \"Who conducted the inspection?\" \"My staff and the company's representatives.\" \"Were there any irregularities?\" \"None that we noticed.\" \"Did you alter the preparation?\" \"No. We followed the instructions supplied with the ingredients. The royal kitchen supervised the entire process.\" The investigator looked through the documents. \"Did you notice anything unusual about the seafood?\" \"No.\" The chef looked exhausted. \"Sir, I have prepared meals for the royal household for twenty-three years. If I had noticed something wrong, I would never have allowed it to reach the tables.\" The investigator said nothing. There was no immediate evidence of negligence. No one had knowingly served anything dangerous. And yet people were becoming ill. *** The news reached the Wilhelmshire estate shortly before noon. Governor Wilhelmshire had received a direct communication from the Crown. His face was grave as he read it. His wife stood beside him. \"What's happened?\" He handed her the document. She read it. Then read it again. \"All the families?\" \"Not all. But many of those who attended the reception have reported illnesses.\" \"How many?\" \"The number is still increasing.\" She looked towards the upper floor. \"Ajax...\" Her husband placed a hand on her shoulder. \"The physician is with him. The Royal Medical Service is sending assistance.\" \"What if—\" \"We don't know yet.\" He spoke calmly, but his expression betrayed him. Elena stood a little distance away. She had heard enough to understand that something was seriously wrong. \"Mother?\" Her mother turned. \"Yes?\" \"Will Ajax be alright?\" The lady of the house knelt before her daughter. \"We're doing everything we can.\" Elena looked down. She had not argued with Ajax that morning. She had not complained about his reading. She had not even asked whether he would attend dinner. For some reason, she wished she had. *** Ajax was beginning to find the physician's repeated visits irritating. Not because he disliked the man. Rather, the questions were becoming repetitive. \"Does your throat hurt?\" \"Yes.\" \"Does the pressure in your chest feel stronger?\" \"A little.\" \"Have you coughed again?\" \"Yes.\" The physician made another note. \"Do you feel dizzy?\" \"Occasionally.\" \"Any unusual sensations?\" Ajax hesitated. He looked towards the window. \"I think so.\" The physician raised his head. \"What kind?\" Ajax struggled to explain. He could still feel the faint circulation he had spent weeks learning to recognise. But the sensation was no longer quite the same. There was a subtle disturbance. Not pain. Not exactly. Something seemed to be interfering with the familiar pattern. He could not determine whether the sensation came from his body or from his own difficulty concentrating. \"I can't describe it properly.\" \"That's alright. Don't force yourself.\" The physician checked his pulse again. Ajax closed his eyes. For a moment, the room seemed very distant. Then he felt it. Something. A faint pressure that did not seem to belong to him. He opened his eyes. The sensation disappeared. \"Doctor?\" \"Yes?\" \"Have you ever encountered an illness that affects Qi?\" The physician looked at him. \"Many illnesses can interfere with Qi circulation.\" \"Not quite what I meant.\" \"What do you mean?\" Ajax looked down at his hands. \"I don't know yet.\" The physician exchanged a glance with the attendant. \"Rest for now. We will investigate that later.\" Ajax did not argue. He was beginning to feel rather tired. *** Elsewhere, the reports continued. A military commander had been admitted to the Royal Medical Hospital. A senior official had collapsed in his residence. Several young students who had attended the reception were now under observation. Some had developed fever. Some could barely stand. Others had begun coughing blood. The Royal Medical Service was overwhelmed. And the number of affected households continued to rise. The Crown issued an emergency order. Every guest who had attended the reception was to be located, examined and placed under observation. The palace kitchens were sealed. The Veylan Maritime Exchange was ordered to suspend the relevant shipment. The royal physicians were instructed to investigate the possibility of a common source. But no one could explain why the illness appeared so suddenly. Or why it seemed to affect people who had been perfectly healthy the previous evening. *** In the Wilhelmshire residence, the afternoon passed slowly. Ajax drifted between sleep and wakefulness. His mother remained beside him. His father moved between the room and the communication chamber, receiving reports from the Crown. Elena had been sent away twice. Both times, she had returned. Eventually, Ajax opened his eyes and found her sitting near the foot of his bed. \"Elena?\" She immediately stood. \"You're awake!\" \"For the moment.\" She approached. \"Do you need anything?\" \"Water, perhaps.\" She brought the glass. Ajax took a small sip. Then he looked at her. \"Shouldn't you be downstairs?\" \"No.\" \"That's a rather convincing argument.\" Elena sat again. For a while, neither spoke. Then she asked quietly: \"Does it hurt?\" Ajax considered the question. \"Somewhat.\" \"Are you scared?\" He looked at her. He could have given her a reassuring answer. He could have said that everything would be fine. But he did not know that. So he simply said: \"I don't understand what's happening.\" Elena lowered her eyes. Ajax reached out and placed a hand over hers. \"That doesn't mean we won't.\" She nodded. His mother watched them from the other side of the room. For the first time that day, she allowed herself to breathe a little more easily. *** That evening, the Royal Medical Service sent another message. The number of affected people had increased. The symptoms were not following any recognised pattern. Ordinary treatments had produced inconsistent results. Some patients were stabilising. Others were worsening. The physicians could not identify a common illness. And there was something else. Several patients had begun reporting unusual sensations. They described pressure beneath the skin, unfamiliar movements within their bodies. The reports were dismissed as symptoms of fever and exhaustion. For now. *** Ajax lay awake long after the room had grown dark. His mother had finally been persuaded to rest. A physician remained outside the door. The house was unusually quiet. He closed his eyes and tried to feel the familiar circulation. It was still there. But something had changed. He could not tell whether it was his body, his senses, or the illness itself. Then, for the briefest moment, he felt something answer. Ajax opened his eyes. The room was empty. He waited. Nothing happened. After a while, he looked towards the ceiling. \"...What exactly is wrong with me?\" No one answered. Far beyond the Wilhelmshire estate, the Crown was searching for the source of an illness that had appeared overnight. And somewhere beneath the confusion, something had begun to awaken."},{"t":"14. The Quiet Sickness","k":"Against the Heavens · chapter","u":"atth/c14.html","s":"Ajax did not tell the physician about the second sensation. He had come close. The man had asked whether anything unusual had happened, and Ajax had opened his mouth to describe a pressure that did not belong to him, a presence that had answered once in the dark and then gone quiet. Then he had looked at the physician's face — tired, harried, already carrying forty other patients — and said instead that he was tired. It was not entirely a lie. --- The household settled into a rhythm of waiting. His mother took up residence in a chair by the window and refused to be moved from it. His father divided his hours between Ajax's room and the communication chamber, where reports arrived at intervals that grew steadily shorter. Elena had been sent to her rooms twice and had returned three times. By the second morning, the estate had acquired the particular silence of a house with a sick person in it. Servants spoke in lowered voices. The kitchen sent up trays that came back almost untouched. Someone had hung a cloth over the mirror in the corridor, and nobody admitted to doing it. Ajax found the cloth more disturbing than the illness. --- The Royal Medical Service sent a second physician on the third day — younger than the first, with the brisk manner of someone who had been told to be efficient and had taken it as a personal instruction. He asked the same questions in a different order. Then he asked one that was new. \"Have you noticed anything moving?\" Ajax looked at him. \"Moving.\" \"Under the skin. Beneath the ribs, sometimes the arms. Several patients have mentioned it.\" The physician's pen hovered. \"The senior physicians believe it is a fever artefact. I am asking anyway.\" \"Why?\" \"Because forty people have reported the same artefact,\" the physician said, \"and I do not like coincidences with that many witnesses.\" Ajax was quiet for a moment. \"Yes,\" he said. \"I have noticed something.\" The pen moved. \"Where?\" \"Behind the ribs. It does not stay in one place.\" Ajax considered how much to say, and decided on all of it. \"It moves against my breathing. When I breathe in, it goes the other way.\" The physician stopped writing. \"Say that again.\" \"When I breathe in, it goes the other way.\" The physician sat down slowly, which Ajax thought was a strange thing to do in the middle of an examination. \"Four days,\" the physician said. \"Four days and nobody has said that. They say it moves. They do not say *how*.\" \"Perhaps they have not been paying attention.\" \"Perhaps.\" The physician looked at him with an expression Ajax could not read. \"Or perhaps you are the first one who knows how to pay attention.\" --- That evening the thing inside him moved again. Ajax had been waiting for it. He had spent the afternoon doing what he had spent a month learning to do — sitting still, letting his attention settle, observing without grasping. His mother thought he was sleeping. He was counting. The Qi was still there. He could find it now without effort, the way one finds a familiar room in the dark. But it had changed. There were places where the network he had sketched in his diary felt *thicker*, and places where it felt thin, as though something had been drinking from it. And beneath it, in the gaps, was the other thing. It was not Qi. Qi was quiet and everywhere, like the temperature of a room. This was local. It had a *here*, and the here was behind his ribs, and it moved. He did not try to touch it. He had learned that much. He simply watched where it went. --- It went toward the thin places. That was the first thing he understood, and it took him most of the night. The creature — he did not yet have a better word, and he resented the word — did not wander. It moved along the network he had drawn in his notebook, and it moved in one direction, and the direction was always toward the places where his Qi was weakest. It was not attacking him. It was *feeding*, and it preferred the parts of him that were already struggling. Ajax lay in the dark with his eyes open and thought about that for a long time. --- On the fifth morning, the physician brought news and did not try to soften it. \"The count is above two hundred. There are cases in four districts and two of the outer towns. The Crown has closed the eastern market.\" \"The food?\" Ajax asked. \"The seafood is still the favourite.\" The physician's mouth twisted. \"It is also becoming less convincing. The Exchange has produced documents for every crate, the kitchen records are clean, and three of the affected households never received a shipment at all.\" \"So it is not the food.\" \"Something is not the food,\" the physician agreed. \"The senior physicians have not yet decided what.\" Ajax looked toward the window. Outside, the orchid trees were shedding petals onto the gravel in a slow, unhurried way that seemed to belong to a different world. \"Physician.\" \"Mm.\" \"You said forty people reported movement.\" \"Forty-one, now.\" \"How many of them are still alive?\" The physician did not answer immediately. \"Most,\" he said at last. \"But not all of them are still themselves.\" --- That was the sentence Ajax carried for the rest of the day. Not *dead*. Not *recovering*. *Not still themselves.* He thought about the thing behind his ribs, moving toward the thin places, feeding where he was weakest. He thought about what would be left of a man after enough of that. He thought about how it would feel to be present while it happened — to notice, as he noticed everything, the exact moment his own attention began to belong to something else. He thought that he would very much prefer not to find out. --- Elena came to him that evening with a book. She put it on the bed without ceremony and sat down in the chair their mother had vacated. \"What is it?\" Ajax asked. \"Something stupid.\" She folded her arms. \"It's about a knight who gets cursed and has to find a flower. There's a hermit. The hermit is the only good part.\" \"Why are you giving it to me?\" \"Because you have been lying in this bed for five days being interesting at everyone, and it is exhausting to watch.\" She looked at the window rather than at him. \"And because you cannot read your own books without writing in them, and this one is mine, so I will know if you do.\" Ajax turned the book over. \"It has a picture of a flower on the cover.\" \"It has a picture of *the* flower.\" \"It looks like a cabbage.\" \"It is a very important cabbage,\" Elena said, and Ajax laughed, and then had to stop because laughing hurt his chest, and Elena's face did something complicated before she rearranged it. \"Elena.\" \"What.\" \"Thank you.\" She stood up. \"Read the hermit part,\" she said, and left. --- He read the hermit part. The hermit was a minor character who appeared for eleven pages and gave the knight advice that the knight ignored. Ajax read it twice. The advice was this: *you cannot take a thing out of a man that has grown into him. You can only give him something better to hold.* Ajax lay in the dark afterward with the book on his chest and did not sleep for a long time. He did not know whether the advice was true. He suspected the author had not known either. But it had the shape of something, and he had spent a month learning to notice shapes. *You can only give him something better to hold.* He thought about the thing behind his ribs, feeding on the thin places. And for the first time since the fever began, he thought: *what if I did not try to take it out.* --- The thought frightened him enough that he got up. He could not walk, so he did not get up in the ordinary sense — he pushed himself upright against the headboard and sat there with his hands in his lap, breathing carefully, while the room settled around him. Then he closed his eyes and did what he had been doing every night for a month. He found the Qi. He found the thing that was not Qi. And instead of watching it, instead of following it, instead of doing any of the careful, patient, observing things he had taught himself to do — He thought about his own name. *Ajax Wilhelmshire.* The second young master. The one who noticed the wiring and the wrong tablecloth and the painted spot on the vase. The one who had asked the stars what crime he had committed, and received no answer, and decided to demand one anyway. It was not a technique. It was not the scripture, which he had not attempted and did not know how to attempt. It was simply the most *him* thing he could think of, held deliberately, the way one holds a lamp up in a dark room. And for one second — one long, strange, entirely unglamorous second — the thing behind his ribs stopped moving. --- Ajax opened his eyes. The room was the same. The cloth was still over the mirror. Somewhere below, a servant was moving a chair. He was breathing hard, and his chest hurt, and he had no idea what he had just done. He picked up his notebook and wrote, in handwriting that was worse than usual: *I do not think it is an illness.* He looked at the sentence for a while. Then he crossed it out — carefully, so it could still be read — and wrote underneath: *I think it is a guest that has not been introduced.* And then, because he was still himself, and because being himself had just done something he did not understand: *I should find out what it wants before it finds out what I want.* --- Outside, the night was very quiet. In the corridor, the physician on watch had fallen asleep in his chair. And in a hundred and forty households across the province, people were waking up in the dark and feeling something move beneath their ribs, and reaching for the bell, and finding that their hands did not quite do what they asked."},{"t":"15. What the Guest Wants","k":"Against the Heavens · chapter","u":"atth/c15.html","s":"By the seventh day the province had a name for it. They called it the quiet sickness, because it did not announce itself. There was no wasting, no lesions, no dramatic collapse. A man would feel a heaviness, then a pressure, and then — according to the physicians, who had begun to write reports in the flat language of people who had run out of explanations — a change in *manner*. The reports used the word carefully. Not madness. Not possession. **Manner.** One of the affected was a clerk in the eastern administration who had begun refusing to sign documents in his own hand. He would sit at his desk for hours, holding the pen, and when asked what he was waiting for, he said that he was waiting to be told which name to write. Another was a woman who had stopped sleeping. Not from pain. She said that when she closed her eyes, she could feel what the others were doing. There were forty-one reports of movement. There were now three hundred and six cases. --- Ajax read all of it. His father had begun bringing the reports up rather than bringing them to the communication chamber, because Ajax asked better questions than the clerks and because — though neither of them said it — the governor had begun to want his son's opinion. On the seventh day Ajax asked for a map. His father brought one. It was a survey of the province, marked with the affected districts in a careful hand, and Ajax spread it across the bed and looked at it for a long time without speaking. \"What do you see?\" his father asked. \"I don't know yet.\" Ajax traced a line with one finger. \"May I have the dates as well?\" \"Dates?\" \"When each case was reported. Not where. When.\" His father looked at him. \"That is an odd thing to want.\" \"I know.\" \"May I ask why?\" Ajax did not look up from the map. \"Because if it is moving, it has a direction,\" he said. \"And if it is spreading, it has a speed. And if I have a direction and a speed, I can work out where it was on the first day.\" His father was silent for a moment. \"Which would tell us where it came from.\" \"Which would tell us where it came from,\" Ajax agreed. --- The dates arrived that afternoon. Ajax spent the rest of the day at the small table by the window, with the map weighted down by a water glass at each corner, and worked. It was not difficult work. It was the same work he had done a hundred times with wiring and tablecloths and flower arrangements — the work of noticing that the world had a pattern and that nobody had bothered to draw it yet. The first cases were at the palace and in a merchant house on the eastern road. The second wave was in the districts between. By the fourth day it had crossed the river. He drew arcs. He drew arrows. He was not precise, and he knew he was not precise, and he wrote *approximate* three times in the margin so that he would not later mistake his own guess for a fact. Then he sat back and looked at what he had drawn. The centre of the arcs was not the palace. The centre was north-east of the palace, past the outer town, in a district of warehouses and rented rooms and people who did not have physicians. Ajax looked at that district for a while. Then he called for his father. --- \"It started there,\" he said. \"Not at the reception. The reception is where it was *found*.\" Governor Wilhelmshire studied the map. \"The Crown has already investigated the warehouse district. They found nothing.\" \"They found nothing because they were looking for a contaminated shipment.\" Ajax tapped the centre of the arcs. \"They should have been looking for a person.\" --- The person was found the next morning. She was a dock worker, forty-three years old, unmarried, no family in the city. She had been ill for eleven days — longer than anyone else — and had been treated for a chest infection by a physician who had not reported her because she had not attended the reception and therefore was not on any of the Crown's lists. She had been coughing blood for nine days. She had been going to work for seven of them. --- The reports stopped using the word \"manner\" after that. The Crown issued a second emergency order, and this one did not seal kitchens. It closed the docks, closed the eastern market, closed the roads out of the province, and authorised the Royal Medical Service to enter any household in which a person had been reported sleepless. It also, in a clause that took three days to become public, authorised the Service to *detain* persons exhibiting changed manner pending examination. Ajax read that clause twice. Then he folded the paper and put it in his desk drawer and did not mention it to anyone. --- His condition had not worsened, which the physicians found interesting and Ajax found alarming. \"You are not getting better,\" the younger physician said, on the ninth day. \"But you are not getting worse, either. You are stable at a level of illness that should be progressing.\" \"Should be?\" \"Every other patient is progressing.\" The physician looked at his notes rather than at Ajax. \"Faster or slower, but always in the same direction. You have been at the same point for four days.\" \"Perhaps I am luckier than they are.\" \"Perhaps.\" The physician hesitated. \"Or perhaps something is holding it still.\" Ajax did not answer. He had not told anyone what he had done that night with his own name. He had written it in his notebook and then written over it, and he had not tried it again, because he did not understand it and because he was afraid that trying it again would break whatever was holding. But he thought about it constantly. *The self imposes its order.* He had read those words in a book with no title, in a dream that was not a dream, and had thought they were philosophy. Now he was lying in a bed with something living behind his ribs, and the philosophy had done something. He did not know what. He did not know whether it had helped or whether he had imagined it. But the thing inside him had stopped for one second when he held his own name up like a lamp, and the physicians were standing at his bedside saying *you should be progressing*, and Ajax Wilhelmshire had spent his whole life noticing things that other people overlooked. He was not going to stop noticing now. --- That night he tried again. Not with his name. His name had stopped working on the second attempt, which he had half expected — it had been a thought, not a technique, and thoughts do not repeat. He tried something smaller. He thought about the garden. The orchid trees shedding petals onto the gravel. The specific way the light came through the eastern windows in the morning and made a stripe across the floorboards that he had watched every day for years without ever once deciding to. He held that. Not the flower, not the idea of the garden — the actual stripe of light, on the actual floorboards, on a specific morning he could still see. The thing behind his ribs shifted. Not stopped. Shifted — a small, careful movement, like a man in a crowded room making space for someone to pass. Ajax held the light-stripe and did not move and did not breathe. And something that was not quite a thought, and not quite a voice, and not quite anything he had a word for, arrived in the back of his mind. It was not language. It was closer to the way one knows the shape of a room before the lamp is lit. But if he had to translate it into words, the words would have been: *You are not empty.* Ajax lay very still. *You are not empty,* the thing said again, or did not say, or simply was. *That is unusual.* --- He did not answer it. He did not know how to answer it. He did not know whether answering would be a mistake. He was seventeen years old and lying in a bed in his father's house with something that had killed three hundred people living behind his ribs, and it had just told him that he was not empty, and he had absolutely no idea what to do. So he did what he had learned to do in a garden under the orchid trees, a month and a lifetime ago. He observed. He observed the thing observing him. He observed the network of Qi with its thin places and its thick places. He observed the way the thing moved toward the thin places and did not seem to know that he could see it doing so. And he observed, with a small cold shock that he would remember for the rest of his life, that the thing was *curious about him*. Not hungry. Not hostile. Curious. *You are not empty,* it had said. *That is unusual.* Ajax had spent his childhood being looked at with pity and his adolescence being looked at with discomfort. He had been the second young master, the one in the chair, the one people were kind to. Nobody had ever looked at him and found him *unusual* in a way that seemed to please them. He thought, absurdly, that he might have liked it. Then he thought that he was lying in bed having a feeling about a plague, and that this was exactly the sort of thing that got a man detained under the new clause. He closed his eyes. *Tomorrow,* he thought. *Tomorrow I will think about this properly.* The thing behind his ribs did not move again that night. It was, Ajax would later reflect, the first night since the fever began that he slept well."},{"t":"Ajax Wilhelmshire","k":"Against the Heavens · Main Cast","u":"atth/lore/atth--ajax-wilhelmshire.html","s":"  Ajax Wilhelmshire\n\n  Status:   main protagonist. POV of ch1–4, 6, 8–13.\n\n   Confirmed\n\n- Eldest son of   Governor Wilhelmshire  ; family is one of the republic's most prominent\n-   Seventeen.   Turned seventeen three months before ch1\n-   Lower body completely paralysed   from an accident; uses a wheelchair. The accident is remembered for \"a kind of pain that made one forget how to scream\"\n- Thick silky black hair, black eyes. Looks   noble and regal rather than handsome  \n- Before the accident: lively, optimistic, enjoyed conversation, dreamed of exploring the skies\n- After: friends stopped visiting, letters thinned. He read more and studied more, hoping that if he became interesting enough they would return. They did not\n- At the banquet he was already changed — patient, dry, watchful\n-   Strengths:   acute observational eye (fixes the flower arrangement, the wiring, the tablecloth, spots the painted vase mark); remembers names; dry humour; reads people accurately\n-   Weaknesses:   not flexible; bad at social gatherings; almost no friends; overly curious; does things his own way; loves to experiment\n- Emotionally he is  \"a volcano which erupts immediately when full\"  — unlike Zhen, who shows emotion gradually\n-   Hobby: poetry.   Enjoys reading and writing it\n- Asked the stars:  \"What had he done to deserve such a fate? What crime had earned him this punishment?\"  and decided that whatever the price, he would pay it to stand again —  \"even if he had to use force\" \n\n   What he can do (as of ch15)\n\n  Perception only.   Four weeks of practice. He can recognise the internal network of \"threads\" even during ordinary activity, and he can feel where his Qi is thin. That is all.\n\nHe has   not   created Demonic Qi. He has not attempted the scripture's method — and Cheonma never wrote down how. See   The Demonic Path  .\n\n  Briefly, twice  , an act of deliberate selfhood (holding his own name, then a specific memory of morning light) made the parasite  pause . He does not know what that was, whether it helped, or whether it was real. It was   instinct, not technique   — and it is the same instinct the hijack will require.\n\n   Physical description (from the original sheet)\n\nThick silky black hair and black eyes. He looks   noble and regal rather than handsome  .\n\nAccording to secret letters his sister exchanges with her friends,  \"if his brother wasn't disabled he would definitely be on top of the 'Ideal Men list to Snatch' list\"  — a secret ranking chart published every year in women's circles, famous across the continent, governed by an unspoken rule that men must never be told about it.\n\n  This is a useful device.   It gives you a running joke (Ajax is the last to know), a way to show the sister's world, and a way for strangers to have opinions about him before he enters a room.\n\n   Inheritance\n\n  Cheonma  's diary → the   Reverse Heaven Divine Scripture  .\n\n  Crucially: he does not know what the scripture really is.   He reads it as a philosophical curiosity and an interesting question. He has not attempted the method — and Cheonma himself wrote  \"I have not understood the method. I have only confirmed the possibility.\" \n\nHe does not know that success would mean stealing from heaven. This must be the engine of the volume:   he is being recruited into a crime he does not know is a crime.  \n\n- Infected with the parasite — see   The Six Calamities  , Calamity I. Fever, blood on the handkerchief, pressure behind the ribs. Has felt something \"answer\" him twice.\n\n   Open questions\n\n- His age relative to the accident: how long ago?   UNRESOLVED  \n- Does he suspect the diary is dangerous? He has noticed the dreams are consistent and give new information each time, which he finds hard to dismiss.   He trusts it.   That trust is the vulnerability.\n"},{"t":"Cheonma","k":"Against the Heavens · Main Cast","u":"atth/lore/atth--cheonma.html","s":"  Cheonma\n\n  Status:   the antagonist-mentor. Deceased before the story begins. Author of Ajax's inheritance.\n\n   Confirmed from the diary (ch3, ch4, ch10)\n\n    Voice\nPersonal, untidy, full of corrections — sentences struck out, rewritten, struck out again.  \"Someone arguing with himself in ink.\"  Later sections grow neater and sharper:  \"the hand of a man who had stopped arguing and started deciding.\" \n\n    His argument\n-  \"I have spent too many years looking upward.\" \n-  \"The heavens do not answer, nor do they care to.\" \n-  \"I have mistaken strength for sovereignty. I have mistaken silence for peace. I have mistaken the heavens for something beyond myself.\" \n-  \"Anything given for free, is a shackle. Everything sweet is poison.\" \n-  \"A man with no self is a river. Whatever falls into him, he carries. Do not be a river. Be a gate.\" \n-  \"To read the heavens, I must first learn to read myself.\" \n-  \"They say the heavens decide who may ascend... I disagree.\" \n-  \"No hand will come down from above to grant me permission. No door will open because I waited politely outside it. And if it is the heavens themselves that stand in my way, then I shall cut the heavens themselves.\" \n-  \"I once wanted to reach the heavens. To become one with that divine realm. But now I know. That can never happen, not until one of us yields. Whatever those orthodox idiots call me, I do not care. I will not go down, no matter what the heavens throw at me.\" \n\n    From the Scripture (ch10)\n- Qi descends, returns. The cultivator who follows the cycle grows purer as the mind approaches heaven.\n- He asked  \"Why?\"  — why should purity of mind determine purity of Qi?\n-  \"The self distinguishes. The self interprets. The self imposes. What passes through it may no longer remain unchanged.\" \n-  \"The mind may be a filter.\" \n-  \"I attempted to impose the self upon the natural flow. The Qi changed. It did not become pure. It became   mine  .\" \n-  \"Natural Qi follows Heaven. The self imposes its order. The flow is altered. The result is   Demonic Qi  .\" \n-  \"I have not understood the method. I have only confirmed the possibility. The self must become the source of the imposed order. Until then, the change remains superficial.\" \n\n    What is known about him\n- He spent   three months   in the wilderness observing nature before he could perceive Qi (ch6)\n- Historians are uncertain whether Cheonma is a   name or a title   (ch6)\n- Different records call him a wandering ascetic, or a man who rejected the authority of the heavens\n- He is called   a heretic   by \"those orthodox idiots\"\n- The stars flickered and the diary shuddered when Ajax spoke his name; Ajax felt something like  \"standing at the foot of an enormous mountain and understanding, for the first time, that he had been mistaking the foothills for the world\" \n\n   The critical thing Ajax does not know\n\nCheonma's method   stops Qi from returning to heaven  . Ajax reads this as philosophy. In the setting's terms it is theft — see   The Two Heavens  . Cheonma knew exactly what he was doing. Ajax does not.\n\n  This asymmetry is the engine of volume 1.   Ajax is being taught by a dead man to commit a crime he has not been told is a crime.\n\n   His style on the page\n\n  Cheonma writes to himself.   He compresses philosophy into two or three lines because he does not need to convince himself. He describes methods vaguely because they were never separable from him. He   leaves blanks   — some deliberate, some because he never got round to them.\n\nHe is not hiding anything from Ajax. He is simply not talking to Ajax at all.\n\n  Consequence for the prose:   never have Cheonma conveniently answer the question Ajax needs. Every gap must be filled by Ajax's own experiment, and the failures must be shown. See   The Demonic Path  .\n\n   What Cheonma got wrong (PROPOSED)\n\nHe says he  \"confirmed the possibility\"  but  \"not understood the method.\"  He never states the method.\n\n  Recommendation:   he did find it eventually — the Scripture is what he left behind — and the reason it is a diary of questions rather than a manual is that   the method cannot be transmitted, only re-derived.   Each practitioner must build it from their own self. That makes Ajax's struggle necessary rather than an information deficit, and it makes Cheonma's failure at the end meaningful rather than accidental.\n\n   Open questions\n\n- Is he alive?   UNRESOLVED   — the diary reads like a last testament, but nothing confirms death\n- Was he human?   UNRESOLVED  \n- Why did the inheritance come to a paralysed boy in a provincial republic?   UNRESOLVED   — this is one of the six mysteries\n- What does  \"not until one of us yields\"  mean? Who is the other party?   UNRESOLVED, and probably a late-volume revelation  \n"},{"t":"Merlin","k":"Against the Heavens · Main Cast","u":"atth/lore/atth--merlin.html","s":"  Merlin\n\n  Status:   the teacher-mentor. Author of Zhen's tome. Almost nothing is established about him yet.\n\n  This note is thin because the manuscript has not earned more.   Do not invent biography. What follows is what the page says, plus the structural role he must play.\n\n  No spells.   Merlin's path has no incantation, gesture, or formula.\n\nZhen reads   threads of fate   for debts of necessity — events that must happen and have been obstructed by power — and does the work to complete them. In return he may name a   reward fitting to the work  : a power, a relic, an opportunity. What he pays is his   choices  : he can only do what must be done, never what he wants. See   Threads of Fate   and   The Two Paths  .\n\n  And unlike Cheonma, Merlin actually teaches.   The tome advises him on how to handle whatever he receives. Zhen has a guide who answers. Ajax has a dead man's notebook with the answers cut out.\n\n   Confirmed on the page (ch5)\n\nThe tome's first lesson is   Perception   — and for Zhen it is not meditation, it is   due diligence  . You cannot see a debt you have not looked at closely enough, and you cannot complete a work you do not understand.\n\n   Magic is not made by imagination alone. \n   Imagination without understanding is a picture without a world. \n   First perceive. Then imagine. Then believe. And finally, understand. \n\nFour beats:   Perceive → Imagine → Believe → Understand.  \n\n-   Perceive   — see what is actually there, not what you expect. Until you can do this, you cannot tell an obligation from an opportunity.\n-   Imagine   — hold the shape of what is owed without distorting it. A work built on a false picture completes the wrong thing.\n-   Believe   — commit to the work.   This is the step that actually spends the choice.   Doubt is the withholding; belief is the surrender of your own preference to what must be done.\n-   Understand   — the work and the worker are no longer separate. The debt closes, and only now may he name his price.\n\n  \"Believe\" is the load-bearing word   — it means  stop hedging and accept the obligation . It is not religious faith. It is the moment he gives up the right to have wanted something else.\n\n    The three exercises (ch5)\n-   Vision   — observe a single object without naming it, without recalling what you know about it. Shape, edges, light, shadow\n-   Sound   — listen without choosing what deserves attention; separate overlapping sounds, notice distance, notice change\n-   Mind   — the mind is not merely for remembering; it is for  arranging . Observe, compare, question.  \"Do not accept the first explanation simply because it is convenient\" \n\nClosing warning:  \"Do not mistake perception for mastery. You have only begun to see.\" \n\n    Zhen's phrasing\nHe calls Merlin  \"the old man\"  — familiar, irreverent, and affectionate.  \"Well, at least the old man is consistent.\" \n\n   The structural role he must play\n\n  Merlin's tome is transmissible. Cheonma's is not.   This is the single most useful contrast in the book.\n\n      Merlin       Cheonma    \n --- --- --- \n  Form   A tome — an enormous book, reinforced spine   A diary and a scripture, worn and vague  \n  Addressed to   A student   Himself  \n  Method   Written down, learnable, stepwise   Vague, gapped, re-derived by each practitioner  \n    Path       The self becomes the passage the world must move through       The self elevates beyond the world    \n    Method       Exchange   — he completes obstructed necessities and names a price     Force   — mimic, corrupt, steal  \n    What he spends       His choices   — he can only do what must be done   Nothing; he takes  \n  Direction of travel   The world completes itself through him   He goes over it  \n  Virtue   Consent   Distance  \n  Failure mode     He can no longer act for himself   — every step chosen, none of them his     Noise   — stolen Qi resisting, carrying his own unresolved thoughts  \n\n  Why this matters structurally:   Zhen can advance by  reading carefully and getting out of the way . Ajax can only advance by  becoming someone  — slower, more painful, and it generates   The Demonic Path  's whisper mechanic.\n\nThis also means Zhen's power curve is naturally faster and more legible than Ajax's.   Use that.   It is not a flaw in the design — it is the reason Zhen can afford to investigate a plague while Ajax is lying in a bed being haunted by himself.\n\nSee   The Two Paths   for the full geometry, including why each man's failure state is the other's virtue.\n\n   The Greek inheritance\n\nMerlin's tradition is the   Sea   civilisation's metaphysics — the  techne  position: a mind that understands nature can work with it. No permission, no rent, no ledger. See   The Two Heavens  .\n\n  Do not name Greece, Merlin's historical sources, or Arthurian material in the text.   Take the mechanism: craft-knowledge, four-beat acquisition, and the moral claim that understanding is legitimate. Discard the costume.\n\n   Open questions\n\n- Is Merlin alive?   UNRESOLVED  \n- Was he human?   UNRESOLVED  \n- Why all four beats — what does  believe  do that  imagine  does not?   UNRESOLVED, and important.   This is the gap a reader will notice first, so it needs a real answer before Zhen's first spell.\n- Does Merlin's path have a failure mode as dangerous as Ajax's whispers?   UNRESOLVED — recommend yes.   The mirror should cost something too. Candidates:  certainty  (a man who has understood the world correctly becomes very hard to argue with), or  distance  (perception trained so hard that he stops feeling anything about what he sees).\n"},{"t":"Zhen Shuri","k":"Against the Heavens · Main Cast","u":"atth/lore/atth--zhen-shuri.html","s":"  Zhen Shuri\n\n  Status:   main protagonist. POV of ch5, ch7.\n\n   Confirmed\n\n-   Seventeen.   Commoner. Family is   nomadic   — they move between towns taking menial work\n- Father repairs household devices; mother does cleaning and cooking; has a   younger sibling  \n- Long brown hair, green eyes. In ch1 he is working as household staff at the Wilhelmshire estate, collar slightly crooked\n-   A transmigrator from Earth   who has memories of the original Zhen Shuri\n-   Engineer.   Designs a compact magical regulator — takes unstable energy and distributes it through smaller channels. The problem he is solving is  cost : existing regulators are bulky or need rare components\n- Cannot afford the academy. His father tells him to keep his job and find a workshop apprenticeship instead\n- Persuaded his family to relocate to the   Central Kingdom   for work and opportunity\n\n   Personality\n\n- Creative, imaginative,   executes before his mind reaches a conclusion  \n- Plans fairly well;   patience is a weakness  \n- Will compromise on small things for a greater good\n- Lazy when at ease; avoids tiring work\n- Does not do much experimentation — unlike Ajax\n- Very talented and   cunning  ; \"basically the great con artist\"\n- Capable when he commits.   Quite emotional  \n\n   Voice\n\nDry, deflecting, self-aware. Defends himself with jokes —  \"And it worked for nearly four seconds.\" / \"Three.\" / \"Four, if we count the moment before it exploded.\"  Genuinely funny in a way Ajax is not: Ajax's humour is observational, Zhen's is verbal.\n\n   What he knows (CONFIRMED, ch7)\n\n- Six calamities will come\n- The first is a sentient, hive-minded, fast-evolving parasite created by Heaven\n- It arrives roughly a month after \"the beginning of the original sequence\"\n-   Ajax Wilhelmshire is the first to contract it  \n- He does   not   know how it spreads, whether it can be detected before symptoms, or whether his memories are reliable\n- His memories are \"fragments,\" and he knows fragments are dangerous\n\n   The mechanic that should carry his arc\n\nHe is holding a   copy of the world's ending   — but it is the version  before  Ajax changed anything. Every act of defiance by Ajax makes Zhen's knowledge slightly more wrong, and   Zhen will not understand why  . That is his volume 1 mystery, and it welds the two plotlines together. See   The Two Heavens  .\n\n   Physical description (from the original sheet)\n\n  Long brown hair, green eyes.   Seventeen.\n\n   Inheritance\n\n  Merlin  's tome. First lesson:   Perception.   See   Qi and the Cycle  .\n\n   Open questions\n\n- Does he know Ajax by name before ch1? (He says  \"I know\"  when Ajax introduces himself — IMPLIED: the name is famous locally, not personal recognition.)   UNRESOLVED  \n- How much of the original novel does he actually remember, in what form — read it, watched it, wrote it?   UNRESOLVED, and important.   It determines how reliable his information is and whether he can be surprised.\n- Does he have any cultivation ability yet?   No — CONFIRMED.   He has done perception exercises only.\n"},{"t":"Qi and the Cycle","k":"Against the Heavens · Power Systems","u":"atth/lore/atth--qi-and-the-cycle.html","s":"  Qi and the Cycle\n\n   What is CONFIRMED on the page\n\nFrom   Reverse Heaven Divine Scripture  , ch10:\n\n   Qi originates from Heaven. \n   It descends to Earth. \n   It returns to Heaven. \n   Thus, the cycle continues. \n\n   The cultivator follows this cycle. \n   The closer the mind approaches Heaven, the purer the Qi. \n   The purer the Qi, the greater its capacity. \n   The meridians expand. The dantian deepens. \n\n   Natural Qi follows Heaven. \n   The self imposes its order. \n   The flow is altered. \n   The result is Demonic Qi. \n\n   Heaven gives the order. \n   The self rejects it. \n   The Qi changes. \n\n   What Ajax has actually achieved (CONFIRMED, ch6–ch9)\n\nThis matters — he is   not   powerful. He is barely a beginner.\n\n  Chapter   Achievement  \n --- --- \n  ch6   Seven days of exercises, nothing. Breaks a crystal casing; feels released energy. First evidence Qi exists  \n  ch7 (old ch8)   Ten days. Can recognise the sensation but loses it when he concentrates. Learns to observe without grasping  \n  ch8 (old ch9)   Two weeks. Perceives an internal network of \"threads.\" Recognises Qi during ordinary activity without meditating  \n  ch9 (old ch10)   Two weeks further. Fills an entire diary. Receives the Reverse Heaven Scripture  \n  ch10–13   Reads the scripture.   Has not attempted the method.   Cheonma himself had not understood it  \n\n  Critical for continuity:   Ajax cannot yet do  anything  with Qi except perceive it. He has no technique, no combat ability, no enhancement. Every fight before the parasite must be won with his eyes and his mouth, not his cultivation.\n\n   The cultivation ladder (PROPOSED)\n\nNeeded so \"progress\" means something. Full detail in   The Demonic Path  .\n\n1.   Perception   — noticing Qi exists. Ajax is here, solidly, through ch15.\n2.   First Imprint   — Demonic Qi exists but he cannot control it.   This is what the hijack gives him.   It is not a promotion; it is a liability.\n3.   Refinement   — making the imprint purer. See the whisper mechanic below.\n4.   Sovereignty   — the filtered Qi no longer returns to heaven at all. Cheonma reached this.\n5.   Reverse Heaven   — the practitioner becomes a source rather than a conduit. Unknown if possible.\n\n  Critical:   Ajax reaches stage 2 at the hijack and does   not   progress past it in volume 1. Stage 2 is the lowest realm, and it makes him worse at fighting than a healthy adult.\n\n   The whisper mechanic (the heart of the system)\n\nWhen Ajax imposes his will on Qi,   the will is not clean.   It carries his redundant thoughts, half-formed intentions, fear, and wants he has not admitted.\n\nThe Demonic Qi therefore carries his noise — and   speaks it back.  \n\n  Whispers are not a demon. They are Ajax's own unfinished thoughts, echoing out of his Qi.\n\n  Consequence:   Ajax's power is capped by his   self-knowledge  . He cannot advance by getting stronger. He advances by understanding something true about himself — which is slow, unpleasant, and dramatic. Refining the Qi  is  the work of understanding himself.\n\nThis is the cleanest constraint available to you. Do not replace it with a power-up.\n\n\n   The three exercises (CONFIRMED, ch3/ch5)\n\n-  Stillness of the Body  — sit, do not control the breath, watch it arrive and leave\n-  Stillness of the Senses  — listen without hunting for a particular sound; sort near from far\n-  Observation of the World  — watch what connects one moment to the next;  \"The world is not a picture. It is a sentence, and most men read only the nouns.\" \n\nThese are the load-bearing lines of the whole book. They should recur, in altered form, at every stage.\n\n   Merlin's counter-method (CONFIRMED, ch5)\n\n   Magic is not made by imagination alone. \n   Imagination without understanding is a picture without a world. \n   First perceive. Then imagine. Then believe. And finally, understand. \n\nZhen's four-beat ladder is deliberately the mirror of Ajax's. Ajax goes   inward   (impose the self on the world). Zhen goes   outward   (understand the world, then act on it).\n\n   Open questions\n\n- Does Qi have a  material  form, or is it only relational?   UNRESOLVED  \n- Can non-cultivators be harmed by Qi manipulation?   UNRESOLVED   — needed for combat stakes\n- What does the Scripture's  \"the self must become the source of the imposed order\"  actually require?   UNRESOLVED   — this is Ajax's volume 1 arc question\n"},{"t":"The Two Paths","k":"Against the Heavens · Power Systems","u":"atth/lore/atth--the-two-paths.html","s":"  The Two Paths\n\nThe philosophical spine of the novel.   This note supersedes any earlier framing of the two inheritances as \"inward vs outward.\"   That was wrong.\n\n   The distinction, in one line each\n\n    Cheonma:   the I elevates itself and goes   beyond   the world — and it gets there by   mimicking the heavens closely enough to take hold, corrupting what it has taken, and keeping it.  \n \n    Merlin:   the I becomes the   passage   through which the world has no choice but to move — and it gets there by   doing what must be done  , until the world's obstructed necessities are completed and he may name a price.\n\nOne   takes by force  . One   pays in full  .\n\nIt is not inward versus outward. It is   transcendence versus immanence  , and each has a matching method:   theft versus exchange.  \n\n   Why each of them chose his path — from his body\n\nThis is the part that makes the whole system feel inevitable rather than assigned.\n\n  Ajax cannot walk.   His lower body is dead. He has spent three years being moved through rooms by other people, watching the world from a chair at the edge of it. Every door he has ever passed through, someone else opened.\n\nSo his path is   elevation.   If he cannot move through the world, he will go  above  it. Transcendence is not a philosophy he selected — it is the compensation a proud, immobile boy would reach for.  \"Whatever the price, he would pay it if it meant he could stand again.\"  And standing, for him, is only the first step of the climb.\n\n  Zhen has a borrowed body.   A soul from elsewhere, wearing a dead commoner's life, holding memories of a story he was not born into. He is  already  a passage — something moving through a vessel that was not made for it. His path is not a change of direction; it is an   intensification of what he already is.  \n\nNeither was given a system that suited a generic protagonist. Each was given the system his own condition had already prepared him for.\n\n   Four positions on the self\n\nThe three the traditions recognise, plus the one Ajax starts from.\n\n  Position   What the self does   Who holds it  \n --- --- --- \n    Prison     The self is trapped by the body; the world moves on without it   Ajax, ch1–13  \n    River     No self at all — carries whatever falls in, undifferentiated   What Cheonma  despises   \n    Gate     The self stands at the threshold and   selects   what passes   Cheonma's stated ideal  \n    Passage     The self   becomes   the opening — nothing to select, everything flows   Merlin  \n\n  Cheonma's own metaphor, and his own betrayal of it.  \n\nHe wrote:  \"A man with no self is a river. Whatever falls into him, he carries. Do not be a river. Be a gate.\" \n\nA gate has a gatekeeper. The I stands there and decides. That is Cheonma's actual doctrine — the self positioned at the threshold, filtering.\n\nBut   he did not become a gate. He climbed.   His practice was elevation, not passage. The man who wrote \"be a gate\" spent his life going  over  the wall instead of standing in it.\n\n  This is your heretic's tragedy, and it is already on the page:   a diary full of crossed-out lines, a man arguing with himself in ink, who articulated the truth in three words and then lived the opposite for forty years. Whether he knew is a choice you should make deliberately.\n\n   The mechanics each path implies\n\n    Cheonma — force: mimic, corrupt, steal\n\nThe I does not ask and does not trade. It   reproduces the pattern of heavenly Qi closely enough to take hold of it, corrupts it with its own will, and keeps it.  \n\nThree acts, in order:\n\n1.   Mimic.   Cheonma cannot create Qi — no one can. So he studies the descending flow until he can reproduce its structure inside himself. This is why his text is full of  observations  rather than techniques: you cannot mimic what you have not watched closely enough.\n2.   Corrupt.   Once the copy holds, he stamps his own will on it, and it stops being heavenly Qi.  \"It did not become pure. It became   mine  .\" \n3.   Steal.   The corrupted Qi cannot complete its return. He has not borrowed it. He has taken it.\n\n-   Progress is distance.   Each advance removes the practitioner further from the world's reach.\n-   What he gains is stolen, so it resists him.   Stolen Qi never agreed to be his. It cooperates only as far as his will holds it.\n-   Success looks like:   nothing can touch you, and nothing belongs to you.\n\n  Why this fits Ajax specifically:   his defining trait is that he notices patterns and reproduces them. He saw the wiring was untidy and fixed it. He saw the arrangement was too wide. He saw the painted spot for what it was.   Mimicry is the one thing Ajax has been doing his whole life.   His observational gift is not a flavour quirk — it is literally how his path works.\n\n    Merlin — exchange: he gives his   choices  \n\n  Zhen does not cast spells. There is no spell.  \n\nThe mechanic is an accounting of   necessity  :\n\n1.   Some events must happen.   Not everything — but some things are owed to the shape of the world. He sees this as a   knot  : many threads of fate converging on one point. See   Threads of Fate  .\n2.   Power obstructs them.   Powerful people prevent what must happen, because it suits them to. A knot under strain is the visual signature.\n3.   When a must-happen is thwarted, a debt exists.   The world is now owed a completion.\n4.   Zhen can read the debt.   He observes, finds what was obstructed, and does the work to finish it.\n5.   Then he may ask for a reward   — anything   fitting to the work  : a power, a relic, an object, an opportunity. Not a gift;  payment . The band is set by the debt, not by his desire. See   Threads of Fate  .\n\n  The price he pays is his choices.  \n\nHe does not decide what to do. He can only do  what must be done . His freedom of action is the currency, and it is spent before he ever names a reward.\n\n-   Progress is scope.   The more debt he can read, the larger the works he may undertake, the larger the reward he may name. He never hits harder — he sees further.\n-   The technique is compliance.   No gesture, no word, no formula. Reading truly and doing the work  is  the technique.\n-   Merlin advises him.   The tome guides him on how to use what he receives — unlike Cheonma, who is silent and left blanks.\n-   Success looks like:   the world cannot complete itself without him — and he can no longer act on his own behalf.\n\n  Why this fits Zhen specifically:   he is a transmigrator. He already surrendered one life's choices to arrive here. His path is the discipline of surrendering them again, deliberately and repeatedly, for payment.\n\n    The consequence that makes both work\n\n    Merlin's works complete themselves because he consented. Ajax's Qi resists because it was taken.  \n\nConsent is the whole difference. A thing given works with you. A thing taken works only while you hold it — and it complains.\n\n    Why his enemies are the powerful\n\nBecause   power is what obstructs necessity.   Every obstruction is somebody's decision. So the moment Zhen starts repairing what was thwarted, he starts undoing the works of people who were relying on the obstruction.\n\nHe is not fighting evil. He is   reversing the effects of power   — and the powerful notice, because he is professionally undoing them.\n\n  This gives you an antagonist class that requires no villain:   landowners, officials, institutions, and families who have each quietly stopped something that was owed, and who will not accept a boy from the docks putting it back.\n\n    The trap in his foreknowledge\n\nZhen knows the plot. That means he can identify debts before anyone else — huge advantage.\n\nBut   once he sees a debt, he owes it.   His knowledge does not merely let him act; it makes him liable. The more he remembers, the more he is bound to complete. His foreknowledge is not a power-up. It is a standing debt he cannot discharge.\n\nAnd the worst question the system can ask him is already available:  he knows a plague must come, and who it must take.  If that is a debt, what does completing it mean?\n\n   The failure mode of each — and they are mirror images\n\n  Ajax's failure: noise from stolen Qi.  \nHe took the Qi, so it resists him. It works only while his will holds it, and it carries every impurity his will put into it — redundant thoughts, half-formed intentions, fear, wants he has not admitted. It arrives as   whispers  : his own unfinished thoughts, echoing out of what he stole. He did not leave the world behind. He carried it inside him in the ugliest possible form, inside something that never agreed to be his.\n\n  Zhen's failure: he can only do what must be done.  \nHis currency is his choices. Every working spends freedom he cannot get back. A man who can only act when compelled, and who is paid for compliance, will eventually find that he cannot remember the last thing he did because he  wanted  to. He becomes the most useful man alive and the least free — and the terrible part is that he chose every step of it.\n\n  Ajax will never be beyond the world, because he carries it. Zhen will never be in the world, because he is only its route.   Two men failing in exactly opposite directions, each for the reason the other would call a virtue.\n\n   The hidden symmetry nobody in the book admits\n\nBoth paths are   domination dressed as its opposite.  \n\n- Cheonma calls   theft   freedom.  I will not go down  is not liberation — it is taking what was not offered and calling the taking a victory.\n- Merlin calls   compliance   service.  Some things must happen and I will make them happen  is not humility. It is the most total claim available: not to fight the world, but to make yourself the one thing it cannot complete itself without.\n\nNeither protagonist sees this about his own path.   Both will see it about the other's  , and both will be right, and neither will be able to use the insight on himself. That is the novel's central irony and it requires no villain.\n\n   Why they need each other — and what the Program is for\n\nFollow the two economies.\n\n-   Ajax takes.   But nothing can be taken from a system whose accounts you cannot read. He is stealing from a ledger he does not understand, which is why he keeps getting caught by his own noise.   Zhen is the one man who can read what is owed.  \n-   Zhen can only do what is necessary.   He has no freedom of action, so he can never act  first  — he can only complete what someone else started. And one of the things that has been started, and left unfinished, is Ajax.\n\nSo they are structurally dependent, and neither can finish alone.   A dual nurture system, with two students, one curriculum, and no explanation.  \n\n  This is the answer to \"what is the Dual Nurture Program for.\"   It is not a contest and not a partnership. It is a   joint procedure   — two paths that only close when brought together, run on two people who were never told what they were being fitted to.\n\nSay it plainly at the right moment and the whole volume retroactively reorganises around it.\n\n   What this changes in the existing bible\n\n-     Qi and the Cycle     — the ladder still holds, but \"Refinement\" for Ajax means  resolving the self , and for Zhen it will mean  reading debts of necessity with greater scope . Same word, opposite labour.\n-     The Demonic Path     — the whisper mechanic is now explained by the elevation thesis: the noise is the world he failed to leave.\n-     Merlin     — his method is exchange, not acquisition. Zhen's power curve should look like  scope , not  strength : each advance lets him see larger debts, never hit harder.\n-     The Two Heavens     — the ledger reading still works, but subordinate to this. Cheonma is not merely embezzling; he is  leaving the jurisdiction .\n\n   Open questions\n\n-   Did Cheonma know he had betrayed his own \"be a gate\" insight?   This decides whether he is a tragic figure or a hypocrite. My read: he knew, and the crossed-out lines are where he knew.\n-   Can he refuse a debt he has already seen?   If yes, he has an out and the trap is soft. If no, he is bound the moment he perceives — which is far crueller and better.   Recommendation: no.   The sight is the obligation.\n-   How does Merlin advise him — and is Merlin alive?   A voice from the tome, writing that appears, a presence in the dream-space? This is a major structural decision and must not be settled accidentally in prose.\n-   What does his first knot look like?   A reader expects a flash. Consider giving them something small and structural instead: threads converging somewhere ordinary, straining, and a reward that feels slightly wrong.\n-   Do the threads belong to the same cosmology as the ledger?   Reading fate as thread and reading it as register are two metaphors for one thing, or two different systems.   Recommendation: one thing.   Threads are how a person sees the ledger — the ledger is how an institution records it. Zhen sees raw, the River civilisation sees filed.\n-   Does either path have a third stage the other cannot reach?   If the geometry is right, the final stage may require both, which is what the Program is testing for.\n-   Who built the Program?   Someone who understood this geometry. That is a much better mystery than \"who gave Ajax a book.\"\n"},{"t":"Threads of Fate","k":"Against the Heavens · Power Systems","u":"atth/lore/atth--threads-of-fate.html","s":"  Threads of Fate\n\nHow Zhen sees necessity. The perception half of   The Two Paths   in practice.\n\n   What he sees\n\nWhen perception is trained far enough, the world stops looking like objects and starts looking like   threads   — lines of consequence running through people, places and events, crossing and binding.\n\nHe does not see the future. He sees   the grain of what is already owed.  \n\n    Reading is structural, not prophetic\n\n  What he sees   What it means  \n --- --- \n  A single thread, thin   An ordinary consequence, unremarkable  \n  Threads running parallel   Coincidence — nothing owed  \n    A knot   — many threads converging on one point   A   canon event.   Something that must happen  \n  A knot pulled tight, threads straining   The event is being   obstructed.   A debt exists  \n    A gap — threads stopping, nothing beyond       No thread.   He cannot see what is owed  \n\n  He counts threads, not visions.   This is important for his voice: he is an engineer, and he reads fate the way he reads a load-bearing diagram. Load paths, stress, convergence. It should feel like structural analysis, not mysticism.\n\n   The three things this gives you\n\n    1. He can predict, but never know\n\nA knot tells him  that  something must happen,   and where, and roughly when.   It does not tell him:\n\n-   why   it must happen\n- what it is for\n- who benefits\n- what happens if it is done badly\n\nSo he acts on incomplete information by design. Every working is a guess made with better information than anyone else has, which is exactly the kind of confidence that ruins people.\n\n    2. Sometimes there are no threads at all\n\nSome events have   no visible thread.   By definition he cannot read them, cannot price them, cannot touch them.\n\n  This is the guardrail that keeps him from being omniscient  , and it must be used often — it is what keeps tension in every scene. When there is no thread, Zhen is just a poor boy with a broken regulator and no magic.\n\n    3. The gaps have a cause\n\n  A thread that stops is not the same as a thread that never existed.  \n\nA stopped thread means something  removed itself  from the weave — took something out of the accounts. By the setting's own logic, that is what   Cheonma  's path does: the I elevates beyond the world and takes what it took out of the ledger with it.\n\n  So Ajax is a hole in the weave.  \n\nZhen will find places where threads simply end, and he will not know why. He will assume his sight is failing. It is not — something is standing where a thread should be, and it is not participating.\n\n  This is the mechanical answer to the decaying foreknowledge, and it should be the reader's first real clue that another path exists.   Do not explain it. Let Zhen keep finding gaps and getting quietly frightened.\n\n   What a debt looks like\n\nA debt is   threads pulled tight against an obstruction.  \n\n1. Threads converge on a point — something must happen there.\n2. They do not resolve. They strain.\n3. Somewhere on the other side of the knot sits   a decision   — a person, an institution, a family — that has stopped it.\n4. Zhen can see the shape of the completion, and the work required.\n\nHe does not need to be told what \"must happen\" means. He can see where the weave is under load.\n\n   Why the powerful are always his enemies\n\nEvery obstruction is somebody's choice. The knot is straining because   a person with authority decided it should not resolve.  \n\nSo Zhen's work is never abstract: it is undoing the effect of a specific decision by a specific person who was relying on it. See   The Two Paths  .\n\n  The antagonists require no villain.   They are landlords, guilds, officials, and families who each quietly stopped something owed and profited by the stopping. Zhen is the man who puts it back, and he is very easy to notice.\n\n   The price: a fitting reward\n\nHe may ask for   anything that fits the work   — a power, a relic, an object, a position, an opportunity.\n\n  The bounty is set by the debt, not by his desire.  \n\n- A small debt can pay a small thing. A vast debt can pay a vast thing.\n- He chooses  within  a band the world sets, and he does not get to widen it. If he asks above the band, he gets nothing, or worse — the request fails and the choice is still spent.\n- He often receives something   precisely fitted and deeply unwelcome  , because the debt's scale does not care what he wanted. This is a drama engine: the world pays him exactly what he is owed, which is frequently not what he asked for.\n\n  Consequence for pacing:   his power growth is gated by the   size of the debts he can read and complete  , not by training montages. He gets stronger by taking on larger obligations — which means his advancement is always a  commitment , never a reward for effort.\n\n   Merlin's hints\n\nThe tome does not just hand him things.   Merlin advises him on how to handle what he receives.  \n\nThis is the sharpest contrast with Cheonma and should be used constantly:\n\n-   Merlin is present.   He responds, guides, warns. He is a teacher.\n-   Cheonma is absent.   He wrote to himself and left blanks. Ajax is alone with a dead man's notebook.\n\nZhen gets instructions and resents them. Ajax gets silence and has to build everything himself. One is being taught; the other is being tested.\n\n  Open question (needs the author):    how  does Merlin advise — a voice from the tome, writing that appears, a presence in the dream-space? And   is Merlin alive?   This is a major structural decision and should not be settled accidentally in prose.\n\n   What Zhen still cannot do\n\n- Move fate. He can only complete what is already owed.\n- Act without a debt.   No debt, no working.  \n- See the absence of thread coming.\n- Read a knot without standing near it.\n- Choose what to want.\n\nThat is the whole of his power, and it is enough to make him dangerous to exactly the people who deserve it.\n"},{"t":"Map Notes","k":"Against the Heavens · Worldbuilding","u":"atth/lore/atth--map-notes.html","s":"  The Continent — map notes\n\n! continent map (continent-map-terrain.png)\n\nWorking terrain map.   Not canon   — the geography is a placeholder until the political layer is settled.\n\n   How it was made\n\nProcedural, from a seed. One command regenerates it identically.\n\n  Step   Script   What it does  \n --- --- --- \n  1    gen height2.py    OpenSimplex fBm heightfield + domain warping + hard border mask  \n  2    raster map2.py    Biome colouring, hillshade, river routing, lakes  \n  3    finish map2.py    Brightness, vignette, compass, legend, scale bar  \n\nScripts live in the Hermes scratch dir.   Seed 9127.   Change the seed for a different continent; change  SHAPE  to make land more or less island-like.\n\nRequirements installed in  ~/AppData/Local/hermes/docenv :  opensimplex ,  shapely ,  numpy ,  pillow .\n\n   Known flaws of this render\n\nBe honest about these — they are all fixable, and none of them matter until the geography is final:\n\n1.   Rivers are too straight and parallel in the east.   Steepest-descent routing on a smooth heightfield produces canals, not meanders. Fix: add erosion, or route along a fine noise-perturbed gradient with a penalty for direction changes.\n2.   High ground is a diffuse wash  , not distinct ridges. The ridge transform is present but the spine is too broad. Fix: narrow the Gaussian band and raise  ridged  amplitude.\n3.   Coastline stroke is thick and uniform   — a bright halo rather than a varied shore. Fix: modulate width by a noise field so headlands are crisper than bays.\n4.   No biome variety   beyond elevation and one moisture field. No forest density, no marsh, no sand seas.\n5.   Unlabelled.   The bay in the north-west is called the  Cael's mouth  in   The Continent   but is not marked here.\n\n   What must be decided before the map is redrawn properly\n\nFrom   The Continent  :\n\n1.   Where does the Register physically live?   → decides where the political centre sits.\n2.   Do the Hanging Isles drift during the story?   → if yes, this map needs a second state.\n3.   Is there a second continent?   → the frame is currently one landmass plus islands.\n\nOnce those are answered, this becomes a finished artefact rather than a working sketch. Right now it exists so you can   see   the Republic's smallness against the Central Kingdom and the Lahir Empire — which is the one thing it already communicates correctly.\n"},{"t":"Republic of Versalis","k":"Against the Heavens · Worldbuilding","u":"atth/lore/atth--republic-of-versalis.html","s":"  Republic of Versalis\n\n  Status:   PROPOSED name. The political entity containing the Wilhelmshire estate and the Central Kingdom. See   02 Open Decisions   — the manuscript never names the republic.\n\n   Confirmed political structure (ch2)\n\n- The country has a   royal family  , and the   crown is the highest authority  \n-   Governors   are appointed to oversee major administrative territories: managing affairs, maintaining institutions, implementing royal policy\n-   Five governors in total  \n- They are   not nobles in the traditional sense.   They do not own the lands they administer and cannot treat territories as personal inheritance\n- Their authority comes from the crown; responsibilities extend only as far as the royal administration permits\n-  \"They were, in a sense, limbs of the royal government. A healthy kingdom needed functioning limbs. But they were still limbs.\"  ←   the best line in ch2 and a load-bearing idea  \n\n   How to deepen it without breaking it\n\nThe Babylonian model slots in underneath what ch2 already says, and makes the \"limbs\" line devastating:\n\n- The governors' authority is a   renewable lease  , not a grant. Renewed annually. That is why the Wilhelmshires host an enormous reception — lease renewal is social, not administrative.\n- Officially the land belongs to   a god  , held in trust. The crown is the god's   tenant-in-chief  ; the governors are sub-tenants.\n- The five governorships correspond to   five treaty cities  , each with its own patron deity, making the \"republic\" a treaty rather than a state.\n- This converts your existing republic from generic-European into something specific and strange,   without changing a single fact established in ch2.  \n\n  Status: PROPOSED.   Do not treat as canon until the author confirms.\n\n   Institutions (CONFIRMED, ch12–ch13)\n\n  Institution   What it is   First appears  \n --- --- --- \n    The Crown     Highest authority; hosts the annual reception for departing students   ch12  \n    Royal Medical Service     Coordinates epidemic response; \"overwhelmed\"   ch13  \n    Royal Maritime Archive     Holds the oldest naval records; accused of neglecting them   ch12  \n    Royal Secretary     Senior officer the governor meets during the reception   ch12  \n    Veylan Maritime Exchange     Commercial shipper; supplied the suspect seafood   ch13  \n    Central Academy / Altos Institute     Where Ajax and Elena are enrolled   ch2, ch5+  \n\n   The Central Kingdom (CONFIRMED as a place, ch7)\n\nZhen's family relocates there for work. It has \"more workshops and industrial facilities,\" higher demand for magical engineering, more competition, and higher cost of living. The academy is there.\n\n  Note the geography problem:   the Central Kingdom is both where Zhen wants to go  for opportunity  and where the academy is. That may mean Ajax's school and Zhen's destination are the same place.   UNRESOLVED   — the author should decide whether they meet there in volume 1.\n\n   Open questions\n\n- Name of the republic —   UNRESOLVED   (manuscript never states it)\n- Name of the ruling house —   UNRESOLVED  \n- The other four governors and their territories —   UNRESOLVED  \n- Where the Wilhelmshire territory sits relative to the Central Kingdom —   UNRESOLVED  \n- How far is the journey? Ch4 gives a   two-month   deadline to term start; ch6 says \"a week\" and ch9 says \"a month.\"   CONFLICT — see   02 Open Decisions    \n"},{"t":"The Continent","k":"Against the Heavens · Worldbuilding","u":"atth/lore/atth--the-continent.html","s":"  The Continent\n\n  Status: PROPOSED.   Nothing here is canon until it appears in a chapter.\n\n    Scale correction (author):   the Republic is   one small country   with five governors. Altos is   not in the Republic   — it sits in the   Central Kingdom  . The world is large and includes   floating islands  . This note has been rebuilt to that scale.\n\n   Design principle\n\n    The map is the record.   Geography is not scenery — it is the physical form of the ledger.\n\nEvery feature should answer a question about   who controls the flow of what is owed.   Rivers are how rent moves. Mountains are where the register does not reach. Coasts are where accounts can be questioned. The map therefore  is  the political system, drawn to scale.\n\nSee   The Two Heavens   and   Threads of Fate  .\n\n   The powers, by size\n\n  Power   Size   Notes  \n --- --- --- \n    The Verge     the continent   informal name for the whole landmass  \n    The Central Kingdom     large, central   where   Altos Institute   sits. The academy is here, not in the Republic  \n    The Lahir Empire     large, southern   home of   Mora's Council of Magic    \n    The Republic       small   — one country, five governors   Ajax's home. Provincial, proud, and irrelevant to anyone outside it  \n    The Hanging Isles     overhead   floating islands; the source of heavenly Qi; not a nation  \n    The Unassessed     far north-east   outside the register entirely  \n\n  The Republic's smallness is a story engine, not a detail.   Ajax is a governor's son — a large man in a small country. The moment he leaves, he is nobody. That is a better humiliation than being a prince in a great kingdom, and it makes the academy genuinely frightening.\n\n   The belts\n\n    I. The north-west — the Republic\nA small river valley on the north-west coast. Five governorships, one crown, an annual reception, and a settled conviction that it matters.\n\n- Its river —   the Cael   — descends from the Ilvan foothills and empties into the northern sea.\n- The Republic is  not  the continent's breadbasket or arbiter. It exports a little, imports a great deal, and is watched by nobody.\n-   This is why the first calamity is so devastating there: nowhere important sends help quickly.  \n- Divination is licensed here. Reading fate without a licence is treason.\n-   This is Ajax's home.   He begins at the top of a very small ledger and is written out of it.\n\n    II. The centre — the Central Kingdom\nThe hinge of the world. Where roads meet, where foundries and maritime exchanges are, where the academy is.\n\n- Not one culture — a   trading seam   where river law and sea custom both half-apply.\n- Crowded, unregistered, impatient. People come here to be lost or made.\n-   Altos Institute is here.   So is   Veylan  , and the shipping that carried the parasite.\n\n    III. The south — the Lahir Empire\nOld, warm, confident. Magic is a profession here, not a heresy.   Mora's Council of Magic   is its crown jewel, and the place Zhen is trying to reach.\n\n    IV. The east — the Ilvan Range and after\nA mountain spine halving the continent. Beyond it, the   Unassessed  : no clerks, no diviners, no assessment.\n\n-   This is where a dead man walks unrecorded.  \n- The Republic's fifth governorship technically sits in the western foothills — the poorest seat, let to whoever will take it.\n\n    V. Above — the Hanging Isles\n  Floating islands.   Visible from the ground, unreachable by road, and the place the setting says   heavenly Qi enters the world.  \n\nThis is the most important addition to the setting, because it makes the metaphysics  physical :\n\n- The cycle in   Reverse Heaven Divine Scripture   — Qi descends, returns — now has an   address.   It comes down at the Isles.\n- Cheonma's theft is no longer abstract: there are places where you can   stand under the leak.  \n- The Register is kept  about  the Isles, even though the Isles themselves cannot be visited.\n-   The Isles drift.   Slowly — but they drift. Where Qi enters the world changes over centuries, and with it which lands are blessed and which are abandoned. That is an entire history of wars, free of charge.\n\n   Why six calamities need a continent this size\n\nEach needs somewhere to happen, and the scale lets the disasters be   geographically distinct   rather than all landing on one city.\n\n      Calamity   Proposed seat   Why  \n --- --- --- --- \n  I   The Parasite   Central Kingdom   A hive mind's ideal foothold: crowds, ports, no single authority  \n  II   UNRESOLVED   the Republic   Small, exposed, the only place Ajax still loves  \n  III   UNRESOLVED   Lahir Empire   —  \n  IV   UNRESOLVED   the Ilvan Range   —  \n  V   UNRESOLVED   the Unassessed   —  \n  VI   UNRESOLVED   the Hanging Isles   The last one should come from the source  \n\n  Do not fix these yet.   But note the shape: the six escalate from  biological  to  metaphysical , and geographically they spiral outward from Ajax's home to the origin of Qi itself. That gives you a series arc a reader can feel without a line of exposition.\n\n   Distances\n\n  Proposed:   the Republic to the Central Kingdom is   six weeks by carriage  , three by fast post. That makes \"two months to term\" (ch4) generous, \"a month before departure\" (ch5, ch9) consistent, and the journey long enough that a carriage can vanish in the middle of it and no one hear for weeks.\n\nSee   02 Open Decisions   for the outstanding timeline conflict.\n\n   Names — PROPOSED, needs author approval\n\n  Thing   Proposed   Note  \n --- --- --- \n  The continent     The Verge     implies an edge to something larger  \n  Ajax's country     The Republic     manuscript never names it; \"Versalis\" is only a folder name  \n  The river     The Cael     short, hard, repeatable  \n  The mountains     The Ilvan Range     halves the continent  \n  The floating islands     The Hanging Isles     —  \n  The frontier     The Unassessed     bureaucratic, not romantic — which is the point  \n\n   What to decide before drawing again\n\n1.   Where does the Register physically live?   A place, an institution, or something stranger. This decides the political centre of gravity.\n2.   Do the Isles move on the timescale of the story?   If yes, one calamity is a  landfall  and you have a clock.\n3.   Is there a second continent?   A world with floating islands probably has more than one shore.\n\n   The map\n\n  continent-map.svg   — working map, not canon. The Republic is drawn deliberately, embarrassingly small.\n"},{"t":"0. Prologue","k":"Killer Petals · chapter","u":"killerpetals/c0.html","s":"Snap — branches, leaves, something behind me shearing loose. I did not look. Looking cost time, and time was the only thing I had left. \"There is no use running, Your Highness.\" An arrow went past my right ear close enough that I felt the air of it and nothing else. I gasped. My heart was a drum in a closed room, and the whole room was my skull. Sweat had soaked through my collar and down my spine. My legs were going. Not yet — not while he could still see me. \"No.\" I pushed. If I stopped here I was a body on a forest floor and nothing more. Running this long starts showing. First the breath goes ragged, then the hearing, then the thoughts. The wind shoved past me, and I went through the brush and the vines like something being chased, because I was. And then the trees simply stopped. The sea. A cliff, and past it the whole grey turning of the water, and the sun going down behind it in that fat, unhurried way that says the day is finished and the world has decided about you already. I turned. Two of them. Black from throat to boot, breathing hard, and pleased about it. I put my left hand in my pocket and rubbed the bronze coin with my thumb, and while my face did nothing at all my eyes went over the cliff edge, the vine, the gap, the water. Four things. One of them would work. \"There is no use running, Your Highness,\" Brant said again, and grinned, because he had said it twice now and the second time is always better for a man like that. \"You say that like I haven't just cost you more than half your squad.\" The grin faltered. His brows came down. Good. Men who are ashamed make mistakes faster than men who are confident. Two facts, then. The good one: I had found a way off this cliff. The bad one: he had found it too, half a heartbeat after me, and he was bigger and he had a sword. I looked at the vine hanging off the tree to my right a moment too long. Deliberately. Let him look at it with me. He did. Then he looked back at me, and I watched him understand, and I watched the understanding arrive too late to be useful. \"Stop him!\" I was already running. The one with the dagger came at me from the side, fast, better than I had counted on, and for a moment we were going to arrive at the cliff at the same time and only one of us had thought about it beforehand. So I stopped running. I turned into him. Close — closer than a prince should ever be to a hired blade — and let him see my eyes go wet, and put everything I had ever learned about being looked at into one whispered sentence. \"I'm interested in men.\" \"Y-you — what?\" He stopped. Not his feet; his whole self, the way a man stops when the world has just said something that does not fit in it. His eyes went wide and then, because the mind will do anything rather than hold two impossible things, his eyes closed. The coin took him on the cheekbone. I had the vine in my right hand before he had finished flinching, and my left hooked through his elbow, and then there was no more cliff under either of us and the sea came up at me like a wall with opinions. Above, the swordsman reached the edge and looked down and heard a snap — the vine, giving — and then a splash, and then bubbles, and then not even bubbles. Brant stood there a while. He was a thorough man. When there was nothing left to look at he turned around and went to tell Aldric that the last of the royal line of Asherforth had drowned in the Estmark Sea with a bronze coin in his pocket and a stranger's arm around his neck."},{"t":"1. Altar of Gods","k":"Killer Petals · chapter","u":"killerpetals/c1.html","s":"\"Phew.\" My breath came back at me off the rock, loud, and I clamped my mouth shut on it. My right hand was closed around a crack in the cliff face about as wide as a bad promise, and the rest of me was hanging off it. I looked down. That was a mistake. The sea was doing something patient down there, the way it does when it has all night. I looked up instead. The sun had already stepped off its stage; the last of it was a stain on the clouds and nothing more. Which left one way. \"Hah. One step at a time.\" I found another crack by feel. Then another. The cliff was wet where the tide reached and slick above that, and my fingers had gone from pain to a kind of ringing numbness, which I knew was worse. If they see me I am dead for sure this time. Except they wouldn't see me. They thought I was fish food. That was the whole point of the coin, the arm, the water — and it was a very good point, and it would keep being a good point right up until my hands failed and the sea found out I had lied to it. I moved right, along a ledge no wider than my foot, keeping my voice down, keeping everything down. \"Ahh—\" A lump of stone went out from under my boot and I squeaked like something small being stepped on, and then I was still, cheek against the wet rock, listening to it fall. It fell for a long time. Then it stopped. One step. Another. The throbbing in my fingers got a rhythm to it, and then a voice, and the voice said things about letting go that I did not agree with. Then I saw light. Dim. Orange. Coming out of the rock — a hairline of it, gone when I blinked and back when I didn't. Anyone could have missed it. Anyone not currently bargaining with their own hands for another five minutes of life. I was so delighted I forgot the fatigue, which is a thing the body lets you do exactly once. \"Just a bit more.\" I got my boot onto something. I lowered my weight onto it one inch at a time. I reached, and found an edge, and it was the edge of a mouth in the cliff, and I grabbed it and hauled and the last of me came up over the lip and I flung myself inside and landed flat on hard bedrock like a sack of things nobody wanted. \"Ah — ah — ah.\" I lay there. The strength went out of me all at once, taking the pain with it, and the exhaustion came down over me like a blanket somebody had been warming for exactly this. Above the ledge were the marks of a hand that had done this before me — a wide crack in the rock, worn smooth at the edges, big enough to fold a person into. A fire had been made there once. Recently. The ash was still soft. Somebody lived up here. Or had. That should have frightened me. I was too tired to be frightened; I filed it, the way you file a sound in a house you're too exhausted to go and check. Despite the pain I crawled my way further in, because the sea was still making its patient noise below and I did not trust it. \"Slurp — yuck.\" I spat the seawater back out of my mouth and looked around like a man looking for one specific thing and willing to be humiliated for it. Then I saw the earthen pot, sitting at the other edge of the cave, in that patient way pots have. I was on it before I had decided to move. Lid off. Water. Not seawater — water, clear, with the cold coming off it. I got both arms around the pot and lifted it and drank like something that had crawled out of the sea, which I was. It went down like a waterfall into a bottomless pit. When I finally set it down my hands were shaking so hard the pot rang on the stone. I slumped back against the wall of the cave and looked out at the last grey line of the sea, and the night coming on, and the rain coming with it — soft at first, then not soft, drumming on the rock mouth of the cave like a hundred knuckles. Somewhere down there, in the dark, a man was telling another man that a prince had drowned. I hoped he was very convincing. I did not mean to sleep. I remember thinking that I should stay awake and listen for the sound of someone climbing. I remember thinking that the water tasted like iron. And I remember the rain, and the wet, and how warm the cave was against my back. Then nothing. *** \"HOLA!\" The man in front sang it out and the carrying pole dug into my chest, and I came awake upside down with my wrists tied to a beam and my ankles tied to the same beam and the whole forest going by at a walking pace. \"ULA ULA ULA!\" someone called back from behind us. They were not soldiers. That was the first thing I understood and the least useful. They wore hides over one shoulder and nothing over the other, and their arms were painted from wrist to elbow in a pattern I did not know — lines and dots, like a road map of somewhere I had never been. Bone through the ear. A cord of small teeth around the throat, and I made myself stop counting the teeth. \"RELEASE ME, I SAID, RELEASE—\" I kept it up until my throat hurt. Nobody looked at me. Not once, not even the ones carrying me — the pole was just a thing they were doing with their shoulders. In the end I stopped, because a prince learns early that a performance without an audience is only noise, and because I needed the strength. We walked a long time. Long enough that I stopped being able to tell the direction by the light. Then the trees opened out and the ground went down and the sound changed — a great hollowness ahead of us, all echoing — and we came out into the dome. The dome was the roof of a world that had been put underground. Circular paths spiralled the walls, one above another, stairs running between them, and everywhere tunnel mouths opened off into dark — more passages than I could count, all of them converging on the floor. It was built the way a thing is built when whoever built it expected to be underground for a long time. The air was warm and wet and smelled of smoke and tallow and something green. Under all of it was a smell I could not place and did not like. My head was ringing from hanging upside down for so long that I could not be sure how long I had been looking before I saw the middle of the room — and once I saw it, I could not look away. In the middle of the dome stood a stone, grey and ancient, and on the stone sat the rose. It was a rose the way a funeral is a gathering. Red, but wrong red — not the red of wine or of blood freshly spilled, though I had seen both — a red that seemed to come from inside and go outward, as if the thing were breathing and had decided to let me see it do it. For a moment there was no forest behind me, no pole under my arms, no rope. Just the rose, and me, and a quiet I could not hear anything through. The stone it sat on was cracked from top to bottom and green with something that had been growing in the cracks a long time before the tribe or the tunnel or the kingdom above us. Garlands hung down its sides — dried, some of them, and some not — and fruit, and skins, and small bundles of something I chose not to identify. They had been bringing things here for a long time. The stone had the look of a thing that had received. \"Ola, hula huh u\" — the words barked out of the old man in the mask, and the two carrying me came to a halt and dumped me on my knees. I twisted, got my head up. More of them were arriving from the passages, drums starting from maybe twenty places at once and settling into one rhythm, the way a crowd will find a heartbeat. They untied me and the relief in my arms was so bright it made my eyes water. Then hands on me, and a bowl at my mouth, and I was drinking something warm and thick before I could decide not to, and my throat closed against it and they kept pouring. Then they were pulling at my clothes, and I fought that — I remember fighting that — and I lost, and they dressed me in cloth that was not cloth exactly, made of small branches bound together with something flexible and alive that was still moving, tied at the shoulder and the hip. Cold air on parts of me that had never been cold in public. The drums getting faster. My hands tied behind my back now. And the stone, and the rose, coming closer with every step, and my good leg giving out on the third step and the rest of the steps made on the other one. \"Gosh — how many times do I have to tell you to stop sneaking out of the castle—\" My mother's voice, out of nowhere, exactly as Sabine had said it a hundred times — cross, and fond, and about to laugh. I had hated that tone. I would have given the whole kingdom I no longer had to be hearing it right then. \"I heard you skipped the basic swordsmanship class again.\" And my father. Looking at me the way he used to look at me — which was not angry, which was the thing I had never been able to explain to anyone, because he never once raised his voice and somehow it was always worse. \"Greetings, Your Highness.\" A girl my age, in the corridor outside a hall I could not name, with the sun behind her. I had stared at her for so long that the corridor had gone quiet around us. I remember that. I remember she had been amused and I had been eleven kinds of ruined and I had said something, and it had been the wrong thing, and she had known it was the wrong thing and been kind about it. *Lynna.* That namesake of hers was not here. The corridor was not here. What was here was a stone with a crack in it and a rose on top of it and my knees on a platform of rough stone and a ringing in my ears that had no business being a sound. My eyes blurred. The tears came and kept coming, and I did not wipe them, because my hands were tied, and because I was busy understanding a thing I had spent a great deal of effort not understanding: that I was not going to see any of them again. Not the corridor. Not the tone. Not the girl with the sun behind her. I ground my teeth and breathed out through my nose and put my face back together by force of will, and it was a very good face, and it fell apart again anyway. They pressed me down onto the platform. The old man in the mask came round to my side and began the hymn. I cannot give you the words. I have tried since, and the best I can say is that they were in an old language, and that the old language had been shaped for something else before it was made to do this. It went up and down in no pattern I could follow. It called, and something answered — not aloud. I felt it answer, in the stone under my knees, the way a staircase feels a foot before the foot arrives. The rose was listening. That is the plainest way I can put it, and the truest. The rose was listening, and as the hymn went on, the red in it began to move on the inside. They brought the sword out. Kord, who was a big man, came out with a blade longer than his arm, and the crowd made the sound a crowd makes when the interesting part is about to start. My teeth went together and rattled. I could not stop them. And underneath the fear there was something else, and I am not proud of it, and I will not pretend it was not there: I was burning. I hated myself for having run away. Yes. I hated myself for how little of that morning I would ever get to change. *Could I have done something?* *What would the palace look like right now — the hall lit, the table set, the whole machine turning without me?* And then the other hands — the ones that have always been in that room. That Sun symbol. I clenched my teeth so hard something at the back of my jaw gave. The corpses. And those fakes standing behind them, calling themselves my parents. The executioner wiped the blade down with a cloth, unhurried, the way a man wipes a tool he has used before and will use again. *I will kill them.* He took his place at my side. The old man raised his hand to the crowd, and the drums stopped so completely that I heard the river, a long way above us, in the rock. *Every one of them.* The blade came up. The light went along the edge of it and off the ceiling of the dome and out of my sight. *Even if — in the process — I lose myself.* The old man's hand came down. *If only that day I had power.* The blade came down after it."},{"t":"2. An Oath of Blood","k":"Killer Petals · chapter","u":"killerpetals/c2.html","s":"I, or maybe only my head, struck the stone floor and rolled, and came to rest at last against the foot of the old stone. The last thing I saw was my own body, headless, convulsing — and the fountain of it going up and out and over, endless, and the stone black with it, and the whole dome turning the colour of the inside of a mouth. *Ah.* *So that is what kind of red it is.* Then the light went out of my eyes, and the last thing I understood was that I had been wrong about the rose, and that being wrong about the rose was going to matter. *** *Where did it go wrong?* \"I apologise, Your Highness, but I have to be somewhere before noon.\" She was annoyed. Not the courtly kind, the true kind, and she did not bother to hide it from me, which I had always taken as a compliment and was probably not one. \"Aww. Even if you went in the evening, you'd have plenty of time to get ready.\" I had her hand. I had got hold of it somewhere between the corridor and the stair and I was not letting go of it, and she was letting me not let go of it, which was as close to a yes as I had ever got out of her. She twisted her wrist. Not hard — she never did anything hard, and that was the whole difficulty with her — and my grip came open, and she turned, and went. Her hair was silver. That is the thing I have kept. Not her face; I have lost the face. The hair going out from her shoulders with the sun on it, and the corridor, and me standing in it with my hand still open, and the distance arriving between us faster than she was walking. I made a move to follow. She turned her head and looked back down her shoulder at me and did not say anything at all. Her eyes were the colour of the ice on the palace reservoir in the third month, and they put me exactly where I was standing, and I did not follow her. *If I had told her that day how much I liked her.* *Would it have been any different?* *** \"How do I look?\" Abel had the expression of somebody who had swallowed something sour and was too well trained to mention it. \"You look absolutely stunning, Your Highness,\" he said, as a matter of fact. I smiled at myself in the mirror and turned a shoulder and looked at that, and I was, I want to be fair to the boy I was, worth looking at. \"Fuuu. Today I'm going to ask her.\" In my head I got down on one knee. In my head I kissed her hand. In my head she laughed the way she laughed when she was not being careful, and said something about how long I had taken about it. \"Your Highness.\" \"Hmm?\" \"This humble servant is known by the name of Abel.\" It barely half reached me. I was fixing my collar. I was wondering whether she would be in blue. I waved him out. He bowed — properly, the full bow, the one the old ones still did — and left, and shut the door quietly behind him, which is a harder thing to do than it looks and which he had been doing for my family for forty-one years. *If I had treated him any better that day. If I had remembered his name.* *Would it have been any different?* I do not think so. But I think about it. That is the whole of it — I do not think it would have changed anything, and I think about it. *** The hall was full and lit and the noise of it came at you in layers — the low one of a hundred conversations, the bright one of glass, and under both of them somebody's musicians being paid not to be interesting. I stood at the side with a glass I was not drinking and shook it, because it gave my hand something to do. My fingers were shaking anyway. I had spent an hour arranging for her to be here. Three days arranging for this to be the night. And now the double doors were shut and the room was too warm and the door was taking an unreasonable amount of time about it. My thumb found the silver coin in my pocket and turned it over, once, twice — the coin Sabine had given me at the first banquet she let me attend, and which she had not said anything about when she gave it, and which I had carried every day since, because it was hers and because I was sentimental and because it was, in the end, the only thing she ever actually handed me. \"Lynna Asherforth and Lord Casimir Vane,\" the herald said — Perrin, who had announced every name in that hall for nineteen years and had never once enjoyed it. I knew what that meant. Everyone in that hall knew what that meant. When the herald gives two names together — a man's and a woman's — he is not announcing two guests. He is announcing a couple. *Cut through the door and the dark and the noise of the hall — see it happen and do not be able to stop it — a rose growing where a hand should be —* I blinked. My heart went down like a stone in a well, and I did not hear it land, and the glass in my hand was very cold and very steady and I was not. \"Ow.\" I took my thumb out of my pocket and it was red, and the coin had a crescent of me on its edge that I could see, and I laughed — I actually laughed, standing alone in a hall with a glass of wine and my whole future walking in through the double doors — because the thing I had been made to do was cut myself and not notice. The doors opened. Lynna was in blue. She was in blue and she was walking beside a young man who matched her stride without seeming to try for it, and her hand was hooked in the crook of his elbow, and she was smiling at him. *It was just blue, I thought. Everyone said blue. I have spent three days imagining what colour and everyone says blue and I am looking at it and there is nothing to it —* \"Lynna,\" I said. She was passing me. I had not intended to say it. She did not turn her head. She was smiling at Casimir Vane, and the crowd was closing around them, and I could not for the life of me have told you whether she had not heard me or had decided not to. Maybe that was why she had to leave at noon. *** The guests had begun collecting their cloaks. I had spent the last hour of the party deciding to leave and then having another glass, and that was the state of mind I was in when I started across the floor. I was going to ask her something. I had not settled what. I had the impression that if I did not ask her something, tonight, then the door would close and the thing would be decided and I would be a man who had been in the room when it happened. \"Lynna.\" Casimir Vane got to her first. My feet stopped. The noise went out of the hall. Not all of it — but the layer that had been around her, the chattering layer, it thinned, and the people near her went still in the way people do when they have understood something before they have been told it. He went down on one knee. He lifted her hand, and with his other hand took out a ring. \"Oh, my—\" \"How bold.\" The whispers spread out from them in rings, the way a struck note spreads. \"Will you accept this ring as the sign of our promise?\" The hall came apart into cheering. Lynna blushed. Her ears went red — her ears, of all things — and the blush moved down along her jaw while she lifted her head and looked around at the crowd with an expression I could not read. \"Y-you. Here,\" she said, and glared at him. Her glare at him was not her glare at me. That is the thing that is still somewhere behind my ribs, even now, even after everything: she can look at him like that and be happy about it, and she never once looked at me like that. She tilted her head this way and that, trying the crowd on for size, and made a small production of being uncertain, and did not step backwards. I could have stopped it. I want to say that clearly, because it took me years to be honest about it: I could have stopped it. A word would have done it. Half a word, in the right voice, into the right ear. I had it — I was a prince, and I was her friend, and I had the right, if anyone did. My legs did not move. Not out of fear — that came later, that is not what happened in the room. My legs did not move because I was standing in a ballroom watching the girl I loved be happy, and some small unbearable honest part of me had noticed the second word and could not look away from it. She was happy. She was happy with him. She had never been unhappy in any way I could fix. When she did not refuse, he smiled at her — he had a good smile, I hate that he had a good smile — and her ears went red again, and he slid the ring onto her finger. *If I had had a bit of courage that day.* *Would it have been any different?* *** *Where did I go wrong?* *What should I have done instead?* *** A pot, an earthen pot. Anew which lay a moment ago, then broken in pieces on ground Another appeared anew beside it- And yet when I saw another, then another and so on came in front of me, each differing in their demeanour [Author note: Demeanour to give them a human quality]……. And yet when I held my gaze, a rose appeared out of nowhere beneath the broken pot, the rose grew and grew in no time taking the pieces of broken pot and covering itself as to recreate what hath been destroyed. And yet when he made me wonder, went forth its roots devouring the vases which were anew withal he took a piece of each vase adding another to the vase he wore akin to a cloak. Cared nought the rose for he devoured when it smote the vases different. Weeping sung whence it went, leaving only annihilation in it’s wake. *** I opened my eyes. I did not believe it. That is the part I want on the record, because everyone since has asked me what it felt like and I have never once said it plainly: I did not believe it, and I was relieved, and the relief was the first thing and the disbelief came after, and I have had a long time now to think about what it means that I got them in that order. My fingers moved. My legs moved, and my chest, and my neck, which I put my hand to, and which was still attached. Not in the wrong place. Attached. With a pulse going through it, fast and uneven, entirely mine. I lay still and let it beat and did not open my eyes, and while I was not looking I found the thing I had been avoiding. My fingers went up the side of it, past the jaw, to where the blade had come down. There was no seam. Not a scar, not a ridge, not the raised line a wound leaves when it heals badly. The skin was whole and unmarked and it was not even the skin of a boy who had been dead an hour — it was the skin of someone who had never in his life been cut at all. That was worse than the sword. I could argue with a sword. I sat up. *A dream, I thought, and I believed it, and it was the stupidest and most human thing I have done in my life. The water was bad. That is all. I have slept in a bad place and had a bad dream and now I am awake and it is still night and nothing has happened.* And having decided it, I committed. The way a man commits to a leg he has just been told is broken — all his weight, at once, so that nobody in the room can see him hesitate. It was water. It was the altar and the drums and the drinking and the fear, and the mind does odd work when it has been frightened past its limit, and I had known men who woke from a bad night convinced of a whole second life. This was that. This was the cave still, in some way I had not untangled yet, and the reef outside, and the morning coming. I was so pleased with this. I want that on the record too. I sat up in a room that smelled of tallow and old smoke and felt the relief go through me warm as drink, and thought: nobody cut my head off. Nobody at all. Just a bad dream and a sore neck and a great deal of luck. And I had been dead since the sun was up, and I had not once, in all of that, wondered how I had got off the altar. That is the part I am ashamed of. Not the fear. The fact that relief came first and thinking came after, and thinking, when it finally arrived, arrived wearing the wrong face. And then I looked at my clothes. Branches, bound together with thread. Tied at the shoulder and the hip. The rope had been cut off my wrists and the marks of it were still there, going around, going purple. The dream had dressed me. I looked around. The dome was there — the circular paths, the stairs, the tunnel mouths. The altar was there. The sword was there, lying on the rough stone platform where someone had put it down and not come back for it. And nothing else was. No tribe. No drums. No old man in the mask. No body — no, that is wrong, there was a body, and I looked at it, and I am not going to describe it here, and afterwards I sat down and was for a while very far away. There was no blood on the stone, either. That was the part my mind would not hold. The stone was dry. The crack in it was dry. Whatever had happened there had been finished for longer than a night. Somebody had been here and tidied. I turned around. The altar. The sword. The empty room. The Rose was gone. And I stood there in clothes made of branches, in a room where I had been killed, with my hand on a head that should not have been on my shoulders, and understood — slowly, the way you understand that the noise outside is not weather but the house coming down — that I had been given something back, and that I did not know who had given it to me, or what they had wanted, or what I had agreed to while I was dead. Something had got me up off that floor. Something had dressed me. I could feel it still, under everything, the way you can feel a word on the tip of your tongue — the warm, patient wrongness of being owed. You are welcome, something said. Not in words. Not in any way I could have repeated to a living person. Something said it anyway, and I felt it in the stone through the soles of my feet, and I understood that a debt had been opened in this room that night, and that I was standing in it, and that it was mine. I put the sword under my arm — because I was not going to be defenceless twice in one week, and because it was there and I was not thinking about its owner — and I walked out of that dome, and it took me two days to find the coast."},{"t":"3. A Fish Too Big?","k":"Killer Petals · chapter","u":"killerpetals/c3.html","s":"I was in the sea, and every breath I did not take was a decision I was taking, and the surface was a bright thing very far above me that I appeared to be arguing with instead of swimming toward. *The sea knows I lied to it.* That was the thought, when thought came. Not the rope. Not the fishermen. Just: there was a night on this water when I told it a prince had drowned, and the water has a long memory and no sense of humour, and it had been waiting for me to fall back into it. My chest gave out first. Then my mouth did on its own, and the sea came in, and the world went quiet and specific, and I was done. *** \"Are them fishes to your likin'?\" A deep, pleased voice, from somewhere behind the man who was looking at me. The man looking at me did not answer straight away. He had a cloth wound round his head against the sun, and the sort of face that had been in this business a long time and had stopped finding anything about it odd. \"Uh — yea. But one of 'em is too big.\" \"Ooh. How big are we talkin'?\" \"Very big.\" \"Is it this big?\" \"No. Even bigger.\" \"Bigger.\" Something put its weight on the deck planking. Planking creaked in a way I could feel through my cheek. I could not lift my head far enough to see who was coming — only boots, and the shadow of a very great deal of man. The boots stopped at my face. \"T-THIS!\" He was enormous. His shirt was losing a war it had been losing for years. He looked down at me and his eyes went round with something that was not yet a thought. \"WE ARE GONNA FEAST TODAY, BOYS!\" The deck answered with a noise that would have moved me to terror if I had had the energy to be terrified. I made an attempt at a voice and it came out as something the sea had left behind. \"Could someone get me out of this.\" Silence. Then, carefully: \"...Oh? The fish is talkin'?\" \"I am not a fish.\" \"He's a fish,\" said the enormous man, delightedly, to nobody in particular, \"he's a talking fish.\" *** \"So you fell in the sea while huntin', you say.\" The enormous man — Capitan Ostrand — had put me in a heel of bread in the shade of the main hatch and sat himself down opposite like a man settling in for the whole afternoon. \"Yes,\" I said, with the best of my memory and none of my truth. \"Huntin' what?\" \"...Birds.\" \"At sea.\" \"They were flying low.\" He turned a piece of bread over in his hands. He had the patience of a man who has heard every lie and has come to enjoy them as a kind of performance. \"Hands,\" he said, around the bread. Somebody behind me took my wrists. I had held them so that the rope marks were underneath, and I had been careful not to put them where they would catch the sun, and none of it made any difference, because the man behind me had been a rope-maker's apprentice before he was anything else and could read a wrist like a page. \"Rope,\" said the man behind me. \"How long, do you reckon?\" \"Two days. Maybe three. They used the good stuff, too — see how it bites.\" Ostrand finished his bread and looked at me with an entirely new interest. The interest of a man who has found something in his catch that is worth more than fish. \"What's your name, boy?\" I did not answer at once, and it was not caution. I had been dead since morning under the name I was born with, and it had not been worth much to anyone living. The moment I opened my mouth with it I would be a prince again — hunted again, and there is no country on the eastern continent where a hunted prince keeps his own name for long. A fisherman two boats over was cursing somebody called Tanno about a net. \"Tanno,\" I said. \"Tanno who?\" \"Just Tanno.\" Ostrand held my eyes for a while, and something went across his face and was put away again. Then he got up, brushed the crumbs off himself, and said to the fishermen in a perfectly level voice, in a perfectly ordinary tone: \"Keep the fishes. Toss the brat back.\" \"Yes, Capitan.\" \"Wait.\" My voice came out flat because there was nothing left in me to put behind it. \"I can't swim.\" The deck went quiet. Not nervous quiet — interested quiet. The man behind me shifted. Somewhere a gull had opinions about all of this. Ostrand turned around. He looked at me for a length of time I could not measure, and whatever had gone across his face before came back and stayed a while, and I became aware of the fact that I was thirteen years old and covered in salt and half somebody's lunch, and that I was, despite all of it, being looked at the way a man looks at a thing he is considering taking with him. \"Well,\" he said at last. \"We'd best find a port, then.\" *** \"Are you serious? I beg you to reconsider.\" \"HARD TO PORT, NETTLE — you worthless sack of root vegetables. HARD TO PORT.\" \"I am at port—\" \"ARE YOU ARGUING WITH ME IN FRONT OF THE BRAT—\" \"Capitan, the sheets—\" \"LET THE CURTAINS GO WILD.\" The sails broke open all at once and the whole ship leaned and the crew howled and went up the ropes and the deck came alive with men who were suddenly all in the way of each other and none of them in the way of the ship, which is the only skill that matters at sea. The hull moved under me and my stomach moved somewhere with it. I had been put in the small boat — Nettle's idea, and a good one, and I would have said so to his face if I had had any air — with two oars, no sail, and a length of rope coiled at my feet that nobody had bothered to take away. I rowed. I did not decide to. The ship pulled away and the gap became a thing I could measure and the measuring was all I had, so I rowed. My palms went from burning to tearing to a kind of numb wet wrongness I made myself not look at. The ship did not stop. Of course it did not stop. I watched the dark shape of it get smaller until it was a thumbprint, and then until it was a smudge, and then until it was the thing I had been looking at for some minutes without it being anything at all. My hands came off the oars before I told them to. *\"Should I just go back?\"* I looked behind me. The sun had gone. The sea was the colour of the inside of a mouth. I opened my palms, and looked, properly this time, and understood that I was not going to be doing any more of that. I closed my eyes. *\"There is no use running, Your Highness.\"* *\"Your Highness, my apologies — I have to be somewhere before noon.\"* Her voice. Silver in the corridor. Going away from me faster than I could walk, because she had somewhere to be and I had been the last thing on the way. *\"All the countries of the eastern continent are now in our grasp. Who could even imagine — their country's ruler swapped with our people.\"* My father, laughing, alone in a room, at a mirror. Laughing too much. Then stopping. Then turning his head. Then something warm came down on the backs of my hands, and I opened my eyes, and there were two small dark eyes just above the gunwale, looking at me the way the world looks at you when you have stopped being interesting to it. A dolphin. She held my gaze for a while, which no dolphin has ever done to me since. Then she turned her snout — deliberately, and with what I would swear was a sense of occasion — toward the bottom of the boat. There was a fish there. A small one, out of the sea, shaking itself to pieces against the boards. \"Do you want it?\" I picked it up. It was cold and frantic and it fit in my hand. I held it out over the rail. She took it the way you take something from a friend. Then she went around and around the boat in a tight circle, delighted, like a dog that has been given a stick and does not know what to do with the joy of it. I laughed. It came out of me entirely without permission, and it was, I want to be clear, the first true thing I had done since I woke up in a cave. And then, under the laugh, the other thing. It came the way a smell comes — the rope round my wrists, the drums, the old man's hand raised — and gone again before I could take hold of it, and I was left with the taste of iron in my mouth and no reason for it on this boat. I had asked for power. I remembered asking. I had asked with my head coming down on the block and had got — this. A boat. A ruined pair of hands. A dolphin. Where was the power, then. What had it cost, and who had paid it, and why could I not remember the price being agreed. And then I saw the rope. It was still there, coiled at my feet, the way it had been the whole time, and I had been looking at it with my hands and not with my head. She was swimming a furrow in the water beside the boat. She would do it again, because she was a dolphin and because she had decided she liked me, and she would do it until the light went and after, in whatever way a dolphin decides things. She had no idea. She had absolutely no idea what she was looking at. I put the rope over the side. \"Heh,\" I heard myself say. \"Heh — heh —\" And it got away from me then, and I laughed until my ribs hurt, bent over the wet boards while the dolphin circled and looked up at me with those two dark eyes, and the last of the sun went, and the sea held absolutely still and let the world know what a liar I was."},{"t":"4. Stop It, Brat","k":"Killer Petals · chapter","u":"killerpetals/c4.html","s":"The sun was high. The weather was lovely. That part was for me. None of the rest of it was. Here is what happened, in the order the ship experienced it. Capitan Ostrand came out on deck with his cup, as he did every morning, and admired the weather, as he did every morning, and drank his tea with a noise like a bucket going down a well. The gulls were up. The water was that flat blue a sailor will tell you he likes and does not like. Then he saw something. No. He saw someone. And that someone was— \"WOOOOOOOO.\" I was sitting in the small boat alongside, at a cruising speed, steering with one oar and trailing the other like a man who had invented the idea. Behind me, at the end of four hundred feet of rope, a dolphin was doing this to me on purpose. \"HOW—\" Ostrand set his cup down. Then he picked it up again and drank from it and set it down. \"How in the great three seas is this possible.\" \"I told you, Capitan,\" said Nettle, appearing at his elbow with the tan cloth wound round his head, \"when the black cats cut the route you're meant to be taking, you turn back.\" \"I — \" Ostrand looked at the boat, and at me in the boat, and at the dolphin, and at the sky, checking. \"I should've listened.\" \"Well.\" Nettle considered it. \"If we find a port, we can toss the kid there. Put him down on the quay and be off about our business.\" \"Aye,\" said Ostrand, and turned to look at me properly. That was the first I noticed it. He had been talking about me all morning in that voice of his: big, cheerful, loud enough to be heard on the other ship of a convoy — and then it was quiet for too long, and Nettle looked at him, and Nettle's face changed. Ostrand's eyes had gone somewhere. He was standing on his own deck with his cup in his hand and his ship under his feet and his crew going about their work and there was nobody in him. \"Capitan?\" Nettle said. Nothing. \"Capitan.\" Nothing. The cup dipped and tea went over the rim of it onto his hand and he did not seem to notice that it was hot. \"CAPITAN. LOOK AT ME.\" Nettle did not shout it like a man shouting at a captain. He shouted it like a man calling somebody back from a long way off. The crew stopped. The whole deck stopped, in the way a deck stops when every man on it has seen this before and knows exactly how long to keep their eyes elsewhere. Ostrand turned his head. His eyes were wet. \"It was not your mistake,\" Nettle said. He had both hands up, not touching him, the way you hold your hands up to a horse. His shoulders were shaking and I could not tell which of them it was coming from. \"Nothing ever was. Say it back to me. Say it.\" Ostrand looked at him for a long moment. Then he looked at me instead, over Nettle's shoulder — at me, in a rowing boat, tied to a dolphin, wet to the skin and grinning like an idiot — and something in his face did a thing I understood very well and did not like at all. It looked at me the way a man looks at something that has arrived when he was sure nothing would ever arrive again. He did not say anything. He walked to the cabin door and opened it and went in and shut it, and the brass of the lock going over was the loudest sound on the water. Nettle stood there with his hands still half-raised. The crew found things to do. Then Nettle lowered his hands, and his eyes came down, and stayed down for a while, and when he lifted them again they landed on me. And in that moment I stopped being a joke in a small boat and became something else to somebody — though what, exactly, I would not learn for a very long time, and should not have wanted to."},{"t":"Abel","k":"Killer Petals · Main Cast","u":"killerpetals/lore/killerpetals--abel.html","s":"  Abel\n\n  Status:   Servant of House Asherforth for 41 years. The conscience of chapter 2.\n\n   Confirmed\n\n-   Forty-one years   in the family's service.\n- He dresses Santino for the banquet and says:  \"You look absolutely stunning, Your\n  Highness,\"    as a matter of fact   — not flattery, a report.\n- He introduces himself with the full old form:  \"This humble servant is known by the\n  name of Abel.\" \n-   Santino barely hears him.    \"It barely half reached me. I was fixing my collar. I\n  was wondering whether she would be in blue.\" \n- He bows  \"properly, the full bow, the one the old ones still did\"  and shuts the door\n  quietly —  \"which is a harder thing to do than it looks and which he had been doing\n  for my family for forty-one years.\" \n\n   Why he matters\n\nAbel is the only person in the draft who   asks for nothing and is given nothing  .\nHe is not a plot device; he is the measuring stick. The narrator puts his own\nindifference on the record:\n\n  \"If I had treated him any better that day. If I had remembered his name.\n  Would it have been any different?\n  I do not think so. But I think about it. That is the whole of it — I do not think it\n  would have changed anything, and I think about it.\"\n\n  PROPOSED:   that paragraph is the moral centre of the chapter. Santino is not\nashamed because it changed the outcome. He is ashamed because it didn't.\n\n   Open\n\n-   Did Abel survive the purge?   He is in the palace when the execution is ordered.\n  UNRESOLVED.\n- His age, family, and whether he served Santino's mother or father first: not stated.\n\n   Note on naming\n\nThe name   Abel   carries a biblical resonance — the acceptable offering, the brother\nkilled by a brother. The text does not invoke it.   PROPOSED:   if that echo is\ndeliberate it is worth one acknowledgement somewhere; if not, leave it alone.\n\n   Related\n\n  Santino   ·   The Royal House of Asherforth   ·   02 Open Decisions  \n"},{"t":"Brant","k":"Killer Petals · Main Cast","u":"killerpetals/lore/killerpetals--brant.html","s":"  Brant\n\n  Status:   Mercenary swordsman. The one who reported the death.\n\n   Confirmed\n\n-   Swordsman  , in black throat to boot, with a squad that Santino killed more than\n  half of before the prologue.\n-   He says the same line twice   —  \"There is no use running, Your Highness.\"  — and\n  Santino notes the second time is  \"always better for a man like that.\" \n-   He grins, and the grin falters   when Santino taunts him about the squad.\n   \"Men who are ashamed make mistakes faster than men who are confident.\" \n-   He is thorough.   After the vine snaps and the water closes,  \"Brant stood there a\n  while. He was a thorough man. When there was nothing left to look at he turned around.\" \n-   He reports to Aldric   that the last of the royal line of Asherforth drowned\n   \"with a bronze coin in his pocket and a stranger's arm around his neck.\" \n\n   Why he matters\n\nBrant is the   proof of Santino's competence  . The prologue's trick works because Brant\nis good: he sees the vine, he understands a half-beat late, and he yells  \"Stop him!\" \nalready knowing it is too late. A lesser antagonist would make the escape cheap.\n\nHe is also the   first lie in the book.   His report is accurate in every detail and\nwrong in its conclusion, and it is the reason Santino survives. Brant is not a fool. He\nwas simply lied to by the sea.\n\n   Where he is now\n\n  UNRESOLVED.   He reported to Aldric and is presumably alive. If Santino's revenge list\nexists, Brant is on it — but Brant was doing a job, not a betrayal, and the text never\nsays Santino blames him. Note the difference: Santino's oath names  \"those fakes standing\nbehind them\" , not the men with swords.\n\n   Related\n\n  The Royal House of Asherforth   ·   Kord and the masked old man   ·   Santino   ·   The Hunt  \n"},{"t":"Capitan Ostrand","k":"Killer Petals · Main Cast","u":"killerpetals/lore/killerpetals--capitan-ostrand.html","s":"  Capitan Ostrand\n\n  Status:   Fishing captain. The first person to look at Santino and want him.\n\n   Confirmed\n\n-   Enormous.   His shirt  \"was losing a war it had been losing for years.\" \n- Captain of a fishing boat with a crew large enough to howl in unison.\n- Loud by default:  \"WE ARE GONNA FEAST TODAY, BOYS!\" ,  \"LET THE CURTAINS GO WILD.\" \n-   Patient with lies.    \"He had the patience of a man who has heard every lie and has\n  come to enjoy them as a kind of performance.\"  He lets Santino's \"hunting birds\" story\n  run without correcting it.\n- He orders Santino thrown back —  \"Keep the fishes. Toss the brat back\"  — and reverses\n  when Santino says he cannot swim, after a look Santino cannot measure:  \"Well. We'd\n  best find a port, then.\" \n-   He dissociates.   Chapter 4: standing on his own deck, cup in hand, tea spilling\n  over his fingers,  \"and there was nobody in him.\" \n-   Nettle talks him back.    \"It was not your mistake. Nothing ever was. Say it back to\n  me. Say it.\"  Both their shoulders were shaking.\n- Coming back, he looks at Santino  \"the way a man looks at something that has arrived\n  when he was sure nothing would ever arrive again.\" \n\n   The dissociation — the important part\n\n  CONFIRMED:   it reads as an established event, not a new one. The crew  \"stopped, in\nthe way a deck stops when every man on it has seen this before and knows exactly how\nlong to keep their eyes elsewhere.\"  Nettle has a script for it. This has happened\nmany times.\n\n  IMPLIED:   the trigger is Santino's presence, or the impossible thing Santino just did\n(being towed by a dolphin). Ostrand's line  \"I should've listened\"  is about the black\ncats and the route — not about Santino.\n\n  UNRESOLVED:   whether this is the first visible cost of Santino's oath — someone near\nhim coming apart — or an old grief of Ostrand's that Santino merely witnesses.   This\ndistinction decides whether the book is a tragedy about collateral damage.   See\n  02 Open Decisions    10.\n\n   What he calls Santino\n\nNothing yet. He asks the name, gets  \"Tanno,\"  and does not use it in the text. He says\n \"boy\"  and  \"the brat.\" \n\n   Related\n\n  Nettle   ·   Santino   ·   The Crew   ·   02 Open Decisions  \n"},{"t":"Casimir Vane","k":"Killer Petals · Main Cast","u":"killerpetals/lore/killerpetals--casimir-vane.html","s":"  Casimir Vane\n\n  Status:   The man Lynna chose. Not yet a villain.\n\n   Confirmed\n\n-   Lord Casimir Vane.   The herald Perrin announces  \"Lynna Asherforth and Lord Casimir\n  Vane\"  together, which in that hall means a couple.\n- He   matches her stride without seeming to try for it.  \n-   He proposes in public  , at the banquet, on one knee:  \"Will you accept this ring as\n  the sign of our promise?\" \n-   He has a good smile.    \"he had a good smile, I hate that he had a good smile.\" \n- Lynna's ears go red. She glares at him —  \"Y-you. Here\"  — and does not step back.\n\n   What's notable\n\n  The text gives him no cruelty whatsoever.   He is not a rival who cheats. He is\ncourteous, well-liked, and the room reacts to him warmly. Santino's hatred is entirely\nhis own, and he knows it:\n\n  \"She was happy. She was happy with him. She had never been unhappy in any way I could\n  fix.\"\n\n  PROPOSED:   Casimir works best as a   decent man  . If he turns out to be a conspirator\nin the replacement plot, the book loses the thing chapter 2 is actually about — that\nSantino had no grievance and lost anyway. Resist making him the villain.\n\n   Open\n\n-   His house and rank.   \"Lord\" and nothing more.\n- Whether he or his family had anything to do with the swap. UNRESOLVED — and the honest\n  answer may be no.\n- Whether he is still alive after the purge. UNRESOLVED.\n\n   Related\n\n  Lynna   ·   Santino   ·   The Royal House of Asherforth  \n"},{"t":"Kord and the masked old man","k":"Killer Petals · Main Cast","u":"killerpetals/lore/killerpetals--kord-and-the-masked-old-man.html","s":"  Kord and the masked old man\n\n  Status:   The execution. Two men, one act.\n\n   Kord — the executioner\n\n  Confirmed:  \n\n-   Big.    \"Kord, who was a big man, came out with a blade longer than his arm.\" \n- He   wipes the blade down with a cloth, unhurried   —  \"the way a man wipes a tool he\n  has used before and will use again.\" \n- He takes his place at Santino's side and waits for the hierophant's hand.\n-   His sword is the one Santino takes   when he wakes. It is still lying on the\n  platform,  \"where someone had put it down and not come back for it.\" \n\n  Small but load-bearing:   Kord's patience is what makes the scene frightening. He is\nnot enjoying it. He is working.\n\n  ⚠   \"Hierophant\" is not the author's word.   The text only ever says  \"the old man\n  in the mask\" . The term is a working label for this vault. Do not use it in\n  narration.\n\n   The masked old man\n\n  Confirmed:  \n\n- An   old man in a mask  , who barks the ritual words:  \"Ola, hula huh u.\" \n- He   sings the hymn   in an old language —  \"the old language had been shaped for\n  something else before it was made to do this.\" \n- He raises his hand to the crowd; the drums stop completely; his hand comes down; the\n  blade follows.\n- He is masked, so   the text never gives him a face.  \n\n  Decision recorded:   he stays nameless as a  role , not a person. He is not \"the priest\"\nand he is not given a name.   The mask is the character.   Do not name him until the\nmanuscript does.\n\n   What the ritual establishes\n\n  CONFIRMED:   this is an old, practised liturgy. The dome is built for long dwelling, the\nstone has  \"the look of a thing that had received\" , garlands and offerings have been\nbrought for a long time, and the drums come  \"from maybe twenty places at once.\"  This is\nnot a one-off murder. It is an institution.\n\n  IMPLIED:   the tribe believes they are feeding something. Whether the rose is what they\nthink it is — or whether something else answers — is the chapter 1 question.\n\n   Related\n\n  The Tribe   ·   The Rose   ·   The Sun Symbol   ·   Santino  \n"},{"t":"Lynna","k":"Killer Petals · Main Cast","u":"killerpetals/lore/killerpetals--lynna.html","s":"  Lynna\n\n  Status:   The girl he loved. Beyond the reach of the text.\n\n   Confirmed\n\n-   Lynna Asherforth   — the herald announces her with the surname, which matches\n  Santino's own house.   See the warning below.  \n-   Silver hair.    \"Her hair was silver. That is the thing I have kept. Not her face;\n  I have lost the face.\" \n-   Eyes the colour of ice on the palace reservoir in the third month.  \n-   She had somewhere to be before noon   the day of the corridor scene.  \"I apologise,\n  Your Highness, but I have to be somewhere before noon.\" \n-   She let him hold her hand   and then took it back  \"not hard — she never did\n  anything hard, and that was the whole difficulty with her.\" \n- At the banquet she wears   blue  . Santino:  \"It was just blue, I thought. Everyone\n  said blue.\" \n-   She does not turn when he says her name   in the hall.\n-   Casimir Vane proposes.   Her ears go red. She makes  \"a small production of being\n  uncertain, and did not step backwards.\"  She accepts.\n\n   The one thing he keeps returning to\n\n  \"Her glare at him was not her glare at me. That is the thing that is still somewhere\n  behind my ribs, even now, even after everything: she can look at him like that and be\n  happy about it, and she never once looked at me like that.\"\n\nAnd, in the ballroom:\n\n  \"She was happy. She was happy with him. She had never been unhappy in any way I could\n  fix.\"\n\n  That second line is the real one.   It is not that she chose another man. It is that\nthere was never a wound for him to heal, so there was never a way in.\n\n   ⚠ Warning — the surname\n\n  Lynna's surname is Asherforth, the same as Santino's royal house.  \n\nThe text never states their relationship. Two readings:\n\n-   Cousins   — the romance works, the herald's \"two names together\" means a match\n  within the noble network, and the tragedy is one of proximity.\n-   Siblings   — the entire chapter 2 reads completely differently and the book becomes\n  something else.\n\n  UNRESOLVED. This is the highest-priority question in the vault   because it changes\nwhat chapter 2  is . Do not write another scene with her until the author answers.\nSee   02 Open Decisions    7.\n\n   As a character\n\n  Thin on the page by design.   Santino has  \"lost the face.\"  We get hair, eyes, a\ntone of voice, and a way of being kind about the wrong thing. He loved her and cannot\nnow describe her, which is more honest than a full portrait would be.\n\n  Do not flesh her out in the bible before the manuscript does.   Anything added here\nwould be invention.\n\n   Related\n\n  Santino   ·   Casimir Vane   ·   02 Open Decisions  \n"},{"t":"Nettle","k":"Killer Petals · Main Cast","u":"killerpetals/lore/killerpetals--nettle.html","s":"  Nettle\n\n  Status:   First mate. The one who keeps the captain upright.\n\n   Confirmed\n\n-   Tan cloth wound round his head   against the sun.\n- Argues with Ostrand about the sails and loses in the most theatrical way available:\n   \"Capitan, the sheets—\"  /  \"LET THE CURTAINS GO WILD.\" \n-   Practical.   It was his idea to put Santino in the small boat.  \"If we find a port,\n  we can toss the kid there. Put him down on the quay and be off about our business.\" \n-   He knows Ostrand's collapse before it starts.   He watches him go and says\n   \"Capitan?\"  twice, then shouts it  \"like a man calling somebody back from a long way\n  off.\" \n-   He has the words for it.    \"It was not your mistake. Nothing ever was. Say it back\n  to me. Say it.\"  Hands up, not touching —  \"the way you hold your hands up to a horse.\" \n-   He calls Santino \"the brat\"   — same as Ostrand.\n\n   Why he matters\n\nNettle is the only character in the draft who   acts competently under pressure without\nbeing asked.   He manages the ship, manages the captain, and manages the strange boy,\nand he complains the whole time.\n\nIn chapter 4, after Ostrand shuts himself in his cabin, Nettle's eyes land on Santino:\n\n  \"And in that moment I stopped being a joke in a small boat and became something else\n  to somebody — though what, exactly, I would not learn for a very long time, and should\n  not have wanted to.\"\n\n  IMPLIED:   Nettle has decided something about Santino. The narrator, telling this from\nlater,   knows what and is refusing to say.   That is a promise to the reader and it has\nto be paid.\n\n   Open\n\n- His real name, origin, and how long he has sailed with Ostrand: not stated.\n- Whether he suspects what Santino is: UNRESOLVED, but he is the one who looked.\n\n   Related\n\n  Capitan Ostrand   ·   Santino   ·   The Crew  \n"},{"t":"Santino","k":"Killer Petals · Main Cast","u":"killerpetals/lore/killerpetals--santino.html","s":"  Santino\n\n  Status:   Protagonist. First-person narrator, thirteen years old, technically dead.\n\n  ⚠   The name \"Santino\" does not appear in the manuscript.   He is never named\n  aloud. Every character calls him  \"Your Highness\" ,  \"boy\" ,  \"the brat\" , or\n  \"Tanno\" (the alias he invents in ch3). The name is the author's working name for\n  him and is used throughout this vault because a note needs a filename.   Do not\n  put it in his mouth or in narration until the author confirms it is canon.  \n\n   Confirmed\n\n-   Prince of Asherforth.   Brant calls him  \"the last of the royal line of Asherforth.\" \n-   Thirteen.   Stated in chapter 3:  \"I was thirteen years old and covered in salt.\" \n-   Pale, lean enough to slip through a crowd.   He runs from armed men and nearly\n  outlasts them.\n-   Carries a bronze coin   in the prologue and a   silver coin   at the banquet.\n  The silver one came from Sabine at the first banquet she let him attend. UNRESOLVED\n  whether these are one coin described twice or two.\n-   Cannot swim.   Said plainly in chapter 3.\n-   His hands are ruined   by rowing in chapter 3, and stay ruined.\n-   Dressed in branches   after the resurrection — bound with something that was\n   \"still moving.\"  He wakes still wearing them.\n-   Owns Kord's sword  , taken from the altar floor.\n\n   What he does that tells us who he is\n\n-   He lies to survive, and it is not a flaw.   The drowning in the prologue, the\n  name Tanno, the birds he was hunting. His first instinct is a performance.\n-   He reads a room.   On the cliff he works out four options and picks one; he\n  deliberately looks at the vine so Brant looks too.\n-   He uses people's assumptions as weapons.    \"I'm interested in men\"  stops a\n  knife because the man cannot hold two impossible things at once.\n-   He is vain.    \"I smiled at myself in the mirror... I was, I want to be fair to\n  the boy I was, worth looking at.\"  He was arranging to propose for three days.\n-   He was careless with people below him.   Abel served his family forty-one years\n  and Santino did not know his name.  \"If I had remembered his name. Would it have\n  been any different? I do not think so. But I think about it.\" \n-   He is not brave in the ballroom.    \"I could have stopped it... My legs did not\n  move because I was standing in a ballroom watching the girl I loved be happy.\" \n-   He is ashamed of the order of his own feelings.   Relief arrived before disbelief\n  after the resurrection, and he has never got over that.\n\n   Reading of the character\n\n  PROPOSED:   Santino's defining trait is that he is  competent and a coward at the\nsame time  — he can get out of a cliff and cannot walk across a ballroom. The book's\nrevenge is therefore not about becoming strong. It is about what he will agree to\nbecause he was never willing to say one honest word when it counted.\n\nThe thesis line, spoken while kneeling on the altar:\n   \"I will kill them. Every one of them. Even if — in the process — I lose myself.\" \n\nAnd the line that lands   after   his head is off:\n   \"If only that day I had power.\" \n\n   The debt\n\n  CONFIRMED:   he wakes with  \"the warm, patient wrongness of being owed\"  and\nunderstands that  \"a debt had been opened in this room that night, and that I was\nstanding in it, and that it was mine.\" \n\n  UNRESOLVED:   what the debt is, who holds it, and what he agreed to while dead.\nHe does not remember agreeing. He does remember asking.\n\n   Open\n\n  02 Open Decisions   — especially whether his heritage is foreign and whether his\nparents were replaced.\n\n   Related\n\n  The Rose   ·   Lynna   ·   Abel   ·   The Tribe   ·   Capitan Ostrand  \n"},{"t":"The Royal House of Asherforth","k":"Killer Petals · Main Cast","u":"killerpetals/lore/killerpetals--the-royal-house-of-asherforth.html","s":"  The Royal House of Asherforth\n\n  Status:   The family that was replaced, or the family that wanted him dead.\n\n   Sabine — the queen\n\n  Confirmed:  \n\n-   Santino's mother.   Her voice arrives during the ritual, exactly as she would say it:\n   \"Gosh — how many times do I have to tell you to stop sneaking out of the castle—\" \n  and  \"I heard you skipped the basic swordsmanship class again.\" \n-  \"Cross, and fond, and about to laugh.\"  He had hated that tone.\n-   She gave him the silver coin   at the first banquet she allowed him to attend —\n   \"and which she had not said anything about when she gave it, and which I had carried\n  every day since, because it was hers and because I was sentimental and because it was,\n  in the end, the only thing she ever actually handed me.\" \n\n  IMPLIED:   that last clause is doing quiet damage. A mother who hands her son exactly\none thing in thirteen years.\n\n   Aldric — the king\n\n  Confirmed:  \n\n-   Santino's father.   Brant reports to him.\n-  \"Looking at me the way he used to look at me — which was not angry, which was the thing\n  I had never been able to explain to anyone, because he never once raised his voice and\n  somehow it was always worse.\" \n-   In the chapter 3 memory:   laughing alone at a mirror —  \"All the countries of the\n  eastern continent are now in our grasp, who could even imagine — their country's ruler\n  swapped with our people\"  —  \"laughing too much. Then stopping. Then turning his head.\" \n\n  That memory is the pivot of the whole book.   See below.\n\n   ⚠ The \"fakes\" — the central unresolved fact\n\nChapter 1, during the ritual, Santino thinks:\n\n  \"And then the other hands — the ones that have always been in that room. That Sun symbol.\n  I clenched my teeth so hard something at the back of my jaw gave. The corpses. And those\n  fakes standing behind them, calling themselves my parents.\"\n\nChapter 3 gives the father's own recorded voice:\n  \"their country's ruler   swapped   with our people\"\n\nTwo readings, and   they are different books:  \n\n  Reading   Means  \n --- --- \n    Replacement     Aldric and Sabine were killed and impersonated. The thing that ordered Santino's execution is the empire, wearing his father's face. He has been hunting the wrong people.  \n    Metaphor     They were always dead to him.  \"Fakes\"  is contempt, not fact. His father really did sell him.  \n\n  UNRESOLVED.   The author's own chapter 2 note says the anti-deity is  \"not evil, only\nnegative\"  — which leans toward replacement, since a negative force would not need a\nmotive to wear a face.\n\n  Do not write chapter 5 assuming either.   Ask.\n\n   Related\n\n  Santino   ·   Abel   ·   The Sun Symbol   ·   02 Open Decisions    2\n"},{"t":"Asherforth","k":"Killer Petals · Worldbuilding","u":"killerpetals/lore/killerpetals--asherforth.html","s":"  Asherforth\n\n  Status:   The kingdom. Also the royal house's name. Its line ends with Santino.\n\n   Confirmed\n\n-   A kingdom.   Brant calls Santino  \"the last of the royal line of Asherforth.\" \n-   The royal house is named Asherforth   — the same name as the kingdom, which is normal\n  for this kind of setting.\n-   It has a palace   with a corridor where Lynna walked  \"with the sun behind her,\"  a\n  ballroom with double doors, a great hall, and a   reservoir that ices over in the third\n  month.  \n-   It has a herald   — Perrin, who has  \"announced every name in that hall for nineteen\n  years.\" \n-   It has a noble network   with houses like Vane, and enough of a social calendar that a\n  banquet is a place where a betrothal gets announced in public.\n-   It is on the eastern continent  , or in its sphere.\n\n   The succession\n\n  CONFIRMED:   Brant reports that the last of the royal line drowned. That is believed.\nSo as far as the world knows,   Asherforth has no heir   — and the throne is either vacant,\nheld by a regent, or claimed by whoever the empire put there.\n\n  UNRESOLVED and important:   who is ruling Asherforth now? The text never says. If Aldric\nand Sabine were replaced, then the thing wearing them is on the throne, and Santino's\nkingdom is occupied by his own face.\n\n   ⚠ The Lynna problem\n\nLynna's surname is   Asherforth  . If that is the royal house, she is a relative of some\ndegree, and the herald's joint announcement with Casimir Vane is a dynastic match.\n\n  UNRESOLVED.   See   02 Open Decisions    7 — this is the single highest-priority question\nin the vault.\n\n   Naming note\n\nA kingdom and its ruling house sharing a name is common and works. But it does mean every\nmention of \"Asherforth\" is ambiguous: the place, or the family. When writing, prefer\n \"the house of Asherforth\"  for the family and bare  \"Asherforth\"  for the kingdom.\n\n   Related\n\n  The Royal House of Asherforth   ·   The World   ·   Santino   ·   Lynna  \n"},{"t":"The Black Cats","k":"Killer Petals · Worldbuilding","u":"killerpetals/lore/killerpetals--the-black-cats.html","s":"  The Black Cats\n\n  Status:   A named hazard on the sea routes. One line of text.\n\n   The only mention\n\nChapter 4, Nettle to Ostrand:\n\n  \"when the black cats cut the route you're meant to be taking, you turn back.\"\n\nAnd Ostrand:  \"I should've listened.\" \n\n   What can be read\n\n- They   \"cut\" a route   — the phrase is about a path being severed, not a ship being attacked.\n- There is a   known rule  : if the black cats cut your route, turn back.\n- Ostrand ignored the rule and kept going, which is how he found Santino.\n- The crew treats it as ordinary seamanship, not superstition or terror. Nettle says it\n   \"as a matter of fact.\" \n\n   Candidate readings — all UNRESOLVED, none canon\n\n  Reading   Note  \n --- --- \n    Weather   — a storm pattern sailors name   Fits \"cut the route\" and the total absence of fear in Nettle's voice  \n    Pirates or a raiding fleet     Fits \"cut\" and would tie the sea to the political plot  \n    A creature or phenomenon     Fits the book's supernatural register, but nothing on the page supports it  \n    A banned current or reef     Fits \"you turn back\" as pure navigation  \n\n  Do not decide this.   It is one line and the author has not indicated which.\n\n   Why it's recorded\n\nBecause it is the   only reason Santino is alive.   If Ostrand had turned back as he should\nhave, the boat would not have been there. Whatever the black cats are, they are the mechanism\nby which the plot reached him — and that is worth remembering when the author decides whether\nthe meeting was chance or arranged.\n\n   Related\n\n  The Crew   ·   Capitan Ostrand   ·   Nettle   ·   02 Open Decisions  \n"},{"t":"The Crew","k":"Killer Petals · Worldbuilding","u":"killerpetals/lore/killerpetals--the-crew.html","s":"  The Crew\n\n  Status:   A working fishing boat. The first place he has ever been anonymous.\n\n   Confirmed\n\n- A   fishing vessel   under Capitan Ostrand, large enough for a deck crew, a main hatch,\n  and sails that can be let go all at once.\n-   Crew traits:   they howl in unison, argue about headings, go up the ropes, and  \"the deck\n  came alive with men who were suddenly all in the way of each other and none of them in the\n  way of the ship, which is the only skill that matters at sea.\" \n-   A former rope-maker's apprentice   among them, who reads Santino's wrists:  \"Two days.\n  Maybe three. They used the good stuff too — see how it bites.\" \n- They   check their eyes elsewhere   when Ostrand collapses — this has happened before.\n-   Nettle   is first mate.   Ostrand   captains.\n- The ship carries a   small boat   with two oars and no sail.\n- There is a   convoy   — Ostrand is loud enough to be heard  \"on the other ship of a convoy.\" \n\n   The route\n\n  CONFIRMED:   they were supposed to be on a different route.  \"when the black cats cut the\nroute you're meant to be taking, you turn back.\"  Ostrand did not turn back.  \"I should've\nlistened.\" \n\n  UNRESOLVED:   what \"the black cats\" are. A hazard, a faction, a sign, or weather. Thread:\n  02 Open Decisions  .\n\n   What they are not\n\nThey are not a navy, not a trading company, and not interested in politics. They are working\npeople who found something in their net. That is why Santino can be nobody here — and why\nthis is the first place in the book where he is safe.\n\n   Names\n\nOnly   Ostrand   and   Nettle   are named. The rope-maker's apprentice and the rest are\nunnamed and should stay so until the manuscript names them.\n\n   Related\n\n  Capitan Ostrand   ·   Nettle   ·   Santino   ·   The Black Cats  \n"},{"t":"The Dome","k":"Killer Petals · Worldbuilding","u":"killerpetals/lore/killerpetals--the-dome.html","s":"  The Dome\n\n  Status:   The temple where he died. Built for permanence, by someone who expected stalling.\n\n   Confirmed\n\n  \"The dome was the roof of a world that had been put underground. Circular paths spiralled\n  the walls, one above another, stairs running between them, and everywhere tunnel mouths\n  opened off into dark — more passages than I could count, all of them converging on the\n  floor. It was built the way a thing is built when whoever built it expected to be\n  underground for a long time.\"\n\n  Details:  \n\n-   Circular   layout. Spiral paths up the walls, stairs between levels.\n-   Uncountable tunnels   opening off the walls, all converging on the floor.\n-   Air:   warm, wet, smelled of smoke, tallow,  \"and something green.\"  Under it a smell\n  he  \"could not place and did not like.\" \n-   Acoustics:   drums started  \"from maybe twenty places at once and settling into one\n  rhythm, the way a crowd will find a heartbeat.\" \n-   A river above it   — heard through the rock when the drums stopped.\n-   Lit by fire  , not daylight. No exit to the surface visible from the floor.\n- Above, in the forest, the entrance is a   sinkhole   — the ground  \"went down and the\n  sound changed — a great hollowness.\" \n\n   The altar\n\n-   Grey, ancient stone  , cracked  \"from top to bottom.\" \n-   Green with something   growing in the cracks  \"a long time before the tribe or the\n  tunnel or the kingdom above us.\" \n-   Hung with offerings:   garlands ( \"dried, some of them, and some not\" ), fruit, skins,\n   \"small bundles of something I chose not to identify.\" \n-  \"The stone had the look of a thing that had received.\" \n- The surface he is forced onto is  \"a platform of rough stone.\" \n\n   What happened here, in order\n\n1. Santino is carried in, still bound, and dumped on his knees.\n2. Untied, then dressed in branch-cloth, then bound again behind his back.\n3. Dragged to the stone. The hymn. The rose moves.\n4. Kord brings the sword. The old man's hand rises, the drums stop.\n5. Beheading. Blood.  \"the whole dome turning the colour of the inside of a mouth.\" \n6.   Time passes.   Two days minimum — the stone is dry, and the tribe has taken everything.\n7. He wakes on the floor. Empty. The rose is gone. Kord's sword remains.\n8. He walks out.   Two days   to reach the coast.\n\n   What is missing when he wakes\n\n  CONFIRMED absent:   the tribe, the drums, the hierophant, the rose, all the blood, the\nofferings, and any explanation.\n\n  CONFIRMED present:   his body, his branch clothes, the rope marks on his wrists, the\nsword, the altar, a corpse he declines to describe.\n\n  \"Somebody had been here and tidied.\"\n\n  IMPLIED:   whoever tidied left the sword and took the rose. Either the tribe moved\neverything, or something else did.\n\n   Related\n\n  The Rose   ·   The Tribe   ·   Kord and the masked old man   ·   03 Chapter Ledger  \n"},{"t":"The Hunt","k":"Killer Petals · Worldbuilding","u":"killerpetals/lore/killerpetals--the-hunt.html","s":"  The Hunt\n\n  Status:   The pursuit that opens the book. Ordered by someone. Nobody says who.\n\n   Confirmed\n\n-   Brant leads it.   A squad of hired men,  \"black from throat to boot.\" \n-   Santino had already killed more than half of them   before the prologue starts.  \"You say\n  that like I haven't just cost you more than half your squad.\" \n-   They are professionals.   They track him through forest, they use arrows, and they do not\n  stop because he is a prince — the honorific is mockery, not deference.\n-   Brant is thorough   and reports the death to   Aldric   personally.\n-   They believe he drowned.   The bronze coin, the stolen arm, the water — Brant watched it\n  all happen and reported it as fact.\n\n   Who ordered it\n\n  UNRESOLVED.   Three candidates on the page, none confirmed:\n\n  Candidate   Basis  \n --- --- \n    Aldric   — Santino's father   Brant reports to him directly.  \n    \"The fakes\"   wearing his parents   Santino's own accusation during the ritual:  \"those fakes standing behind them.\"   \n    The empire     If the rulers were swapped, then the order came from whoever swapped them.  \n\n  Note the distinction that matters:   the men with swords are  not  on Santino's revenge\nlist. His oath names  \"them\"  and  \"those fakes\"  — the ones behind the corpses — not the\nmercenaries doing a job. Whether that distinction survives his resurrection is a character\nquestion, not a plot one.\n\n   The squad's fate\n\n-   More than half dead   before the prologue.\n- The   dagger man   went over the cliff with Santino and drowned.\n-   Brant survived.   Presumably the rest of the squad too.\n\n  UNRESOLVED:   is Brant still hunting him, or does he believe the report?\n\n   Why the prologue works\n\nBecause it is a   competent escape that is actually a death  . Everything in it is true —\nthe coin, the whispered distraction, the vine, the water — and the conclusion is still wrong.\nThat is the trick the book opens with, and it teaches the reader how to read everything after\nit: watch what he does, not what he says happened.\n\n   Related\n\n  Brant   ·   The Royal House of Asherforth   ·   The Black Cats  \n"},{"t":"The Tribe","k":"Killer Petals · Worldbuilding","u":"killerpetals/lore/killerpetals--the-tribe.html","s":"  The Tribe\n\n  Status:   The congregation that killed him. Not soldiers, and that is the frightening part.\n\n   Confirmed\n\n  Appearance:  \n\n- Hides over one shoulder and nothing over the other.\n-   Arms painted wrist to elbow   in an unknown pattern —  \"lines and dots, like a road map\n  of somewhere I had never been.\" \n-   Bone through the ear.   A   cord of small teeth   around the throat — enough that\n  Santino  \"made myself stop counting.\" \n- They carry prisoners on a   carrying pole  , tied wrists and ankles, upside down.\n\n  Behaviour:  \n\n- They shout in a call-and-response:  \"HOLA!\"  answered by  \"ULA ULA ULA!\" \n-   They do not look at their prisoner.    \"Nobody looked at me. Not once, not even the ones\n  carrying me — the pole was just a thing they were doing with their shoulders.\" \n- They have   practised liturgy  : drums in twenty places, a hymn in an old language, a\n  masked celebrant, a fixed order of events.\n- They   feed the stone  , regularly and long-term.\n- They   dress the sacrifice   in branch-cloth — made of  \"small branches bound together\n  with something flexible and alive that was still moving.\" \n- They   make him drink   something  \"warm and thick\"  before the rite.\n- They took   everything   and left the sword.\n\n   The alarm in chapter 1\n\n  \"They were not soldiers. That was the first thing I understood and the least useful.\"\n\n  IMPLIED:   Santino expected soldiers because the men hunting him were hired blades. The\ntribe is not part of that hunt. He was taken by something entirely separate, which means his\nexecution may not have been ordered by his father at all.\n\n  UNRESOLVED:   who told them to take him. Or whether anyone did.\n\n   Their language\n\nFragments only:  \"Ola, hula huh u\"  from the hierophant. No glossary exists. Do not\nconstruct one — the text gives nothing to build on.\n\n   Whether they worship the rose\n\n  CONFIRMED:   they bring offerings to the stone the rose sits on.\n  UNRESOLVED:   whether the rose is what they think it is, or whether they have been feeding\nsomething that merely resembles it. The author's own note says the anti-deity is\n \"not evil, only negative\"  — see   The Sun Symbol  .\n\n   Where they are now\n\nGone. They took the rose, the offerings, and the drums, and left the dome empty and dry.\n  They are still out there and they have the rose.  \n\n   Related\n\n  The Dome   ·   The Rose   ·   Kord and the masked old man   ·   The Sun Symbol  \n"},{"t":"The World","k":"Killer Petals · Worldbuilding","u":"killerpetals/lore/killerpetals--the-world.html","s":"  The World\n\n  Status:   Two continents named indirectly. Most of the map is blank.\n\n   Established geography\n\n  Name   What it is   Confidence  \n --- --- --- \n    Asherforth     Santino's kingdom. Its royal line ends with him, per Brant.   CONFIRMED as a kingdom  \n    The Estmark Sea     The sea beneath the cliff. Santino drowns in it, twice.   CONFIRMED  \n    The eastern continent     Said to be  \"in our grasp\"  in Aldric's recorded voice. Holds at least the unnamed empire, Asherforth, and client kingdoms.   CONFIRMED as a name  \n    The palace     Corridors, a reservoir ( \"ice on the palace reservoir in the third month\" ), a ballroom with double doors, the Wilhelmshire-style great hall.   CONFIRMED  \n    The dome     Underground temple. See   The Dome  .   CONFIRMED  \n    The forest / the cliff     Where the prologue happens. Unnamed.   CONFIRMED as terrain  \n    The cave     Where he shelters between cliff and capture. Has a used fire pit.   CONFIRMED  \n\n  Not established:   names for the empire, the city, the tribe, the port they are heading\nfor, or the other three seas implied by  \"the great three seas.\" \n\n   The \"three seas\"\n\nOstrand says  \"How in the great three seas is this possible.\"  That implies a known set of\nthree seas, of which the Estmark is presumably one.   UNRESOLVED   which the others are.\n\n   Politics\n\n  CONFIRMED from Aldric's memory:    \"All the countries of the eastern continent are now in\nour grasp... their country's ruler swapped with our people.\" \n\nThat is a specific accusation: not conquest, but   replacement  . Rulers swapped for agents.\nWhether it is true, a lie inside a memory, or the raving of a compromised man is the\nquestion in   02 Open Decisions    2.\n\n  IMPLIED:   if it is true, then the empire's method is not war. It is substitution — and\nSantino is one of the few people in the world positioned to notice, because he is already\nsupposed to be dead.\n\n   Calendar\n\n-   \"The third month\"   — winter, since the reservoir is iced.\n- No year, era, or dating system is given.\n\n   Seasons and weather\n\nMonsoon-like rain at the cave.  \"The sun going down behind it in that fat, unhurried way.\" \nWet cliffs, cold wind, humid underground air smelling  \"of smoke and tallow and something\ngreen.\"  The world is wet and overcast more often than not.\n\n   Related\n\n  The Dome   ·   The Sun Symbol   ·   Asherforth   ·   02 Open Decisions  \n"},{"t":"Nakshatra","k":"A Letter to God · Characters","u":"alettertogod/lore/alettertogod--nakshatra.html","s":"  Nakshatra\n\n  Status: PROPOSED   — assembled from two chapters only.\n\n   On the page\n\n     \n --- --- \n  Age   School-going. Exact age not stated.  \n  Build   Pale, skinny, lean enough to slip through a crowd \"without much effort\".  \n  Hair   Short, messy, black — \"the only remarkable thing in him\".  \n  Eyes   Big, black. Dark circles under them, which the narration chooses to point at.  \n  Dress   Black blazer with gold patterns on wrist, collar and shoulders; plain white shirt; navy blue necktie; black trousers; brown belt and shoes.  \n  Carries   A brown leather handbag/backpack, at least three compartments.  \n  Address   Two-storey house with dark blue bedroom walls, marble floors, small front-yard garden, red brick boundary wall.  \n\n   What he does that tells us who he is\n\n-   He gets up after saying he won't.   \"I don't want to go today\" — then he sits up anyway. That single beat is the most characterising thing in the draft.\n-   His hands work without him.   Making tea is muscle memory; he does it while still half asleep.\n-   He pauses at the empty room opposite his own   and waits a minute, \"maybe hoping for it to open.\" He does this every morning, apparently.\n-   He notices a date and does arithmetic.   14 August 2034; two years.\n-   He stops at the letterbox.   Raises his hand. Stops halfway. Leaves.\n-   He does not answer roll call.   He is at the window, watching nothing in particular.\n-   He does not plead   when sent out. He goes \"obediently without making any plea for forgiveness.\"\n-   He cannot be seen.   In the corridor, people pass and \"no one seemed to notice him.\"\n-   He pinches his fingers   while waiting, \"as time started to weigh on his conscience.\"\n\n   Reading of the character\n\n  PROPOSED:   this is a boy in grief who has built a routine precisely so he does\nnot have to feel it. The tea, the uniform, the walk, the train — all of it is\nscaffolding. The two things he cannot do are open that door and post that letter.\n\nIf that reading is right, the novel is about the day the routine breaks.\n\n   Open\n\n- What happened two years ago —   02 Open Decisions  \n- Whose room is opposite his\n- What the letter says\n- Why nobody sees him\n\n   Related\n\n  01 Master Context   ·   02 Open Decisions   ·   03 Chapter Ledger   ·   04 Voice and Style  \n"},{"t":"The World of 2034","k":"A Letter to God · World and Setting","u":"alettertogod/lore/alettertogod--the-world-of-2034.html","s":"  The World of 2034\n\n  Status: PROPOSED   — only what the two chapters show.\n\n   What the text establishes\n\n  Date:   14 August 2034. A Thursday, though the text does not say so.\n\n  Technology:   the only future-facing object on the page is a   smartphone   —\n\"the screen said\", a music playlist, air pods. Everything else is ordinary:\ngas cylinder, steel cup, tea strainer, plastic chair, sliding glass door, steel\nguard railings, a minivan collecting waste.   The year is not doing much yet.  \n\n  Place:   an Indian setting. Names are Lalbagh, Malipur, Janaknagar. Whether\nthese are real, renamed, or invented is   UNRESOLVED  .\n\n  Weather:   a monsoon-adjacent morning. Cloudy, threatening rain, the smell of\nsoil, cold wind, wet grass, dew on the wooden and steel benches.\n\n   Named locations\n\n  Name   What it is   Status  \n --- --- --- \n    Janaknagar Station     Where he boards the Gati Express   UNRESOLVED — real or invented?  \n    Malipur     The train's origin   UNRESOLVED  \n    Lalbagh     The train's destination, presumably his school's city   UNRESOLVED  \n  The   Gati Express     White train with a code on the coaches   CONFIRMED by the text  \n  His house   Two storeys; ground floor with kitchen, drawing room, bathroom at the far end; dark blue bedroom above   CONFIRMED  \n  The school   Classroom with benches, a teacher's raised desk and platform, windows facing out   CONFIRMED  \n\n   The house, as catalogued\n\nThe draft spends real space on this, so it matters:\n\n-   Upstairs:   his bedroom (double bed, polished brown wood, wrinkled sheet, dark\n  blue walls) and one door opposite his own that stays closed.\n-   Stairs:   down to marble floor, \"invading the drowsiness\".\n-   Ground floor:   total silence. Light switch; kitchen immediately beside it.\n-   Kitchen:   an L-shaped platform along two walls, shelves below, two empty\n  compartments meant for a gas cylinder or storage.\n-   Next room:   drawing room, large sliding glass door onto the front yard garden.\n-   Bathroom:   far end of the wall sheltering kitchen and drawing room.\n-   Entrance:   two doors — an old western red cedar door older than the boy, and\n  outside it a thick carbon steel gate.\n-   Boundary:   red brick wall with a box cut into it reading   'LETTER BOX'  .\n\n   The thing the world is missing\n\nNothing supernatural has happened in two chapters. If this book has a speculative\nelement,   it has not appeared yet.   Do not assume one, and do not invent one.\n\n   Related\n\n  00 Canon Rules   ·   01 Master Context   ·   02 Open Decisions   ·   03 Chapter Ledger  \n"}];